export default {"id": "microsoft/playwright", "owner": "microsoft", "repo": "playwright", "description": "Playwright is a framework for Web Testing and Automation across Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit", "stars": "89.5k", "repositoryReadme": "https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/blob/main/README.md", "sample": true, "lastIndexed": "2026-11-22T19:20:33.533Z", "commit": "0492267", "generatedBy": "deepseek-v4.1-flash-free (opencode) + claude-haiku-4-5 (claude)", "source": {"type": "github", "url": "https://github.com/microsoft/playwright", "commit": "049226728d2da8054db18b04782a9848b755855e"}, "pages": [{"path": "overview.md", "title": "Project Overview", "parent": null, "description": "High-level purpose of the Playwright repository: a cross-browser automation and testing framework, its distribution channels (Test runner, CLI, MCP, library, VS Code extension), and how the docs, packages, and browser patch tooling fit together.", "files": ["README.md", "package.json", "CONTRIBUTING.md", "LICENSE", "NOTICE", "AGENTS.md"], "markdown": "# Project Overview\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [README.md](README.md)\n- [package.json](package.json)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [LICENSE](LICENSE)\n- [NOTICE](NOTICE)\n- [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md)\n</cite>\n\n## What Playwright Is\n\nPlaywright is a framework for web automation and testing. It drives Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit with a single API — in tests, in scripts, and as a tool for AI agents (`README.md:L7`).\n\nThe repository root declares the package `playwright-internal` as `private: true`, version `1.66.0-next`, described as \"A high-level API to automate web browsers\", licensed Apache-2.0, authored by Microsoft Corporation, and requiring Node.js 20 or later (`package.json:L2-L16`). The `NOTICE` file states that the software contains code derived from the Puppeteer project under the Apache 2.0 license (`NOTICE:L1-L5`), and the license text carries copyright notices for Microsoft Corporation and Google Inc. (`LICENSE:L189-L190`).\n\n`CONTRIBUTING.md` frames the repository as a \"multi-package repository that uses npm workspaces\", directing browser-API work to `packages/playwright-core` and test-runner work to `packages/playwright` (`CONTRIBUTING.md:L38`). This matches the root `workspaces` field, which includes every package under `packages/*` (`package.json:L55-L57`).\n\n**Section sources**\n- [README.md:L7-L7](README.md#L7-L7)\n- [package.json:L1-L17](package.json#L1-L17)\n- [package.json:L55-L57](package.json#L55-L57)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L38-L38](CONTRIBUTING.md#L38-L38)\n- [NOTICE:L1-L5](NOTICE#L1-L5)\n- [LICENSE:L189-L190](LICENSE#L189-L190)\n\n## Distribution Channels\n\nThe README presents five ways to consume Playwright, each with its own install path (`README.md:L11-L19`):\n\n| Channel | Best for | Install |\n|---|---|---|\n| Playwright Test | End-to-end testing | `npm init playwright@latest` |\n| Playwright CLI | Coding agents (Claude Code, Copilot) | `npm i -g @playwright/cli@latest` |\n| Playwright MCP | AI agents and LLM-driven automation | `npx @playwright/mcp@latest` |\n| Playwright Library | Browser automation scripts | `npm i playwright` |\n| VS Code Extension | Test authoring and debugging in VS Code | Marketplace install link |\n\nPlaywright Test is described as a full-featured test runner with browser isolation, auto-waiting, and web-first assertions; it can also be added manually with `npm i -D @playwright/test` followed by `npx playwright install` (`README.md:L23-L38`). The Playwright Library exposes `chromium` for screenshot capture, PDF generation, device emulation, and network interception without a test runner (`README.md:L212-L271`). The MCP server gives AI agents browser control through the Model Context Protocol using structured accessibility snapshots and element refs (`README.md:L159-L206`). The CLI is positioned as more token-efficient than MCP because commands avoid loading large tool schemas and accessibility trees into the model context (`README.md:L113`).\n\nPlaywright is additionally available for Python, .NET, and Java (`README.md:L305-L307`).\n\n**Section sources**\n- [README.md:L11-L19](README.md#L11-L19)\n- [README.md:L23-L38](README.md#L23-L38)\n- [README.md:L113-L113](README.md#L113-L113)\n- [README.md:L159-L206](README.md#L159-L206)\n- [README.md:L212-L271](README.md#L212-L271)\n- [README.md:L305-L307](README.md#L305-L307)\n\n## Cross-Browser Support\n\nPlaywright supports Chromium, WebKit, and Firefox on Linux, macOS, and Windows, with headless and headed execution on all platforms; Chromium uses Chrome for Testing by default (`README.md:L295-L303`). The README embeds generated version badges for Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit in the header and in the support table, marked by `GEN:...` comment markers (`README.md:L3`, `README.md:L299-L301`).\n\nBecause Playwright ships patched browser builds, the repository contains browser patch tooling under `browser_patches/`. The tree includes a WebKit embedder with macOS (`browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/`) and Windows (`browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/`) host applications, build and run scripts (`browser_patches/webkit/pw_run.sh`, `browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh`, `browser_patches/webkit/UPSTREAM_CONFIG.sh`), and a `browser_patches/winldd/` directory with a `PrintDeps.cpp` dependency printer and its own `README.md`. The root `package.json` exposes a `roll` script that runs `node utils/roll_browser.js` (`package.json:L46`), consistent with this browser-roll workflow.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [README.md:L295-L303](README.md#L295-L303)\n- [README.md:L3-L3](README.md#L3-L3)\n- [README.md:L299-L301](README.md#L299-L301)\n- [package.json:L46-L46](package.json#L46-L46)\n\n## Repository Layout\n\n`AGENTS.md` provides the clearest map of the monorepo (`AGENTS.md:L6-L39`). Its package table distinguishes the browser automation engine, the public package, the test-runner entry point, a standalone client, and the internal RPC protocol definitions:\n\n| Package | npm name | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| `playwright-core` | `playwright-core` | Browser automation engine: client, server, dispatchers, protocol |\n| `playwright` | `playwright` | Test runner + browser automation (public package) |\n| `playwright-test` | `@playwright/test` | Test runner entry point |\n| `playwright-client` | `@playwright/client` | Standalone client package |\n| `protocol` | *(internal)* | RPC protocol definitions (`spec/*.yml` → generated validators and channel types) |\n\n`AGENTS.md` also lists per-browser distributions (`playwright-chromium`, `playwright-firefox`, `playwright-webkit`) and binary packages (`playwright-browser-chromium`, `playwright-browser-firefox`, `playwright-browser-webkit`), plus tooling packages such as `html-reporter`, `trace-viewer`, `recorder`, `web`, and `injected` (`AGENTS.md:L16-L29`).\n\nKey directories named there are `tests/` for all test suites, `docs/src/` as the source of truth for public TypeScript types, `docs/src/api/` for per-class API reference, `utils/` for build scripts and code generation, and `browser_patches/` for browser engine patches (`AGENTS.md:L31-L39`).\n\n**Section sources**\n- [AGENTS.md:L6-L39](AGENTS.md#L6-L39)\n\n## How Docs, Code, and Types Interlock\n\nDocumentation is not an afterthought in this repository: `docs/src` is treated as the source of truth. `CONTRIBUTING.md` requires that every part of the public API be documented in `docs/src` in the same change that adds or changes the API, using markdown files with a custom structure, and notes that various other files are generated from that API specification (`CONTRIBUTING.md:L49-L55`). It warns that some files are generated by the build, so the watch process can override edits made in the wrong file — explicitly citing TypeScript types for the API as generated from `docs/src` (`CONTRIBUTING.md:L40`).\n\nThe root scripts reflect this coupling. The `doc` script runs `node utils/doclint/cli.js` (`package.json:L37`), and the aggregate `lint` script chains `eslint`, `tsc`, `doc`, `check-deps`, channel generation, type generation, test linting, type tests, package consistency, and a dependency-age check (`package.json:L38`). The parallel `flint` script runs the same set of checks concurrently (`package.json:L41`). `AGENTS.md` documents `flint` as running eslint, tsc, doclint, check-deps, generate_channels, generate_types, lint-tests, test-types, lint-packages, code-snippet linting, and check-dependency-age (`AGENTS.md:L50-L58`).\n\nThe documentation site itself lives elsewhere: `CONTRIBUTING.md` states that `playwright.dev` is a separate repository and that docs from `docs/src` are frequently rolled there, with a \"roll docs\" procedure for unusual changes (`CONTRIBUTING.md:L139-L146`).\n\n**Section sources**\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L40-L40](CONTRIBUTING.md#L40-L40)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L49-L55](CONTRIBUTING.md#L49-L55)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L139-L146](CONTRIBUTING.md#L139-L146)\n- [package.json:L37-L41](package.json#L37-L41)\n- [AGENTS.md:L50-L58](AGENTS.md#L50-L58)\n\n## Development Workflow and Test Surfaces\n\nContributors must run Node.js 20 or later, then `npm ci`, `npm run watch`, and `npx playwright install` (`CONTRIBUTING.md:L20-L36`). `AGENTS.md` documents `npm run build` for a full build and `npm run watch` for watch mode, advising that generated files such as types, channels, and validators are produced automatically by watch (`AGENTS.md:L41-L48`).\n\nThe root `package.json` defines per-browser and per-suite test entry points: `ctest`, `ftest`, and `wtest` for the library config filtered to Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit respectively; `wvtest` for webview; `atest` for Android; `etest` for Electron; `itest` for installation; `stest` for stress; `biditest` for BiDi; `ttest` for the test runner suite; and `test-mcp` / `ctest-mcp` for MCP tools (`package.json:L19-L34`). `CONTRIBUTING.md` identifies the two most important local suites as library tests (`npm run ctest`, or the slower `npm run test` across three browsers) and test runner tests (`npm run ttest`) (`CONTRIBUTING.md:L61-L75`). Tests are required to be hermetic and to work on macOS, Linux, and Windows (`CONTRIBUTING.md:L79`).\n\n`AGENTS.md` adds a fixture-based decision rule for where a test belongs: needing `browser`/`browserType`/`context` points to `tests/library/`, while needing only `page` plus `server` points to `tests/page/` (`AGENTS.md:L80-L89`). It also documents the DEPS system, where `DEPS.list` files enforce import boundaries, with client code never importing server code and vice versa — communication is only through the protocol (`AGENTS.md:L106-L111`).\n\n**Section sources**\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L20-L36](CONTRIBUTING.md#L20-L36)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L61-L79](CONTRIBUTING.md#L61-L79)\n- [package.json:L19-L34](package.json#L19-L34)\n- [AGENTS.md:L41-L48](AGENTS.md#L41-L48)\n- [AGENTS.md:L80-L89](AGENTS.md#L80-L89)\n- [AGENTS.md:L106-L111](AGENTS.md#L106-L111)\n\n## Contribution and Licensing Model\n\nPlaywright requires a corresponding issue for every contribution except minor documentation fixes, and unsolicited pull requests without a linked issue or prior approval are closed (`CONTRIBUTING.md:L5-L16`). Commit messages follow the Semantic Commit Messages format with labels `fix`, `feat`, `docs`, `test`, `devops`, and `chore` (`CONTRIBUTING.md:L83-L99`). New dependencies face a high bar and should be discussed in an issue first (`CONTRIBUTING.md:L126-L128`). Most contributions require a Contributor License Agreement (`CONTRIBUTING.md:L148-L156`), and the project adopts the Microsoft Open Source Code of Conduct (`CONTRIBUTING.md:L158-L162`).\n\nThe repository is Apache-2.0 licensed (`package.json:L17`), with the full license terms in `LICENSE` and the Puppeteer derivation noted in `NOTICE`. The README points to the contribution guide and changelog among its resources (`README.md:L309-L318`).\n\n**Section sources**\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L5-L16](CONTRIBUTING.md#L5-L16)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L83-L99](CONTRIBUTING.md#L83-L99)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L126-L128](CONTRIBUTING.md#L126-L128)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L148-L162](CONTRIBUTING.md#L148-L162)\n- [package.json:L17-L17](package.json#L17-L17)\n- [README.md:L309-L318](README.md#L309-L318)\n"}, {"path": "glossary.md", "title": "Glossary", "parent": null, "description": "Definitions of core Playwright domain terms such as browser, browser context, page, frame, locator, actionability, ARIA snapshot, trace, and test project.", "files": ["docs/src/languages.md", "docs/src/browsers.md", "docs/src/browser-contexts.md", "docs/src/locators.md", "docs/src/actionability.md", "docs/src/aria-snapshots.md"], "markdown": "# Glossary\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [docs/src/languages.md](docs/src/languages.md)\n- [docs/src/browsers.md](docs/src/browsers.md)\n- [docs/src/browser-contexts.md](docs/src/browser-contexts.md)\n- [docs/src/locators.md](docs/src/locators.md)\n- [docs/src/actionability.md](docs/src/actionability.md)\n- [docs/src/aria-snapshots.md](docs/src/aria-snapshots.md)\n</cite>\n\nThis glossary defines the core terms used in the attached Playwright documentation.\n\n## Core object model\n\nPlaywright's runtime model is hierarchical: a browser launches contexts, contexts contain pages, and pages expose locators.\n\n| Term | Definition |\n| :- | :- |\n| **Browser** | A launched browser instance created from `chromium`, `firefox`, or `webkit`. It can create browser contexts. |\n| **Browser context** | An isolated, incognito-like profile created by a browser. Contexts are fast and cheap to create and remain isolated even inside one browser. |\n| **Page** | A page belonging to a context. The test runner creates a context per test and provides a default `Page` in it. |\n| **Locator** | A way to find element(s) on a page \"at any moment.\" It is the basis of auto-waiting and retry-ability; each action locates an up-to-date DOM element. |\n| **FrameLocator** | A locator scoped to a frame selected by a selector. Locator-building methods are available on `Locator` and `FrameLocator`, so locators can be chained through a frame. |\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"chromium / firefox / webkit\"] -->|launch| B[\"Browser\"]\n  B -->|newContext| C[\"BrowserContext\"]\n  C -->|newPage| P[\"Page\"]\n  P -->|\"locator / getBy*\"| L[\"Locator\"]\n  P -->|frameLocator| F[\"FrameLocator\"]\n  F -->|\"getBy*\"| L\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [docs/src/browser-contexts.md:L42-L46](docs/src/browser-contexts.md#L42-L46)\n- [docs/src/locators.md:L152-L159](docs/src/locators.md#L152-L159)\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/browser-contexts.md:L8-L12](docs/src/browser-contexts.md#L8-L12)\n- [docs/src/browser-contexts.md:L26-L46](docs/src/browser-contexts.md#L26-L46)\n- [docs/src/locators.md:L8-L9](docs/src/locators.md#L8-L9)\n- [docs/src/locators.md:L111-L121](docs/src/locators.md#L111-L121)\n- [docs/src/locators.md:L152-L159](docs/src/locators.md#L152-L159)\n\n## Browser families and channels\n\nEach Playwright version requires specific browser binary versions, so updating Playwright may require reinstalling browsers. Playwright runs on Chromium, WebKit, Firefox, branded Chrome/Edge, and emulated tablet/mobile devices.\n\n| Term | Definition |\n| :- | :- |\n| **Chromium** | Open-source Chromium build used for Chrome/Edge-like testing. Playwright ships a regular Chromium build for headed work and a separate chromium headless shell for headless mode. |\n| **Chromium headless shell** | The headless Chromium binary used when the `channel` option is not specified. |\n| **Chromium new headless mode** | Opt-in mode selected with `channel: 'chromium'`; the docs call it \"the real Chrome browser,\" more authentic and feature-rich than the headless shell. |\n| **Firefox** | Playwright's Firefox build matches recent Firefox Stable. Branded Firefox is not supported because Playwright relies on patches. |\n| **WebKit** | Derived from the latest WebKit main branch, often before Safari incorporates updates. Branded Safari is not supported because Playwright relies on patches. |\n| **Branded channel** | A Chrome or Edge release channel: `chrome`, `msedge`, `chrome-beta`, `msedge-beta`, `chrome-dev`, `msedge-dev`, `chrome-canary`, `msedge-canary`. |\n| **Device emulation** | Running tests against emulated tablet or mobile devices from Playwright's device registry. |\n\nPlaywright can operate against branded browsers already on the machine, but does not install them by default.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/browsers.md:L8-L10](docs/src/browsers.md#L8-L10)\n- [docs/src/browsers.md:L160-L1223](docs/src/browsers.md#L160-L1223)\n- [docs/src/browsers.md:L337-L345](docs/src/browsers.md#L337-L345)\n- [docs/src/browsers.md:L367-L373](docs/src/browsers.md#L367-L373)\n- [docs/src/browsers.md:L444-L448](docs/src/browsers.md#L444-L448)\n- [docs/src/browsers.md:L582-L590](docs/src/browsers.md#L582-L590)\n\n## Isolation and browser contexts\n\nTest isolation means each test runs independently, with its own local storage, session storage, and cookies. Playwright implements isolation with browser contexts, which are equivalent to incognito-like profiles. A context is created for each test by the test runner, which also supplies a default page; when using the library directly, contexts are created manually. Multiple contexts can exist in one test for multi-user scenarios, and contexts can also emulate mobile devices, permissions, locale, and color scheme.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/browser-contexts.md:L8-L12](docs/src/browser-contexts.md#L8-L12)\n- [docs/src/browser-contexts.md:L26-L46](docs/src/browser-contexts.md#L26-L46)\n- [docs/src/browser-contexts.md:L73-L77](docs/src/browser-contexts.md#L73-L77)\n- [docs/src/browser-contexts.md:L79-L90](docs/src/browser-contexts.md#L79-L90)\n\n## Locators and frames\n\nLocators are the central mechanism for auto-waiting and retry-ability. They represent a way to find elements at any moment, and every action re-locates the element so a re-rendered DOM is handled correctly. Recommended built-in locators are:\n\n| Locator builder | Finds by |\n| :- | :- |\n| `Page.getByRole` | explicit and implicit accessibility attributes |\n| `Page.getByText` | text content |\n| `Page.getByLabel` | associated label text |\n| `Page.getByPlaceholder` | placeholder text |\n| `Page.getByAltText` | text alternative |\n| `Page.getByTitle` | title attribute |\n| `Page.getByTestId` | configured test id attribute (`data-testid` by default) |\n\nLocator builders can be chained. A **frame** is addressed through `page.frameLocator(selector)`, and the returned `FrameLocator` scopes subsequent locator calls to that frame:\n\n```js\nconst locator = page\n    .frameLocator('#my-frame')\n    .getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' });\n\nawait locator.click();\n```\n\nAll locators work with elements in Shadow DOM by default, except that XPath does not pierce shadow roots and closed-mode shadow roots are unsupported.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/locators.md:L8-L21](docs/src/locators.md#L8-L21)\n- [docs/src/locators.md:L111-L121](docs/src/locators.md#L111-L121)\n- [docs/src/locators.md:L152-L160](docs/src/locators.md#L152-L160)\n- [docs/src/locators.md:L700-L704](docs/src/locators.md#L700-L704)\n\n## Actionability\n\nActionability is the set of checks Playwright performs before an action. Playwright auto-waits for the relevant checks to pass and only then acts; if they do not pass within the timeout, the action fails with `TimeoutError`. For `Locator.click`, the locator must resolve to exactly one element and the element must be visible, stable, receive events, enabled, and not inert.\n\n| Check | Definition |\n| :- | :- |\n| **Visible** | Non-empty bounding box and no `visibility:hidden`. Zero-size and `display:none` elements are not visible; `opacity:0` elements are visible. |\n| **Stable** | Same bounding box for at least two consecutive animation frames. |\n| **Enabled** | Not disabled. Disabled covers form controls with `[disabled]`, controls inside a disabled `fieldset`, and descendants of `[aria-disabled=true]`. |\n| **Editable** | Enabled and not readonly. Readonly covers form controls with `[readonly]`, or `[aria-readonly=true]` on a role that supports it. |\n| **Not Inert** | Not part of an inert subtree. Elements with the `inert` attribute and their descendants are inert. |\n| **Receives Events** | The element is the hit target of the pointer event at the action point; another element, often an overlay, does not capture it. |\n\nThe `force` option disables non-essential actionability checks; for `Locator.click`, forcing skips the check that the target actually receives click events.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/actionability.md:L8-L17](docs/src/actionability.md#L8-L17)\n- [docs/src/actionability.md:L44-L48](docs/src/actionability.md#L44-L48)\n- [docs/src/actionability.md:L82-L121](docs/src/actionability.md#L82-L121)\n\n## ARIA snapshots\n\nAn ARIA snapshot is a YAML representation of the accessibility tree of a page. A snapshot template is a constraint, not necessarily a complete serialization. Each node follows `- role \"name\" [attribute=value]`, where role is the ARIA or HTML role, the quoted name is the accessible name, and bracketed attributes describe states such as `checked`, `disabled`, `expanded`, `level`, or `pressed`.\n\n```yaml\n- heading \"Title\" [level=1]\n- heading \"Subtitle\" [level=2]\n```\n\n`PageAssertions.toMatchAriaSnapshot` compares a page's accessible structure with a template; `LocatorAssertions.toMatchAriaSnapshot` does the same for a locator's scope. Matching is case-sensitive, normalizes whitespace, and is order-sensitive. A single-node template can match at any depth; multiple top-level nodes must match under the same parent, in order. Partial matching omits attributes or accessible names, while the `/children` property controls strictness:\n\n| Mode | Behavior |\n| :- | :- |\n| `contain` (default) | Matches if all specified children are present in order. |\n| `equal` | Direct children exactly match the specified list in order. |\n| `deep-equal` | Children and nested children exactly match the specified list in order. |\n\n`Page.ariaSnapshot` and `Locator.ariaSnapshot` generate the YAML representation programmatically.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/aria-snapshots.md:L142-L160](docs/src/aria-snapshots.md#L142-L160)\n- [docs/src/aria-snapshots.md:L168-L170](docs/src/aria-snapshots.md#L168-L170)\n- [docs/src/aria-snapshots.md:L210-L219](docs/src/aria-snapshots.md#L210-L219)\n- [docs/src/aria-snapshots.md:L222-L237](docs/src/aria-snapshots.md#L222-L237)\n- [docs/src/aria-snapshots.md:L292-L298](docs/src/aria-snapshots.md#L292-L298)\n- [docs/src/aria-snapshots.md:L430-L438](docs/src/aria-snapshots.md#L430-L438)\n\n## Test projects\n\nA **test project** is a named entry in the `projects` array of the Playwright configuration. Each project has a `name` and a `use` block containing options such as a device descriptor or browser channel. Projects let the same tests run across multiple browsers, viewports, and branded browsers; Playwright runs all projects by default, and `--project` selects one. The browser guide's example defines projects named `chromium`, `firefox`, `webkit`, `Mobile Chrome`, `Mobile Safari`, `Google Chrome`, and `Microsoft Edge`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/browsers.md:L165-L233](docs/src/browsers.md#L165-L233)\n\n## Language bindings\n\nAll core browser-automation features are supported in every language; testing ecosystem integration differs.\n\n| Language | Test integration |\n| :- | :- |\n| JavaScript/TypeScript | Playwright's own test runner, with parallelization, screenshot assertions, an HTML reporter, and automatic tracing. |\n| Python | Pytest plugin, providing context isolation and multiple browser configurations. |\n| Java | Any framework such as JUnit or TestNG. |\n| .NET | MSTest, NUnit, xUnit, and xUnit v3 base classes. |\n\n**Automatic tracing.** The JavaScript/TypeScript test runner is documented as providing automatic tracing alongside parallelization, screenshot assertions, and an HTML reporter; the attached material does not describe trace contents or the trace viewer.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/languages.md:L6-L34](docs/src/languages.md#L6-L34)\n"}, {"path": "architecture.md", "title": "Architecture", "parent": null, "description": "Landing page for repository architecture: monorepo layout, build and lint pipeline, and the browser patch subsystem that produces the bundled browsers. How the repository is organized as an npm workspaces monorepo, its TypeScript path aliases and ignore rules, and the npm scripts plus ESLint/TypeScript configuration that build, type-check, and lint the codebase.", "files": ["README.md", "package.json", "docs/src/intro-js.md", "browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh", "packages/dashboard/index.html", "tsconfig.json", ".gitignore", "eslint.config.mjs", "CONTRIBUTING.md", "packages/dashboard/package.json", "packages/extension/package.json", "packages/extension/manifest.json", "packages/playwright/cli.js", "packages/playwright/index.d.ts", "packages/playwright/index.js", "packages/playwright/index.mjs", "packages/playwright/src/agents/agentParser.ts", "packages/playwright/src/agents/generateAgents.ts", "packages/playwright/src/cli/reportActions.ts", "packages/playwright/src/cli/testActions.ts", "packages/playwright/src/common/config.ts", "packages/playwright/src/common/configLoader.ts"], "markdown": "# Architecture\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [README.md](README.md)\n- [package.json](package.json)\n- [docs/src/intro-js.md](docs/src/intro-js.md)\n- [browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh](browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh)\n- [packages/dashboard/index.html](packages/dashboard/index.html)\n- [tsconfig.json](tsconfig.json)\n- [.gitignore](.gitignore)\n- [eslint.config.mjs](eslint.config.mjs)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [packages/dashboard/package.json](packages/dashboard/package.json)\n- [packages/extension/package.json](packages/extension/package.json)\n- [packages/extension/manifest.json](packages/extension/manifest.json)\n- [packages/playwright/cli.js](packages/playwright/cli.js)\n- [packages/playwright/index.d.ts](packages/playwright/index.d.ts)\n- [packages/playwright/index.js](packages/playwright/index.js)\n- [packages/playwright/index.mjs](packages/playwright/index.mjs)\n- [packages/playwright/src/agents/agentParser.ts](packages/playwright/src/agents/agentParser.ts)\n- [packages/playwright/src/agents/generateAgents.ts](packages/playwright/src/agents/generateAgents.ts)\n- [packages/playwright/src/cli/reportActions.ts](packages/playwright/src/cli/reportActions.ts)\n- [packages/playwright/src/cli/testActions.ts](packages/playwright/src/cli/testActions.ts)\n- [packages/playwright/src/common/config.ts](packages/playwright/src/common/config.ts)\n- [packages/playwright/src/common/configLoader.ts](packages/playwright/src/common/configLoader.ts)\n</cite>\n\nPlaywright is organized as a single npm-workspaces monorepo whose root package (`playwright-internal`, private, `1.66.0-next`) coordinates several publishable and internal packages, a documentation source tree, a large test suite, and a separate subsystem that patches and builds the bundled browsers. The root `package.json` declares `\"workspaces\": [\"packages/*\"]` and requires Node `>=20`.\n\n## Monorepo Layout\n\nThe workspace glob pulls in every directory under `packages/`. The two packages that matter for the shipped product are `packages/playwright-core` (browser automation APIs) and `packages/playwright` (the test runner). `packages/playwright` is a thin wrapper over the core package: `index.js` does `module.exports = require('playwright-core')`, `index.d.ts` re-exports `playwright-core`, and `index.mjs` re-exports plus provides a default export. Its CLI entry `cli.js` requires `./lib/program` and calls `program.parse(process.argv)`.\n\n| Area | Path | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| Core automation | `packages/playwright-core` | Browser APIs, server, client, protocol |\n| Test runner | `packages/playwright` | Runner, CLI, reporters, config loading |\n| UI bundles | `packages/dashboard`, `packages/trace-viewer`, `packages/html-reporter`, `packages/recorder`, `packages/web` | React/Vite front ends |\n| Browser extension | `packages/extension` | MV3 extension (`manifest.json` declares `debugger`, `tabs`, `tabGroups`, `storage` permissions) |\n| Docs source | `docs/src` | Markdown API reference that generates TypeScript types |\n| Test suites | `tests/*` | Library, test-runner, MCP, extension, installation suites |\n| Browser patches | `browser_patches/` | Sources for patched browser builds |\n\nThe dashboard package is `private` and `\"type\": \"module\"`, with `index.html` mounting `/src/index.tsx` into `#root`. The extension package is also `private` and versioned independently at `0.6.0`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [package.json:L1-L13](package.json#L1-L13)\n- [package.json:L55-L57](package.json#L55-L57)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L38-L40](CONTRIBUTING.md#L38-L40)\n- [packages/playwright/index.js:L17-L17](packages/playwright/index.js#L17-L17)\n- [packages/playwright/index.d.ts:L17-L17](packages/playwright/index.d.ts#L17-L17)\n- [packages/playwright/index.mjs:L16-L18](packages/playwright/index.mjs#L16-L18)\n- [packages/playwright/cli.js:L18-L19](packages/playwright/cli.js#L18-L19)\n- [packages/dashboard/package.json:L1-L5](packages/dashboard/package.json#L1-L5)\n- [packages/dashboard/index.html:L16-L28](packages/dashboard/index.html#L16-L28)\n- [packages/extension/package.json:L1-L18](packages/extension/package.json#L1-L18)\n- [packages/extension/manifest.json:L1-L19](packages/extension/manifest.json#L1-L19)\n\n## TypeScript Configuration and Path Aliases\n\nThe root `tsconfig.json` is a type-check-only configuration: `noEmit: true`, `strict: true`, `isolatedModules: true`, `moduleResolution: \"bundler\"`, `jsx: \"react-jsx\"`, and `useUnknownInCatchVariables: false`. It includes `packages` and excludes generated or separately-configured trees: `packages/*/lib`, `packages/extension`, and `packages/playwright-core/src/tools/skills`.\n\nPath aliases encode the architectural boundary between type-only imports and real dependency imports — the file's own comment notes that `@foo/*` is for importing types only, while `.../lib/*` means require the dependency:\n\n| Alias | Target |\n|---|---|\n| `@dashboard/*` | `packages/dashboard/src/*` |\n| `@trace-viewer/*` | `packages/trace-viewer/src/*` |\n| `@html-reporter/*` | `packages/html-reporter/src/*` |\n| `@injected/*` | `packages/injected/src/*` |\n| `@isomorphic/*` | `packages/isomorphic/*` |\n| `@utils/*` | `packages/utils/*` |\n| `@protocol/*` | `packages/protocol/src/*` |\n| `@recorder/*` | `packages/recorder/src/*` |\n| `@web/*` | `packages/web/src/*` |\n| `playwright-core/lib/*` | `packages/playwright-core/src/*` |\n| `playwright/lib/*` | `packages/playwright/src/*` |\n\nIgnore rules keep build output out of version control: `.gitignore` lists `node_modules/`, `/test-results/`, `/coverage/`, `.local-browsers/`, `/packages/playwright-core/src/generated`, `packages/*/lib/`, `drivers/`, `/firefox/`, and per-package `LICENSE`/`NOTICE`/`README.md` files that are generated at publish time.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [tsconfig.json:L1-L48](tsconfig.json#L1-L48)\n- [.gitignore:L1-L44](.gitignore#L1-L44)\n\n## Build, Type-Check, and Lint Pipeline\n\nThe build is driven by small Node scripts rather than a bundler CLI: `build` runs `node utils/build/build.js`, `watch` runs the same script with `--watch --lint`, and `clean` runs `node utils/build/clean.js`. Type checking is `tsc -p . && tsc -p packages/extension/tsconfig.json && tsc -p packages/extension/tsconfig.ui.json`, covering the root project plus the two extension projects that the root config excludes.\n\n`npm run lint` is a composite gate, and `flint` runs the same checks concurrently:\n\n```text\nnpm run eslint && npm run tsc && npm run doc && npm run check-deps &&\nnode utils/generate_channels.js && node utils/generate_types/ &&\nnpm run lint-tests && npm run test-types && npm run lint-packages &&\nnpm run check-dependency-age\n```\n\nAdditional guards are `lint-packages` (`node utils/workspace.js --ensure-consistent`), `check-deps` (`node utils/check_deps.js`), `check-dependency-age`, and `test-types`, which regenerates types and type-checks both `utils/generate_types/test` and `tests/`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"npm run lint\"] --> B[\"eslint --cache\"]\n  A --> C[\"tsc -p . (root + extension)\"]\n  A --> D[\"utils/doclint/cli.js\"]\n  A --> E[\"check_deps.js\"]\n  A --> F[\"generate_channels.js + generate_types\"]\n  A --> G[\"lint_tests.js\"]\n  A --> H[\"test-types\"]\n  A --> I[\"workspace.js --ensure-consistent\"]\n  A --> J[\"check_dependency_age.js\"]\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [package.json:L35-L45](package.json#L35-L45)\n\n`eslint.config.mjs` is a flat config exporting an array. A leading `ignores` block excludes generated and vendored trees (`packages/*/lib/`, `packages/playwright-core/src/generated/*`, `packages/playwright-core/src/third_party/`, `packages/playwright-core/src/tools/skills/`, `utils/`, `examples`, `tests/assets/`). `baseRules` enforces style and anti-patterns — single quotes, 2-space `@stylistic/indent`, `brace-style: 1tbs`, `prefer-const`, `no-var`, `eqeqeq` — plus a mandatory copyright header via `notice/notice` using `utils/copyright.js`.\n\nLayered overrides encode package boundaries: `packages/**/*.ts` bans `process.exit`/`process.stdout`/`process.stderr` in favor of `gracefullyProcessExitDoNotHang`; `packages/playwright-core/src/client/**` and `packages/protocol/src/**` may not use Node globals; `packages/injected/src/**` may not use `window`, `document`, `setTimeout` and similar, with messages pointing at `InjectedScript` equivalents; `packages/isomorphic/**` is restricted from both Node and web globals; `packages/playwright-core/src/tools/**` may not import from `client/`; and `packages/playwright-core/src/**` blocks `**/coreBundle` imports outside `entry/`. Server and utils files get a custom `progress/await-must-use-progress` rule from `utils/eslint-plugin-progress`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [package.json:L35-L48](package.json#L35-L48)\n- [eslint.config.mjs:L39-L68](eslint.config.mjs#L39-L68)\n- [eslint.config.mjs:L70-L216](eslint.config.mjs#L70-L216)\n- [eslint.config.mjs:L327-L500](eslint.config.mjs#L327-L500)\n\n## Browser Patch Subsystem\n\n`browser_patches/` holds the sources used to produce Playwright's bundled browsers, kept alongside the JavaScript packages but outside the npm workspace. The visible subtrees are `webkit/` (an embedder with `embedder/Playwright/` containing `mac/` Objective-C sources plus a `CMakeLists.txt`, `win/` C++ sources plus a `CMakeLists.txt`, an Xcode project, and shared `Configurations/*.xcconfig`), `winldd/` (a `PrintDeps` Visual Studio project plus `build.sh`, `buildwin.bat`, `archive.sh`, `clean.sh`, `BUILD_NUMBER`, `EXPECTED_BUILDS`), and the shared `roll_from_upstream.sh`.\n\nThe roll script takes a path to a `playwright-browsers` checkout and an optional filter, validating that the directory contains `browser_patches/build_flavors.sh` as a marker rather than trusting the directory name. It then `rsync -av --delete` each file set into place:\n\n| Filter | Synced paths |\n|---|---|\n| `firefox` | `./firefox/juggler/`, `./firefox/patches/`, `./firefox/preferences/`, `./firefox/UPSTREAM_CONFIG.sh` |\n| `webkit` | `./webkit/embedder/`, `./webkit/patches/`, `./webkit/pw_run.sh`, `./webkit/UPSTREAM_CONFIG.sh` |\n| `winldd` | `./winldd/` |\n| *(none)* | all three sets |\n\nAn unknown filter prints the supported list and exits non-zero. The `--delete` flag makes the local directories match upstream exactly. Note that `.gitignore` ignores `/firefox/`, so the Firefox patch tree is not tracked here while `browser_patches/webkit/` and `browser_patches/winldd/` are.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh:L1-L71](browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh#L1-L71)\n- [.gitignore:L41-L44](.gitignore#L41-L44)\n\n## Development and Test Entry Points\n\n`CONTRIBUTING.md` documents the loop: `npm ci`, then `npm run watch`, then `npx playwright install`; generated files (for example TypeScript types derived from `docs/src`) can be overwritten by the watch process if edited in the wrong place. Tests are grouped by script: `ctest` / `ftest` / `wtest` run the library config against a single browser project, `test` runs all browsers, `ttest` runs the test-runner suite via the stable test runner in `tests/playwright-test`, and further configs cover `wvtest`, `atest`, `etest`, `itest`, `stest`, `biditest`, `test-mcp`, and `test-extension`. Custom browser binaries are injected with `CRPATH`, `WKPATH`, or `FFPATH`.\n\nThe runtime configuration path these tests exercise starts at `configLoader.ts`, which loads the user config file, validates it, and constructs `FullConfigInternal` in `config.ts`; that class normalizes projects, workers, retries, reporters, and web servers, resolving reporters and setup scripts relative to the config directory.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L31-L45](CONTRIBUTING.md#L31-L45)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L57-L79](CONTRIBUTING.md#L57-L79)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L130-L137](CONTRIBUTING.md#L130-L137)\n- [package.json:L18-L34](package.json#L18-L34)\n- [packages/playwright/src/common/configLoader.ts:L102-L125](packages/playwright/src/common/configLoader.ts#L102-L125)\n- [packages/playwright/src/common/config.ts:L44-L142](packages/playwright/src/common/config.ts#L44-L142)\n"}, {"path": "contributing.md", "title": "Contributing and Community", "parent": null, "description": "How to contribute code and docs, the code of conduct, issue filing guidance, and the agent-facing repository conventions.", "files": ["CONTRIBUTING.md", "CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md", "FILING_ISSUES.md", "AGENTS.md"], "markdown": "# Contributing and Community\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [FILING_ISSUES.md](FILING_ISSUES.md)\n- [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md)\n</cite>\n\n## Choosing an Issue and Submission Policy\n\nPlaywright **requires a corresponding issue** for every contribution, with the exception of minor documentation fixes. If a bug or feature is not listed, file a new issue first so maintainers and the community can give early feedback before work starts on a pull request. State clearly whether you intend to work on it; after triage, maintainers decide whether the task goes to the **core team**, an **automated agent**, or a **community contributor**. Only after the issue is assigned to you should you submit a PR.\n\nPull requests without a linked issue or prior approval are closed, and PRs that miss the quality bar or lack human oversight (including low-quality agentic submissions) are closed without explanation.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[File an issue<br/>state intent to work] --> B[Triage by maintainers]\n  B --> C{Assigned to?}\n  C -->|Core team| D[Maintainers implement]\n  C -->|Automated agent| E[Agent implements]\n  C -->|Community contributor| F[You implement and open PR]\n  F --> G[Code review]\n  G --> H[Maintainer merges]\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L3-L16](CONTRIBUTING.md#L3-L16)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L116-L122](CONTRIBUTING.md#L116-L122)\n\n**Section sources**\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L3-L16](CONTRIBUTING.md#L3-L16)\n\n## Local Development Setup\n\nThe documented prerequisite is Node.js 20 or later, verified with `node --version`. Fork the repository if you plan to send a pull request, then clone and enter it:\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/microsoft/playwright\ncd playwright\n```\n\nDependencies and the watch build are installed with:\n\n```bash\nnpm ci\nnpm run watch\nnpx playwright install\n```\n\nPlaywright is a multi-package repository using npm workspaces: browser APIs live in `packages/playwright-core`, and the test runner in `packages/playwright`. Some files are generated by the build, so the watch process can override edits made in the wrong file — API TypeScript types, for instance, are generated from `docs/src`. Coding style is fully defined in `eslint.config.mjs`, and the linter is run with `npm run lint`. Comments should have an explicit purpose; code that needs comments should be rewritten instead.\n\nEvery part of the public API must be documented in `docs/src` in the same change that adds or changes it, using the markdown files with custom structure found there; other files are generated from the API specification and regenerate automatically under `npm run watch`. Larger changes also need documentation guide updates, raised during code review.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L18-L58](CONTRIBUTING.md#L18-L58)\n\n## Tests and Commit Messages\n\nPlaywright requires a test for almost any new or modified functionality; a pure refactoring is the stated exception. The two most important local suites are library tests and test runner tests:\n\n```bash\n# fast path runs all tests in Chromium\nnpm run ctest\n\n# slow path runs all tests in three browsers\nnpm run test\n```\n\n```bash\nnpm run ttest\n```\n\nPlaywright's own tests use Playwright under the hood, so the public test-running documentation applies. Tests must be *hermetic* — not dependent on external services — and must work on macOS, Linux and Windows.\n\nCommits follow Semantic Commit Messages in the shape `label(namespace): title`, with optional newline-separated description in present tense and an optional footer carrying \"fixes\" / \"references\" attribution:\n\n| Label | Meaning |\n|-------|---------|\n| `fix` | bug fixes |\n| `feat` | new features |\n| `docs` | documentation-only changes |\n| `test` | test-only changes |\n| `devops` | changes to the CI or build |\n| `chore` | everything else |\n\nAll submissions, including those by project members, require review through GitHub pull requests; keep diffs small, split into multiple PRs if necessary, and a maintainer merges after review. New dependencies carry a very high bar, including upgrades of existing dependencies — discuss in an issue and get a maintainer's green light first. To run tests against a custom browser binary, point `CRPATH`, `WKPATH` or `FFPATH` at the executable, for example `CRPATH=<path-to-executable> npm run ctest`, and `DEBUG=pw:browser` is noted as useful for debugging custom builds.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L57-L122](CONTRIBUTING.md#L57-L122)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L124-L137](CONTRIBUTING.md#L124-L137)\n\n## Filing a Bug Report\n\n`FILING_ISSUES.md` asks reporters to confirm they are on the latest Playwright release and to check existing GitHub issues for duplicates before filing. The **Bug Report** template should be followed and filled out thoroughly, listing clear reproduction steps, expected versus actual behavior, and system info from `npx envinfo --preset playwright`.\n\nReproductions must be minimal: start a fresh project with `npm init playwright@latest new-project`, add only the code or DOM needed to show the problem, and use major frameworks (React, Angular, a static HTTP server) only when necessary. Extra libraries should be avoided, and suspect dependencies will not be installed. The rationale: issues without a repro usually turn out to be misconfigurations or usage errors, private projects cannot be debugged for the reporter, and each confirmed bug gets a test in the repository — so the repro must be as clean as possible. The bottom line: a minimal, public repro, or assistance is unlikely.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [FILING_ISSUES.md:L1-L35](FILING_ISSUES.md#L1-L35)\n\n## Code of Conduct and Contributor License Agreement\n\nMost contributions require agreeing to a Contributor License Agreement granting Microsoft the rights to use the contribution. Details are at `https://cla.opensource.microsoft.com`; a CLA bot checks each PR automatically and decorates it accordingly, and the agreement is needed only once across all repositories using the CLA.\n\nThe project has adopted the Microsoft Open Source Code of Conduct, documented in `CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`, which links the code of conduct, the FAQ, and the contact address `opencode@microsoft.com` for questions or concerns. `CONTRIBUTING.md` repeats the same contact for additional questions and comments.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md:L148-L162](CONTRIBUTING.md#L148-L162)\n- [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md:L1-L9](CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md#L1-L9)\n\n## Agent-Facing Repository Conventions\n\n`AGENTS.md` adds conventions aimed at coding agents working in the repository, and maps the monorepo: `playwright-core` (automation engine), `playwright` (test runner plus automation), `playwright-test` as `@playwright/test`, `playwright-client` as `@playwright/client`, and the internal `protocol` package that generates validators and channel types from `spec/*.yml`. Tooling packages include `html-reporter`, `trace-viewer`, `recorder`, `web` and `injected`. `docs/src` is called the **source of truth** for public TypeScript types.\n\nBuild and verification are `npm run build` and `npm run watch`; generated files (types, channels, validators) come from watch automatically. `npm run flint` runs all lint checks in parallel — eslint, tsc, doclint, check-deps, channel and type generation, test linting, package linting, snippet linting, and a dependency-age check (every version in `package-lock.json` must be at least 7 days old unless a newer version fixes a high or critical advisory). Run `flint` only before uploading a PR, only after `npm run build`, and not before each commit; do not use `tsc --noEmit` or individual lint commands.\n\nTest scope guidance favors extending an existing test over adding a new one and generalizing regression cases rather than multiplying them. Regression tests are tagged with an issue annotation and avoid manual setup and teardown in favor of fixtures. Import boundaries are enforced through `DEPS.list` files checked by `flint`: client code never imports server code and vice versa, with communication only through the protocol. Commit messages use the same `label(scope): description` form, branches for issue fixes are named `fix-<issue-number>`, and no agent attribution footers are permitted in any artifact.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [AGENTS.md:L1-L58](AGENTS.md#L1-L58)\n- [AGENTS.md:L91-L111](AGENTS.md#L91-L111)\n- [AGENTS.md:L122-L175](AGENTS.md#L122-L175)\n"}, {"path": "docs-docs.md", "title": "Documentation Source", "parent": null, "description": "Landing page for the docs/src tree: the authored guides and generated API reference pages that power playwright.dev, organized into getting started, core concepts, test runner, tooling, and API reference.", "files": ["docs/src/intro-js.md", "docs/src/intro-python.md", "docs/src/intro-csharp.md", "docs/src/intro-java.md", "docs/src/languages.md", "docs/src/getting-started-vscode-js.md"], "markdown": "# Documentation Source\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [docs/src/intro-js.md](docs/src/intro-js.md)\n- [docs/src/intro-python.md](docs/src/intro-python.md)\n- [docs/src/intro-csharp.md](docs/src/intro-csharp.md)\n- [docs/src/intro-java.md](docs/src/intro-java.md)\n- [docs/src/languages.md](docs/src/languages.md)\n- [docs/src/getting-started-vscode-js.md](docs/src/getting-started-vscode-js.md)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `docs/src` tree holds the authored Markdown sources for the Playwright documentation site. The four `intro-*` files are the installation landing pages, one per supported language, and all four deliberately share the same front matter identity — `id: intro` and `title: \"Installation\"` — so that each language site renders its own \"Installation\" page at a predictable route. The JavaScript page states the framework's scope plainly: Playwright Test \"is an end-to-end test framework for modern web apps\" that \"bundles test runner, assertions, isolation, parallelization and rich tooling,\" supporting Chromium, WebKit and Firefox on Windows, Linux and macOS, locally or in CI, headless or headed, with mobile emulation for Chrome (Android) and Mobile Safari.\n\n`docs/src/languages.md` acts as the cross-language router: it explains that all core browser-automation features exist in every language while \"testing ecosystem integration is different,\" and it links each language to both its documentation site and its GitHub repository (`microsoft/playwright`, `microsoft/playwright-python`, `microsoft/playwright-java`, `microsoft/playwright-dotnet`).\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/intro-js.md:L1-L10](docs/src/intro-js.md#L1-L10)\n- [docs/src/languages.md:L1-L36](docs/src/languages.md#L1-L36)\n\n## Language Entry Points\n\nEach installation page follows the same narrative spine — introduction, install, example test, run, update, system requirements, \"What's next\" — but adapts the mechanics to the language's packaging system.\n\n| Guide | Test harness described | Install command shown | Run command shown |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `docs/src/intro-js.md` | Playwright Test (bundled runner) | `npm init playwright@latest` | `npx playwright test` |\n| `docs/src/intro-python.md` | Playwright Pytest plugin | `pip install pytest-playwright` | `pytest` |\n| `docs/src/intro-csharp.md` | MSTest / NUnit / xUnit / xUnit v3 base classes | `dotnet add package Microsoft.Playwright.NUnit` | `dotnet test` |\n| `docs/src/intro-java.md` | Maven module, any test framework | `mvn compile exec:java -D exec.mainClass=\"org.example.App\"` | `mvn compile exec:java -D exec.mainClass=\"org.example.App\"` |\n\nThe JavaScript page additionally documents the generated scaffold (`playwright.config.ts`, `package.json`, `package-lock.json`, `tests/example.spec.ts`), the HTML report via `npx playwright show-report`, and UI Mode via `npx playwright test --ui`. The Python page notes a Linux-specific dependency: starting with Playwright 1.65 the bundled Node.js 26 requires `libatomic.so.1`, installed with `apt-get update && apt-get install -y libatomic1`. The .NET page requires a build step before browser installation so that `playwright.ps1` exists in `bin`, then runs `pwsh bin/Debug/net8.0/playwright.ps1 install`. The Java page ships its Maven snippet with a `%%VERSION%%` placeholder in the dependency block.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    L[\"languages.md<br/>Supported languages\"] --> JS[\"intro-js.md\"]\n    L --> PY[\"intro-python.md\"]\n    L --> CS[\"intro-csharp.md\"]\n    L --> JV[\"intro-java.md\"]\n    JS --> VSC[\"getting-started-vscode-js.md\"]\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [docs/src/languages.md:L10-L36](docs/src/languages.md#L10-L36)\n- [docs/src/intro-js.md:L68-L70](docs/src/intro-js.md#L68-L70)\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/intro-js.md:L17-L140](docs/src/intro-js.md#L17-L140)\n- [docs/src/intro-python.md:L18-L108](docs/src/intro-python.md#L18-L108)\n- [docs/src/intro-csharp.md:L12-L112](docs/src/intro-csharp.md#L12-L112)\n- [docs/src/intro-java.md:L12-L127](docs/src/intro-java.md#L12-L127)\n\n## Shared Authoring Conventions\n\nThe pages are MDX, not plain Markdown. Two conventions recur across the files:\n\n- **Tabbed command variants.** Install, run, update and report commands are wrapped in `<Tabs groupId=... defaultValue=... values={[...]}>` with one `<TabItem value=\"...\">` per package manager. JavaScript uses `groupId=\"js-package-manager\"` with `npm`, `yarn`, `pnpm`; Python uses `groupId=\"package-managers\"` with `pip`, `poetry`, `uv`; .NET uses `groupId=\"test-runners\"` with `mstest`, `nunit`, `xunit`, `xunit-v3`.\n- **Relative `.md` cross-links.** Links point at source files rather than built URLs, e.g. `./writing-tests.md`, `./running-tests.md`, `./codegen-intro.md`, `./trace-viewer-intro.md`, `./test-configuration.md`, `./test-reporters.md#html-reporter`, `./test-ui-mode.md`, `./test-runners.md`. Anchors may also target the page itself, as in `/intro.md#installing-playwright`.\n- **Imported site components.** `docs/src/getting-started-vscode-js.md` imports `LiteYouTube` from `@site/src/components/LiteYouTube` and embeds videos by `id` and `title`, showing that authored pages can pull in components from the site package.\n- **Code-fence titles.** Fences carry language plus optional title, e.g. ``` ```py title=\"test_example.py\" ```, ``` ```csharp title=\"UnitTest1.cs\" ```, ``` ```java title=\"src/main/java/org/example/App.java\" ```.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/intro-js.md:L25-L58](docs/src/intro-js.md#L25-L58)\n- [docs/src/intro-python.md:L36-L70](docs/src/intro-python.md#L36-L70)\n- [docs/src/intro-csharp.md:L14-L56](docs/src/intro-csharp.md#L14-L56)\n- [docs/src/getting-started-vscode-js.md:L1-L15](docs/src/getting-started-vscode-js.md#L1-L15)\n\n## System Requirements by Language\n\nThe \"System requirements\" section is present in all four installation pages and shares an identical operating-system baseline: Windows 11+, Windows Server 2019+ or WSL; macOS 14 (Sonoma) or later; Debian 12 / 13 and Ubuntu 22.04 / 24.04 / 26.04 on x86-64 or arm64. Only the runtime line differs.\n\n| Guide | Runtime requirement as written |\n| --- | --- |\n| `docs/src/intro-js.md` | Node.js: latest 22.x, 24.x or 26.x |\n| `docs/src/intro-python.md` | Python 3.8 or higher |\n| `docs/src/intro-csharp.md` | Distributed as a .NET Standard 2.0 library; .NET 8 recommended |\n| `docs/src/intro-java.md` | Java 8 or higher |\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/intro-js.md:L303-L308](docs/src/intro-js.md#L303-L308)\n- [docs/src/intro-python.md:L146-L151](docs/src/intro-python.md#L146-L151)\n- [docs/src/intro-csharp.md:L284-L289](docs/src/intro-csharp.md#L284-L289)\n- [docs/src/intro-java.md:L129-L134](docs/src/intro-java.md#L129-L134)\n\n## Editor Getting Started Guide\n\n`docs/src/getting-started-vscode-js.md` is the JavaScript-specific walkthrough for the Playwright VS Code extension. It lists prerequisites (Node.js LTS, Visual Studio Code), then a three-step setup: install the extension from the marketplace, run `Test: Install Playwright` from the Command Palette, and pick browsers — with a note that these choices can later be changed in `playwright.config.ts`. The remainder is organized as Core Features (running, debugging, CodeGen) and Advanced Features (project dependencies, global setup, multiple configurations), closing with a \"Quick Reference\" table mapping each action to its VS Code gesture, such as `Record new` for recording a test and `Pick locator` for locator capture. It cross-links out to `./trace-viewer.md`, `./codegen.md`, `./test-projects.md`, `./writing-tests.md` and `./ci-intro.md`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/getting-started-vscode-js.md:L17-L45](docs/src/getting-started-vscode-js.md#L17-L45)\n- [docs/src/getting-started-vscode-js.md:L134-L172](docs/src/getting-started-vscode-js.md#L134-L172)\n"}, {"path": "architecture/browser-patches/upstream-roll.md", "title": "Rolling Browsers from Upstream", "parent": null, "description": "How patches are reapplied when Chromium, Firefox, or WebKit is rolled to a new upstream revision, including the configuration and run wrapper for the patched WebKit build.", "files": ["browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh", "browser_patches/webkit/UPSTREAM_CONFIG.sh", "browser_patches/webkit/pw_run.sh", "browser_patches/webkit/.gitignore"], "markdown": "# Rolling Browsers from Upstream\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh](browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/UPSTREAM_CONFIG.sh](browser_patches/webkit/UPSTREAM_CONFIG.sh)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/pw_run.sh](browser_patches/webkit/pw_run.sh)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/.gitignore](browser_patches/webkit/.gitignore)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nPlaywright maintains browser patches in a separate `playwright-browsers` checkout. When a browser target is rolled to a new upstream revision, the patched files are copied back into this repository with `browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh`. The script is a one-way sync: it reads from an external checkout and writes into the local `browser_patches/` tree, using `rsync` with `--delete` so that files removed upstream are removed locally as well.\n\nThe script only handles Firefox, WebKit, and `winldd`. Chromium patches are not part of the file lists it copies.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh:L1-L5](browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh#L1-L5)\n- [browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh:L32-L67](browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh#L32-L67)\n\n## Invocation and Argument Validation\n\nThe script accepts one or two positional arguments:\n\n```bash\nUsage: $0 <path to playwright-browsers checkout> [firefox|webkit|winldd]\n```\n\nValidation happens in three steps before any copying occurs:\n\n| Check | Behavior on failure |\n|---|---|\n| Argument count must be 1 or 2 | Prints usage line, exits `1` |\n| First argument must be an existing directory | Prints `ERROR: the source directory ... does not exist`, exits `1` |\n| Source must contain `browser_patches/build_flavors.sh` | Prints `ERROR: ... does not look like a playwright-browsers checkout`, exits `1` |\n\nThe third check exists because, on CI, the checkout directory may have an arbitrary name, so the script detects a valid checkout by a marker file rather than by directory name.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh:L7-L30](browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh#L7-L30)\n\n### Path Normalization\n\nAfter validation, the script resolves the source to a physical absolute path and changes into its own directory so that all destination paths are relative to the locations of the patch files:\n\n```bash\nSOURCE_DIRECTORY=\"$(cd \"$1\" && pwd -P)\"\nFILTER=\"${2:-}\"\ntrap \"cd $(pwd -P)\" EXIT\ncd \"$(dirname \"$0\")\"\nSCRIPT_PATH=$(pwd -P)\n```\n\nThe `trap` restores the original working directory on exit, so invoking the script does not leave the shell in a different directory. The optional second argument becomes `FILTER`; when omitted it is the empty string, which means \"everything\".\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh:L17-L23](browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh#L17-L23)\n\n## File Sets per Browser Target\n\nEach target is described by a bash array of relative paths, all rooted at `browser_patches/`:\n\n| Target | Paths copied |\n|---|---|\n| Firefox | `./firefox/juggler/`, `./firefox/patches/`, `./firefox/preferences/`, `./firefox/UPSTREAM_CONFIG.sh` |\n| WebKit | `./webkit/embedder/`, `./webkit/patches/`, `./webkit/pw_run.sh`, `./webkit/UPSTREAM_CONFIG.sh` |\n| winldd | `./winldd/` |\n\nThe `FILTER` value selects which array is used. An empty filter concatenates all three arrays, an unrecognized value prints `ERROR: unknown filter '${FILTER}'; supported filters: firefox, webkit, winldd` and exits `1`.\n\nNote that for WebKit the `embedder/` directory, the run wrapper `pw_run.sh`, and the upstream pin `UPSTREAM_CONFIG.sh` travel together — rolling WebKit means keeping the embedder application and its launcher in sync with the patched engine.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh:L32-L67](browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh#L32-L67)\n\n## The Sync Loop\n\nThe actual roll is a single loop over the selected file list:\n\n```bash\nfor file in \"${files[@]}\"; do\n  rsync -av --delete \"${SOURCE_DIRECTORY}/browser_patches/${file}\" \"${SCRIPT_PATH}/${file}\"\ndone\n```\n\nThe semantics worth noting:\n\n- `-a` preserves permissions, timestamps, and recurses, which matters for executable scripts such as `pw_run.sh`.\n- `--delete` removes destination entries that no longer exist in the source, so deleted patches and deleted embedder sources do not linger.\n- The source path is always prefixed with `SOURCE_DIRECTORY/browser_patches/`, and the destination with `SCRIPT_PATH/`, so both sides are addressed relative to their respective `browser_patches/` roots.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh:L69-L71](browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh#L69-L71)\n\n## Roll Flow\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Invoke roll_from_upstream.sh\"] --> B{\"1 or 2 args?\"}\n    B -- no --> B1[\"Print usage, exit 1\"]\n    B -- yes --> C{\"Source dir exists?\"}\n    C -- no --> C1[\"ERROR: source dir does not exist\"]\n    C -- yes --> D{\"build_flavors.sh present?\"}\n    D -- no --> D1[\"ERROR: not a playwright-browsers checkout\"]\n    D -- yes --> E[\"Resolve SOURCE_DIRECTORY and SCRIPT_PATH\"]\n    E --> F{\"FILTER value\"}\n    F -- firefox --> G[\"firefox_files\"]\n    F -- webkit --> H[\"webkit_files\"]\n    F -- winldd --> I[\"winldd_files\"]\n    F -- empty --> J[\"all three arrays\"]\n    G --> K[\"rsync -av --delete per path\"]\n    H --> K\n    I --> K\n    J --> K\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh:L7-L71](browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh#L7-L71)\n\n## The WebKit Upstream Pin\n\nWebKit's upstream state is captured entirely in `browser_patches/webkit/UPSTREAM_CONFIG.sh`, a three-variable shell file:\n\n```bash\nREMOTE_URL=\"https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit.git\"\nBASE_BRANCH=\"main\"\nBASE_REVISION=\"aa697ddd0312b191de5fb13b580f19159d39c26f\"\n```\n\n`BASE_REVISION` is the exact commit the WebKit patch series is written against, and `BASE_BRANCH` records the branch that revision belongs to. Rolling to a new upstream revision means updating this file together with the `embedder/` and `patches/` directories, which is why all four WebKit paths sit in the same array passed to `rsync`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/UPSTREAM_CONFIG.sh:L1-L3](browser_patches/webkit/UPSTREAM_CONFIG.sh#L1-L3)\n- [browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh:L39-L44](browser_patches/roll_from_upstream.sh#L39-L44)\n\n## Running the Patched WebKit Build\n\n`browser_patches/webkit/pw_run.sh` is the launcher for the patched WebKit. It computes `SCRIPT_PATH` from its own location, dispatches on `uname`, and refuses other platforms:\n\n```bash\nSCRIPT_PATH=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")\" ; pwd -P)\"\nif [[ \"$(uname)\" == \"Darwin\" ]]; then\n  runOSX \"$@\"\nelif [[ \"$(uname)\" == \"Linux\" ]]; then\n  runLinux \"$@\"\nelse\n  echo \"ERROR: cannot run on this platform!\" 1>&2\n  exit 1;\nfi\n```\n\nBoth platform functions locate a build directory by probing a fixed sequence of candidate locations and give up with an error message if none match.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/pw_run.sh:L75-L83](browser_patches/webkit/pw_run.sh#L75-L83)\n\n### macOS: locating Playwright.app\n\nThe macOS path uses the `WK_CHECKOUT_PATH` environment variable, defaulting to `$HOME/webkit`, and then tests three locations in order:\n\n| Order | Condition | `DYLIB_PATH` |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `${SCRIPT_PATH}/EXPECTED_BUILDS` is a file **and** `$WK_CHECKOUT_PATH/WebKitBuild/Release/Playwright.app` is a directory | `$WK_CHECKOUT_PATH/WebKitBuild/Release` |\n| 2 | `$SCRIPT_PATH/Playwright.app` is a directory | `$SCRIPT_PATH` |\n| 3 | `$SCRIPT_PATH/WebKitBuild/Release/Playwright.app` is a directory | `$SCRIPT_PATH/WebKitBuild/Release` |\n\nIf none match, it prints `Cannot find a Playwright.app in neither location` to stderr and exits `1`. Otherwise it executes the binary with the framework search paths pointed at the same directory:\n\n```bash\nPLAYWRIGHT=\"$DYLIB_PATH/Playwright.app/Contents/MacOS/Playwright\"\nDYLD_FRAMEWORK_PATH=\"$DYLIB_PATH\" DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH=\"$DYLIB_PATH\" \"$PLAYWRIGHT\" \"$@\"\n```\n\nSetting both `DYLD_FRAMEWORK_PATH` and `DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH` lets the app find the WebKit frameworks built alongside it rather than any system copy.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/pw_run.sh:L3-L22](browser_patches/webkit/pw_run.sh#L3-L22)\n\n### Linux: GTK vs WPE selection\n\nOn Linux the script picks between the GTK and WPE builds by scanning the argument list for `--headless`:\n\n| Variable | Default | With `--headless` |\n|---|---|---|\n| `DEPENDENCIES_FOLDER` | `WebKitBuild/DependenciesGTK` | `WebKitBuild/DependenciesWPE` |\n| `MINIBROWSER_FOLDER` | `minibrowser-gtk` | `minibrowser-wpe` |\n| `BUILD_FOLDER` | `WebKitBuild/GTK` | `WebKitBuild/WPE` |\n\nIt then probes, in order:\n\n1. `$SCRIPT_PATH/$MINIBROWSER_FOLDER` — a bundled MiniBrowser next to the script.\n2. `$WK_CHECKOUT_PATH/$BUILD_FOLDER` — a build inside the WebKit checkout.\n3. `$SCRIPT_PATH/MiniBrowser` — a single binary next to the script.\n4. `$SCRIPT_PATH/$BUILD_FOLDER` — a build inside the script directory.\n\nFailure prints `Cannot find a MiniBrowser.app in neither location` and exits `1`. In cases 2 and 4 the script exports `GIO_EXTRA_MODULES`, prepends `LD_LIBRARY_PATH`, and sets `WEBKIT_INJECTED_BUNDLE_PATH` to the build's `Release/lib`. The source comments note that these extra variables are only needed when calling MiniBrowser directly from the build folder, because the zip-bundle MiniBrowser already sets them itself. The final invocation forces a WebKit flag:\n\n```bash\nWEBKIT_FORCE_COMPLEX_TEXT=\"1\" \"$MINIBROWSER\" \"$@\"\n```\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/pw_run.sh:L24-L73](browser_patches/webkit/pw_run.sh#L24-L73)\n\n## Checkout Directory Hygiene\n\n`browser_patches/webkit/.gitignore` contains a single entry, `/checkout`, which keeps a locally placed WebKit checkout out of version control while still allowing it to live inside the webkit patch directory. Combined with the `WK_CHECKOUT_PATH` override in `pw_run.sh`, this supports both an in-tree checkout under `browser_patches/webkit/checkout` and one elsewhere such as `$HOME/webkit`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/.gitignore:L1-L1](browser_patches/webkit/.gitignore#L1-L1)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/pw_run.sh:L3-L5](browser_patches/webkit/pw_run.sh#L3-L5)\n"}, {"path": "architecture/browser-patches/webkit-macos.md", "title": "WebKit Embedder on macOS", "parent": null, "description": "The Cocoa application shell that hosts WebKit for Playwright on macOS: app delegate, browser window controller, XIBs, toolbar items, and entry point.", "files": ["browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.h", "browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.m", "browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindow.xib", "browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindowController.h", "browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindowController.m", "browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/main.m", "browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/MainMenu.xib", "browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/Playwright_Prefix.pch", "browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/CMakeLists.txt", "browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/MBToolbarItem.h", "browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/MBToolbarItem.m"], "markdown": "# WebKit Embedder on macOS\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.h](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.h)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.m](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.m)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindow.xib](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindow.xib)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindowController.h](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindowController.h)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindowController.m](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindowController.m)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/main.m](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/main.m)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/MainMenu.xib](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/MainMenu.xib)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/Playwright_Prefix.pch](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/Playwright_Prefix.pch)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/CMakeLists.txt](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/CMakeLists.txt)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/MBToolbarItem.h](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/MBToolbarItem.h)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/MBToolbarItem.m](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/MBToolbarItem.m)\n</cite>\n\nThe macOS embedder is a small Cocoa application named `Playwright` that hosts `WKWebView` instances. It consists of an entry point (`main.m`), an application delegate that parses command-line flags and owns page/data-store lifecycle (`AppDelegate`), a window controller with an associated XIB (`BrowserWindowController`, `BrowserWindow.xib`), and a menu bar nib (`MainMenu.xib`). A shared prefix header and a single CMake target tie the pieces together.\n\n## Process Entry and Build Wiring\n\n`main.m` sets the `WebKitLinkedOnOrAfterEverything` user default and then hands control to `NSApplicationMain`, so all UI wiring comes from the nib files rather than programmatic startup.\n\n```objc\nint main(int argc, char *argv[])\n{\n    [[NSUserDefaults standardUserDefaults] setBool:YES forKey:@\"WebKitLinkedOnOrAfterEverything\"];\n\n    return NSApplicationMain(argc,  (const char **) argv);\n}\n```\n\n`Playwright_Prefix.pch` is force-included into every translation unit; it imports `Cocoa.h` and `WebKit.h`, defines `ENABLE_LOGGING 0`, and compiles `LOG(...)` into a no-op unless logging is enabled.\n\nThe CMake file lists exactly four sources — `AppDelegate.m`, `BrowserWindowController.m`, `main.m` and `MBToolbarItem.m` — links the `WebKit` library plus `-framework Cocoa`, and builds a `MACOSX_BUNDLE` whose `Info.plist` is taken from `mac/Info.plist`. Both XIBs are compiled with `ibtool` into `Playwright.app/Contents/Resources` by the `PlaywrightNibs` custom target, which the executable depends on.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/main.m:L26-L33](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/main.m#L26-L33)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/Playwright_Prefix.pch:L26-L37](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/Playwright_Prefix.pch#L26-L37)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/CMakeLists.txt:L1-L43](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/CMakeLists.txt#L1-L43)\n\n## Application Delegate: Arguments and Modes\n\n`BrowserAppDelegate` conforms to `NSApplicationDelegate`, `WKNavigationDelegate`, `WKUIDelegate`, `_WKBrowserInspectorDelegate` and `WKDownloadDelegate`, and holds sets for headless windows, browser contexts and pending dialogs. `NSApplication` is extended with a `browserAppDelegate` accessor that simply casts `self.delegate`.\n\n`-init` scans `NSProcessInfo.processInfo.arguments` (skipping `argv[0]`). Any argument not prefixed with `--` becomes `_initialURL`; the prefixed forms are stripped by fixed offsets:\n\n| Argument | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `--user-data-dir=<path>` | `_userDataDir`, substring from index 16 |\n| `--proxy=<url>` | `_proxyServer`, substring from index 8 |\n| `--proxy-bypass-list=<list>` | `_proxyBypassList`, substring from index 20 |\n| `--headless` | `_headless` flag |\n| `--no-startup-window` | `_noStartupWindow` flag |\n| `--inspector-pipe` | `_inspectorPipe` flag |\n\nIn headless mode the delegate allocates `_headlessWindows` and `_dialogs`, switches the activation policy to `NSApplicationActivationPolicyAccessory`, and calls `beginActivityWithOptions:reason:` with `ActivityReason` (`@\"Batch headless process\"`). Otherwise it calls `[NSApp activateIgnoringOtherApps:YES]`. `awakeFromNib` enables the automatic \"Customize Touch Bar\" menu item when the selector exists.\n\n`applicationDidFinishLaunching:` updates the Cmd-N key equivalent for the \"new WebKit2 window\" menu item (headful only), initializes the remote inspector pipe when `--inspector-pipe` is present, and returns early for `--no-startup-window`. Otherwise it forces creation of the default configuration and schedules a non-repeating 0.05 s timer that calls `createNewPage:withURL:` with the initial URL or `about:blank`; the delay is commented as necessary because creating the first `NSWindow` immediately makes it invisible in headless mode.\n\nThe launch path is:\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Main as main.m\n    participant NSAppMain as NSApplicationMain\n    participant Nib as MainMenu.xib\n    participant Delegate as BrowserAppDelegate\n    Main->>NSAppMain: NSApplicationMain(argc, argv)\n    NSAppMain->>Nib: load main nib\n    Nib->>Delegate: instantiate and connect as NSApp.delegate\n    NSAppMain->>Delegate: applicationDidFinishLaunching:\n    Delegate->>Delegate: createNewPage:withURL: (after 0.05s)\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/main.m:L28-L33](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/main.m#L28-L33)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/MainMenu.xib:L7-L11](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/MainMenu.xib#L7-L11)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/MainMenu.xib:L331-L331](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/MainMenu.xib#L331-L331)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.m:L252-L275](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.m#L252-L275)\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.h:L29-L54](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.h#L29-L54)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.m:L46-L53](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.m#L46-L53)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.m:L84-L137](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.m#L84-L137)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.m:L252-L282](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.m#L252-L282)\n\n## Web View Configuration Defaults\n\nBoth the persistent data store and the default `WKWebViewConfiguration` are lazily created `static` singletons on the delegate.\n\n`persistentDataStore` builds a `_WKWebsiteDataStoreConfiguration`. When `_userDataDir` is set it selects `_WKUnifiedOriginStorageLevelNone` (\"local storage state should be stored in separate dirs for persistent contexts\") and points each subsystem at a subdirectory of that root: `cookie.db`, `CacheStorage`, `IndexedDB`, `LocalStorage`, `MediaCache`, `MediaKeys`, `NetworkCache`, `ResourceLoadStatistics`, `ServiceWorkers` and `WebSQL`. The proxy configuration is applied and `setPreventsSystemHTTPProxyAuthentication:YES` is always set.\n\n`defaultConfiguration` sets `_controlledByAutomation = true`, enables element fullscreen, developer extras, media devices and mock capture devices, disables hidden-page DOM timer throttling, auto-increase and page-visibility process suppression, and sets the autoplay policy to `_WKWebsiteAutoplayPolicyAllowWithoutSound`. A `_WKProcessPoolConfiguration` with `forceOverlayScrollbars = YES` supplies the process pool.\n\n`proxyConfiguration:WithBypassList:` translates a `--proxy=` URL into a CFStream proxy dictionary: `socks5://` maps to the SOCKS host/port keys, anything else to both HTTP and HTTPS host/port keys, and a bypass list is split on commas into `ExceptionsList`. It returns `nil` when no proxy server is supplied.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.m:L140-L250](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.m#L140-L250)\n\n## Page and Browser Context Creation\n\n`createNewPage:withURL:` resolves a configuration via `sessionConfiguration:` — which copies the default configuration and swaps in the data store and process pool of the matching `_WKBrowserContext` — then dispatches on `_headless`:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"createNewPage:withURL:\"] --> B[\"sessionConfiguration:\"]\n    B --> C{\"_headless\"}\n    C -- yes --> D[\"createHeadlessPage:withURL:\"]\n    C -- no --> E[\"createHeadfulPage:withURL:\"]\n    D --> F[\"borderless off-screen NSWindow + WKWebView\"]\n    E --> G[\"BrowserWindowController initWithConfiguration:\"]\n```\n\n- `createHeadfulPage:withURL:` allocates a `BrowserWindowController` with the configuration, calls `loadURLString:`, makes the window visible and returns the controller's `webView`. The comment notes that web view lifecycle controls the controller's lifetime.\n- `createHeadlessPage:withURL:` creates a 1280×720 borderless `NSWindow` with `_NSBackingStoreUnbuffered`, offset by `(-10000, …)` relative to the first screen (referencing Playwright issues 36711 and 42265). It sets `NSWindowCollectionBehaviorTransient | NSWindowCollectionBehaviorIgnoresCycle` so Mission Control does not scale to the off-screen window, disables `_windowOcclusionDetectionEnabled`, adds the `WKWebView` as a resizing subview, loads the URL via `_webkit_URLWithUserTypedString`, registers the window in `_headlessWindows`, and assigns the delegate as navigation and UI delegate.\n\n`createBrowserContext:WithBypassList:` builds a non-persistent data store configuration, falling back to the process-wide `_proxyServer`/`_proxyBypassList` when per-context values are empty, and stores the resulting `_WKBrowserContext` in `_browserContexts`. `deleteBrowserContext:` removes the context whose data store session ID matches. `quit` terminates the app via a delayed `terminate:`.\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.m:L286-L358](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.m#L286-L358)\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.m:L286-L397](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.m#L286-L397)\n\n## Browser Window Controller and BrowserWindow.xib\n\n`BrowserWindowController.h` declares `CustomWindow` (an `NSWindow` subclass overriding `constrainFrameRect:toScreen:`) and the controller's action surface: back/forward, full-window web view, reload, save as PDF, save as Web Archive, zoom in/out/reset, show/hide inspector, `setPageScale:` and `setViewScale:`, plus `initWithConfiguration:`, `loadURLString:` and `webView`.\n\n`CustomWindow` clamps the frame so a window never exceeds the top of its screen and never loses its 35-point control bar below the bottom.\n\n`BrowserWindow.xib` sets `BrowserWindowController` as File's Owner and connects ten outlets: `backButton`, `containerView`, `forwardButton`, `lockButton`, `progressIndicator`, `reloadButton`, `share`, `toggleUseShrinkToFitButton`, `toolbar`, `urlText` and `window`. The window is a `CustomWindow` with titled/closable/miniaturizable/resizable and full-size content view masks; its content view holds a single `containerView` that the controller fills with the web view. The window's delegate outlet is connected back to File's Owner.\n\n`-initWithConfiguration:` loads the `BrowserWindow` nib, copies the configuration, derives `_isPrivateBrowsingWindow` from `websiteDataStore.isPersistent`, clears the full-size content view style mask and orders the window front. `-awakeFromNib` creates the `WKWebView` in `containerView`, disables occlusion detection, enables magnification and back/forward gestures, binds the progress indicator's hidden/value bindings to `loading`/`estimatedProgress`, registers KVO for `title`, `URL` and `hasOnlySecureContent`, sets the navigation/UI delegates, subscribes to several `_WKRenderingProgressEvent` values, sets `_usePlatformFindUI = NO`, and installs a `PlaywrightNSTextFinder` whose hide callback calls `_hideFindUI`.\n\n`-windowDidLoad` applies `NSWindowToolbarStyleExpanded` and replaces toolbar button images with SF Symbols on macOS 11+, and makes the share button act on left mouse down. `windowWillClose:` removes the web view, clears the finder callback, releases the controller and posts two application-defined events to drain autorelease pools.\n\n`updateTitle:` formats the window title as `🎭 Playwright: <title>`, falling back to the URL's last path component or `_web_userVisibleString`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindowController.h:L28-L53](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindowController.h#L28-L53)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindowController.m:L66-L140](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindowController.m#L66-L140)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindowController.m:L187-L261](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindowController.m#L187-L261)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindowController.m:L463-L495](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindowController.m#L463-L495)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindowController.m:L550-L558](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindowController.m#L550-L558)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindow.xib:L8-L39](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindow.xib#L8-L39)\n\n## Menu Bar, Toolbar Items, and Validation\n\n`MainMenu.xib` makes `NSApplication` the File's Owner, connects its `delegate` outlet to the `BrowserAppDelegate` object, and defines six top-level menus:\n\n| Menu | Notable items and actions |\n| --- | --- |\n| Playwright | About Playwright (`orderFrontStandardAboutPanel:`), Preferences…, Services, Hide/Hide Others/Show All, Quit (`terminate:`) |\n| File | Open Location (`openLocation:`), Close, Save, Save As…, Save As PDF… (`saveAsPDF:`), Save As WebArchive... (`saveAsWebArchive:`), Page Setup, Print |\n| Edit | Undo/Redo, Cut/Copy/Paste/Delete/Select All, Find submenu (`performTextFinderAction:` tags 1–7) |\n| View | Zoom In/Out/Reset Zoom, Zoom Text Only (`toggleZoomMode:`), Page Scale (tags 1–4 → `setPageScale:`), View Scale (tags 1–4 → `setViewScale:`), Reload Page |\n| Debug | Show Web Inspector (⌥⌘I → `showHideWebInspector:`) |\n| Window | Minimize, Zoom, Bring All to Front |\n\nThe window toolbar is `iconOnly`/`regular` and its default items are Go Back, Go Forward, Refresh, Lock, Location, Share and Progress. Every button item is declared with `customClass=\"MBToolbarItem\"`, which overrides `-validate` to enable itself only when `[[self target] validateUserInterfaceItem:self]` returns `YES`.\n\n| Toolbar item | Action |\n| --- | --- |\n| Go Back | `goBack:` |\n| Go Forward | `goForward:` |\n| Refresh | `reload:` |\n| Lock | `showCertificate:` |\n| Use Shrink To Fit | `toggleShrinkToFit:` |\n| Share | `share:` |\n| Location (text field) | `fetch:` |\n| Progress (spinner) | none |\n\n`toggleShrinkToFit:` flips `_useShrinkToFit`, swaps between `NSExitFullScreenTemplate` and `NSEnterFullScreenTemplate`, and switches the layout mode between `_WKLayoutModeDynamicSizeComputedFromMinimumDocumentSize` and `_WKLayoutModeViewSize`. `validateUserInterfaceItem:` delegates back/forward validation to the web view, and `validateMenuItem:` updates the titles/states of the full-window, zoom-mode, inspector and scale menu items.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/MainMenu.xib:L7-L331](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/MainMenu.xib#L7-L331)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindow.xib:L40-L172](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindow.xib#L40-L172)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/MBToolbarItem.h:L26-L27](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/MBToolbarItem.h#L26-L27)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/MBToolbarItem.m:L28-L33](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/MBToolbarItem.m#L28-L33)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindowController.m:L417-L456](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindowController.m#L417-L456)\n\n## Dialogs, Navigation Policy, and Downloads\n\nDialog handling differs by mode. In headless mode the delegate creates a `WebViewDialog` object per alert/confirm/prompt/before-unload panel, stores it in `_dialogs`, and defers the completion handler until `handleJavaScriptDialog:accept:value:` is called; prompts pass `value` back only when accepted. `webViewDidClose:` dismisses any pending dialog for that web view and closes/removes the matching headless window. Storage-access requests are always accepted.\n\nIn headful mode `BrowserWindowController` presents `NSAlert` sheets on the window, with \"OK\"/\"Cancel\" buttons, an accessory `NSTextField` for prompts, and \"Leave Page\"/\"Stay On Page\" for before-unload. `handleJavaScriptDialog:value:` ends the sheet with the appropriate return code. File inputs are served by `NSOpenPanel`, honoring `allowsMultipleSelection`.\n\n`AppDelegate` implements the navigation policy for headless pages:\n\n- `decidePolicyForNavigationAction:` returns `WKNavigationActionPolicyDownload` when `shouldPerformDownload` is set; a middle click with Command or Shift opens a new headless page and cancels the original navigation; otherwise `_canHandleRequest` decides allow vs. cancel.\n- `decidePolicyForNavigationResponse:` allows non-HTTP responses, downloads responses whose MIME type cannot be shown, downloads `application/pdf` and `text/pdf`, and downloads anything with a `Content-Disposition` starting with `attachment`.\n- Both `didBecomeDownload:` callbacks assign the delegate as the download's delegate, and `download:decideDestinationUsingResponse:suggestedFilename:completionHandler:` completes with `nil`.\n\nHeadful windows create new windows through `webView:createWebViewWithConfiguration:forNavigationAction:windowFeatures:`, which allocates another `BrowserWindowController` and returns its private `_webView`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.m:L399-L548](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/AppDelegate.m#L399-L548)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindowController.m:L573-L707](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/mac/BrowserWindowController.m#L573-L707)\n"}, {"path": "architecture/browser-patches/webkit-windows.md", "title": "WebKit Embedder on Windows", "parent": null, "description": "The Win32 host application for WebKit on Windows: main window, WebKit browser window wrapper, shared helpers, precompiled headers, and WinMain entry point.", "files": ["browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/CMakeLists.txt", "browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/Common.cpp", "browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/Common.h", "browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/DialogHelper.h", "browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/MainWindow.cpp", "browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/MainWindow.h", "browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/PlaywrightReplace.h", "browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/stdafx.cpp", "browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/stdafx.h", "browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/WebKitBrowserWindow.cpp", "browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/WebKitBrowserWindow.h", "browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/WinMain.cpp"], "markdown": "# WebKit Embedder on Windows\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/CMakeLists.txt](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/CMakeLists.txt)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/Common.cpp](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/Common.cpp)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/Common.h](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/Common.h)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/DialogHelper.h](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/DialogHelper.h)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/MainWindow.cpp](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/MainWindow.cpp)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/MainWindow.h](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/MainWindow.h)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/PlaywrightReplace.h](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/PlaywrightReplace.h)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/stdafx.cpp](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/stdafx.cpp)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/stdafx.h](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/stdafx.h)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/WebKitBrowserWindow.cpp](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/WebKitBrowserWindow.cpp)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/WebKitBrowserWindow.h](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/WebKitBrowserWindow.h)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/WinMain.cpp](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/WinMain.cpp)\n</cite>\n\n## Build Target and Source Layout\n\nThe Windows embedder is a single Win32 executable named `Playwright`, declared through `WEBKIT_EXECUTABLE_DECLARE(Playwright)` and `WEBKIT_EXECUTABLE(Playwright)` and marked `WIN32_EXECUTABLE ON` so it links as a GUI application rather than a console program.\n\nThe target compiles six sources: `Common.cpp`, `MainWindow.cpp`, `PlaywrightLib.rc`, `WebKitBrowserWindow.cpp`, `WinMain.cpp` and `stdafx.cpp`. `WebKitBrowserWindow.cpp` is appended a second time in the `ENABLE_WEBKIT` block, alongside `WebKit::WebKit`; the base library set is `WebKit::WTF`, `comctl32`, `shlwapi` and `user32`. Definitions are `_UNICODE` plus `ENABLE_WEBKIT`, and `WIN_CAIRO` is added when `WTF_PLATFORM_WIN_CAIRO` is set. Private include directories are `${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}` and `${WebCore_PRIVATE_FRAMEWORK_HEADERS_DIR}`.\n\n| CMake element | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Executable | `Playwright` (`WIN32_EXECUTABLE ON`) |\n| Sources | `Common.cpp`, `MainWindow.cpp`, `PlaywrightLib.rc`, `WebKitBrowserWindow.cpp`, `WinMain.cpp`, `stdafx.cpp` |\n| Definitions | `_UNICODE`, `ENABLE_WEBKIT`, optional `WIN_CAIRO` |\n| Libraries | `WebKit::WTF`, `comctl32`, `shlwapi`, `user32`, `WebKit::WebKit` |\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/CMakeLists.txt:L1-L37](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/CMakeLists.txt#L1-L37)\n\n## Entry Point and Startup Sequence\n\nThe process entry point is `wWinMain` in `WinMain.cpp`. It stores the instance handle in the global `hInst`, initializes common controls with `ICC_STANDARD_CLASSES`, parses the command line into the global `g_options`, and isolates the pasteboard when running headless via `WebCore::PlatformPasteboard::setIsolated(g_options.headless)`.\n\nTwo optional modes are handled before any window exists: `--inspector-pipe` calls `WKInspectorInitializeRemoteInspectorPipe(configureDataStore, WebKitBrowserWindow::createPageCallback, []() { PostQuitMessage(0); })`, and `--desktop` calls `computeFullDesktopFrame()`. COM is then initialized with `OleInitialize(nullptr)`, and DPI awareness is set to `DPI_AWARENESS_CONTEXT_UNAWARE` through the soft-linked `SetProcessDpiAwarenessContext` symbol from `user32`.\n\nAfter setting `s_headless` and calling `MainWindow::configure(g_options.inspectorPipe, g_options.disableAcceleratedCompositing)`, the startup window is created unless `--no-startup-window` was passed. That path builds a `WKWebsiteDataStoreConfiguration`, redirects cache, CacheStorage, IndexedDB, LocalStorage, WebSQL, MediaKeys, ResourceLoadStatistics and ServiceWorker directories under `--user-data-dir` when supplied, creates a `WKContext` and data store, applies proxy settings through `configureDataStore`, and calls `MainWindow::init` with a freshly created `WKPageConfiguration`. The window then loads `g_options.requestedURL` or `about:blank`.\n\nFinally, two accelerator tables (`IDC_PLAYWRIGHT` and `IDR_ACCELERATORS_PRE`) are loaded, a `RunLoop::setWindowsMessageHandler` callback translates accelerators (the main table only for `MainWindow::isInstance` messages), and `RunLoop::run()` is entered inside a `__try`/`__except` block that funnels faults into `createCrashReport`. `OleUninitialize()` runs on exit.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[wWinMain] --> B[parseCommandLine into g_options]\n    B --> C{inspectorPipe?}\n    C -- yes --> D[WKInspectorInitializeRemoteInspectorPipe]\n    C -- no --> E[OleInitialize and DPI unaware]\n    D --> E\n    E --> F{noStartupWindow?}\n    F -- no --> G[Configure WKWebsiteDataStoreConfiguration from userDataDir]\n    G --> H[Create WKContext and data store, MainWindow::init]\n    H --> I[loadURL requestedURL or about:blank]\n    I --> J[Load accelerator tables]\n    F -- yes --> J\n    J --> K[RunLoop::run inside __try/__except]\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/WinMain.cpp:L73-L157](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/WinMain.cpp#L73-L157)\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/WinMain.cpp:L47-L167](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/WinMain.cpp#L47-L167)\n\n## Command-Line Options\n\n`parseCommandLine` walks `CommandLineToArgvW(GetCommandLineW(), &argc)` starting at index 1 and fills a `CommandLineOptions` struct. Unknown arguments become the requested URL, but only the first one, since the assignment is guarded by `!options.requestedURL`.\n\n| Switch | Field | Effect in code |\n|---|---|---|\n| `--desktop` | `useFullDesktop` | `computeFullDesktopFrame()` sizes the window to the work area |\n| `--inspector-pipe` | `inspectorPipe` | Enables the remote inspector pipe and sets `s_controlledRemotely` |\n| `--user-data-dir=<path>` | `userDataDir` | Root for all website data store directories |\n| `--curl-proxy=<url>` | `curloptProxy` | Passed to `WKWebsiteDataStoreEnableCustomNetworkProxySettings` |\n| `--curl-noproxy=<list>` | `curloptNoproxy` | No-proxy list for the same call |\n| `--headless` | `headless` | Sets `s_headless`, isolates pasteboard, hides chrome |\n| `--no-startup-window` | `noStartupWindow` | Skips main window creation entirely |\n| `--disable-accelerated-compositing` | `disableAcceleratedCompositing` | Disables the WebKit preference |\n| *(first other argument)* | `requestedURL` | Loaded instead of `about:blank` |\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/Common.cpp:L173-L201](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/Common.cpp#L173-L201)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/Common.h:L34-L48](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/Common.h#L34-L48)\n\n## Main Window and Browser Chrome\n\n`MainWindow` is the top-level Win32 window and implements the `BrowserWindowClient` interface, whose single method is `activeURLChanged(std::wstring)`. Static state (`s_windowClass`, `s_numInstances`, `s_controlledRemotely`, `s_disableAcceleratedCompositing`) is shared by all instances, and `configure()` is called once before any window is created.\n\n`registerClass` loads the class name from the `IDC_PLAYWRIGHT` string resource and registers a `WNDCLASSEX` using `MainWindow::WndProc`, `IDI_PLAYWRIGHT` icons and the `IDC_PLAYWRIGHT` menu. `isInstance(HWND)` compares a window's class name against `s_windowClass`, which the accelerator handler uses to scope translation.\n\n`init` first creates `WKPreferences`, attaches it to the page configuration, disables media capabilities, enables developer extras, and disables accelerated compositing when configured. The main window is created with `WS_OVERLAPPEDWINDOW`, plus `WS_EX_NOACTIVATE` when headless. In non-headless mode `createToolbar` builds a `TOOLBARCLASSNAME` control with a 24-pixel image list from `IDB_TOOLBAR`, three buttons (`Back`, `Forward`, `Reload`) and a separator that doubles as the URL bar slot at index `kToolbarURLBarIndex = 3`. A child `EDIT` control is subclassed with `EditProc`, which selects all text on `WM_SETFOCUS` and turns Enter into `WM_COMMAND`/`IDC_URL_BAR`. Headless mode instead deletes the menu and skips the toolbar.\n\nLayout is handled by `resizeToolbar`, `rescaleToolbar` and `resizeSubViews`; the browser view is positioned below the toolbar's client-space bottom, or fills the whole client area when headless. `WndProc` routes `WM_APPCOMMAND` browser keys to `navigateForwardOrBackward`/`reload`, maps menu and accelerator IDs (`IDM_NEW_WINDOW`, `IDM_CLOSE_WINDOW`, `IDM_ABOUT`, `IDM_WEB_INSPECTOR`, history, zoom, `IDM_RELOAD`), forwards `WM_SIZE` and `WM_DPICHANGED`, and on `WM_NCDESTROY` deletes the object, posting `WM_QUIT` only when not controlled remotely and no instances remain.\n\n`loadURL` converts existing paths and UNC paths with `UrlCreateFromPath`, prefixes `http://` when no scheme is present and the target is not `about:blank`, and then delegates to the browser window. `activeURLChanged` writes the current URL back into the URL bar.\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class BrowserWindowClient {\n        <<interface>>\n        +activeURLChanged(wstring)\n    }\n    class MainWindow {\n        +init(HINSTANCE, WKPageConfigurationRef)\n        +loadURL(wstring)\n        +hwnd()\n        +browserWindow()\n        -createToolbar(HINSTANCE)\n        -resizeSubViews()\n    }\n    class WebKitBrowserWindow {\n        +loadURL(BSTR)\n        +reload()\n        +navigateForwardOrBackward(bool)\n        +launchInspector()\n        +resetZoom()\n    }\n    BrowserWindowClient <|.. MainWindow\n    MainWindow --> WebKitBrowserWindow : owns unique_ptr\n    WebKitBrowserWindow --> BrowserWindowClient : m_client\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/MainWindow.h:L36-L83](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/MainWindow.h#L36-L83)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/WebKitBrowserWindow.h:L32-L86](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/WebKitBrowserWindow.h#L32-L86)\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/MainWindow.cpp:L38-L238](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/MainWindow.cpp#L38-L238)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/MainWindow.cpp:L240-L464](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/MainWindow.cpp#L240-L464)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/MainWindow.h:L36-L83](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/MainWindow.h#L36-L83)\n\n## WebKit Browser Window Wrapper\n\n`WebKitBrowserWindow` wraps a `WKViewRef` created by `WKViewCreate(rect, conf, mainWnd)` and immediately marked in-window. The constructor installs four WK client structs on the page, all with `clientInfo = this` and a `toWebKitBrowserWindow` cast helper for callbacks:\n\n| Client struct | Version | Callbacks registered |\n|---|---|---|\n| `WKPageNavigationClientV0` | 0 | `didReceiveAuthenticationChallenge` |\n| `WKPageUIClientV14` | 14 | `createNewPage`, `didNotHandleKeyEvent`, `close`, JS alert/confirm/prompt, before-unload, `handleJavaScriptDialog`, `getWindowFrame` |\n| `WKPageStateClientV0` | 0 | `didChangeTitle`, `didChangeIsLoading`, `didChangeActiveURL` |\n| `WKPagePolicyClientV1` | 1 | `decidePolicyForResponse`, `decidePolicyForNavigationAction` |\n\nThe constructor ends with `WKPageSetControlledByAutomation(page, true)` and `resetZoom()`, which sets the page zoom to `WebCore::deviceScaleFactorForWindow(hwnd())`. `zoomIn` and `zoomOut` multiply the current factor by `1.25` and `0.8`.\n\nNavigation is thin: `loadURL` calls `WKPageLoadURL` with a URL built by `createWKURL`, `reload` calls `WKPageReload`, history uses `WKPageGoForward`/`WKPageGoBack`, and `launchInspector` shows the inspector from `WKPageGetInspector`. `didChangeTitle` appends `\" [WebKit]\"` to the page title and sets it as the main window caption; `didChangeActiveURL` forwards to `m_client.activeURLChanged`.\n\nAuthentication challenges are resolved in `didReceiveAuthenticationChallenge`: server-trust challenges are auto-accepted when `canTrustServerCertificate` returns true, other schemes prompt via `askCredential` when not headless, and the listener falls back to a null credential. `canTrustServerCertificate` caches host-to-PEM pairs in `m_acceptedServerTrustCerts`, returns false outright in headless mode, and otherwise shows the trust dialog built from host, verification error, description and the PEM chain produced by `createPEMString`.\n\nJavaScript dialogs do not block: each callback retains its result listener into a member (`m_alertDialog`, `m_confirmDialog`, `m_promptDialog`, `m_beforeUnloadDialog`), and `handleJavaScriptDialog` calls the matching listener with the accept flag and optional value before releasing it. The destructor releases any listener still held. Popups go through `createNewPage`, which calls `createViewCallback(configuration, false)` and leaks the retained page per the API contract, while `createPageCallback` is the automation-facing variant that navigates to `about:blank`. Shift/Ctrl left-clicks on links open a new window via `decidePolicyForNavigationAction`, and attachments or unrenderable MIME types are downloaded in `decidePolicyForResponse`. Unhandled key events are forwarded to the main window with `PostMessage`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/WebKitBrowserWindow.cpp:L44-L407](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/WebKitBrowserWindow.cpp#L44-L407)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/WebKitBrowserWindow.h:L32-L86](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/WebKitBrowserWindow.h#L32-L86)\n\n## Shared Helpers and Dialog Plumbing\n\n`Common.cpp` holds process-wide state (`hInst`, `s_windowPosition`, `s_windowSize`, `s_headless`) and the shared utilities declared in `Common.h`:\n\n- `computeFullDesktopFrame` reads `SPI_GETWORKAREA` and scales the work area by `WebCore::deviceScaleFactorForWindow(nullptr)`.\n- `getAppDataFolder` resolves `CSIDL_LOCAL_APPDATA` and appends the executable's base name.\n- `createCrashReport` writes `CrashReport.dmp` with `MiniDumpWithFullMemory` in `_DEBUG` and `MiniDumpNormal` otherwise, then calls `processCrashReport`.\n- `askCredential` and `askServerTrustEvaluation` implement dialog subclasses and return `std::optional<Credential>` / `bool`.\n- `replaceString`, `createString(WKStringRef)`, `createString(WKURLRef)`, `createUTF8String`, `createWKString` and `createWKURL` handle the `_bstr_t`/`std::wstring`/WK string boundary, converting through UTF-8.\n\n`DialogHelper.h` supplies the `Dialog` base class used by both dialogs. `run` calls `DialogBoxParam` with `doalogProc`, which stashes the instance pointer in `DWLP_USER` on `WM_INITDIALOG` and dispatches to `handle`. The handler invokes `setup()` and `update()` on init, maps `IDOK`/`IDCANCEL` to `ok()`/`cancel()` plus `close()`, and forwards other commands to `command(wmId)`. Subclasses can override `validate()` and `updateOkButton()`, and use `setText`, `getText`, `setEnabled`, `item` and the `RadioGroup` helper for control access.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/Common.cpp:L40-L264](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/Common.cpp#L40-L264)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/Common.h:L50-L75](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/Common.h#L50-L75)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/DialogHelper.h:L32-L153](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/DialogHelper.h#L32-L153)\n\n## Precompiled Headers and Replacement Hook\n\n`stdafx.h` is the precompiled header. It includes `cmakeconfig.h` when `HAVE_CONFIG_H` and `BUILDING_WITH_CMAKE` are both defined, sets `WIN32_LEAN_AND_MEAN`, defines `__STDC_LIMIT_MACROS`, and pulls in the Win32 and COM headers (`comdef.h`, `comip.h`, `commctrl.h`, `commdlg.h`, `comutil.h`, `objbase.h`, `shellapi.h`, `shlwapi.h`, `windows.h`, `wininet.h`) plus `wtf/Platform.h`, `JavaScriptCore/JSExportMacros.h` and `WebCore/PlatformExportMacros.h`. A disabled `#if 0` block documents optional Visual Studio leak detection. `stdafx.cpp` contains only `#include \"stdafx.h\"` and is compiled to produce the precompiled type information.\n\n`PlaywrightReplace.h` is a one-line customization seam: it defines `processCrashReport(const wchar_t* fileName)` as a `MessageBox` titled \"Crash Report\". This is the function `createCrashReport` invokes after writing the minidump, so downstream embedders can replace crash handling by editing this header rather than the surrounding code.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/stdafx.h:L31-L73](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/stdafx.h#L31-L73)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/stdafx.cpp:L26-L33](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/stdafx.cpp#L26-L33)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/PlaywrightReplace.h:L25-L29](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/PlaywrightReplace.h#L25-L29)\n- [browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/Common.cpp:L84-L116](browser_patches/webkit/embedder/Playwright/win/Common.cpp#L84-L116)\n"}, {"path": "architecture/browser-patches/winldd.md", "title": "Windows Dependency Scanner (winldd)", "parent": null, "description": "The winldd utility that inspects Windows DLL dependencies, plus its build scripts, expected build manifest, and MSBuild project files.", "files": ["browser_patches/winldd/README.md", "browser_patches/winldd/BUILD_NUMBER", "browser_patches/winldd/EXPECTED_BUILDS", "browser_patches/winldd/archive.sh", "browser_patches/winldd/build.sh", "browser_patches/winldd/buildwin.bat", "browser_patches/winldd/clean.sh", "browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.cpp", "browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.sln", "browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.vcxproj", "browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.vcxproj.filters", "browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.vcxproj.user"], "markdown": "# Windows Dependency Scanner (winldd)\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [browser_patches/winldd/README.md](browser_patches/winldd/README.md)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/BUILD_NUMBER](browser_patches/winldd/BUILD_NUMBER)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/EXPECTED_BUILDS](browser_patches/winldd/EXPECTED_BUILDS)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/archive.sh](browser_patches/winldd/archive.sh)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/build.sh](browser_patches/winldd/build.sh)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/buildwin.bat](browser_patches/winldd/buildwin.bat)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/clean.sh](browser_patches/winldd/clean.sh)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.cpp](browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.cpp)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.sln](browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.sln)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.vcxproj](browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.vcxproj)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.vcxproj.filters](browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.vcxproj.filters)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.vcxproj.user](browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.vcxproj.user)\n</cite>\n\n`winldd` is a small Windows console utility, built from `browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.cpp`, that loads a `.exe` or `.dll` and enumerates the DLLs it imports, printing each one with the full path it resolves to. The directory also contains the shell/batch scripts that drive the build, the MSBuild solution and project files, and two manifest files that record the current build number and the expected archive name.\n\n## Purpose and Output Format\n\nThe `README.md` describes the tool as the Windows counterpart of Linux `ldd`: it loads the specified files and tries to resolve every referenced DLL, printing results in the format `<lib name> => <full path> | \"no found\"`. Dependencies are minimized by linking all C runtime libraries statically; the one remaining dynamic dependency is `dbghelp.dll`, which the README states is preinstalled on all Windows machines.\n\nThe build instructions in the README are to open `PrintDeps.sln` in Visual Studio 2019 and build the `x64/Release` configuration, noting that the output binary is committed into `bin/PrintDeps.exe` and bundled in every npm package.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/winldd/README.md:L1-L12](browser_patches/winldd/README.md#L1-L12)\n\n## Runtime Behavior of PrintDeps.cpp\n\nThe program is a single translation unit. `main` treats every command-line argument as a file to inspect; with no arguments it prints usage and returns `-1`. For each argument it echoes the file name to stdout, calls `printDependencies`, and keeps the return value of the last call, which becomes the process exit code.\n\n```cpp\nint main(int argc, char* argv[])\n{\n    if (argc <= 1)\n    {\n        return printUsage();\n    }\n    int res = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i < argc; ++i)\n    {\n        std::cout << argv[i] << std::endl;\n        res = printDependencies(argv[i]);\n    }\n    return res;\n}\n```\n\n`printUsage` prints the build stamp followed by the accepted argument shape:\n\n```text\nVersion: r<BUILD_NUMBER> Usage:\n  PrintDeps FILE...\n```\n\nThe `BUILD_NUMBER` token is a preprocessor macro supplied by the build, not a literal in the source.\n\nDependency resolution works in two stages:\n\n1. `printDependencies` calls `SetDllDirectoryA(\".\")`, then loads the target with `LoadLibraryEx(library, NULL, DONT_RESOLVE_DLL_REFERENCES | LOAD_LIBRARY_SEARCH_USER_DIRS | LOAD_LIBRARY_SEARCH_SYSTEM32)`. On failure it writes `Failed to load <file>  Error: <message>` to stderr and returns `-1`.\n2. `getDependencies` walks the PE import directory: `ImageDirectoryEntryToData(hMod, true, IMAGE_DIRECTORY_ENTRY_IMPORT, &size)` returns the `IMAGE_IMPORT_DESCRIPTOR` array, and the loop continues while `pImportDesc->Name` is non-zero, which the in-source comment identifies as the array terminator.\n\nFor each descriptor, the DLL name is read at `(BYTE*)hMod + pImportDesc->Name` and the dependency is loaded with the same `LoadLibraryEx` flags. If loading succeeds, `GetModuleFileName` fills a `_MAX_PATH` buffer and the result is stored as the resolved path; a zero result logs `Failed to get library file name: ...` to stderr. The library is then released with `FreeLibrary`. If the dependency cannot be loaded, the map value stays at the initialized string `not found`, and the pair is stored regardless.\n\nResults are printed as a `std::map` iteration, so entries appear in name order:\n\n```cpp\nfor (const auto iter : deps)\n{\n    std::cout << \"    \" << iter.first << \" => \" << iter.second << std::endl;\n}\n```\n\nEach line is indented with four spaces and uses ` => ` as the separator. Note that the literal placeholder in the source is `not found` (two words, no quotes), while the README describes the format as `\"no found\"`; the source is authoritative for actual output. Error text for the whole file comes from `getLastErrorString`, which formats the result of `GetLastError()` via `FormatMessage` with `FORMAT_MESSAGE_ALLOCATE_BUFFER | FORMAT_MESSAGE_FROM_SYSTEM` and frees the buffer with `LocalFree`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.cpp:L41-L64](browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.cpp#L41-L64)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.cpp:L66-L95](browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.cpp#L66-L95)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.cpp:L97-L134](browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.cpp#L97-L134)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/README.md:L3-L7](browser_patches/winldd/README.md#L3-L7)\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"main(argc, argv)\"] --> B[\"argc <= 1: printUsage, return -1\"]\n  A --> C[\"printDependencies(file) for each argument\"]\n  C --> D[\"SetDllDirectoryA('.')\"]\n  D --> E[\"LoadLibraryEx(file, DONT_RESOLVE_DLL_REFERENCES ...)\"]\n  E -->|NULL| F[\"stderr: Failed to load ... return -1\"]\n  E -->|HMODULE| G[\"getDependencies\"]\n  G --> H[\"ImageDirectoryEntryToData(IMAGE_DIRECTORY_ENTRY_IMPORT)\"]\n  H --> I[\"loop while pImportDesc->Name\"]\n  I --> J[\"LoadLibraryEx(dllName, ...)\"]\n  J --> K[\"GetModuleFileName, else keep 'not found'\"]\n  K --> L[\"deps[dllName] = path\"]\n  L --> M[\"stdout: four spaces + name + ' => ' + path\"]\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.cpp:L66-L95](browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.cpp#L66-L95)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.cpp:L97-L134](browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.cpp#L97-L134)\n\n## Build Scripts\n\nThree shell scripts and one batch file make up the build entry points.\n\n| Script | Behavior |\n| --- | --- |\n| `build.sh` | Changes to its own directory, sources `../utils.sh`, and if `is_win` is true runs `/c/Windows/System32/cmd.exe \"/c buildwin.bat\"`; otherwise prints an error to stderr and exits `1`. |\n| `buildwin.bat` | Reads `BUILD_NUMBER` from the file into a variable, exports it as `CL=/DBUILD_NUMBER=%BUILD_NUMBER%`, calls `\"%VS_PATH%\\VC\\Auxiliary\\Build\\vcvars64.bat\"`, then runs `devenv %~dp0\\PrintDeps.sln /build \"Release|x64\"`. |\n| `clean.sh` | Changes to its own directory and removes the `./x64` output tree with `rm -rf ./x64`. |\n| `archive.sh` | Validates its output path and zips `./x64/Release/PrintDeps.exe` (see below). |\n\nAll three shell scripts begin with `set -e`, `set +x`, and a `trap \"cd $(pwd -P)\" EXIT` so the caller's working directory is restored on exit. `build.sh` and `clean.sh` then `cd \"$(dirname $0)\"` to run from the `winldd` directory.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/winldd/build.sh:L1-L14](browser_patches/winldd/build.sh#L1-L14)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/buildwin.bat:L1-L5](browser_patches/winldd/buildwin.bat#L1-L5)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/clean.sh:L1-L8](browser_patches/winldd/clean.sh#L1-L8)\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"build.sh\"] -->|is_win| B[\"buildwin.bat\"]\n  B --> C[\"read BUILD_NUMBER, set CL=/DBUILD_NUMBER\"]\n  B --> D[\"vcvars64.bat from %VS_PATH%\"]\n  D --> E[\"devenv PrintDeps.sln /build Release x64\"]\n  E --> F[\"x64/Release/PrintDeps.exe\"]\n  F --> G[\"archive.sh\"]\n  G --> H[\"7z archive of PrintDeps.exe\"]\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [browser_patches/winldd/build.sh:L5-L14](browser_patches/winldd/build.sh#L5-L14)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/buildwin.bat:L1-L5](browser_patches/winldd/buildwin.bat#L1-L5)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/archive.sh:L40-L42](browser_patches/winldd/archive.sh#L40-L42)\n\n## MSBuild Project Configuration\n\n`PrintDeps.sln` is a Visual Studio 16 (2019) solution file containing a single project, `PrintDeps`, with GUID `{90C6CF9B-BED7-41E9-904D-50BD303BACC8}`. It defines `Debug|x64`, `Debug|x86`, `Release|x64`, and `Release|x86` solution configurations; the `x86` entries map to the project's `Win32` platform.\n\n`PrintDeps.vcxproj` declares the same four `ProjectConfiguration` entries and sets `VCProjectVersion` to `16.0`, `Keyword` to `Win32Proj`, `RootNamespace` to `PrintDeps`, and `WindowsTargetPlatformVersion` to `10.0`. All four configurations use `ConfigurationType` `Application` and `PlatformToolset` `v142`. The Release configurations additionally enable `WholeProgramOptimization`.\n\nThe linker and compiler settings that matter for this tool:\n\n| Setting | Release|x64 | Release|Win32 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `SubSystem` | `Console` | `Console` |\n| `RuntimeLibrary` | `MultiThreaded` (static CRT) | `MultiThreadedDLL` |\n| `AdditionalDependencies` | `dbghelp.lib` | not set |\n| `SetChecksum` | `true` | not set |\n| `EnableCOMDATFolding` / `OptimizeReferences` | `true` | `true` |\n| `FunctionLevelLinking` / `IntrinsicFunctions` | `true` | `true` |\n| `GenerateDebugInformation` | `true` | `true` |\n\nThe `MultiThreaded` runtime library on `Release|x64` is what realizes the README's static-CRT claim, and `dbghelp.lib` supplies `ImageDirectoryEntryToData`. `PrintDeps.vcxproj.filters` only groups `PrintDeps.cpp` under a `Source Files` filter (alongside unused `Header Files` and `Resource Files` filter definitions), and `PrintDeps.vcxproj.user` is an empty `PropertyGroup` with no user-specific overrides.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.sln:L1-L31](browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.sln#L1-L31)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.vcxproj:L1-L55](browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.vcxproj#L1-L55)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.vcxproj:L126-L152](browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.vcxproj#L126-L152)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.vcxproj.filters:L1-L22](browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.vcxproj.filters#L1-L22)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.vcxproj.user:L1-L4](browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.vcxproj.user#L1-L4)\n\n## Build Manifest and Versioning\n\nTwo plain-text manifest files sit next to the scripts:\n\n- `BUILD_NUMBER` holds a single line, `1007`. `buildwin.bat` reads this file with `SET /p BUILD_NUMBER=<BUILD_NUMBER` and passes it to the compiler as `CL=/DBUILD_NUMBER=%BUILD_NUMBER%`, which is how the value reaches the `BUILD_NUMBER` token printed by `printUsage`.\n- `EXPECTED_BUILDS` holds a single line, `winldd-win64.zip`, naming the archive artifact the build pipeline is expected to produce.\n\nBumping the tool version therefore requires editing `BUILD_NUMBER` and rebuilding; no version string is hardcoded in `PrintDeps.cpp`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/winldd/BUILD_NUMBER:L1-L1](browser_patches/winldd/BUILD_NUMBER#L1-L1)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/EXPECTED_BUILDS:L1-L1](browser_patches/winldd/EXPECTED_BUILDS#L1-L1)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/buildwin.bat:L1-L2](browser_patches/winldd/buildwin.bat#L1-L2)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.cpp:L115-L119](browser_patches/winldd/PrintDeps.cpp#L115-L119)\n\n## Archiving a Distribution\n\n`archive.sh` packages the built binary. It takes one positional argument, the absolute path of the output zip, and supports `-h` / `--help` to print its usage text: `usage: <script> [output-absolute-path]`, followed by the description \"Generate distributable .zip archive from ./x64 folder that was previously built.\"\n\nBefore doing any work it rejects four conditions, each with a specific message:\n\n| Check | Message |\n| --- | --- |\n| Path does not start with `/` | `ERROR: path $ZIP_PATH is not absolute` |\n| Path does not end in `.zip` | `ERROR: path $ZIP_PATH must have .zip extension` |\n| Path already exists as a file | `ERROR: path $ZIP_PATH exists; can't do anything.` |\n| Parent directory does not exist | `ERROR: folder for path $($ZIP_PATH) does not exist.` |\n\nIt then verifies the platform with `if [[ \"$(uname)\" != MINGW* ]]` and exits with `ERROR: this script only supports MINGW (windows)` when the check fails. Finally it enters `./x64/Release` and runs `7z a \"$ZIP_PATH\" ./PrintDeps.exe`, so the archive contains the single executable produced by the `Release|x64` build.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [browser_patches/winldd/archive.sh:L1-L42](browser_patches/winldd/archive.sh#L1-L42)\n- [browser_patches/winldd/README.md:L9-L12](browser_patches/winldd/README.md#L9-L12)\n"}, {"path": "architecture/client-server.md", "title": "Client-Server Architecture", "parent": null, "description": "Design of Playwright's client-server communication layer, object lifecycle, and RPC dispatch patterns.", "files": ["eslint.config.mjs"], "markdown": "# Client-Server Architecture\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [eslint.config.mjs](eslint.config.mjs)\n</cite>\n\n## Package Structure\n\nPlaywright's client-server architecture is enforced at the package level through strict import boundaries defined in the build configuration. The core packages are organized into three distinct layers: client, protocol, and server, located within `packages/playwright-core/src/`.\n\nThe **client layer** (`packages/playwright-core/src/client/`) contains the public API surface and runs in user code. The **protocol layer** (`packages/protocol/src/`) defines RPC message types and channel specifications for communication between client and server. The **server layer** (`packages/playwright-core/src/server/`) implements browser automation and runs in a separate process or thread.\n\nThis three-layer division isolates concerns: client code consumes APIs, the protocol serializes requests and responses, and the server executes operations against browsers. The separation ensures that client code never depends directly on server implementation details.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [eslint.config.mjs:L475-L487](eslint.config.mjs#L475-L487)\n- [eslint.config.mjs:L489-L500](eslint.config.mjs#L489-L500)\n\n## Import Boundaries and Dispatch Isolation\n\nStrict import restrictions enforce architectural boundaries between layers. Tools code, located in `packages/playwright-core/src/tools/`, is explicitly prevented from importing from the client layer.\n\n```typescript\n\"no-restricted-imports\": [\n  \"error\",\n  {\n    patterns: [\n      {\n        group: [\"**/client\", \"**/client/**\"],\n        message: \"tools/ must not import from client/\",\n      }\n    ],\n  },\n],\n```\n\nThis restriction ensures that command-line tools and infrastructure code operate independently of the client API, preventing circular dependencies and maintaining tool portability.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [eslint.config.mjs:L417-L443](eslint.config.mjs#L417-L443)\n\n## Entry Points and Core Bundle Management\n\nThe `packages/playwright-core/src/entry/` directory is designated for entry point modules that may import the bundled core. All other code in `src/` must import types and utilities directly rather than through the bundled `coreBundle`.\n\n```typescript\n{\n  group: [\"**/coreBundle\"],\n  message: \"coreBundle can only be imported from entry/ files. Use direct imports instead.\",\n}\n```\n\nThis restriction ensures that only public entry points (such as the main Playwright exports) use the bundled core, while internal server and client code uses direct imports for better build optimization and type safety.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [eslint.config.mjs:L374-L390](eslint.config.mjs#L374-L390)\n\n## Isomorphic and Injected Code\n\nCode that runs in both client and server contexts, or is injected into browser pages, is isolated in separate packages with their own type and import restrictions. The `packages/isomorphic/` directory contains code that must work in both Node.js and browser environments, while `packages/injected/src/` contains scripts injected into page execution contexts.\n\nThese packages apply restricted global access rules to prevent accidental use of environment-specific APIs that would break portability.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [eslint.config.mjs:L445-L472](eslint.config.mjs#L445-L472)\n"}, {"path": "architecture/protocol-rpc.md", "title": "Protocol and RPC Layer", "parent": null, "description": "Protocol channel definitions, RPC message formats, and bidirectional communication between client and server.", "files": ["utils/generate_channels.js", "utils/avd_install.sh", "utils/avd_recreate.sh", "utils/bisect-chromium.mjs", "utils/check_audit.js", "utils/doclint/api_parser.js", "utils/doclint/cli.js"], "markdown": "# Protocol and RPC Layer\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [utils/generate_channels.js](utils/generate_channels.js)\n- [utils/avd_install.sh](utils/avd_install.sh)\n- [utils/avd_recreate.sh](utils/avd_recreate.sh)\n- [utils/bisect-chromium.mjs](utils/bisect-chromium.mjs)\n- [utils/check_audit.js](utils/check_audit.js)\n- [utils/doclint/api_parser.js](utils/doclint/api_parser.js)\n- [utils/doclint/cli.js](utils/doclint/cli.js)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nPlaywright uses a code-generated RPC protocol to enable bidirectional communication between client and server. The protocol layer abstracts the network transport and enforces type safety on all messages exchanged between the browser automation client and the internal server that controls browsers. Channel definitions are defined declaratively in YAML, then compiled into TypeScript type definitions and runtime validators.\n\n## Code Generation Pipeline\n\nThe file [utils/generate_channels.js](utils/generate_channels.js) is a build-time code generator that transforms protocol specifications into TypeScript type definitions. The generator reads a protocol definition (loaded via `protocol_spec` module), parses interface, object, and enum definitions, then outputs five artifacts:\n\n- `packages/protocol/src/structs.d.ts` — shared type definitions for objects and enums\n- `packages/playwright-core/src/client/channels.d.ts` — client-side channel interfaces\n- `packages/playwright-core/src/server/channels.d.ts` — server-side channel interfaces (marked as Dispatcher)\n- `packages/isomorphic/protocolMetainfo.ts` — method metadata and flags\n- `packages/protocol/src/validator.ts` — runtime validation schemas\n\nThis approach ensures that all RPC messages are type-safe at compile time and can be validated at runtime before deserialization.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [utils/generate_channels.js:L1-L50](utils/generate_channels.js#L1-L50)\n- [utils/generate_channels.js:L483-L487](utils/generate_channels.js#L483-L487)\n\n## Channel Definitions and Interfaces\n\nA channel represents a bidirectional RPC endpoint. Each channel interface in the protocol spec generates three TypeScript constructs:\n\n**Initializer types** define the parameters passed when a channel is created. For each channel named `Foo`, the generator produces `FooInitializer`, which is a type object containing the initialization properties.\n\n**Channel interfaces** define the methods and events that can be called or received on that channel. A channel interface extends any parent channel (supporting inheritance) and includes:\n- An initializer type\n- Method declarations with typed parameters and return values\n- Event declarations with typed event parameters\n\n**Event target interfaces** expose only the event subscriptions (`on('eventName', callback)` for clients or `_dispatchEvent(event, params)` for dispatchers).\n\nThe generator also creates trait types (`InitializerTraits`, `EventsTraits`, `EventTargetTraits`) that use TypeScript conditional types to route message types to the correct channel interface based on runtime type checks.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [utils/generate_channels.js:L239-L310](utils/generate_channels.js#L239-L310)\n- [utils/generate_channels.js:L287-L310](utils/generate_channels.js#L287-L310)\n\n## Methods, Events, and Parameters\n\nMethods are declared with named parameters and a return type. For each method, the generator produces three type definitions:\n\n| Type Name | Purpose |\n|-----------|---------|\n| `${ChannelName}${MethodName}Params` | Input parameters to the method |\n| `${ChannelName}${MethodName}Result` | Return value from the method |\n| `${ChannelName}${MethodName}Options` | Only optional parameters (used for selective sending) |\n\nSimilarly, events are defined with optional parameters. For each event, a corresponding event type `${ChannelName}${EventName}Event` is generated.\n\nMethods can be tagged with metadata flags such as `slowMo`, `snapshot`, `pause`, `input`, and `isAutoWaiting`. These flags are stored in method metainfo and used by the test runner for special handling. The generator validates that methods marked as `internal` do not have public-facing flags like `snapshot` or `pause`, and that non-internal methods have a `title` and (if they accept a selector parameter) an explicit `subtitle`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [utils/generate_channels.js:L313-L390](utils/generate_channels.js#L313-L390)\n- [utils/generate_channels.js:L346-L356](utils/generate_channels.js#L346-L356)\n\n## Type System and Validation\n\nThe protocol uses a validator schema system with primitive validators (`tString`, `tBoolean`, `tInt`, `tFloat`, `tArray`, `tObject`, `tEnum`, `tChannel`). Complex types are built compositionally:\n\n- Arrays are validated with `tArray(innerType)`\n- Objects are validated with `tObject({ field: validator, ... })`\n- Optional fields are wrapped with `tOptional(validator)`\n- Channel references use `tChannel(['Type1', 'Type2'])` to allow polymorphic dispatch\n\nEnums are validated against a fixed set of string literals. When a parameter type refers to another defined type in the protocol (such as a struct or enum), the validator uses `tType('TypeName')` to defer to the appropriate validator. This allows circular type references and late binding of type definitions.\n\nEach generated validator is registered in the `scheme` global object during module initialization, enabling runtime message validation before deserialization and providing clear error messages on protocol violations.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [utils/generate_channels.js:L34-L82](utils/generate_channels.js#L34-L82)\n- [utils/generate_channels.js:L141-L163](utils/generate_channels.js#L141-L163)\n- [utils/generate_channels.js:L189-L191](utils/generate_channels.js#L189-L191)\n\n## Generated Artifacts and Inheritance\n\nThe generator handles channel inheritance: interfaces can extend other interfaces, and derived channels inherit method and event definitions from their ancestors. When generating validators for inherited types, derived channels inherit the validators of their parent, allowing the dispatcher to route messages correctly.\n\nStruct types (objects and enums) are generated once into a shared `structs.d.ts` file that both client and server import and re-export. This shared definition ensures that struct shape is consistent across the client/server boundary. Interface channels are generated separately for client and server because the method signatures differ: clients call methods with a timeout context, while dispatchers call methods with a progress context.\n\nThe generator also validates protocol consistency: it confirms that methods have required metadata fields, that internal methods do not expose debugging flags, and that renderParams (used by the recorder) reference valid method parameters. All generated files include a copyright header and a note that they are auto-generated.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [utils/generate_channels.js:L207-L237](utils/generate_channels.js#L207-L237)\n- [utils/generate_channels.js:L318-L345](utils/generate_channels.js#L318-L345)\n- [utils/generate_channels.js:L449-L487](utils/generate_channels.js#L449-L487)\n"}, {"path": "packages-packages.md", "title": "Packages", "parent": null, "description": "Landing page for the in-repository tooling packages: the trace/recording dashboard UI and the browser extension that connects Playwright to a real Chrome profile.", "files": ["packages/dashboard/package.json", "packages/dashboard/index.html", "packages/extension/package.json", "packages/extension/manifest.json", "packages/extension/design.md", "packages/extension/vite.config.mts", "packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.d.ts", "packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.js", "packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.mjs", "packages/playwright-browser-firefox/install.js", "packages/playwright-firefox/cli.js", "packages/playwright-firefox/index.d.ts", "packages/playwright-firefox/index.js", "packages/playwright-firefox/index.mjs", "packages/playwright-firefox/install.js", "packages/recorder/src/callLog.tsx", "packages/recorder/src/index.tsx", "packages/recorder/src/recorder.tsx", "packages/recorder/src/recorderTypes.d.ts", "packages/recorder/src/vite-env.d.ts", "packages/recorder/vite.config.ts"], "markdown": "# Packages\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [packages/dashboard/package.json](packages/dashboard/package.json)\n- [packages/dashboard/index.html](packages/dashboard/index.html)\n- [packages/extension/package.json](packages/extension/package.json)\n- [packages/extension/manifest.json](packages/extension/manifest.json)\n- [packages/extension/design.md](packages/extension/design.md)\n- [packages/extension/vite.config.mts](packages/extension/vite.config.mts)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.d.ts](packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.d.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.js](packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.js)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.mjs](packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.mjs)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-firefox/install.js](packages/playwright-browser-firefox/install.js)\n- [packages/playwright-firefox/cli.js](packages/playwright-firefox/cli.js)\n- [packages/playwright-firefox/index.d.ts](packages/playwright-firefox/index.d.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-firefox/index.js](packages/playwright-firefox/index.js)\n- [packages/playwright-firefox/index.mjs](packages/playwright-firefox/index.mjs)\n- [packages/playwright-firefox/install.js](packages/playwright-firefox/install.js)\n- [packages/recorder/src/callLog.tsx](packages/recorder/src/callLog.tsx)\n- [packages/recorder/src/index.tsx](packages/recorder/src/index.tsx)\n- [packages/recorder/src/recorder.tsx](packages/recorder/src/recorder.tsx)\n- [packages/recorder/src/recorderTypes.d.ts](packages/recorder/src/recorderTypes.d.ts)\n- [packages/recorder/src/vite-env.d.ts](packages/recorder/src/vite-env.d.ts)\n- [packages/recorder/vite.config.ts](packages/recorder/vite.config.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `packages/` directory holds the tooling that ships alongside the Playwright libraries. Three groups are visible in the attached sources: a React dashboard UI, a Chrome extension that lets Playwright clients drive the user's own browser profile, and thin wrapper packages that publish Playwright for Firefox.\n\n| Package directory | Package name | Version | Role |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `packages/dashboard` | `@playwright/dashboard` | `0.0.0` | Private Vite/React dashboard page |\n| `packages/extension` | `@playwright/extension` | `0.6.0` | Manifest V3 extension with a service worker and two UI pages |\n| `packages/recorder` | — | — | React recorder/inspector UI built into `playwright-core` |\n| `packages/playwright-firefox` | — | — | Firefox distribution wrapper with a CLI entry |\n| `packages/playwright-browser-firefox` | — | — | Firefox browser install hook |\n\n`@playwright/dashboard` is marked `\"private\": true` with `\"type\": \"module\"`, and its only declared dependencies are the `@browser-logos/*` icon packages for Chrome, Chromium, Edge, Firefox and Safari variants ([packages/dashboard/package.json:L1-L17](packages/dashboard/package.json#L1-L17)). `@playwright/extension` is also private, targets `node >= 20`, and is licensed Apache-2.0 ([packages/extension/package.json:L1-L17](packages/extension/package.json#L1-L17)).\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/package.json:L1-L17](packages/dashboard/package.json#L1-L17)\n- [packages/extension/package.json:L1-L17](packages/extension/package.json#L1-L17)\n\n## Dashboard UI\n\nThe dashboard is a single-page Vite application. `index.html` sets the document title to `Playwright Dashboard`, links `/playwright-logo.svg` as the favicon, declares `<div id=\"root\">` as the mount point, and loads `/src/index.tsx` as a module ([packages/dashboard/index.html:L16-L28](packages/dashboard/index.html#L16-L28)).\n\nThe source folder contains the React entry point plus feature modules such as `dashboard.tsx`, `recording.tsx`, `screencast.tsx`, `debuggerPanel.tsx`, `sessionSidebar.tsx`, `settingsView.tsx`, `annotateView.tsx`, `annotations.tsx`, `transport.ts`, `dashboardChannel.ts` and `dashboardModel.ts`, along with matching stylesheets (`dashboard.css`, `recording.css`, `annotations.css`, `grid.css`, `modal.css`).\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/index.html:L16-L28](packages/dashboard/index.html#L16-L28)\n- [packages/dashboard/package.json:L1-L17](packages/dashboard/package.json#L1-L17)\n\n## Browser Extension\n\nThe extension connects Playwright clients — the library, the MCP server and the CLI — to tabs in the user's own browser profile, and more than one client can be connected at once ([packages/extension/design.md:L3-L3](packages/extension/design.md#L3-L3)). Its design rests on one invariant: **each tab has zero owners or one owner, and `TabRegistry` is the only object that changes an owner** ([packages/extension/design.md:L5-L5](packages/extension/design.md#L5-L5)).\n\n### Manifest and permissions\n\n`manifest.json` declares Manifest V3 with the permissions `debugger`, `activeTab`, `tabs`, `tabGroups` and `storage`, host permission `<all_urls>`, and a module service worker at `lib/background.mjs` ([packages/extension/manifest.json:L1-L35](packages/extension/manifest.json#L1-L35)). The action and extension icons are 16/32/48/128 px PNGs, and a fixed `key` is embedded in the manifest.\n\n### Runtime model\n\n| Class | File | Count | Responsibility |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `Extension` | `background.ts` | 1 | Service worker entry point; builds the other objects and answers runtime messages |\n| `ConnectionBroker` | `connectionBroker.ts` | 1 | Handshake, relay URL bookkeeping, tab reservation, session lifetime |\n| `TabPicker` | `tabPicker.ts` | 1 | Free groups and free tabs offered to a new client |\n| `TabRegistry` | `tabRegistry.ts` | 1 | Owner table (`claim`, `release`, `transfer`, `releaseAll`) and reservations |\n| `OwnedTab` | `ownedTab.ts` | 1 per owned tab | Debugger attach/detach, CDP commands, badge, announcements |\n| `ClientSession` | `clientSession.ts` | 1 per client | Per-client protocol, command checks, events for owned tabs |\n| `Channel` | `channel.ts` | 1 per client | WebSocket framing: JSON, message ids, responses, errors |\n| `TabGroupManager` | `tabGroupManager.ts` | 1 | Keeps Chrome group membership and ownership identical |\n| `ChromeListeners` | `chromeListeners.ts` | 1 | Adds and removes all Chrome tab, group and debugger listeners together |\n\nTab lifecycle is `Free → Owned → Attached`, with `release` returning a tab to `Free` from either `Owned` or `Attached` ([packages/extension/design.md:L24-L42](packages/extension/design.md#L24-L42)). The extension speaks protocol version 2 over the WebSocket and validates every command in `ClientSession`; a tab that does not exist returns the same error text as a tab owned by another client ([packages/extension/design.md:L88-L100](packages/extension/design.md#L88-L100)).\n\n`checkCdpMethod` refuses CDP methods that mutate browser-level state — cookie and cache clearing, permission grants, `Browser.setDownloadBehavior`, and `Target.*` creation/attachment — while read methods such as `Storage.getCookies` pass ([packages/extension/design.md:L112-L121](packages/extension/design.md#L112-L121)).\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  Connect[\"ui/connect.tsx<br/>connect page\"] -->|\"connectionRequested, getCandidates, connect\"| Broker[\"ConnectionBroker\"]\n  Manage[\"ui/manage.tsx<br/>manage page\"] -->|\"getStatus, disconnect\"| Broker\n  Broker --> Picker[\"TabPicker\"]\n  Broker --> Channel[\"Channel<br/>(WebSocket)\"]\n  Broker --> Session[\"ClientSession\"]\n  Session --> Registry[\"TabRegistry\"]\n  Registry --> Owned[\"OwnedTab<br/>(one per tab)\"]\n  Registry --> Groups[\"TabGroupManager\"]\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [packages/extension/design.md:L53-L65](packages/extension/design.md#L53-L65)\n- [packages/extension/design.md:L160-L171](packages/extension/design.md#L160-L171)\n- [packages/extension/design.md:L67-L67](packages/extension/design.md#L67-L67)\n\n### Build\n\n`vite.config.mts` builds two environments into `dist/`. The `client` environment uses `src/ui` as root, copies `../../icons/*` and `../../manifest.json`, and emits `src/ui/connect.html` and `src/ui/manage.html` to `lib/ui/[name].js` without minification. The `sw` environment consumes `client` and emits `src/background.ts` as an ES library to `lib/background` ([packages/extension/vite.config.mts:L22-L70](packages/extension/vite.config.mts#L22-L70)).\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/extension/manifest.json:L1-L35](packages/extension/manifest.json#L1-L35)\n- [packages/extension/design.md:L3-L5](packages/extension/design.md#L3-L5)\n- [packages/extension/design.md:L24-L42](packages/extension/design.md#L24-L42)\n- [packages/extension/design.md:L88-L121](packages/extension/design.md#L88-L121)\n- [packages/extension/vite.config.mts:L22-L70](packages/extension/vite.config.mts#L22-L70)\n\n## Recorder UI\n\nThe recorder is the inspector front end. `src/index.tsx` applies the theme, imports the shared web styles and codicon font, and renders `<Recorder/>` into `#root` ([packages/recorder/src/index.tsx:L17-L26](packages/recorder/src/index.tsx#L17-L26)).\n\n`recorder.tsx` renders a toolbar with Record, Pick locator, the four assertion modes (visibility, text, value, snapshot), copy, resume/pause/step, a `SourceChooser` target selector, clear and a settings dialog for theme and \"Generate assertions\". Keyboard shortcuts bind `F8` to pause/resume and `F10` to step. The main pane is a read-only `CodeMirrorWrapper`, and the sidebar is a `TabbedPane` with `Locator`, `Log` and `Aria` tabs ([packages/recorder/src/recorder.tsx:L158-L272](packages/recorder/src/recorder.tsx#L158-L272)).\n\nThe backend is a `Proxy` that forwards every method call to `window.sendCommand({ method, params })`, and incoming messages are dispatched through `window.dispatch` into the `RecorderFrontend` handlers `modeChanged`, `sourcesUpdated`, `pageNavigated`, `pauseStateChanged`, `callLogsUpdated`, `sourceRevealRequested` and `elementPicked` ([packages/recorder/src/recorder.tsx:L59-L101](packages/recorder/src/recorder.tsx#L59-L101), [packages/recorder/src/recorder.tsx:L274-L284](packages/recorder/src/recorder.tsx#L274-L284)).\n\n`callLog.tsx` renders each call log entry with a chevron toggle, an icon derived from `status` (`done` → `codicon-check`, `in-progress` → `codicon-clock`, `paused` → `codicon-debug-pause`, `error` → `codicon-error`) and a duration formatted with `msToString` ([packages/recorder/src/callLog.tsx:L36-L70](packages/recorder/src/callLog.tsx#L36-L70)). The shared types in `recorderTypes.d.ts` define the `Mode` union, `CallLog`, `Source`, `RecorderBackend` and `RecorderFrontend` contracts ([packages/recorder/src/recorderTypes.d.ts:L22-L121](packages/recorder/src/recorderTypes.d.ts#L22-L121)).\n\nBuild aliases map `@isomorphic` to `../isomorphic` and `@web` to `../web/src`, and output goes to `../playwright-core/lib/vite/recorder` with `manualChunks: undefined` ([packages/recorder/vite.config.ts:L27-L41](packages/recorder/vite.config.ts#L27-L41)).\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/recorder/src/index.tsx:L17-L26](packages/recorder/src/index.tsx#L17-L26)\n- [packages/recorder/src/recorder.tsx:L158-L284](packages/recorder/src/recorder.tsx#L158-L284)\n- [packages/recorder/src/callLog.tsx:L36-L70](packages/recorder/src/callLog.tsx#L36-L70)\n- [packages/recorder/src/recorderTypes.d.ts:L22-L121](packages/recorder/src/recorderTypes.d.ts#L22-L121)\n- [packages/recorder/vite.config.ts:L27-L41](packages/recorder/vite.config.ts#L27-L41)\n\n## Firefox Wrapper Packages\n\n`packages/playwright-firefox` is a thin distribution layer. `index.js` re-exports `playwright-core` via `module.exports`, `index.mjs` re-exports all named exports plus a default, and `index.d.ts` declares `export * from 'playwright-core'` ([packages/playwright-firefox/index.js:L17-L17](packages/playwright-firefox/index.js#L17-L17), [packages/playwright-firefox/index.mjs:L16-L18](packages/playwright-firefox/index.mjs#L16-L18), [packages/playwright-firefox/index.d.ts:L17-L17](packages/playwright-firefox/index.d.ts#L17-L17)). Its `cli.js` bootstraps `playwright-core/lib/bootstrap`, takes `program` from `utilsBundle`, calls `libCli.decorateProgram(program)` and parses `process.argv` ([packages/playwright-firefox/cli.js:L18-L22](packages/playwright-firefox/cli.js#L18-L22)).\n\nBoth Firefox packages ship an identical `install.js`: it resolves `registry.installBrowsersForNpmInstall` from `playwright-core/lib/coreBundle` only when `utils.isLikelyNpxGlobal()` is false, silently skips in a dev build where the require throws, and otherwise calls `install(['firefox'])` ([packages/playwright-firefox/install.js:L17-L27](packages/playwright-firefox/install.js#L17-L27), [packages/playwright-browser-firefox/install.js:L17-L27](packages/playwright-browser-firefox/install.js#L17-L27)).\n\n`packages/playwright-browser-firefox` has no runtime exports: `index.js`, `index.mjs` and `index.d.ts` contain only the license header and, for the `.d.ts`, `export {}` ([packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.js:L1-L16](packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.js#L1-L16), [packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.mjs:L1-L16](packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.mjs#L1-L16), [packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.d.ts:L17-L17](packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.d.ts#L17-L17)).\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/playwright-firefox/index.js:L17-L17](packages/playwright-firefox/index.js#L17-L17)\n- [packages/playwright-firefox/index.mjs:L16-L18](packages/playwright-firefox/index.mjs#L16-L18)\n- [packages/playwright-firefox/index.d.ts:L17-L17](packages/playwright-firefox/index.d.ts#L17-L17)\n- [packages/playwright-firefox/cli.js:L18-L22](packages/playwright-firefox/cli.js#L18-L22)\n- [packages/playwright-firefox/install.js:L17-L27](packages/playwright-firefox/install.js#L17-L27)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-firefox/install.js:L17-L27](packages/playwright-browser-firefox/install.js#L17-L27)\n"}, {"path": "packages/dashboard/dashboard-app-shell.md", "title": "Dashboard App Shell and Styling", "parent": null, "description": "The dashboard shell layout, shared styling system, icon set, transport layer, and build-time environment declarations.", "files": ["packages/dashboard/src/index.tsx", "packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.tsx", "packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.css", "packages/dashboard/src/grid.css", "packages/dashboard/src/common.css", "packages/dashboard/src/colors.css", "packages/dashboard/src/icons.tsx", "packages/dashboard/src/transport.ts", "packages/dashboard/src/vite-env.d.ts", "packages/dashboard/.gitignore", "packages/playwright-test/cli.js", "packages/playwright-test/index.d.ts", "packages/playwright-test/index.js", "packages/playwright-test/index.mjs", "packages/playwright-test/reporter.d.ts", "packages/playwright-test/reporter.js", "packages/playwright-test/reporter.mjs", "packages/web/playwright.config.ts", "packages/web/playwright/gallery/main.tsx", "packages/web/playwright/vite.config.ts", "packages/web/src/ansi2html.ts", "packages/web/src/components/codeMirrorModule.tsx", "packages/web/src/components/codeMirrorWrapper.spec.ts", "packages/web/src/components/codeMirrorWrapper.story.tsx", "packages/web/src/components/codeMirrorWrapper.tsx", "packages/web/src/components/dialogToolbarButton.tsx", "packages/web/src/components/errorMessage.tsx", "packages/web/src/components/expandable.spec.ts", "packages/web/src/components/expandable.story.tsx", "packages/web/src/components/expandable.tsx", "packages/web/src/components/gridView.tsx", "packages/web/src/components/listView.tsx", "packages/web/src/components/sourceChooser.tsx", "packages/web/src/components/splitView.spec.ts"], "markdown": "# Dashboard App Shell and Styling\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/index.tsx](packages/dashboard/src/index.tsx)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.tsx](packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.tsx)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.css](packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.css)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/grid.css](packages/dashboard/src/grid.css)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/common.css](packages/dashboard/src/common.css)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/colors.css](packages/dashboard/src/colors.css)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/icons.tsx](packages/dashboard/src/icons.tsx)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/transport.ts](packages/dashboard/src/transport.ts)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/vite-env.d.ts](packages/dashboard/src/vite-env.d.ts)\n- [packages/dashboard/.gitignore](packages/dashboard/.gitignore)\n- [packages/playwright-test/cli.js](packages/playwright-test/cli.js)\n- [packages/playwright-test/index.d.ts](packages/playwright-test/index.d.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-test/index.js](packages/playwright-test/index.js)\n- [packages/playwright-test/index.mjs](packages/playwright-test/index.mjs)\n- [packages/playwright-test/reporter.d.ts](packages/playwright-test/reporter.d.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-test/reporter.js](packages/playwright-test/reporter.js)\n- [packages/playwright-test/reporter.mjs](packages/playwright-test/reporter.mjs)\n- [packages/web/playwright.config.ts](packages/web/playwright.config.ts)\n- [packages/web/playwright/gallery/main.tsx](packages/web/playwright/gallery/main.tsx)\n- [packages/web/playwright/vite.config.ts](packages/web/playwright/vite.config.ts)\n- [packages/web/src/ansi2html.ts](packages/web/src/ansi2html.ts)\n- [packages/web/src/components/codeMirrorModule.tsx](packages/web/src/components/codeMirrorModule.tsx)\n- [packages/web/src/components/codeMirrorWrapper.spec.ts](packages/web/src/components/codeMirrorWrapper.spec.ts)\n- [packages/web/src/components/codeMirrorWrapper.story.tsx](packages/web/src/components/codeMirrorWrapper.story.tsx)\n- [packages/web/src/components/codeMirrorWrapper.tsx](packages/web/src/components/codeMirrorWrapper.tsx)\n- [packages/web/src/components/dialogToolbarButton.tsx](packages/web/src/components/dialogToolbarButton.tsx)\n- [packages/web/src/components/errorMessage.tsx](packages/web/src/components/errorMessage.tsx)\n- [packages/web/src/components/expandable.spec.ts](packages/web/src/components/expandable.spec.ts)\n- [packages/web/src/components/expandable.story.tsx](packages/web/src/components/expandable.story.tsx)\n- [packages/web/src/components/expandable.tsx](packages/web/src/components/expandable.tsx)\n- [packages/web/src/components/gridView.tsx](packages/web/src/components/gridView.tsx)\n- [packages/web/src/components/listView.tsx](packages/web/src/components/listView.tsx)\n- [packages/web/src/components/sourceChooser.tsx](packages/web/src/components/sourceChooser.tsx)\n- [packages/web/src/components/splitView.spec.ts](packages/web/src/components/splitView.spec.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Bootstrap and Shell Composition\n\n`packages/dashboard/src/index.tsx` bootstraps the dashboard outside React. It applies the theme, creates a `DashboardClient` with `'ws'`, constructs a `DashboardModel`, and forwards document visibility to the model through `model.setVisible(!document.hidden)` on `visibilitychange`. The `App` component subscribes to the model and rerenders on change.\n\nThe outer shell is a `SplitView` with `orientation='horizontal'`, `sidebarIsFirst`, a 320px sidebar, a 220px minimum, and the setting name `dashboardSessionSidebar`. The sidebar renders `SessionSidebar`; the main area wraps `Dashboard` in `dashboard-shell-main`. The React root is cached on the DOM node as `__dashboardRoot` so an HMR update reuses it instead of calling `createRoot` twice.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[index.tsx: DashboardClient + DashboardModel] --> B[App]\n  B --> C[SplitView: horizontal, sidebar first]\n  C --> D[SessionSidebar]\n  C --> E[dashboard-shell-main]\n  E --> F[Dashboard]\n  F --> G[SplitView: vertical actions panel]\n  G --> H[DebuggerPanel]\n  G --> I[dashboard-main]\n  I --> J[toolbar]\n  I --> K[viewport-wrapper]\n  K --> L[browser-window]\n  L --> M[browser-chrome]\n  L --> N[screen with display image]\n  F --> O[AnnotateOverlay / AnnotateSidebar]\n  F --> P[Recording modal]\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/index.tsx:L31-L53](packages/dashboard/src/index.tsx#L31-L53)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.tsx:L251-L432](packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.tsx#L251-L432)\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/index.tsx:L17-L60](packages/dashboard/src/index.tsx#L17-L60)\n\n## Dashboard Workspace Layout\n\n`Dashboard` reads `tabs`, `mode`, `recording`, `liveFrame`, `annotateSession`, `pendingCapture`, `debuggerPanelOpen`, `debuggerPaused`, and `apiCalls` from `model.state`. Its root is `main.dashboard-view`, with `interactive` and `has-annotate-sidebar` classes added conditionally.\n\nThe inner `SplitView` is vertical, with a 260px actions panel, 140px minimum, setting name `dashboardActionsPanel`, and `sidebarHidden={!debuggerPanelOpen}`. The main column contains:\n\n- A 40px toolbar with interactive, screenshot/annotate, record, and actions-panel buttons.\n- A viewport whose `browser-window` dimensions are computed from the measured viewport and the live frame aspect ratio.\n- A `browser-chrome` bar hidden while `recording.phase === 'recording'`.\n- A focusable `.screen` containing the base64 JPEG `display` image and an optional `debugger-action-point`.\n\nThe browser chrome contains Back, Forward, and Reload buttons plus an omnibox. Navigation and input actions are gated by `interactive`; when inactive, clicks flash the interactive-mode button. The omnibox runs `smartUrl()` on Enter before calling `model.navigate()`. Mouse, wheel, and keyboard events are translated to model calls through `clientToViewport` and `getImageLayout`. The overlay text is `Select a session` when no tab is selected.\n\nThe same component renders `AnnotateOverlay`, `AnnotateSidebar`, and the stopped-recording `Recording` modal when their model state is present.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.tsx:L67-L95](packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.tsx#L67-L95)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.tsx:L96-L130](packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.tsx#L96-L130)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.tsx:L150-L232](packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.tsx#L150-L232)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.tsx:L251-L432](packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.tsx#L251-L432)\n\n## Shared Styling System\n\nStylesheets are layered locally and through the shared web package. `index.tsx` imports `colors.css`, `@web/common.css`, and the dashboard’s own `common.css`; `dashboard.tsx` imports `dashboard.css`. Theme application comes from `@web/theme`.\n\n`colors.css` defines a large `--color-*` palette on `:root`. `:root.light-mode` sets `color-scheme: light`; `:root.dark-mode` sets `color-scheme: dark` and overrides the palette, including foreground, canvas, border, accent, success, attention, danger, and scale variables. Dashboard styles consume these variables rather than hard-coded theme colors.\n\n`common.css` applies a global box-sizing reset, removes default margins and padding, gives `html`, `body`, and `#root` full height, sets the body colors and system font stack, and resets buttons. It also sets `svg { fill: none; }` to override `@web/common.css`.\n\n`dashboard.css` defines the shell’s flex layouts, mode-toggle colors, the 40px toolbar and browser chrome, the browser window frame, the omnibox, viewport sizing, the screencast display with `object-fit: contain`, the screen overlay, and animations for interactive/record flashing and the recording label. The interactive toggle uses `--interactive-orange`; recording uses `--recording-red`.\n\n`grid.css` supplies the grid/session layout: `.grid-view`, toolbar, scrollable content, workspace headers, session chips, status dots, and a fixed 533×300 screencast container.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/index.tsx:L17-L22](packages/dashboard/src/index.tsx#L17-L22)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/common.css:L17-L60](packages/dashboard/src/common.css#L17-L60)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/colors.css:L23-L24](packages/dashboard/src/colors.css#L23-L24)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/colors.css:L309-L348](packages/dashboard/src/colors.css#L309-L348)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/colors.css:L457-L462](packages/dashboard/src/colors.css#L457-L462)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.css:L17-L104](packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.css#L17-L104)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.css:L106-L172](packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.css#L106-L172)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.css:L215-L312](packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.css#L215-L312)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/grid.css:L17-L105](packages/dashboard/src/grid.css#L17-L105)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/grid.css:L194-L218](packages/dashboard/src/grid.css#L194-L218)\n\n## Icon Set\n\n`icons.tsx` combines browser-logo assets with inline SVG components. Browser SVGs are imported from `@browser-logos/*` for Chromium, Chrome channels, Firefox channels, Safari, and Edge. `browserIconUrls` maps browser names and launch channels to those SVGs; `BrowserIcon` prefers a mapped `channel`, falls back to `browserName`, renders `<img className='browser-icon-img'>`, and otherwise shows the browser name’s uppercased initial in `.browser-icon-fallback`.\n\n| Group | Components | Rendering notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Navigation | `ChevronLeftIcon`, `ChevronRightIcon`, `ReloadIcon` | 24×24 viewBox, stroked with `currentColor` |\n| Dashboard modes | `LockIcon`, `LockOpenIcon`, `ScreenshotRegionIcon` | 20×20 filled SVGs using `currentColor` |\n| General actions | `CloseIcon`, `PlusIcon`, `DownloadIcon` | Small inline SVGs |\n| Panels and settings | `InspectorPanelIcon`, `GearIcon` | Inline SVG panels/settings artwork |\n\n`dashboard.tsx` uses `ChevronLeftIcon`, `ChevronRightIcon`, and `ReloadIcon` in browser chrome, `LockIcon`/`LockOpenIcon` for the interactive toggle, and `ScreenshotRegionIcon` for screenshot/annotate mode. Record and actions-panel buttons use shared `ToolbarButton` codicon names (`'record'`, `'list-unordered'`).\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/icons.tsx:L17-L64](packages/dashboard/src/icons.tsx#L17-L64)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/icons.tsx:L66-L134](packages/dashboard/src/icons.tsx#L66-L134)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.tsx:L262-L313](packages/dashboard/src/dashboard.tsx#L262-L313)\n\n## WebSocket Transport\n\n`transport.ts` implements a small WebSocket request/response layer. Each outbound `send(method, params)` increments `_lastId`, serializes `{ id, method, params }`, and stores a pending promise resolver/rejector. Before the socket opens, messages are queued in `_sendQueue`; on `onopen` the queue is flushed. Incoming messages with an `id` resolve or reject the matching pending promise. Messages with `method` are dispatched to `onevent(method, params)`.\n\nInvalid JSON closes the socket. On close, all pending requests reject with `Connection closed`, the queue is cleared, and `onclose` receives the close reason. `sendNoReply()` calls `send()` and swallows rejection. `close()` closes the underlying WebSocket. This is the transport shape used alongside the dashboard’s WebSocket-backed client, created in `index.tsx` via `DashboardClient.create('ws')`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/transport.ts:L17-L92](packages/dashboard/src/transport.ts#L17-L92)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/index.tsx:L31-L32](packages/dashboard/src/index.tsx#L31-L32)\n\n## Build and Test Environment Declarations\n\n`packages/dashboard/src/vite-env.d.ts` contains a single triple-slash reference to `vite/client`, providing Vite’s ambient client types. The dashboard `.gitignore` excludes logs, `node_modules`, `dist`, `dist-ssr`, `*.local`, and common editor directories.\n\nThe shared web package has a separate Vite configuration for its component gallery. `packages/web/playwright/vite.config.ts` sets the Vite root to the parent directory, enables the React plugin, and serves on port `3102` with `strictPort: true`. Its comment states that it serves the gallery at `/playwright/gallery/index.html` for component tests.\n\n`packages/web/playwright.config.ts` points `baseURL` and `webServer.url` at `http://localhost:3102/playwright/gallery/index.html`, with the web-server command `npx vite --config playwright/vite.config.ts` and `reuseExistingServer: !process.env.CI`. The gallery entry `packages/web/playwright/gallery/main.tsx` imports shared `common.css`, theme, codicon styles, and VS Code colors, then loads stories through `import.meta.glob` and exposes `mount`/`unmount` helpers on `window`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/vite-env.d.ts:L1-L1](packages/dashboard/src/vite-env.d.ts#L1-L1)\n- [packages/dashboard/.gitignore:L1-L24](packages/dashboard/.gitignore#L1-L24)\n- [packages/web/playwright/vite.config.ts:L17-L30](packages/web/playwright/vite.config.ts#L17-L30)\n- [packages/web/playwright.config.ts:L17-L53](packages/web/playwright.config.ts#L17-L53)\n- [packages/web/playwright/gallery/main.tsx:L17-L51](packages/web/playwright/gallery/main.tsx#L17-L51)\n"}, {"path": "packages/dashboard/recording-and-screencast.md", "title": "Recording, Screencast, and Session Sidebar", "parent": null, "description": "How the dashboard renders live screencasts, replays recordings, and organizes multiple sessions in the sidebar.", "files": ["packages/dashboard/src/recording.tsx", "packages/dashboard/src/recording.css", "packages/dashboard/src/screencast.tsx", "packages/dashboard/src/sessionSidebar.tsx", "packages/dashboard/src/sessionSidebar.css", "packages/dashboard/src/transport.ts", "packages/dashboard/src/dashboardChannel.ts", "packages/playwright-chromium/cli.js", "packages/playwright-chromium/index.d.ts", "packages/playwright-chromium/index.js", "packages/playwright-chromium/index.mjs", "packages/playwright-chromium/install.js", "packages/protocol/src/serializers.ts", "packages/protocol/src/structs.d.ts", "packages/protocol/src/validator.ts", "packages/protocol/src/validatorPrimitives.ts", "packages/utils/ascii.ts", "packages/utils/chromiumChannels.ts", "packages/utils/comparators.ts", "packages/utils/crypto.ts", "packages/utils/debug.ts", "packages/utils/debugLogger.ts", "packages/utils/env.ts", "packages/utils/eventsHelper.ts", "packages/utils/fileUtils.ts", "packages/utils/hostPlatform.ts", "packages/utils/httpServer.ts"], "markdown": "# Recording, Screencast, and Session Sidebar\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/recording.tsx](packages/dashboard/src/recording.tsx)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/recording.css](packages/dashboard/src/recording.css)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/screencast.tsx](packages/dashboard/src/screencast.tsx)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/sessionSidebar.tsx](packages/dashboard/src/sessionSidebar.tsx)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/sessionSidebar.css](packages/dashboard/src/sessionSidebar.css)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/transport.ts](packages/dashboard/src/transport.ts)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/dashboardChannel.ts](packages/dashboard/src/dashboardChannel.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-chromium/cli.js](packages/playwright-chromium/cli.js)\n- [packages/playwright-chromium/index.d.ts](packages/playwright-chromium/index.d.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-chromium/index.js](packages/playwright-chromium/index.js)\n- [packages/playwright-chromium/index.mjs](packages/playwright-chromium/index.mjs)\n- [packages/playwright-chromium/install.js](packages/playwright-chromium/install.js)\n- [packages/protocol/src/serializers.ts](packages/protocol/src/serializers.ts)\n- [packages/protocol/src/structs.d.ts](packages/protocol/src/structs.d.ts)\n- [packages/protocol/src/validator.ts](packages/protocol/src/validator.ts)\n- [packages/protocol/src/validatorPrimitives.ts](packages/protocol/src/validatorPrimitives.ts)\n- [packages/utils/ascii.ts](packages/utils/ascii.ts)\n- [packages/utils/chromiumChannels.ts](packages/utils/chromiumChannels.ts)\n- [packages/utils/comparators.ts](packages/utils/comparators.ts)\n- [packages/utils/crypto.ts](packages/utils/crypto.ts)\n- [packages/utils/debug.ts](packages/utils/debug.ts)\n- [packages/utils/debugLogger.ts](packages/utils/debugLogger.ts)\n- [packages/utils/env.ts](packages/utils/env.ts)\n- [packages/utils/eventsHelper.ts](packages/utils/eventsHelper.ts)\n- [packages/utils/fileUtils.ts](packages/utils/fileUtils.ts)\n- [packages/utils/hostPlatform.ts](packages/utils/hostPlatform.ts)\n- [packages/utils/httpServer.ts](packages/utils/httpServer.ts)\n</cite>\n\nThe dashboard package splits the live-view and playback concerns into small components: `Screencast` streams frames from the channel into an `<img>`, `Recording` presents a finished capture as a video in a modal, and `SessionSidebar` turns the session list into workspace groups of contexts and tabs. All three sit on top of the `DashboardChannel` event/method surface, which is framed by `Transport` over a WebSocket.\n\n## Live Screencast Rendering\n\n`Screencast` takes a single `client` prop of type `DashboardClientChannel` and holds one piece of state, `frameSrc`. On mount it registers a listener for the `frame` event and, on every event, stores `'data:image/jpeg;base64,' + params.data` — so frames arrive as base64 JPEG payloads rather than a video stream. The effect returns `client.off('frame', listener)`, removing the subscription when the component unmounts or the client changes. Until the first frame arrives it renders `<div className='screencast-placeholder'>Connecting...</div>`; afterwards it renders `<img className='screencast-frame' alt='screencast' src={frameSrc} />`.\n\nThe event payload is declared as `{ data: string; viewportWidth: number; viewportHeight: number }` in `dashboardChannel.ts`, but `Screencast` only consumes `data`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/screencast.tsx:L22-L37](packages/dashboard/src/screencast.tsx#L22-L37)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/dashboardChannel.ts:L46-L55](packages/dashboard/src/dashboardChannel.ts#L46-L55)\n\n## Recording Preview Modal\n\n`Recording` is presentation-only. It accepts `blob`, `blobUrl`, `onSave` and `onClose`, and renders a `modal-overlay` with `role='dialog'`, `aria-modal='true'` and `aria-label='Recording preview'`. The toolbar contains the title `Recording`, a `ToolbarButton` titled `Save recording` wrapping `DownloadIcon`, and a `ToolbarButton` with `icon='close'` titled `Discard`. Saving runs `await onSave(blob)` inside a `React.useCallback` keyed on `onSave` and `blob`; discarding calls `onClose`.\n\nThe body contains a `<video className='recording-video' src={blobUrl} controls autoPlay />`. `recording.css` gives the body a black background and constrains the video with `max-width: 100%`, `max-height: 100%`, `object-fit: contain` and `display: block`.\n\nCapture itself is driven through channel methods rather than this component: `startRecording(): Promise<void>`, `stopRecording(): Promise<{ streamId: string }>`, and `readStream(params: { streamId: string }): Promise<{ data: string; eof: boolean }>`.\n\n| Channel method | Declared signature |\n|---|---|\n| `startRecording` | `(): Promise<void>` |\n| `stopRecording` | `(): Promise<{ streamId: string }>` |\n| `readStream` | `({ streamId: string }): Promise<{ data: string; eof: boolean }>` |\n| `screenshot` | `(): Promise<{ data: string; viewportWidth: number; viewportHeight: number; ariaSnapshot: string }>` |\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/recording.tsx:L23-L63](packages/dashboard/src/recording.tsx#L23-L63)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/recording.css:L17-L26](packages/dashboard/src/recording.css#L17-L26)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/dashboardChannel.ts:L94-L97](packages/dashboard/src/dashboardChannel.ts#L94-L97)\n\n## Session Grouping in the Sidebar\n\n`SessionSidebar` reads `sessions`, `clientInfo`, `loadingSessions` and `tabs` from `model.state`, rendering a `<nav className='dashboard-shell-sidebar' aria-label='Sessions'>` whose header holds an `h2` titled `Sessions` and a `SettingsButton`.\n\nSessions are bucketed by `session.workspaceDir || 'Global'`, each bucket sorted by `title.localeCompare`, and the group matching `clientInfo?.workspaceDir || 'Global'` is placed first; remaining groups are sorted by workspace key. `normalizeWorkspacePath` leaves `Global` and empty values untouched, prefixes a `/` on relative paths containing `/`, and collapses the home directory to `~` (or `~/...`). The CSS ellipsizes the header with `direction: rtl` and `unicode-bidi: plaintext`, keeping the trailing path segments visible.\n\nEmpty and loading states are announced: `Loading sessions...` while `loadingSessions`, and `No open sessions.` when the list is empty, both with `role='status'` and `aria-live='polite'`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[sessions from model.state] --> B[group by workspaceDir or 'Global']\n  B --> C[sort titles, current workspace first]\n  C --> D[per session: session.browser.guid]\n  D --> E[tabsByBrowserAndContext: browser to context to tabs]\n  E --> F[one row per context]\n  F --> G[ListView of tabs]\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/sessionSidebar.tsx:L79-L149](packages/dashboard/src/sessionSidebar.tsx#L79-L149)\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/sessionSidebar.tsx:L43-L56](packages/dashboard/src/sessionSidebar.tsx#L43-L56)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/sessionSidebar.tsx:L103-L131](packages/dashboard/src/sessionSidebar.tsx#L103-L131)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/sessionSidebar.css:L17-L103](packages/dashboard/src/sessionSidebar.css#L17-L103)\n\n## Context and Tab Rows\n\nFor each session the sidebar looks up `tabsByBrowserAndContext.get(session.browser.guid)` and derives rows: if `allTabs === null` it emits one row with `tabs: undefined`; if there are no contexts it emits one row with `tabs: []`; otherwise one row per context. `tabs === undefined` renders `Loading tabs...`, while an empty array reaches the list's `noItemsMessage='No tabs open.'`. A row is marked `active` when it contains a tab whose `context` equals the selected tab's context and which is itself `selected`.\n\nEach row header shows `BrowserIcon` with `browserName` and the optional launch `channel`, a `session-browser-close` button calling `model.closeSession(session)`, the session title, and — only when a context GUID exists — a `sidebar-session-new-tab` button calling `model.newTab(guid, row.contextGuid)`. Hover behaviour is CSS-only: `.session-browser-icon-wrap:hover` fades the icon out (`opacity: 0`) and reveals the close button, while `.sidebar-tab-close` appears on entry hover or `:focus-visible`.\n\n`TabListView` (`ListView<Tab>`) receives `items`, `id={tab => tab.page}`, `selectedItem={activeTab}`, `onSelected={tab => model.selectTab(tab)}` and a `render` of `TabRow`. `TabRow` prefers `tab.faviconUrl` as an image, otherwise renders a placeholder letter from the URL hostname (leading `www.` stripped, first character uppercased). It shows `tab.title || 'New Tab'` and `tab.url || 'about:blank'`, plus a close button that calls `e.stopPropagation()` before `model.closeTab(tab)`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/sessionSidebar.tsx:L31-L77](packages/dashboard/src/sessionSidebar.tsx#L31-L77)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/sessionSidebar.tsx:L138-L198](packages/dashboard/src/sessionSidebar.tsx#L138-L198)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/sessionSidebar.css:L111-L190](packages/dashboard/src/sessionSidebar.css#L111-L190)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/sessionSidebar.css:L228-L307](packages/dashboard/src/sessionSidebar.css#L228-L307)\n\n## Channel and Transport Plumbing\n\n`DashboardChannel` enumerates both directions: commands such as `selectTab`, `newTab`, `closeSession`, `setVisible`, `navigate`, the recording trio, `submitAnnotation` and debugger controls, plus typed `on`/`off` registration for `sessions`, `tabs`, `frame`, `annotate`, `cancelAnnotate`, `apiCalls`, `debuggerPaused` and `debuggerSource`.\n\n`Transport` wraps a `WebSocket`. Outgoing messages are `{ id, method, params }` with a monotonically increasing `_lastId`; replies are matched by `msg.id` against `_pending` and either resolved with `msg.result` or rejected with `new Error(msg.error)`. Messages with `msg.method` are forwarded to `onevent`. Before the socket opens, `send` pushes serialized messages onto `_sendQueue`, which is flushed in `onopen`. Closing sets `_closed`, clears the queue, rejects every pending entry with `Connection closed`, and invokes `onclose(event.reason)`. `sendNoReply` delegates to `send` and swallows the rejection, which suits fire-and-forget commands.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/dashboardChannel.ts:L77-L106](packages/dashboard/src/dashboardChannel.ts#L77-L106)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/transport.ts:L17-L91](packages/dashboard/src/transport.ts#L17-L91)\n"}, {"path": "packages/dashboard/annotations.md", "title": "Annotations and Image Export", "parent": null, "description": "The annotation overlay model: drawing views, annotation images, layout math, and zipping annotated screenshots for export.", "files": ["packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx", "packages/dashboard/src/annotationImage.ts", "packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx", "packages/dashboard/src/annotations.css", "packages/dashboard/src/annotationZip.ts", "packages/dashboard/src/imageLayout.ts", "packages/playwright-browser-webkit/index.d.ts", "packages/playwright-browser-webkit/index.js", "packages/playwright-browser-webkit/index.mjs", "packages/playwright-browser-webkit/install.js", "packages/playwright-webkit/cli.js", "packages/playwright-webkit/index.d.ts", "packages/playwright-webkit/index.js", "packages/playwright-webkit/index.mjs", "packages/playwright-webkit/install.js", "packages/trace-viewer/bundle.ts", "packages/trace-viewer/src/index.tsx", "packages/trace-viewer/src/snapshotMain.ts", "packages/trace-viewer/src/sw-main.ts", "packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/main.ts", "packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/progress.ts", "packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/traceLoaderBackends.ts", "packages/trace-viewer/src/third_party/devtools.ts", "packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/actionList.tsx", "packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/annotationsTab.tsx", "packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/ariaModeView.tsx", "packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/attachmentsTab.tsx", "packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/browserFrame.tsx", "packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/callTab.tsx"], "markdown": "# Annotations and Image Export\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx](packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotationImage.ts](packages/dashboard/src/annotationImage.ts)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx](packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotations.css](packages/dashboard/src/annotations.css)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotationZip.ts](packages/dashboard/src/annotationZip.ts)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/imageLayout.ts](packages/dashboard/src/imageLayout.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-webkit/index.d.ts](packages/playwright-browser-webkit/index.d.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-webkit/index.js](packages/playwright-browser-webkit/index.js)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-webkit/index.mjs](packages/playwright-browser-webkit/index.mjs)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-webkit/install.js](packages/playwright-browser-webkit/install.js)\n- [packages/playwright-webkit/cli.js](packages/playwright-webkit/cli.js)\n- [packages/playwright-webkit/index.d.ts](packages/playwright-webkit/index.d.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-webkit/index.js](packages/playwright-webkit/index.js)\n- [packages/playwright-webkit/index.mjs](packages/playwright-webkit/index.mjs)\n- [packages/playwright-webkit/install.js](packages/playwright-webkit/install.js)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/bundle.ts](packages/trace-viewer/bundle.ts)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/index.tsx](packages/trace-viewer/src/index.tsx)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/snapshotMain.ts](packages/trace-viewer/src/snapshotMain.ts)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/sw-main.ts](packages/trace-viewer/src/sw-main.ts)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/main.ts](packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/main.ts)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/progress.ts](packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/progress.ts)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/traceLoaderBackends.ts](packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/traceLoaderBackends.ts)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/third_party/devtools.ts](packages/trace-viewer/src/third_party/devtools.ts)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/actionList.tsx](packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/actionList.tsx)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/annotationsTab.tsx](packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/annotationsTab.tsx)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/ariaModeView.tsx](packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/ariaModeView.tsx)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/attachmentsTab.tsx](packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/attachmentsTab.tsx)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/browserFrame.tsx](packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/browserFrame.tsx)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/callTab.tsx](packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/callTab.tsx)\n</cite>\n\n## Overlay and Sidebar Composition\n\n`AnnotateSidebar` renders a frame list from `session.frames`, showing each screenshot as a base64 PNG and overlaying annotation rectangles as percentages of the frame viewport. Hovering a comment sets `hoveredAnnotationId`, which highlights the matching thumbnail rectangle. Clicking a comment calls `model.selectAnnotateFrame(frame.id, a.id)`, while clicking a thumbnail calls it with only the frame id. The sidebar also contains a feedback textarea bound to `session.feedback`, a submit button that awaits `onSubmit`, and a remove button for each frame.\n\n`AnnotateOverlay` is a dialog with a title bar, Save, Clear, and Done controls. Its canvas contains the screenshot image and the `Annotations` layer. Save calls `buildAnnotatedImage` with the image element, viewport dimensions, and frame annotations, then downloads the result with a sanitized filename containing a timestamp. Clear resets annotations to an empty array and clears the annotation selection.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx:L26-L30](packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx#L26-L30)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx:L32-L138](packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx#L32-L138)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx:L56-L78](packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx#L56-L78)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx:L80-L97](packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx#L80-L97)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx:L111-L135](packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx#L111-L135)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx:L146-L170](packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx#L146-L170)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx:L172-L220](packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx#L172-L220)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotations.css:L237-L459](packages/dashboard/src/annotations.css#L237-L459)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotations.css:L461-L546](packages/dashboard/src/annotations.css#L461-L546)\n\n## Drawing Model and Interaction\n\nAn annotation is `{ id, x, y, width, height, text, color }`, with coordinates stored in viewport space. Six palette colors are defined as space-separated RGB triples, and new annotations default to the first color. IDs are generated as `ann-` plus a random suffix.\n\nThe drawing layer supports creating, moving, resizing, editing, and deleting rectangles:\n\n- A mouse press on empty space starts a draft rectangle; mouse movement updates it, and mouse release normalizes the rectangle and creates an annotation only if both dimensions are at least `MIN_ANNOTATION_SIZE` (4).\n- A mouse press inside an existing annotation selects it and starts a move drag. Hit testing scans annotations from last to first.\n- A selected, non-editing annotation renders eight resize handles (`nw`, `n`, `ne`, `e`, `se`, `s`, `sw`, `w`). `applyDrag` updates the appropriate edges and re-normalizes the rectangle.\n- Keyboard arrows nudge the selected annotation by 5 viewport pixels, or 1 when Shift is held. Delete/Backspace removes it.\n- Escape cancels a draft, closes an active editor, or clears selection, in that order.\n- Double-clicking a rectangle or its label opens the text editor. The popover shows Cancel/Done when editing an existing annotation, or Discard/Add when the annotation is newly created.\n\nThe `editSnapshot` state preserves the annotation as it was when editing began; Cancel restores that snapshot. The color picker changes both the edited annotation and the active color for subsequent annotations.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx:L24-L38](packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx#L24-L38)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx:L40-L92](packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx#L40-L92)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx:L105-L157](packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx#L105-L157)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx:L192-L321](packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx#L192-L321)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx:L323-L344](packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx#L323-L344)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx:L346-L499](packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx#L346-L499)\n\n## Mapping Between Screen and Viewport Coordinates\n\n`getImageLayout` models an `object-fit: contain` image. It compares the image aspect ratio with the element aspect ratio, then computes the rendered width, rendered height, and centering offsets. If the image is wider than the element, width fills the element and vertical offsets are applied; otherwise height fills the element and horizontal offsets are applied.\n\n`clientToViewport` converts a browser client coordinate into viewport coordinates by subtracting the image bounding rectangle and layout offsets, dividing by the rendered size, and multiplying by the viewport dimensions. Results are rounded.\n\n`viewportRectToScreenStyle` performs the inverse mapping for rendering: it computes the image’s base position relative to the annotation screen container, then scales each rectangle field by the rendered image size and viewport size. The `Annotations` component recalculates this layout on every render and forces a re-render on window resize or image load.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/imageLayout.ts:L17-L45](packages/dashboard/src/imageLayout.ts#L17-L45)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx:L94-L103](packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx#L94-L103)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx:L161-L173](packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx#L161-L173)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx:L215-L231](packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx#L215-L231)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx:L339-L344](packages/dashboard/src/annotations.tsx#L339-L344)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx:L56-L78](packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx#L56-L78)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotations.css:L30-L43](packages/dashboard/src/annotations.css#L30-L43)\n\n## Rendering Annotations into a PNG\n\n`buildAnnotatedImage` returns `null` when the image has no natural dimensions or the viewport dimensions are missing. Otherwise it creates a canvas at the image’s natural size, draws the screenshot, and computes independent horizontal and vertical scale factors from the canvas size divided by the viewport size.\n\nEach annotation is converted from viewport coordinates to canvas coordinates using those scales. The rectangle is filled with a 12% opacity wash and stroked with the solid annotation color. Line width scales with the image but is never less than 2 pixels. When text exists, a filled label is drawn above the rectangle using measured text width, scaled padding, and a font size derived from the vertical scale, with a minimum of 11 pixels. The canvas is exported as a PNG blob.\n\n`saveAnnotationAsDownload` prefers `window.showSaveFilePicker` when available, suggesting the filename, starting in Downloads, and writing the blob through a writable stream. An `AbortError` returns `false`; other errors are rethrown. Without the picker, it creates an object URL, clicks a temporary download link, removes the link, and revokes the URL.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[AnnotateOverlay onSave] --> B[buildAnnotatedImage]\n  B --> C[canvas at natural image size]\n  C --> D[draw screenshot]\n  D --> E[scale and draw rectangles and labels]\n  E --> F[canvas.toBlob PNG]\n  F --> G[saveAnnotationAsDownload]\n  G --> H[showSaveFilePicker]\n  G --> I[anchor download fallback]\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx:L155-L165](packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx#L155-L165)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotationImage.ts:L19-L67](packages/dashboard/src/annotationImage.ts#L19-L67)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotationImage.ts:L69-L108](packages/dashboard/src/annotationImage.ts#L69-L108)\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotationImage.ts:L17-L67](packages/dashboard/src/annotationImage.ts#L17-L67)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotationImage.ts:L69-L108](packages/dashboard/src/annotationImage.ts#L69-L108)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx:L155-L165](packages/dashboard/src/annotateView.tsx#L155-L165)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotations.css:L226-L234](packages/dashboard/src/annotations.css#L226-L234)\n\n## Packaging Annotated Screenshots as a Zip\n\n`buildAnnotationZip` uses the no-worker deflate build of `@zip.js/zip.js`, disables web workers, and creates a `ZipWriter` backed by a `BlobWriter` with the `application/zip` MIME type. The resulting archive contains a Markdown summary and, per frame, a screenshot and optional aria snapshot.\n\nFor each frame, the Markdown receives a heading with the frame index, session title, tab title, URL, and viewport dimensions. If the frame has PNG data, the function writes `annotations-<index>.png` and links it in Markdown. If the frame has an aria snapshot, it writes `annotations-<index>.yaml` containing one comment line per annotation with coordinates and text, followed by the aria snapshot text, and links that file as well. Annotations with non-empty text are also listed as Markdown bullets.\n\nThe feedback text is prepended to the Markdown when present. The final `feedback.md` entry is always written, and `writer.close()` resolves the archive blob.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotationZip.ts:L17-L23](packages/dashboard/src/annotationZip.ts#L17-L23)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/annotationZip.ts:L25-L62](packages/dashboard/src/annotationZip.ts#L25-L62)\n"}, {"path": "packages/dashboard/debugger-panel.md", "title": "Debugger Panel, Modals, and Settings", "parent": null, "description": "The debugging side panel, modal infrastructure, and settings view used inside the dashboard.", "files": ["packages/dashboard/src/debuggerPanel.tsx", "packages/dashboard/src/debuggerPanel.css", "packages/dashboard/src/modal.css", "packages/dashboard/src/settingsView.tsx", "packages/dashboard/src/settingsView.css", "packages/html-reporter/bundle.ts", "packages/html-reporter/playwright.config.ts", "packages/html-reporter/src/chip.spec.ts", "packages/html-reporter/src/chip.story.tsx", "packages/html-reporter/src/chip.tsx", "packages/html-reporter/src/copyToClipboard.tsx", "packages/html-reporter/src/expandable.tsx", "packages/html-reporter/src/filter.ts", "packages/html-reporter/src/gantt.tsx", "packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.spec.ts", "packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.story.tsx", "packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.tsx", "packages/html-reporter/src/icons.tsx", "packages/html-reporter/src/images.ts", "packages/html-reporter/src/index.tsx", "packages/html-reporter/src/labels.tsx", "packages/html-reporter/src/links.tsx", "packages/html-reporter/src/loadedReport.ts", "packages/html-reporter/src/metadataView.tsx", "packages/html-reporter/src/reportView.tsx", "packages/html-reporter/src/sampleReport.ts"], "markdown": "# Debugger Panel, Modals, and Settings\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/debuggerPanel.tsx](packages/dashboard/src/debuggerPanel.tsx)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/debuggerPanel.css](packages/dashboard/src/debuggerPanel.css)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/modal.css](packages/dashboard/src/modal.css)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/settingsView.tsx](packages/dashboard/src/settingsView.tsx)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/settingsView.css](packages/dashboard/src/settingsView.css)\n- [packages/html-reporter/bundle.ts](packages/html-reporter/bundle.ts)\n- [packages/html-reporter/playwright.config.ts](packages/html-reporter/playwright.config.ts)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/chip.spec.ts](packages/html-reporter/src/chip.spec.ts)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/chip.story.tsx](packages/html-reporter/src/chip.story.tsx)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/chip.tsx](packages/html-reporter/src/chip.tsx)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/copyToClipboard.tsx](packages/html-reporter/src/copyToClipboard.tsx)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/expandable.tsx](packages/html-reporter/src/expandable.tsx)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/filter.ts](packages/html-reporter/src/filter.ts)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/gantt.tsx](packages/html-reporter/src/gantt.tsx)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.spec.ts](packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.spec.ts)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.story.tsx](packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.story.tsx)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.tsx](packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.tsx)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/icons.tsx](packages/html-reporter/src/icons.tsx)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/images.ts](packages/html-reporter/src/images.ts)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/index.tsx](packages/html-reporter/src/index.tsx)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/labels.tsx](packages/html-reporter/src/labels.tsx)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/links.tsx](packages/html-reporter/src/links.tsx)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/loadedReport.ts](packages/html-reporter/src/loadedReport.ts)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/metadataView.tsx](packages/html-reporter/src/metadataView.tsx)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/reportView.tsx](packages/html-reporter/src/reportView.tsx)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/sampleReport.ts](packages/html-reporter/src/sampleReport.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Debugger Panel Layout and Run Controls\n\n`DebuggerPanel` is a `React.FC` that receives a single `DashboardModel` prop and reads four fields from `model.state`: `apiCalls`, `debuggerPaused`, `debuggerPauseRequested`, and `debuggerSource`. The component is explicitly noted as modeled after the recorder's `CallLogView` and debug toolbar.\n\nThe panel root carries `debugger-panel` plus conditional `paused` and `pause-pending` classes. The pending state is computed as `debuggerPauseRequested && !debuggerPaused`. The top `Toolbar` contains a static \"Actions\" title, three `ToolbarButton`s, and conditional status text:\n\n| Control | Icon | Title / aria-label | Disabled when | Action |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Resume | `debug-continue` | `Resume (F8)` / `Resume` | not paused | `model.debuggerResume()` |\n| Pause | `debug-pause` | `Pause (F8)` / `Pause` | paused | `model.debuggerPause()`; `toggled` while pending |\n| Step over | `debug-step-over` | `Step over (F10)` / `Step over` | not paused | `model.debuggerStep()` |\n\nStatus text is `Paused` while paused and `Pausing before the next action…` while a pause is pending. The two enabled run controls use the `debugger-run-control` class, which the stylesheet colors with `--color-success-fg` when not disabled. Paused and pending states tint the toolbar with `--color-attention-subtle`, and `.debugger-status` uses `--color-attention-fg`.\n\nA document-level `keydown` listener implements the shortcuts: `F8` calls `debuggerResume()` when paused, otherwise `debuggerPause()`; `F10` calls `debuggerStep()` only while paused. Both call `preventDefault()`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/debuggerPanel.tsx:L28-L77](packages/dashboard/src/debuggerPanel.tsx#L28-L77)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/debuggerPanel.tsx:L49-L64](packages/dashboard/src/debuggerPanel.tsx#L49-L64)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/debuggerPanel.css:L17-L48](packages/dashboard/src/debuggerPanel.css#L17-L48)\n\n## Action Log and Source Split\n\nBelow the toolbar, a `SplitView` is configured with `orientation='horizontal'`, `sidebarSize={380}`, `minSidebarSize={220}`, `settingName='dashboardDebuggerSource'`, and `sidebarHidden={!debuggerSource}`. The `main` slot is the call log; the `sidebar` slot is `SourceView`.\n\nThe call log renders \"No actions yet\" when `apiCalls` is empty, then one `debugger-call` element per call, keyed by `call.id` and tagged with `call.status`. Each header shows a chevron, the call title, an optional location label, and a status icon for non-success calls (`codicon-loading codicon-modifier-spin` for `running`, `codicon-error` for `error`). Locations are reduced to the basename via `split(/[\\\\/]/).pop()` and rendered as `file:line` when a line exists, otherwise just the file.\n\nExpansion is controlled by a `Map<string, boolean>` of per-call overrides. A call is expandable only when it has logs or an error, and its default expansion is driven by status: `call.status !== 'success'`. Clicking an expandable header writes the inverse of the current state into a copied map. Log messages are trimmed and rendered in `debugger-call-message`; the error message uses the `error` modifier and is hidden when collapsed.\n\nScrolling behavior uses `stickToBottomRef`, initialized to `true`. A layout effect scrolls `messagesEndRef` into view on each `apiCalls` change only while sticking, and the log's `onScroll` recomputes sticking as `scrollTop + clientHeight >= scrollHeight - 40`, so a user who scrolls up keeps their position during live updates.\n\n`SourceView` renders \"No source\" when `source` is `null`. Otherwise it shows a header with the file basename and the full path as `title`, followed by a read-only, line-numbered `CodeMirrorWrapper` fed `source.text`, `source.language`, `source.highlight`, and `source.revealLine`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/debuggerPanel.tsx:L78-L140](packages/dashboard/src/debuggerPanel.tsx#L78-L140)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/debuggerPanel.tsx:L142-L152](packages/dashboard/src/debuggerPanel.tsx#L142-L152)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/debuggerPanel.css:L50-L159](packages/dashboard/src/debuggerPanel.css#L50-L159)\n\n## Modal Styling\n\n`modal.css` supplies the dashboard's full-screen dialog presentation. `.modal-overlay` is a fixed, inset-0 flex container that centers its child over a `rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55)` backdrop at `z-index: 1000` with 24px padding. `.modal` fills that space as a column flexbox with the canvas background, a default border, 10px radius, and a `0 10px 40px` shadow.\n\nThe dialog chrome is split into `.modal-toolbar` (40px tall, right-aligned controls, overlay background, bottom border), `.modal-title` (auto right margin, 13px, weight 600), and `.modal-body` (flex: 1, centered content, `min-height: 0`, 16px padding). A light-mode override swaps the toolbar background to `--color-canvas-subtle`. These are style definitions only; the sources shown do not include the component that applies them.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/modal.css:L17-L72](packages/dashboard/src/modal.css#L17-L72)\n\n## Settings Popup\n\n`SettingsButton` is a self-contained toolbar control. It holds `open` state, obtains `[theme, setTheme]` from `useThemeSetting()`, and keeps a `containerRef` on its wrapper. While open, it registers document-level `mousedown` and `keydown` listeners: a mousedown outside the container closes the popup, and `Escape` closes it. Both listeners are removed when the popup closes or the component unmounts.\n\nThe trigger is a `ToolbarButton` with class `settings-gear-btn`, title `Settings`, and `toggled={open}`; its child is a local `GearIcon`. When open, a `settings-popup` div renders a single `setting-row` labelled \"Theme\". Each entry in `kThemeOptions` becomes a `setting-option`, marked `selected` when `theme === o.value`; clicking one calls `setTheme(o.value as Theme)` and closes the popup.\n\nStyling anchors the popup with `.settings-button-container { position: relative }` and positions `.settings-popup` at `top: 100%; right: 0` with a 4px offset, overlay background, 8px radius, `min-width: 160px`, overlay shadow, and `z-index: 100`. Labels are 11px uppercase with letter spacing, options are 28px rows with hover and selected colors. Note this is a popup panel, distinct from the overlay-based `.modal` styles above.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/dashboard/src/settingsView.tsx:L23-L77](packages/dashboard/src/settingsView.tsx#L23-L77)\n- [packages/dashboard/src/settingsView.css:L17-L87](packages/dashboard/src/settingsView.css#L17-L87)\n"}, {"path": "packages/extension/extension-architecture.md", "title": "Extension Architecture and Message Channel", "parent": null, "description": "The extension's service worker entry point, its typed message channel, and the design notes that describe the client/server split.", "files": ["packages/extension/design.md", "packages/extension/src/background.ts", "packages/extension/src/channel.ts", "packages/extension/src/types.ts", "packages/extension/.gitignore", "packages/injected/src/ariaSnapshot.ts", "packages/injected/src/ariaSnapshotDistiller.ts", "packages/injected/src/bidiInsertText.ts", "packages/injected/src/bindingsController.ts", "packages/injected/src/clock.ts", "packages/injected/src/consoleApi.ts", "packages/injected/src/coverageScript.ts", "packages/injected/src/domUtils.ts", "packages/injected/src/highlight.ts"], "markdown": "# Extension Architecture and Message Channel\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [packages/extension/design.md](packages/extension/design.md)\n- [packages/extension/src/background.ts](packages/extension/src/background.ts)\n- [packages/extension/src/channel.ts](packages/extension/src/channel.ts)\n- [packages/extension/src/types.ts](packages/extension/src/types.ts)\n- [packages/extension/.gitignore](packages/extension/.gitignore)\n- [packages/injected/src/ariaSnapshot.ts](packages/injected/src/ariaSnapshot.ts)\n- [packages/injected/src/ariaSnapshotDistiller.ts](packages/injected/src/ariaSnapshotDistiller.ts)\n- [packages/injected/src/bidiInsertText.ts](packages/injected/src/bidiInsertText.ts)\n- [packages/injected/src/bindingsController.ts](packages/injected/src/bindingsController.ts)\n- [packages/injected/src/clock.ts](packages/injected/src/clock.ts)\n- [packages/injected/src/consoleApi.ts](packages/injected/src/consoleApi.ts)\n- [packages/injected/src/coverageScript.ts](packages/injected/src/coverageScript.ts)\n- [packages/injected/src/domUtils.ts](packages/injected/src/domUtils.ts)\n- [packages/injected/src/highlight.ts](packages/injected/src/highlight.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Service Worker Entry Point\n\n`packages/extension/src/background.ts` is the start point of the service worker: the module ends with `new Extension();`. The `Extension` constructor wires the object graph in one place — `ChromeListeners`, `TabRegistry`, `TabGroupManager`, then `TabPicker` and `ConnectionBroker` — and stores `Promise.all([registry.ready, groups.ready])` in `this._ready` so every incoming message can wait for initialization to finish.\n\nTwo listeners are registered in the constructor:\n\n| Listener | Behavior |\n| --- | --- |\n| `chrome.runtime.onMessage` | Bound to `_onMessage`, dispatches page messages |\n| `chrome.action.onClicked` | Calls `_showManagePage()` |\n\n`_showManagePage()` builds the URL with `chrome.runtime.getURL('manage.html')`, queries for an existing tab with that URL, creates one if absent, otherwise activates it and focuses its window via `chrome.windows.update`.\n\nBecause Chrome does not support promise-based message handlers (the source cites `https://issues.chromium.org/issues/40753031`), `_onMessage` calls `_handleMessage` and resolves the callback manually, returning `true` to keep the response channel open. Every reply is normalized:\n\n```ts\nthis._handleMessage(message, sender.tab?.id).then(\n    result => sendResponse({ success: true, ...result }),\n    error => sendResponse({ success: false, error: error.message }));\n```\n\n`_handleMessage` awaits `this._ready` first, then switches on `message.type`. The sender's tab id (`sender.tab?.id`) is what identifies a connect page, and it is passed to the broker as `senderTabId!` for `connectionRequested` and `connect`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/extension/src/background.ts:L26-L59](packages/extension/src/background.ts#L26-L59)\n- [packages/extension/src/background.ts:L61-L81](packages/extension/src/background.ts#L61-L81)\n\n## Typed Messages Between Pages and Worker\n\nThe page-to-worker protocol is a discriminated union declared in `packages/extension/src/types.ts` as `PageMessage`. It is internal to the extension, and `design.md` documents the same six messages. The dispatch in `background.ts` maps each variant to one collaborator:\n\n| Message | Payload | Handled by |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `connectionRequested` | `mcpRelayUrl` | `ConnectionBroker.request(senderTabId, mcpRelayUrl)` |\n| `getCandidates` | — | `TabPicker.candidates()` |\n| `connect` | `options: SessionOptions`, `selection: Selection` | `ConnectionBroker.connect(senderTabId, options, selection)` |\n| `getStatus` | — | Returns `{ sessions: this._broker.status() }` |\n| `disconnect` | `sessionId: number` | `ConnectionBroker.disconnect(sessionId)` |\n| `keepalive` | — | Returns nothing; the message itself resets the service worker idle timer |\n\nThe supporting types describe what flows across that boundary: `SessionOptions` carries `clientName`, an optional `tabGroup` with `title`/`color`, and `background`; `Selection` is `{ groupId?, tabIds? }`; `Candidates` bundles `groups` (`CandidateGroup[]`, each with `id`, `title`, `color`, `tabs`) and free `tabs`; `SessionStatus` is `{ id, clientName, tabIds }`. `GroupColor` is a template literal type over `chrome.tabGroups.Color`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  CP[\"Connect page\"] -- \"PageMessage\" --> SW[\"Extension (background.ts)\"]\n  MP[\"Manage page\"] -- \"PageMessage\" --> SW\n  SW --> P[\"TabPicker\"]\n  SW --> B[\"ConnectionBroker\"]\n  B -- \"opens\" --> C[\"Channel (WebSocket)\"]\n  C <--> R[\"Relay (CDPRelayServer)\"]\n  B --> S[\"ClientSession\"]\n  S --> T[\"OwnedTab / chrome.tabs\"]\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [packages/extension/src/background.ts:L61-L78](packages/extension/src/background.ts#L61-L78)\n- [packages/extension/src/types.ts:L20-L55](packages/extension/src/types.ts#L20-L55)\n- [packages/extension/design.md:L51-L66](packages/extension/design.md#L51-L66)\n- [packages/extension/design.md:L160-L171](packages/extension/design.md#L160-L171)\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/extension/src/types.ts:L18-L55](packages/extension/src/types.ts#L18-L55)\n- [packages/extension/design.md:L162-L171](packages/extension/design.md#L162-L171)\n\n## The WebSocket Channel\n\n`packages/extension/src/channel.ts` owns the only WebSocket in the extension, one per client. The transport is JSON with message ids: `Channel.open(url)` constructs the socket and races `onopen` against `onerror` and a `5000` ms timer (`OPEN_TIMEOUT_MS`); any failure closes the socket and rethrows as `Failed to connect to MCP relay: <message>`.\n\nOutbound traffic is either an event or a response:\n\n- `sendEvent(method, params)` sends `{ method, params }`.\n- Inside `_onMessage`, a successful `onCommand` produces `{ id, result: result ?? {} }`, and a rejection produces `{ id, error: error.message }`.\n\nInbound traffic is parsed with `JSON.parse`. If parsing fails, the channel cannot address an error response because the command id is unknown, so it closes with `Error parsing message: <message>`. The `onCommand` and `onClose` hooks are optional properties assigned by the owner of the channel.\n\nClosing is idempotent: `close(reason)` calls `this._ws.close(1000, reason)` and then `_didClose()` directly, because `onclose` fires asynchronously. `_didClose` guards on a `_closed` flag and invokes `onClose` at most once; the `closed` getter exposes the same flag. `_send` silently drops messages unless `readyState === WebSocket.OPEN`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/extension/src/channel.ts:L18-L92](packages/extension/src/channel.ts#L18-L92)\n\n## Client/Server Split and Ownership\n\n`design.md` states the central rule: each tab has zero or one owner, and `TabRegistry` is the only object that changes an owner. Clients are Playwright processes connecting through their relay (`CDPRelayServer`); inside the extension a connected client is a `ClientSession`. The design notes assign one Chrome API to one class — `chrome.debugger` to `OwnedTab`, `chrome.tabGroups` to `TabGroupManager`, the WebSocket to `Channel`.\n\n| Class | File | Responsibility |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `Extension` | `background.ts` | Service worker start point; runtime messages after startup cleanup |\n| `ConnectionBroker` | `connectionBroker.ts` | Handshake, relay URL per connect page, opens `Channel`, creates `ClientSession`, keeps worker alive |\n| `TabPicker` | `tabPicker.ts` | Free groups and free tabs; applies a selection |\n| `TabRegistry` | `tabRegistry.ts` | Owner table; `claim`, `release`, `transfer`, `releaseAll`; reserved tabs |\n| `OwnedTab` | `ownedTab.ts` | Debugger of one tab, badge, announcement |\n| `ClientSession` | `clientSession.ts` | Protocol for one client; checks commands, sends events for owned tabs only |\n| `Channel` | `channel.ts` | WebSocket JSON, ids, responses, errors |\n\nThree invariants hold at all times: a tab has zero or one owner; with grouping, a tab is in the session's group if and only if the session owns it; and a session may command only owned tabs and no method from the refused list. Clients may read browser-level data but cannot change browser-level state. On the server side, `CDPRelayServer` (`crExtensionRelay.ts`) is the WebSocket server for both Playwright and the extension and waits for `extension.initialized`; `BrowserModel` translates CDP into `chrome.*` commands and back; `ExtensionConnection` handles message ids and responses.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/extension/design.md:L3-L22](packages/extension/design.md#L3-L22)\n- [packages/extension/design.md:L49-L66](packages/extension/design.md#L49-L66)\n- [packages/extension/design.md:L238-L248](packages/extension/design.md#L238-L248)\n\n## Service Worker Lifetime and Cleanup\n\nSessions, the owner table and pending connect pages live only in service worker memory, and Chrome stops an idle worker after 30 seconds. Two timers prevent that: `ConnectionBroker` calls an extension API every 20 seconds while sessions are open, and a waiting connect page sends `keepalive` every 20 seconds. Chrome listeners for tab, tab group and debugger events are attached only while a session is open or a page waits, so ordinary browsing does not wake the worker.\n\nIf the worker stops anyway, its sessions close and clients reconnect on their next tool call. A new worker cleans up before answering any message: `TabGroupManager` records client group ids in `chrome.storage.session` and the new worker grays each recorded group, and debuggers left attached by the previous worker are detached and their badges cleared, while debuggers owned by other tools stay attached.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/extension/design.md:L220-L236](packages/extension/design.md#L220-L236)\n- [packages/extension/src/background.ts:L61-L63](packages/extension/src/background.ts#L61-L63)\n"}, {"path": "packages/extension/tab-management.md", "title": "Tab Registry, Groups, and Picker", "parent": null, "description": "How the extension tracks owned tabs, groups Playwright-controlled tabs in Chrome, and lets users pick a tab to share.", "files": ["packages/extension/src/tabRegistry.ts", "packages/extension/src/tabGroupManager.ts", "packages/extension/src/tabPicker.ts", "packages/extension/src/ownedTab.ts", "packages/extension/src/background.ts", "packages/isomorphic/abortSignal.ts", "packages/isomorphic/ariaSnapshot.ts", "packages/isomorphic/ariaSnapshotRenderer.ts", "packages/isomorphic/assert.ts", "packages/isomorphic/base64.ts", "packages/isomorphic/codeFrame.ts", "packages/isomorphic/codegen/actions.d.ts", "packages/isomorphic/codegen/csharp.ts", "packages/isomorphic/codegen/java.ts", "packages/isomorphic/codegen/javascript.ts", "packages/isomorphic/codegen/jsonl.ts", "packages/isomorphic/codegen/language.ts", "packages/isomorphic/codegen/languages.ts", "packages/isomorphic/codegen/python.ts"], "markdown": "# Tab Registry, Groups, and Picker\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [packages/extension/src/tabRegistry.ts](packages/extension/src/tabRegistry.ts)\n- [packages/extension/src/tabGroupManager.ts](packages/extension/src/tabGroupManager.ts)\n- [packages/extension/src/tabPicker.ts](packages/extension/src/tabPicker.ts)\n- [packages/extension/src/ownedTab.ts](packages/extension/src/ownedTab.ts)\n- [packages/extension/src/background.ts](packages/extension/src/background.ts)\n- [packages/isomorphic/abortSignal.ts](packages/isomorphic/abortSignal.ts)\n- [packages/isomorphic/ariaSnapshot.ts](packages/isomorphic/ariaSnapshot.ts)\n- [packages/isomorphic/ariaSnapshotRenderer.ts](packages/isomorphic/ariaSnapshotRenderer.ts)\n- [packages/isomorphic/assert.ts](packages/isomorphic/assert.ts)\n- [packages/isomorphic/base64.ts](packages/isomorphic/base64.ts)\n- [packages/isomorphic/codeFrame.ts](packages/isomorphic/codeFrame.ts)\n- [packages/isomorphic/codegen/actions.d.ts](packages/isomorphic/codegen/actions.d.ts)\n- [packages/isomorphic/codegen/csharp.ts](packages/isomorphic/codegen/csharp.ts)\n- [packages/isomorphic/codegen/java.ts](packages/isomorphic/codegen/java.ts)\n- [packages/isomorphic/codegen/javascript.ts](packages/isomorphic/codegen/javascript.ts)\n- [packages/isomorphic/codegen/jsonl.ts](packages/isomorphic/codegen/jsonl.ts)\n- [packages/isomorphic/codegen/language.ts](packages/isomorphic/codegen/language.ts)\n- [packages/isomorphic/codegen/languages.ts](packages/isomorphic/codegen/languages.ts)\n- [packages/isomorphic/codegen/python.ts](packages/isomorphic/codegen/python.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Wiring in the Service Worker\n\nThe three components are constructed in one place. `Extension` builds a shared `ChromeListeners` instance, then a `TabRegistry`, then a `TabGroupManager` on top of the registry, and finally a `TabPicker` over both. Readiness is the conjunction of `registry.ready` and `groups.ready`, and every message handler awaits it before dispatching.\n\n```ts\nconst listeners = new ChromeListeners();\nconst registry = new TabRegistry(listeners);\nconst groups = new TabGroupManager(registry, listeners);\nthis._ready = Promise.all([registry.ready, groups.ready]);\nthis._picker = new TabPicker(registry, groups);\nthis._broker = new ConnectionBroker(registry, groups, this._picker, listeners);\n```\n\nTwo message types reach these components directly: `getCandidates` returns `this._picker.candidates()`, and `connect` passes `senderTabId`, options and selection to `this._broker.connect(...)`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/extension/src/background.ts:L31-L78](packages/extension/src/background.ts#L31-L78)\n\n## Ownership Tracking in TabRegistry\n\n`TabRegistry` keeps a `Map<number, OwnedTab>` of owned tabs plus a `Set<number>` of reserved tab ids. A reserved tab \"has no owner, and no client can claim it\": `reserve()` adds the id and calls `release()`, and `isFree()` is true only when a tab is neither owned nor reserved.\n\nOwnership queries are small and direct: `ownedTab(tabId)`, `ownerOf(tabId)`, and `tabIdsOf(session)`. `claim()` is the single write path and enforces the invariants in order:\n\n| Condition | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| `owner === session` | no-op, returns silently |\n| another owner exists | throws `'This tab is already connected to another client'` |\n| id is reserved | throws `'This tab is a connect page'` |\n| otherwise | stores a new `OwnedTab` and fires `onTabClaimed` |\n\n`release()` deletes the entry, calls `tab.session.tabReleased(tabId)`, then awaits `tab.dispose()`. `transfer()` is `release()` followed by `claim()`, and `releaseAll(session)` maps `release` over `tabIdsOf(session)`.\n\nThe registry also listens for lifecycle events. A tab created with an `openerTabId` that belongs to an owner is claimed by that same owner — this is how popups stay with their client. `onRemoved` drops any reservation, notifies `session.tabClosed(tabId, removeInfo)`, and disposes. `onUpdated` with a defined `changeInfo.url` calls `didNavigate`, and `chrome.debugger.onEvent` / `onDetach` are forwarded to the matching `OwnedTab`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/extension/src/tabRegistry.ts:L23-L121](packages/extension/src/tabRegistry.ts#L23-L121)\n\n## OwnedTab: Announcement, Badge, and Debugger\n\nEach `OwnedTab` holds its `tabId`, its owning `session`, and three booleans (`_announced`, `_attached`, `_disposed`). The constructor sets `_announced` to `!announce` and immediately calls `_announce(tab)`, so a tab is announced unless the caller passes `{ announce: false }`.\n\n`_announce()` is gated twice: it returns early when already announced or when `!isDebuggableUrl(tab.url)`. `isDebuggableUrl` rejects only URLs starting with `chrome:`, `edge:`, or `devtools:`. Announcement means `this.session.tabClaimed(tab)` — the comment states the client then attaches the debugger to each announced tab.\n\n`attach(requiredVersion)` calls `chrome.debugger.attach`, re-checks `_disposed` (detaching immediately if disposed meanwhile), and then marks the tab attached. Attachment state drives the toolbar badge: attached tabs get text `'✓'`, background `'#4CAF50'`, and title `'Connected to Playwright client'`; detaching clears both text and title. `didNavigate` re-shows the badge because \"Chrome resets the badge of a tab on navigation.\"\n\nA `target_closed` detach reason can be spurious, so `_reannounceLater()` schedules a re-announce after `REANNOUNCE_DELAY_MS` (150 ms), rate-limited by `REANNOUNCE_COOLDOWN_MS` (3000 ms), and skips disposed tabs.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/extension/src/ownedTab.ts:L20-L145](packages/extension/src/ownedTab.ts#L20-L145)\n\n## Grouping Playwright Tabs in Chrome\n\n`TabGroupManager` maintains `Map<ClientSession, ClientTabGroup>`. Registration is opt-in per session: `register()` returns immediately when `session.options.tabGroup` is absent. Otherwise it validates the color against `CLIENT_COLORS` (`green`, `blue`, `purple`, `orange`, `pink`, `cyan`, `yellow`, `red`), falling back to `_firstFreeColor()`, and throws `Unsupported tab group color \"...\"` for anything else. The default title is `Playwright · ${session.options.clientName}`.\n\nGroup membership is synchronized in both directions:\n\n- **Registry → group.** `onTabClaimed` triggers `_moveIntoGroup`, which skips the call when the tab is already in the session's group and swallows errors from `group.add` — the comment notes \"Chrome does not put the tab of a popup window into a group.\"\n- **Group → registry.** `tabs.onUpdated` with `changeInfo.groupId` triggers `_onTabChangedGroup`. After awaiting `pendingGroupChanges` (so self-inflicted changes are ignored), it compares group owner with tab owner: a reserved tab in an owned group is ungrouped, an owned group claims the tab via `registry.transfer`, and a tab moved out of its group is released.\n\n`changeGroups()` serializes all group mutations through a single promise chain, so two first tabs of a client cannot create two groups, and `retryOnDrag` retries with delays of 100/200/400/800 ms while Chrome reports `user may be dragging a tab`.\n\nPersistence handles service-worker restarts. `_recordClientGroups()` writes group ids to `chrome.storage.session` under `clientGroupIds`; `_orphanGroupsOfPreviousServiceWorker()` reads that key on startup, repaints each stale group `grey` via `markOrphan`, and removes the key. `orphan(session)` does the same for a session that goes away.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"TabRegistry.claim\"] -->|onTabClaimed| B[\"_moveIntoGroup\"]\n  B --> C[\"ClientTabGroup.add / changeGroups\"]\n  D[\"tabs.onUpdated groupId\"] --> E[\"_onTabChangedGroup\"]\n  E --> F{\"groupOwner vs owner\"}\n  F -->|groupOwner, reserved| G[\"ungroup\"]\n  F -->|groupOwner| H[\"registry.transfer\"]\n  F -->|no groupOwner, owned session| I[\"registry.release\"]\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [packages/extension/src/tabGroupManager.ts:L66-L147](packages/extension/src/tabGroupManager.ts#L66-L147)\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/extension/src/tabGroupManager.ts:L23-L199](packages/extension/src/tabGroupManager.ts#L23-L199)\n\n## Candidate Discovery and Selection\n\n`TabPicker.candidates()` queries all tabs and `groups.unclaimedGroups()` in parallel. A group is offered only if every one of its tabs satisfies `registry.isFree` — \"A group is taken as a whole, so all its tabs must be free.\" Individual tabs must additionally pass `isDebuggableUrl(tab.url)`. The result matches the `Candidates` shape with `groups` (id, title, color, tabs) and `tabs`.\n\n`apply(selection, session, connectTabId)` resolves the selection to tabs, claims each one, and returns them. Resolution branches in `_selectedTabs`:\n\n| Selection | Behavior |\n| --- | --- |\n| `groupId` defined | Re-runs `candidates()`, throws `'This tab group is already connected to another client'` if the group is gone, calls `groups.adopt(group.id, session)`, returns all group tabs |\n| `tabIds` non-empty | `chrome.tabs.get` for each id |\n| neither | Navigates the connect tab to `about:blank` and returns that tab |\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/extension/src/tabPicker.ts:L25-L69](packages/extension/src/tabPicker.ts#L25-L69)\n"}, {"path": "packages/extension/cdp-and-sessions.md", "title": "CDP Policy, Sessions, and Connection Broker", "parent": null, "description": "Chrome DevTools Protocol permissions, per-client sessions, the connection broker, and Chrome event listeners that keep clients in sync.", "files": ["packages/extension/src/cdpPolicy.ts", "packages/extension/src/clientSession.ts", "packages/extension/src/connectionBroker.ts", "packages/extension/src/chromeListeners.ts", "packages/extension/src/channel.ts", "packages/playwright-browser-chromium/index.d.ts", "packages/playwright-browser-chromium/index.js", "packages/playwright-browser-chromium/index.mjs", "packages/playwright-browser-chromium/install.js", "packages/playwright-client/index.d.ts", "packages/playwright-client/src/index.ts", "packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/async_hooks.ts", "packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/colors.ts", "packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/crypto.ts", "packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/events.ts", "packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/fs.ts", "packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/inspector.ts", "packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/path.ts", "packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/processShim.ts", "packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/stream.ts", "packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/util.ts"], "markdown": "# CDP Policy, Sessions, and Connection Broker\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [packages/extension/src/cdpPolicy.ts](packages/extension/src/cdpPolicy.ts)\n- [packages/extension/src/clientSession.ts](packages/extension/src/clientSession.ts)\n- [packages/extension/src/connectionBroker.ts](packages/extension/src/connectionBroker.ts)\n- [packages/extension/src/chromeListeners.ts](packages/extension/src/chromeListeners.ts)\n- [packages/extension/src/channel.ts](packages/extension/src/channel.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-chromium/index.d.ts](packages/playwright-browser-chromium/index.d.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-chromium/index.js](packages/playwright-browser-chromium/index.js)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-chromium/index.mjs](packages/playwright-browser-chromium/index.mjs)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-chromium/install.js](packages/playwright-browser-chromium/install.js)\n- [packages/playwright-client/index.d.ts](packages/playwright-client/index.d.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-client/src/index.ts](packages/playwright-client/src/index.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/async_hooks.ts](packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/async_hooks.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/colors.ts](packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/colors.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/crypto.ts](packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/crypto.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/events.ts](packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/events.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/fs.ts](packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/fs.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/inspector.ts](packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/inspector.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/path.ts](packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/path.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/processShim.ts](packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/processShim.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/stream.ts](packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/stream.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/util.ts](packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/util.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## The CDP Method Policy\n\n`packages/extension/src/cdpPolicy.ts` defines a small allow/deny layer for Chrome DevTools Protocol methods. It declares a module-level `Set` named `BROWSER_STATE_METHODS` for methods that change browser-wide rather than tab-scoped state, and exposes `checkCdpMethod(method)`.\n\n| Category | Methods |\n| --- | --- |\n| Cookie and cache state | `Storage.setCookies`, `Storage.clearCookies`, `Network.clearBrowserCookies`, `Network.clearBrowserCache` |\n| Permissions | `Browser.grantPermissions`, `Browser.resetPermissions`, `Browser.setPermission` |\n| Download behavior | `Browser.setDownloadBehavior` |\n| Target lifecycle | `Target.createTarget`, `Target.closeTarget`, `Target.attachToTarget`, `Target.activateTarget` |\n\n`checkCdpMethod` throws when the method is in that set, with the message `${method} is not allowed: it changes the state of the browser, not of the tab`. It returns `void` otherwise. This is not a general CDP filter; it names only the browser-scoped methods above.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/extension/src/cdpPolicy.ts:L18-L36](packages/extension/src/cdpPolicy.ts#L18-L36)\n\n## Per-Client Sessions\n\n`ClientSession` in `packages/extension/src/clientSession.ts` binds one `Channel` to one `TabRegistry` and a `SessionOptions` object: the constructor stores the options, assigns `channel.onCommand` to `_handleCommand`, and wires `channel.onClose` to the optional `onClose`.\n\n| Method | Event sent |\n| --- | --- |\n| `didInitialize()` | `extension.initialized` with `[]` |\n| `tabClaimed(tab)` | `chrome.tabs.onCreated` with `[tab]` |\n| `tabClosed(tabId, removeInfo)` | `chrome.tabs.onRemoved` with `[tabId, removeInfo]` |\n| `debuggerDetached(tabId, reason)` | `chrome.debugger.onDetach` with `[{ tabId }, reason]` |\n| `cdpEvent(source, method, params)` | `chrome.debugger.onEvent` with `[source, method, params]` |\n\n`tabReleased(tabId)` sends nothing itself; it delegates to `debuggerDetached(tabId, 'target_closed')`, since `chrome.debugger.detach` does not fire `onDetach` for the caller.\n\nInbound commands are dispatched over a fixed set: `chrome.debugger.attach`, `chrome.debugger.detach`, `chrome.debugger.sendCommand`, `chrome.tabs.create`, `chrome.tabs.remove`. The `sendCommand` path calls `checkCdpMethod(cdpMethod)` before delegating to the owned tab, forwarding `target.sessionId` with method and params. `chrome.tabs.create` creates the tab then claims it with `{ announce: false }`, because the response announces it.\n\nOwnership comes from `_ownedTab(target)`, which accepts only a target with a `tabId` and no `targetId` or `extensionId`, resolves it via `this._registry.ownedTab(target.tabId!)`, and throws `Tab ${target?.tabId} is not owned by this client` when the tab's `session` is not this session.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/extension/src/clientSession.ts:L18-L108](packages/extension/src/clientSession.ts#L18-L108)\n\n## The Channel Transport\n\n`packages/extension/src/channel.ts` implements the per-session WebSocket transport. `Channel.open(url)` constructs a `WebSocket`, resolves on `onopen`, rejects with `WebSocket error` on `onerror`, and applies a 5000 ms `OPEN_TIMEOUT_MS` timeout; failures are rethrown as `Failed to connect to MCP relay: ${error.message}`.\n\n`_onMessage` parses JSON and closes the channel with `Error parsing message: ...` on failure, otherwise awaits `onCommand!(message.method, message.params ?? [])` and sends `{ id, result }` or `{ id, error }`. `_send` writes only when `readyState === WebSocket.OPEN`. `close(reason)` calls `_ws.close(1000, reason)` then `_didClose()` immediately, since `onclose` is asynchronous; `_didClose` is idempotent via `_closed` and invokes `onClose` once.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/extension/src/channel.ts:L18-L93](packages/extension/src/channel.ts#L18-L93)\n\n## Connection Broker Lifecycle\n\n`packages/extension/src/connectionBroker.ts` orchestrates the handoff from a pending connection to a live session, holding `_relayUrls` keyed by connect tab id, `_sessions` keyed by an incrementing `_lastSessionId`, and a `_keepaliveTimer`.\n\n1. `request(connectTabId, relayUrl)` records the URL, calls `_didChangeActivity()`, then awaits `registry.reserve(connectTabId)` and `groups.ungroup(connectTabId)` — Chrome can open the connect page inside a client's group.\n2. `connect(...)` reads and deletes the pending URL (throwing `Pending client connection closed` if absent), unreserves the tab, opens the channel, creates a `ClientSession`, assigns a session id, and registers `onClose` to delete the session, refresh activity, release tabs, and orphan the group.\n\nInside a `try` block it registers the session with the group manager, applies `selection` via `_picker.apply(...)`, calls `session.didInitialize()`, and calls `_show(tabs, connectTabId, options.background)`; errors trigger `session.close(error.message)` and are rethrown. `disconnect(sessionId)` closes the session with `'User disconnected'`, and `status()` maps sessions to `{ id, clientName, tabIds }`.\n\n`_show` activates and focuses the first tab when not in background mode (errors swallowed with `.catch(() => {})`) and removes the connect tab if it is not among the resulting tabs. The constructor also registers a `chrome.tabs.onRemoved` listener that deletes the tab's relay URL and refreshes activity.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/extension/src/connectionBroker.ts:L27-L125](packages/extension/src/connectionBroker.ts#L27-L125)\n\n## Activity, Keepalive, and Listener Gating\n\n`_didChangeActivity()` does two jobs: it calls `listeners.setEnabled(this._sessions.size > 0 || this._relayUrls.size > 0)`, and it manages a keepalive interval — with sessions and no timer it starts `setInterval(() => void chrome.runtime.getPlatformInfo(), KEEPALIVE_INTERVAL_MS)` where `KEEPALIVE_INTERVAL_MS = 20_000`; with no sessions it clears the timer. The comment explains why: Chrome stops an idle service worker and its sessions, while an extension API call resets the idle timer.\n\n`packages/extension/src/chromeListeners.ts` supplies the gating. `ChromeListeners` stores `{ event, listener }` pairs and an `_enabled` flag; `add(event, listener)` records the pair and attaches it only if already enabled, and `setEnabled(enabled)` returns early when state is unchanged, otherwise flipping the flag and adding or removing every recorded listener. Its header notes that Chrome starts a stopped service worker for each event with a listener, so listeners are attached only while a client needs them.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n  participant Page as Connect page\n  participant Broker as ConnectionBroker\n  participant Reg as TabRegistry\n  participant Ch as Channel\n  participant Sess as ClientSession\n  participant Lst as ChromeListeners\n  Page->>Broker: request(connectTabId, relayUrl)\n  Broker->>Broker: _relayUrls.set + _didChangeActivity\n  Broker->>Lst: setEnabled(true)\n  Broker->>Reg: reserve(connectTabId)\n  Page->>Broker: connect(connectTabId, options, selection)\n  Broker->>Ch: Channel.open(relayUrl)\n  Broker->>Sess: new ClientSession(channel, registry, options)\n  Broker->>Reg: picker.apply(selection, session, connectTabId)\n  Broker->>Sess: didInitialize()\n  Sess->>Ch: sendEvent('extension.initialized', [])\n  Sess-->>Broker: onClose\n  Broker->>Reg: releaseAll(session)\n  Broker->>Lst: setEnabled(false) when idle\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [packages/extension/src/connectionBroker.ts:L50-L112](packages/extension/src/connectionBroker.ts#L50-L112)\n- [packages/extension/src/chromeListeners.ts:L19-L40](packages/extension/src/chromeListeners.ts#L19-L40)\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/extension/src/connectionBroker.ts:L103-L112](packages/extension/src/connectionBroker.ts#L103-L112)\n- [packages/extension/src/chromeListeners.ts:L17-L41](packages/extension/src/chromeListeners.ts#L17-L41)\n\n## Client-Side Counterparts\n\nThe browser-facing client connects to a relay endpoint, not Chrome. `packages/playwright-client/src/index.ts` implements `connect(wsEndpoint, browserName, options)`, opening a `WebSocket` to the endpoint with `browser` and `launch-options` query parameters, awaiting `open` or `error`, wiring a `Connection` both ways, closing it on socket `close`, then returning `playwright._preLaunchedBrowser()` from `connection.initializePlaywright()`. `Options` carries only an optional `headless` flag, mirrored in `packages/playwright-client/index.d.ts`.\n\nBecause this runs in a browser, `packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/` replaces Node built-ins: `fs` and `path` throw `fs is not available in the browser` / `path is not available in the browser`, `stream.promises.pipeline` rejects, `AsyncLocalStorage` is a pass-through, `crypto.randomBytes` uses `globalThis.crypto.getRandomValues`, and `processShim.ts` injects `{ env, platform: 'browser', argv }`. `colors.ts` exports `noColors`, `events.ts` exposes a minimal `EventEmitter` with `defaultMaxListeners = 10`, `inspector.url()` returns `undefined`, and `util.inspect` stringifies values.\n\nThe `packages/playwright-browser-chromium` files shown (`index.d.ts`, `index.js`, `index.mjs`) contain only license headers and, in `index.d.ts`, `export {}`. `install.js` requires `playwright-core/lib/coreBundle`, skips installation when `utils.isLikelyNpxGlobal()` is true, and otherwise calls `registry.installBrowsersForNpmInstall(['chromium', 'chromium-headless-shell', 'ffmpeg'])`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/playwright-client/src/index.ts:L21-L39](packages/playwright-client/src/index.ts#L21-L39)\n- [packages/playwright-client/index.d.ts:L17-L25](packages/playwright-client/index.d.ts#L17-L25)\n- [packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/crypto.ts:L17-L24](packages/playwright-client/src/nodeStubs/crypto.ts#L17-L24)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-chromium/install.js:L17-L27](packages/playwright-browser-chromium/install.js#L17-L27)\n"}, {"path": "packages/extension/extension-ui.md", "title": "Extension UI", "parent": null, "description": "The extension's popup/options UI, including the auth token screen and shared style tokens.", "files": ["packages/extension/src/ui/authToken.tsx", "packages/extension/src/ui/authToken.css", "packages/extension/src/ui/colors.css", "packages/extension/src/ui/common.css", "packages/extension/src/ui/connect.tsx", "packages/extension/src/ui/copyToClipboard.tsx", "packages/extension/src/ui/icons.tsx", "packages/extension/src/ui/manage.tsx", "packages/extension/src/ui/tabItem.tsx", "packages/playwright-core/bin/install_media_pack.ps1", "packages/playwright-core/bin/install_webkit_wsl.ps1", "packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_chrome_beta_linux.sh", "packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_chrome_beta_mac.sh", "packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_chrome_beta_win.ps1", "packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_chrome_stable_linux.sh", "packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_chrome_stable_mac.sh", "packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_chrome_stable_win.ps1", "packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_beta_linux.sh", "packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_beta_mac.sh", "packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_beta_win.ps1", "packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_dev_linux.sh", "packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_dev_mac.sh", "packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_dev_win.ps1", "packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_stable_linux.sh", "packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_stable_mac.sh", "packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_stable_win.ps1", "packages/playwright-core/cli.js", "packages/playwright-core/index.d.ts", "packages/playwright-core/index.js", "packages/playwright-core/index.mjs", "packages/playwright-core/src/androidServerImpl.ts", "packages/playwright-core/src/bootstrap.ts", "packages/playwright-core/src/browserServerImpl.ts", "packages/playwright-core/src/cli/browserActions.ts", "packages/playwright-core/src/cli/driver.ts", "packages/playwright-core/src/cli/installActions.ts", "packages/playwright-core/src/cli/program.ts"], "markdown": "# Extension UI\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/authToken.tsx](packages/extension/src/ui/authToken.tsx)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/authToken.css](packages/extension/src/ui/authToken.css)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/colors.css](packages/extension/src/ui/colors.css)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/common.css](packages/extension/src/ui/common.css)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/connect.tsx](packages/extension/src/ui/connect.tsx)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/copyToClipboard.tsx](packages/extension/src/ui/copyToClipboard.tsx)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/icons.tsx](packages/extension/src/ui/icons.tsx)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/manage.tsx](packages/extension/src/ui/manage.tsx)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/tabItem.tsx](packages/extension/src/ui/tabItem.tsx)\n- [packages/playwright-core/bin/install_media_pack.ps1](packages/playwright-core/bin/install_media_pack.ps1)\n- [packages/playwright-core/bin/install_webkit_wsl.ps1](packages/playwright-core/bin/install_webkit_wsl.ps1)\n- [packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_chrome_beta_linux.sh](packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_chrome_beta_linux.sh)\n- [packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_chrome_beta_mac.sh](packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_chrome_beta_mac.sh)\n- [packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_chrome_beta_win.ps1](packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_chrome_beta_win.ps1)\n- [packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_chrome_stable_linux.sh](packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_chrome_stable_linux.sh)\n- [packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_chrome_stable_mac.sh](packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_chrome_stable_mac.sh)\n- [packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_chrome_stable_win.ps1](packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_chrome_stable_win.ps1)\n- [packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_beta_linux.sh](packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_beta_linux.sh)\n- [packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_beta_mac.sh](packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_beta_mac.sh)\n- [packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_beta_win.ps1](packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_beta_win.ps1)\n- [packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_dev_linux.sh](packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_dev_linux.sh)\n- [packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_dev_mac.sh](packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_dev_mac.sh)\n- [packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_dev_win.ps1](packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_dev_win.ps1)\n- [packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_stable_linux.sh](packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_stable_linux.sh)\n- [packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_stable_mac.sh](packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_stable_mac.sh)\n- [packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_stable_win.ps1](packages/playwright-core/bin/reinstall_msedge_stable_win.ps1)\n- [packages/playwright-core/cli.js](packages/playwright-core/cli.js)\n- [packages/playwright-core/index.d.ts](packages/playwright-core/index.d.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-core/index.js](packages/playwright-core/index.js)\n- [packages/playwright-core/index.mjs](packages/playwright-core/index.mjs)\n- [packages/playwright-core/src/androidServerImpl.ts](packages/playwright-core/src/androidServerImpl.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-core/src/bootstrap.ts](packages/playwright-core/src/bootstrap.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-core/src/browserServerImpl.ts](packages/playwright-core/src/browserServerImpl.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-core/src/cli/browserActions.ts](packages/playwright-core/src/cli/browserActions.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-core/src/cli/driver.ts](packages/playwright-core/src/cli/driver.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-core/src/cli/installActions.ts](packages/playwright-core/src/cli/installActions.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-core/src/cli/program.ts](packages/playwright-core/src/cli/program.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Style foundation: `common.css` and `colors.css`\n\nThe UI is styled from two global stylesheets rather than per-component rules. `common.css` sets `color-scheme: light dark` on `:root`, styles `body` with the system font stack and `var(--color-canvas-subtle)` as the page background, and defines the shared layout primitives:\n\n| Class | Role |\n| --- | --- |\n| `.page` | Centered column, `max-width: 672px`, `padding: 40px 16px 64px` |\n| `.card`, `.card-header`, `.card-body`, `.card-footer` | Bordered container with a footer separated by `--color-border-muted` |\n| `.muted` | 12px secondary text in `--color-fg-muted` |\n| `.status-banner` with `.error` / `.connected` / `.empty` | Header banner variants |\n| `.warning-banner` | Attention-styled box using `--color-attention-subtle` and `--color-attention-muted` |\n| `.button` with `.default` / `.primary` | 32px-high buttons mapped to `--color-btn-*` tokens |\n| `.icon-button` | 28×28 transparent icon button with `--color-neutral-muted` hover |\n| `.list`, `.tab-item`, `.tab-checkbox`, `.tab-favicon`, `.tab-title`, `.tab-url` | Tab and tab-group rows |\n\n`colors.css` supplies the actual values: a large GitHub Primer palette (MIT-licensed, GitHub Inc.) declared as custom properties on `:root`, plus a dark-mode override inside `@media(prefers-color-scheme: dark)`. Components therefore never hard-code colors — `authToken.css` references `--color-canvas-subtle` and `--color-border-default` directly, and `icons.tsx` imports `./colors.css` so the tokens exist wherever icons are rendered.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/common.css:L17-L39](packages/extension/src/ui/common.css#L17-L39)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/common.css:L83-L127](packages/extension/src/ui/common.css#L83-L127)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/common.css:L129-L176](packages/extension/src/ui/common.css#L129-L176)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/common.css:L178-L240](packages/extension/src/ui/common.css#L178-L240)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/common.css:L242-L333](packages/extension/src/ui/common.css#L242-L333)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/colors.css:L1-L23](packages/extension/src/ui/colors.css#L1-L23)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/colors.css:L309-L347](packages/extension/src/ui/colors.css#L309-L347)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/colors.css:L457-L459](packages/extension/src/ui/colors.css#L457-L459)\n\n## Page shell and reusable components\n\n`tabItem.tsx` holds the small component kit every page composes:\n\n- `Button` renders `<button className={'button ' + variant}>` for `primary` or `default`.\n- `PageHeader` renders `icons/icon-48.png` as `.page-logo`, the title `Playwright Extension`, and optional children (used for the \"Manage clients\" link).\n- `ListItem` renders an icon, title, optional subtitle, and — when `checked` is not `undefined` — a checkbox inside a `<label>` with the `selected` class.\n- `TabItem` prefers `tab.favIconUrl` and falls back to `icons.page()` after an `onError`, showing `Untitled` when the tab has no title.\n\n`icons.tsx` exports inline SVG factories (`cross`, `check`, `alert`, `page`, `copy`, `refresh`, `chevronDown`), all carrying the `octicon` class except `page`, which uses `tab-favicon`.\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n  Manage[\"manage.tsx — ManageApp\"] --> Auth[\"authToken.tsx — AuthTokenSection\"]\n  Manage --> Kit[\"tabItem.tsx — Button / PageHeader / TabItem\"]\n  Connect[\"connect.tsx — ConnectApp\"] --> Auth\n  Connect --> Kit\n  Auth --> Copy[\"copyToClipboard.tsx — CopyToClipboard\"]\n  Copy --> Icons[\"icons.tsx\"]\n  Kit --> Icons\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/manage.tsx:L17-L22](packages/extension/src/ui/manage.tsx#L17-L22)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/connect.tsx:L18-L24](packages/extension/src/ui/connect.tsx#L18-L24)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/authToken.tsx:L17-L20](packages/extension/src/ui/authToken.tsx#L17-L20)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/copyToClipboard.tsx:L17-L18](packages/extension/src/ui/copyToClipboard.tsx#L17-L18)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/tabItem.tsx:L17-L18](packages/extension/src/ui/tabItem.tsx#L17-L18)\n\n## The manage page\n\n`manage.tsx` mounts `ManageApp` into `#root` and is the options-style page that lists active clients. It sends `{ type: 'getStatus' }` to the background worker, resolves each session's `tabIds` through `chrome.tabs.get` (dropping tabs that no longer exist), and re-runs the load whenever `chrome.tabs.onCreated`, `onUpdated`, or `onRemoved` fire.\n\nEach connection renders a card with the `clientName`, a tab count (`1 tab` or `N tabs`), a `Disconnect` button that sends `{ type: 'disconnect', sessionId }`, and a list of `TabItem`s whose click handler activates the tab and focuses its window. With no connections, a `.status-banner.empty` card explains that the CLI or MCP server must be started with the `--extension` flag. The token card is always appended last via `<AuthTokenSection />`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/manage.tsx:L24-L96](packages/extension/src/ui/manage.tsx#L24-L96)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/manage.tsx:L98-L103](packages/extension/src/ui/manage.tsx#L98-L103)\n\n## Auth token section\n\n`AuthTokenSection` is the authentication screen shown on the manage page. State is initialized from `getOrCreateAuthToken`, which reads `localStorage['auth-token']` and creates one if absent. Tokens come from `crypto.getRandomValues` over a 32-byte `Uint8Array`, base64-encoded with `btoa` and made URL-safe by mapping `+`→`-`, `/`→`_`, and removing `=`.\n\nThe rendered card has three regions:\n\n| Region | Content |\n| --- | --- |\n| `card-header` | `<h2>Token</h2>` |\n| `card-body` | Explanatory `.muted` text plus `code` showing `PLAYWRIGHT_MCP_EXTENSION_TOKEN=<token>` |\n| `card-footer` | Note that rotation invalidates the old token, and a `Rotate token` default button |\n\nThe footer button regenerates the token, writes it to `localStorage`, and updates state. `authToken.css` styles `.auth-token-container` as a flex row with a subtle background and border, and `.auth-token-code` as a monospace 12px block that wraps anywhere so long tokens stay visible.\n\n`CopyToClipboard` renders an `.icon-button` with `aria-label`/`title` of \"Copy to clipboard\". It calls `navigator.clipboard.writeText(value)`, switches to a check icon on success or a cross on failure, and reverts to the copy icon after three seconds; the icon also resets whenever `value` changes.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/authToken.tsx:L22-L49](packages/extension/src/ui/authToken.tsx#L22-L49)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/authToken.tsx:L51-L69](packages/extension/src/ui/authToken.tsx#L51-L69)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/authToken.tsx:L71-L78](packages/extension/src/ui/authToken.tsx#L71-L78)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/authToken.css:L17-L34](packages/extension/src/ui/authToken.css#L17-L34)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/copyToClipboard.tsx:L27-L53](packages/extension/src/ui/copyToClipboard.tsx#L27-L53)\n\n## Connection prompt and status states\n\n`connect.tsx` reuses the same kit for the connection dialog. It parses `client`, `tabGroup`, `tabGroupTitle`, `tabGroupColor`, `background`, `mcpRelayUrl`, `token`, and `protocolVersion` from the query string, rejects relay URLs whose host is not `127.0.0.1` or `[::1]`, and compares the requested protocol version against `SUPPORTED_PROTOCOL_VERSION = 2`.\n\n`StatusBanner` maps status types to icons: `check` for `connected`, `cross` for `error`, and none for `connecting`. A version mismatch renders `VersionMismatchError`, which links to the Chrome Web Store entry and the extension README. While `connecting`, a `.warning-banner` warns that approving exposes the whole browser, including signed-in sessions and cookies, and points at the Manage clients page for token rotation.\n\nCandidates are grouped into `Tab groups` and `Tabs` lists; group rows use a `.group-dot` colored from the `GROUP_COLORS` map that mirrors Chrome's tab-group palette. The footer summarizes the selection — `1 tab group selected.`, `1 tab selected.`, `N tabs selected.`, or `Nothing selected. The client gets a new blank tab.` — next to a primary `Allow` button that posts `{ type: 'connect', options, selection }` to the background worker.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/connect.tsx:L30-L66](packages/extension/src/ui/connect.tsx#L30-L66)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/connect.tsx:L68-L192](packages/extension/src/ui/connect.tsx#L68-L192)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/connect.tsx:L194-L252](packages/extension/src/ui/connect.tsx#L194-L252)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/tabItem.tsx:L20-L74](packages/extension/src/ui/tabItem.tsx#L20-L74)\n- [packages/extension/src/ui/icons.tsx:L17-L62](packages/extension/src/ui/icons.tsx#L17-L62)\n"}, {"path": "packages/playwright-core.md", "title": "Playwright Core Package", "parent": null, "description": "Core package containing browser automation engine, protocol client, server, and dispatcher implementations.", "files": ["packages/playwright-core/package.json", "packages/injected/src/ariaSnapshot.ts", "packages/injected/src/ariaSnapshotDistiller.ts", "packages/injected/src/bidiInsertText.ts", "packages/injected/src/bindingsController.ts", "packages/injected/src/clock.ts"], "markdown": "# Playwright Core Package\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [packages/playwright-core/package.json](packages/playwright-core/package.json)\n- [packages/injected/src/ariaSnapshot.ts](packages/injected/src/ariaSnapshot.ts)\n- [packages/injected/src/ariaSnapshotDistiller.ts](packages/injected/src/ariaSnapshotDistiller.ts)\n- [packages/injected/src/bidiInsertText.ts](packages/injected/src/bidiInsertText.ts)\n- [packages/injected/src/bindingsController.ts](packages/injected/src/bindingsController.ts)\n- [packages/injected/src/clock.ts](packages/injected/src/clock.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Package Overview\n\n`playwright-core` is the foundational npm package that provides browser automation engine capabilities, including the protocol client, server components, and dispatcher implementations. The package is published at version 1.66.0-next and requires Node.js 20 or later. It serves as the core automation layer upon which other Playwright packages build, exposing both programmatic APIs and command-line interfaces for interacting with browsers.\n\nThe package exports multiple entry points to support different use cases: a main CommonJS and ES module interface via `index.js` and `index.mjs`, a bootstrap loader, bundled utilities, and specialized CLI programs. A command-line tool `playwright-core` is also exposed via the `cli.js` bin entry.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/playwright-core/package.json:L1-L34](packages/playwright-core/package.json#L1-L34)\n\n## Core Exports and Bundles\n\nThe package structure supports modular consumption through several export paths. The main entry point provides TypeScript definitions from `index.d.ts` and runtime JavaScript. Additional exports include `lib/bootstrap` for initialization, `lib/coreBundle` for the core browser automation engine, and `lib/utilsBundle` for utility functions. The `lib/tools/cli-client/program` export exposes the command-line interface as a programmatic module. This architecture allows consumers to import only the pieces they need, reducing bundle size for different integration scenarios.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/playwright-core/package.json:L17-L34](packages/playwright-core/package.json#L17-L34)\n\n## Injected Browser Scripts\n\nPlaywright Core injects several utility scripts into browser pages to enable advanced interaction capabilities beyond standard browser APIs.\n\n**ARIA Snapshot Generation** generates a structured accessibility tree from the DOM. The `generateAriaTree()` function traverses the DOM and builds an ARIA node hierarchy with role, name, children, properties, and bounding boxes. It supports multiple modes—`'ai'` for AI consumption with interactable refs, `'default'` for standard accessibility matching, `'codegen'` for test generation with regex patterns, and `'autoexpect'` for auto-generating assertions on visible elements. The snapshot includes metadata linking elements to their refs and tracking which elements contributed to computed accessible names.\n\n**ARIA Distillation** post-processes snapshots to reduce verbosity without losing information. A plugin chain applies transformations: `mergeStringChildren` coalesces adjacent text tokens, `unwrapSingleChildGenerics` collapses unnecessary wrapper elements, `removeNamelessImages` removes decorative images, `removeRedundantNames` strips names that merely repeat content already represented in the tree, and `inlineTextIntoGeneric` bubbles text up through nameless wrappers. The distillation pipeline runs differently for AI vs. normal modes, with AI mode applying aggressive compression.\n\n**BiDi Text Insertion** handles text input through the WebDriver BiDi protocol. The `bidiInsertText()` function locates the focused element (descending through shadow DOM), dispatches text to input fields or textareas with caret positioning, inserts text into content-editable elements with proper range handling, and routes nested frames back to the caller for recursion.\n\n**Bindings Controller** bridges the gap between browser and host contexts. It serializes function arguments, maintains a request-response sequence counter, and resolves promises with results or errors delivered from the host. Bindings are attached to `globalThis` for direct function-call syntax, and the controller handles cleanup when bindings are removed.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/injected/src/ariaSnapshot.ts:L25-L80](packages/injected/src/ariaSnapshot.ts#L25-L80)\n- [packages/injected/src/ariaSnapshotDistiller.ts:L45-L99](packages/injected/src/ariaSnapshotDistiller.ts#L45-L99)\n- [packages/injected/src/bidiInsertText.ts:L19-L65](packages/injected/src/bidiInsertText.ts#L19-L65)\n- [packages/injected/src/bindingsController.ts:L33-L92](packages/injected/src/bindingsController.ts#L33-L92)\n\n## Testing Infrastructure: Fake Clock\n\nThe `ClockController` class implements a deterministic clock for testing. It manages timers (setTimeout, setInterval, requestAnimationFrame, requestIdleCallback), allows fast-forwarding or pausing time, and optionally syncs with real time. The clock logs operations (install, setSystemTime, pauseAt, resume, runFor, fastForward) and replays them when needed, supporting scripts that call time-dependent APIs at different points. Timer execution is ordered and can throw first-encountered errors after advancing to target times. The clock can create fake Date and Intl.DateTimeFormat objects that return controlled time values.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/injected/src/clock.ts:L71-L222](packages/injected/src/clock.ts#L71-L222)\n"}, {"path": "packages/playwright-test.md", "title": "Playwright Test Runner", "parent": null, "description": "Test runner package providing fixtures, reporters, configuration, and test execution framework.", "files": ["packages/playwright-test/package.json", "packages/isomorphic/abortSignal.ts", "packages/isomorphic/ariaSnapshot.ts", "packages/isomorphic/ariaSnapshotRenderer.ts", "packages/isomorphic/assert.ts", "packages/isomorphic/base64.ts", "packages/isomorphic/codeFrame.ts", "packages/isomorphic/codegen/actions.d.ts", "packages/isomorphic/codegen/csharp.ts", "packages/isomorphic/codegen/java.ts"], "markdown": "# Playwright Test Runner\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [packages/playwright-test/package.json](packages/playwright-test/package.json)\n- [packages/isomorphic/abortSignal.ts](packages/isomorphic/abortSignal.ts)\n- [packages/isomorphic/ariaSnapshot.ts](packages/isomorphic/ariaSnapshot.ts)\n- [packages/isomorphic/ariaSnapshotRenderer.ts](packages/isomorphic/ariaSnapshotRenderer.ts)\n- [packages/isomorphic/assert.ts](packages/isomorphic/assert.ts)\n- [packages/isomorphic/base64.ts](packages/isomorphic/base64.ts)\n- [packages/isomorphic/codeFrame.ts](packages/isomorphic/codeFrame.ts)\n- [packages/isomorphic/codegen/actions.d.ts](packages/isomorphic/codegen/actions.d.ts)\n- [packages/isomorphic/codegen/csharp.ts](packages/isomorphic/codegen/csharp.ts)\n- [packages/isomorphic/codegen/java.ts](packages/isomorphic/codegen/java.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Playwright Test Runner is published as the `@playwright/test` package, providing a high-level testing framework for browser automation. The package exports a command-line interface, reporter API, and main test execution framework that coordinate test execution across Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit browsers. It depends on the `playwright` core package and requires Node.js 20 or later.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/playwright-test/package.json:L1-L35](packages/playwright-test/package.json#L1-L35)\n\n## Package Exports\n\nThe test runner exposes three primary entry points:\n\n| Export | Purpose |\n|--------|---------|\n| `.` (main) | Main test framework entry point with TypeScript definitions |\n| `./cli` | Command-line interface for running tests |\n| `./reporter` | Reporter API for consuming and formatting test results |\n\nThe command-line binary `playwright` is registered in the `bin` field, allowing tests to be executed directly from the command line after package installation.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/playwright-test/package.json:L17-L29](packages/playwright-test/package.json#L17-L29)\n\n## Assertion Infrastructure\n\nThe test runner includes assertion utilities for validating test outcomes. The core assertion function enforces a predicate with optional error messaging. Additionally, abort signal utilities track assertion failures with detailed context, allowing tests to gracefully handle aborted assertions when async operations are cancelled or timeouts occur.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/isomorphic/assert.ts:L17-L19](packages/isomorphic/assert.ts#L17-L19)\n- [packages/isomorphic/abortSignal.ts:L17-L19](packages/isomorphic/abortSignal.ts#L17-L19)\n\n## ARIA Snapshot Testing\n\nThe test runner supports accessibility testing through ARIA snapshot assertions. Snapshots capture the computed accessibility tree of a page as YAML, encoding roles, names, properties (checked, disabled, expanded, invalid, level, pressed, required, selected), and element hierarchy. The snapshot parser validates YAML structure and generates error diagnostics pinpointing parsing failures by line and column. Rendered snapshots normalize whitespace and optionally convert dynamic strings (UUIDs, timestamps, file sizes) into regex patterns for robust comparison across test runs.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/isomorphic/ariaSnapshot.ts:L45-L82](packages/isomorphic/ariaSnapshot.ts#L45-L82)\n- [packages/isomorphic/ariaSnapshotRenderer.ts:L27-L129](packages/isomorphic/ariaSnapshotRenderer.ts#L27-L129)\n\n## Code Generation for Multiple Languages\n\nThe test runner can generate automated test code in multiple languages through language-specific generators. The C# generator supports library mode (standalone scripts) and test framework modes (MSTest, NUnit, xUnit), producing appropriate class structures and async/await patterns. The Java generator similarly supports library and JUnit modes. Both generators convert recorded user actions (click, fill, navigate, assert) into idiomatic code, handling browser context options, device emulation, HAR recording routes, and storage state management.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/isomorphic/codegen/csharp.ts:L28-L127](packages/isomorphic/codegen/csharp.ts#L28-L127)\n- [packages/isomorphic/codegen/java.ts:L28-L112](packages/isomorphic/codegen/java.ts#L28-L112)\n- [packages/isomorphic/codegen/actions.d.ts:L19-L128](packages/isomorphic/codegen/actions.d.ts#L19-L128)\n\n## Error Reporting and Diagnostics\n\nError output includes syntax-highlighted code frames showing the exact line where a test failed with optional column markers and custom messages. The code frame renderer supports configurable context (lines above and below the error location) and respects color settings for terminal output. Base64 utilities support test artifacts and encoded data handling. These diagnostics infrastructure components enable the test runner to produce readable failure reports with source location context.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/isomorphic/codeFrame.ts:L35-L59](packages/isomorphic/codeFrame.ts#L35-L59)\n- [packages/isomorphic/base64.ts:L17-L22](packages/isomorphic/base64.ts#L17-L22)\n"}, {"path": "packages/trace-viewer.md", "title": "Trace Viewer", "parent": null, "description": "Interactive trace viewer UI for inspecting test execution, network requests, and DOM snapshots.", "files": ["packages/trace-viewer/package.json", "packages/trace-viewer/vite.config.ts", "packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.d.ts", "packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.js", "packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.mjs", "packages/playwright-browser-firefox/install.js", "packages/trace-viewer/bundle.ts", "packages/trace-viewer/src/index.tsx", "packages/trace-viewer/src/snapshotMain.ts", "packages/trace-viewer/src/sw-main.ts", "packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/main.ts", "packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/progress.ts", "packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/traceLoaderBackends.ts", "packages/trace-viewer/src/third_party/devtools.ts", "packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/actionList.tsx", "packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/annotationsTab.tsx", "packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/ariaModeView.tsx"], "markdown": "# Trace Viewer\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [packages/trace-viewer/package.json](packages/trace-viewer/package.json)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/vite.config.ts](packages/trace-viewer/vite.config.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.d.ts](packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.d.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.js](packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.js)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.mjs](packages/playwright-browser-firefox/index.mjs)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-firefox/install.js](packages/playwright-browser-firefox/install.js)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/bundle.ts](packages/trace-viewer/bundle.ts)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/index.tsx](packages/trace-viewer/src/index.tsx)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/snapshotMain.ts](packages/trace-viewer/src/snapshotMain.ts)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/sw-main.ts](packages/trace-viewer/src/sw-main.ts)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/main.ts](packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/main.ts)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/progress.ts](packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/progress.ts)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/traceLoaderBackends.ts](packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/traceLoaderBackends.ts)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/third_party/devtools.ts](packages/trace-viewer/src/third_party/devtools.ts)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/actionList.tsx](packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/actionList.tsx)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/annotationsTab.tsx](packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/annotationsTab.tsx)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/ariaModeView.tsx](packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/ariaModeView.tsx)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nTrace Viewer is an interactive React-based UI for inspecting Playwright test execution. It enables developers to step through test actions, view DOM snapshots at different execution phases, inspect network requests, and explore accessibility trees captured during test runs. The viewer supports both archived traces (ZIP format) and live traces served via HTTP, making it suitable for local development and CI/CD environments.\n\n## Architecture\n\nTrace Viewer uses a client-service worker architecture to load and serve trace data. The build system in `packages/trace-viewer/vite.config.ts` defines two environments: `client` for the main UI and `sw` for the service worker. Both are compiled separately into `packages/playwright-core/lib/vite/traceViewer`.\n\nThe main entry point (`packages/trace-viewer/src/index.tsx`) detects whether a trace is live by checking if it ends with `.json`, then renders either `LiveWorkbenchLoader` or `WorkbenchLoader` accordingly. When running over HTTP, a service worker is registered to intercept fetch requests and serve trace data from multiple sources.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/trace-viewer/vite.config.ts:L46-L82](packages/trace-viewer/vite.config.ts#L46-L82)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/index.tsx:L25-L35](packages/trace-viewer/src/index.tsx#L25-L35)\n\n## Trace Loading and Backend Systems\n\nTrace Viewer supports two backend systems for loading traces. The `ZipTraceLoaderBackend` handles archived traces by reading ZIP files using the `@zip.js` library, extracting entries on demand. The `FetchTraceLoaderBackend` handles live traces by reading a JSON manifest that maps entry names to fetch-able paths. This backend also converts Dropbox share URLs into direct download URLs.\n\nThe service worker (`packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/main.ts`) manages trace lifecycle, caching loaded traces per URL and performing garbage collection when clients disconnect. It prevents redundant loading of the same trace across multiple browser tabs and handles errors gracefully with user-readable messages about invalid trace formats or permission issues.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/traceLoaderBackends.ts:L26-L82](packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/traceLoaderBackends.ts#L26-L82)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/main.ts:L94-L136](packages/trace-viewer/src/sw/main.ts#L94-L136)\n\n## Action Timeline and Test Flow\n\nThe action list component (`packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/actionList.tsx`) displays test actions in a hierarchical tree structure that reflects nesting and execution order. Each action shows its method name, parameters, duration, and result status. The viewer supports filtering actions by time range (selected boundaries) and by text search. Actions are marked with badges indicating error or warning counts, and skipped test steps receive a distinct visual indicator.\n\nActions can render attachments and console output. The title formatting system uses template markers (e.g., `{url}`, `{selector}`) to substitute action parameters into human-readable descriptions. Protocol metadata informs how parameters are formatted for each SDK language.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/actionList.tsx:L35-L49](packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/actionList.tsx#L35-L49)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/actionList.tsx:L180-L219](packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/actionList.tsx#L180-L219)\n\n## Snapshots and Accessibility Trees\n\nTrace Viewer captures DOM and accessibility snapshots at three execution phases: before action, during action, and after action. The ARIA mode view (`packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/ariaModeView.tsx`) renders snapshots as YAML-formatted accessibility trees, highlighting DOM elements by their bounding boxes when the user hovers over roles and attributes.\n\nSnapshots are served by the service worker through the `/snapshot/` endpoint. The viewer fetches raw snapshot data and resolves coordinates into viewport space, accounting for iframe nesting. When both screenshot and ARIA snapshot are available, the UI displays them side-by-side, with element highlighting synchronized between the visual and semantic views.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/ariaModeView.tsx:L40-L84](packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/ariaModeView.tsx#L40-L84)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/ariaModeView.tsx:L144-L199](packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/ariaModeView.tsx#L144-L199)\n\n## Utilities, Export, and Metadata\n\nThe DevTools utilities module (`packages/trace-viewer/src/third_party/devtools.ts`) generates reproducible curl and fetch commands for network requests captured during traces. These commands filter unsafe or automatically-set headers (Accept-Encoding, Host, Cookie handling rules differ by platform) and escape special characters for both Windows and POSIX shells. The viewer also supports exporting requests as fetch calls compatible with both browser and Node.js environments, automatically adjusting mode and header handling based on the target runtime.\n\nThe annotations tab (`packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/annotationsTab.tsx`) displays metadata attached to test runs by the test framework, including test annotations with type and description fields. Descriptions support linkification, making it easy to reference external issues or documentation directly from the trace viewer UI.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/third_party/devtools.ts:L65-L185](packages/trace-viewer/src/third_party/devtools.ts#L65-L185)\n- [packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/annotationsTab.tsx:L23-L38](packages/trace-viewer/src/ui/annotationsTab.tsx#L23-L38)\n"}, {"path": "packages/html-reporter.md", "title": "HTML Reporter", "parent": null, "description": "HTML test report generator with interactive UI for viewing test results, failures, and traces.", "files": ["packages/html-reporter/package.json", "packages/html-reporter/vite.config.ts", "packages/html-reporter/bundle.ts", "packages/html-reporter/playwright.config.ts", "packages/html-reporter/src/chip.spec.ts", "packages/html-reporter/src/chip.story.tsx", "packages/html-reporter/src/chip.tsx", "packages/html-reporter/src/copyToClipboard.tsx", "packages/html-reporter/src/expandable.tsx", "packages/html-reporter/src/filter.ts", "packages/html-reporter/src/gantt.tsx", "packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.spec.ts", "packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.story.tsx", "packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.tsx", "packages/html-reporter/src/icons.tsx", "packages/html-reporter/src/images.ts"], "markdown": "# HTML Reporter\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [packages/html-reporter/package.json](packages/html-reporter/package.json)\n- [packages/html-reporter/vite.config.ts](packages/html-reporter/vite.config.ts)\n- [packages/html-reporter/bundle.ts](packages/html-reporter/bundle.ts)\n- [packages/html-reporter/playwright.config.ts](packages/html-reporter/playwright.config.ts)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/chip.spec.ts](packages/html-reporter/src/chip.spec.ts)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/chip.story.tsx](packages/html-reporter/src/chip.story.tsx)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/chip.tsx](packages/html-reporter/src/chip.tsx)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/copyToClipboard.tsx](packages/html-reporter/src/copyToClipboard.tsx)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/expandable.tsx](packages/html-reporter/src/expandable.tsx)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/filter.ts](packages/html-reporter/src/filter.ts)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/gantt.tsx](packages/html-reporter/src/gantt.tsx)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.spec.ts](packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.spec.ts)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.story.tsx](packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.story.tsx)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.tsx](packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.tsx)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/icons.tsx](packages/html-reporter/src/icons.tsx)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/images.ts](packages/html-reporter/src/images.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nThe HTML Reporter is an interactive React-based viewer for test results generated by Playwright tests. Located at `packages/html-reporter/`, it provides a web UI for browsing test outcomes, filtering results, and examining test details including failure information and execution timelines. The reporter is built as a single-page application and embedded directly into the Playwright test runner's report output.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/html-reporter/package.json:L1-L6](packages/html-reporter/package.json#L1-L6)\n\n## Build & Deployment\n\nThe HTML Reporter uses Vite for bundling and is configured to output a self-contained bundle. The build process generates two files: `report.js` (the React application and all dependencies) and `report.css` (styling). The compiled output is placed into `packages/playwright-core/lib/vite/htmlReport/`, where it is then packaged for distribution with the core Playwright library.\n\nA custom Vite plugin strips Apache license comment blocks during the build process, reducing bundle size. All assets are inlined with a 100MB limit to ensure the report is a single, portable file. CSS is not split across chunks; all styles are combined into one `report.css` file.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/html-reporter/vite.config.ts:L26-L54](packages/html-reporter/vite.config.ts#L26-L54)\n- [packages/html-reporter/bundle.ts:L19-L31](packages/html-reporter/bundle.ts#L19-L31)\n\n## UI Components\n\nThe HTML Reporter provides reusable React components for building an interactive test results interface:\n\n**Chip**: A collapsible container with an optional expandable header. Chips can be stateless (rendering as a heading) or stateful with expand/collapse behavior controlled via button click or keyboard (Enter/Space). The component supports a custom body render prop and optional footer content, with full ARIA support for accessibility.\n\n**Expandable**: A simple wrapper around the HTML `<details>` element that provides expand/collapse behavior with animated arrow icons. State is managed internally.\n\n**CopyToClipboard**: A button component that copies a given string to the clipboard. It displays transient feedback—showing a checkmark on success, an X on failure, and returning to the copy icon after 3 seconds.\n\n**GanttChart**: An SVG-based timeline visualization showing task durations. The chart automatically scales tick intervals based on the total duration (choosing 10s, 30s, 5m, or 10m intervals) and displays labels, duration text, and colored bars for each task.\n\n**GlobalFilterView & HeaderView**: The main header and navigation area that displays test statistics (passed, failed, flaky, skipped counts), a search input, and a settings button. The header integrates filter logic and allows users to toggle filters by test status or enter custom search queries.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/chip.tsx:L25-L83](packages/html-reporter/src/chip.tsx#L25-L83)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/expandable.tsx:L21-L46](packages/html-reporter/src/expandable.tsx#L21-L46)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/copyToClipboard.tsx:L28-L59](packages/html-reporter/src/copyToClipboard.tsx#L28-L59)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/gantt.tsx:L35-L209](packages/html-reporter/src/gantt.tsx#L35-L209)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.tsx:L30-L169](packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.tsx#L30-L169)\n\n## Filtering & Search\n\nTest results can be filtered using a query syntax that supports multiple filter types:\n\n| Filter Type | Syntax | Example | Behavior |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Project | `p:` | `p:chromium` | Match tests from a specific browser project |\n| Status | `s:` | `s:failed` | Filter by outcome: `passed`, `failed`, `flaky`, `skipped` |\n| Text | plain text | `login` | Search test name, file path, tags, and other metadata |\n| Labels | `@` | `@smoke` | Match test tags |\n| Annotations | `annot:` | `annot:issue=123` | Filter by annotation type and description |\n| Negation | `!` | `!s:skipped` | Exclude results (prefix any filter) |\n\nQueries support quoted phrases with spaces: `\"long test name\"`. The filter engine caches search values per test for performance. Status filters default to excluding skipped tests unless explicitly included. URL search parameters (`?q=...`) persist filter state across page reloads.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/filter.ts:L24-L172](packages/html-reporter/src/filter.ts#L24-L172)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.spec.ts:L51-L70](packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.spec.ts#L51-L70)\n\n## Testing\n\nThe HTML Reporter uses Playwright for component and integration testing. Component tests are located alongside source files (`.spec.ts`) and use the `@playwright/test` testing framework with the component testing library. Tests mount React components directly and verify their behavior—expand/collapse interactions, keyboard navigation, ARIA attributes, and UI rendering.\n\nA local Vite dev server runs on port 3101 during testing, serving the component stories and test fixtures. Test reporters are configured per environment: local runs use HTML reporting with auto-open on failure, while CI runs generate JSON, blob, and Parquet reports for aggregation. The test configuration blocks service workers and enables trace capture on first retry for debugging.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/html-reporter/playwright.config.ts:L26-L55](packages/html-reporter/playwright.config.ts#L26-L55)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/chip.spec.ts:L17-L78](packages/html-reporter/src/chip.spec.ts#L17-L78)\n- [packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.spec.ts:L17-L49](packages/html-reporter/src/headerView.spec.ts#L17-L49)\n"}, {"path": "packages/recorder.md", "title": "Recorder Package", "parent": null, "description": "Test recorder UI for generating test code from browser interactions.", "files": ["packages/recorder/package.json", "packages/recorder/vite.config.ts", "packages/playwright-browser-webkit/index.d.ts", "packages/playwright-browser-webkit/index.js", "packages/playwright-browser-webkit/index.mjs", "packages/playwright-browser-webkit/install.js", "packages/recorder/src/callLog.tsx", "packages/recorder/src/index.tsx", "packages/recorder/src/recorder.tsx", "packages/recorder/src/recorderTypes.d.ts", "packages/recorder/src/vite-env.d.ts"], "markdown": "# Recorder Package\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [packages/recorder/package.json](packages/recorder/package.json)\n- [packages/recorder/vite.config.ts](packages/recorder/vite.config.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-webkit/index.d.ts](packages/playwright-browser-webkit/index.d.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-webkit/index.js](packages/playwright-browser-webkit/index.js)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-webkit/index.mjs](packages/playwright-browser-webkit/index.mjs)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-webkit/install.js](packages/playwright-browser-webkit/install.js)\n- [packages/recorder/src/callLog.tsx](packages/recorder/src/callLog.tsx)\n- [packages/recorder/src/index.tsx](packages/recorder/src/index.tsx)\n- [packages/recorder/src/recorder.tsx](packages/recorder/src/recorder.tsx)\n- [packages/recorder/src/recorderTypes.d.ts](packages/recorder/src/recorderTypes.d.ts)\n- [packages/recorder/src/vite-env.d.ts](packages/recorder/src/vite-env.d.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nThe recorder package provides a React-based UI for the Playwright Test Inspector. It captures browser interactions and generates corresponding test code, allowing users to build tests by performing actions in a live browser rather than writing code manually. The UI communicates with a backend controller that orchestrates recording, element inspection, and test code generation across multiple programming languages.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/recorder/src/index.tsx:L1-L26](packages/recorder/src/index.tsx#L1-L26)\n- [packages/recorder/src/recorder.tsx:L37-L45](packages/recorder/src/recorder.tsx#L37-L45)\n\n## Recording Modes\n\nThe recorder operates in multiple modes to support different testing workflows:\n\n| Mode | Purpose |\n|------|---------|\n| `recording` | Captures user interactions and generates test code |\n| `inspecting` | Picks elements on the page to generate locators |\n| `recording-inspecting` | Records interactions while element picker is active |\n| `assertingText` | Records text content assertions on elements |\n| `assertingVisibility` | Records visibility assertions on elements |\n| `assertingValue` | Records value assertions on form inputs |\n| `assertingSnapshot` | Records snapshot assertions |\n| `standby` | Idle state between recording sessions |\n| `none` | Uninitialized state |\n\nThe UI provides toolbar buttons for initiating each assertion mode and toggling between recording and standby states.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/recorder/src/recorderTypes.d.ts:L22-L31](packages/recorder/src/recorderTypes.d.ts#L22-L31)\n- [packages/recorder/src/recorder.tsx:L158-L204](packages/recorder/src/recorder.tsx#L158-L204)\n\n## UI Architecture\n\nThe main Recorder component organizes its interface into three sections:\n\n**Toolbar**: Control buttons for recording, inspection, assertions, and navigation. The toolbar also displays the current page title and provides access to settings (theme, auto-expect option). Keyboard shortcuts support pause/resume (F8) and step-over (F10) operations when paused.\n\n**Code Editor**: Main view showing generated test code with syntax highlighting. The editor is read-only and displays code for the currently selected target file from a dropdown menu.\n\n**Sidebar with Tabs**: Three-tab interface for locator inspection, call log review, and ARIA snapshot matching. The Locator tab accepts user input to test CSS/text locators against the page. The Log tab displays a history of recorded actions with status indicators and optional error messages. The Aria tab allows YAML-based ARIA snapshot templates for accessibility testing.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/recorder/src/recorder.tsx:L161-L244](packages/recorder/src/recorder.tsx#L161-L244)\n\n## Call Log Display\n\nThe call log tracks the execution history of recorded actions. Each entry in the call log shows a title, optional subtitle, status icon, and execution duration. The CallLogView component renders expandable log entries that display additional messages and errors when needed.\n\nCall log statuses include:\n- `done`: Successfully executed\n- `in-progress`: Currently executing  \n- `paused`: Execution paused at this step\n- `error`: Failed execution with error message\n\nUsers can toggle log entry expansion to view detailed messages associated with each recorded action.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/recorder/src/callLog.tsx:L23-L62](packages/recorder/src/callLog.tsx#L23-L62)\n- [packages/recorder/src/recorderTypes.d.ts:L62-L73](packages/recorder/src/recorderTypes.d.ts#L62-L73)\n\n## Backend Communication\n\nThe recorder UI communicates with a backend controller through a proxy-based interface. The frontend exposes a `RecorderFrontend` interface that receives updates from the backend, while the backend exposes a `RecorderBackend` interface that accepts commands from the UI:\n\n**Backend Commands**:\n- `setMode({ mode })`: Change recorder operation mode\n- `setAutoExpect({ autoExpect })`: Toggle automatic assertion generation\n- `resume()`: Resume execution from pause\n- `pause()`: Pause execution\n- `step()`: Execute one step while paused\n- `highlightRequested({ selector?, ariaTemplate? })`: Request UI highlighting of a matched element\n- `fileChanged({ fileId })`: Switch to a different generated code file\n- `clear()`: Clear recorded code\n\n**Frontend Events**:\n- `modeChanged`: Recorder mode changed\n- `pauseStateChanged`: Execution pause state changed\n- `sourcesUpdated`: Available generated code sources updated\n- `callLogsUpdated`: Call log entries updated\n- `elementPicked`: User picked an element via inspector\n- `pageNavigated`: Target page URL changed\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/recorder/src/recorderTypes.d.ts:L103-L122](packages/recorder/src/recorderTypes.d.ts#L103-L122)\n- [packages/recorder/src/recorder.tsx:L50-L101](packages/recorder/src/recorder.tsx#L50-L101)\n\n## Build Configuration\n\nThe recorder is built using Vite with React support. The build outputs a single bundled file to `playwright-core/lib/vite/recorder` for integration into the core test runner. The package depends on the `yaml` library for parsing ARIA snapshot templates.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/recorder/package.json:L1-L9](packages/recorder/package.json#L1-L9)\n- [packages/recorder/vite.config.ts:L23-L42](packages/recorder/vite.config.ts#L23-L42)\n"}, {"path": "packages/web-ui.md", "title": "Web UI Components", "parent": null, "description": "Shared web UI component library used by Dashboard, Trace Viewer, HTML Reporter, and Recorder.", "files": ["packages/web/package.json", "packages/playwright-browser-chromium/index.d.ts", "packages/playwright-browser-chromium/index.js", "packages/playwright-browser-chromium/index.mjs", "packages/playwright-browser-chromium/install.js", "packages/utils/ascii.ts", "packages/utils/chromiumChannels.ts", "packages/utils/comparators.ts", "packages/utils/crypto.ts", "packages/utils/debug.ts", "packages/utils/debugLogger.ts", "packages/utils/env.ts", "packages/utils/eventsHelper.ts", "packages/utils/fileUtils.ts", "packages/utils/hostPlatform.ts", "packages/utils/httpServer.ts"], "markdown": "# Web UI Components\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [packages/web/package.json](packages/web/package.json)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-chromium/index.d.ts](packages/playwright-browser-chromium/index.d.ts)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-chromium/index.js](packages/playwright-browser-chromium/index.js)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-chromium/index.mjs](packages/playwright-browser-chromium/index.mjs)\n- [packages/playwright-browser-chromium/install.js](packages/playwright-browser-chromium/install.js)\n- [packages/utils/ascii.ts](packages/utils/ascii.ts)\n- [packages/utils/chromiumChannels.ts](packages/utils/chromiumChannels.ts)\n- [packages/utils/comparators.ts](packages/utils/comparators.ts)\n- [packages/utils/crypto.ts](packages/utils/crypto.ts)\n- [packages/utils/debug.ts](packages/utils/debug.ts)\n- [packages/utils/debugLogger.ts](packages/utils/debugLogger.ts)\n- [packages/utils/env.ts](packages/utils/env.ts)\n- [packages/utils/eventsHelper.ts](packages/utils/eventsHelper.ts)\n- [packages/utils/fileUtils.ts](packages/utils/fileUtils.ts)\n- [packages/utils/hostPlatform.ts](packages/utils/hostPlatform.ts)\n- [packages/utils/httpServer.ts](packages/utils/httpServer.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `web` package is a shared UI component library used across multiple Playwright tooling applications. Located at `packages/web`, it provides reusable web UI components and utilities for building interactive web-based interfaces. The package is marked private within the monorepo and serves as a dependency for the HTML Reporter, Trace Viewer, Dashboard, and Recorder applications.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/web/package.json:L1-L4](packages/web/package.json#L1-L4)\n\n## Dependencies\n\nThe web package declares dependencies on three key libraries that provide core UI and terminal functionality:\n\n| Dependency | Version | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| `codemirror` | 5.65.18 | Code editor widget for displaying and editing source code |\n| `@xterm/xterm` | ^5.5.0 | Terminal emulator for rendering terminal output in the browser |\n| `@xterm/addon-fit` | ^0.10.0 | Addon for xterm that enables automatic terminal resizing |\n| `yaml` | ^2.8.3 | YAML parsing and serialization for configuration handling |\n\nThese dependencies enable the package to provide rich interactive features across consumer applications, including syntax-highlighted code editing, embedded terminal emulation, and configuration file handling.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [packages/web/package.json:L6-L11](packages/web/package.json#L6-L11)\n\n## Consumer Applications\n\nThe shared web UI component library is consumed by four primary Playwright tooling applications:\n\n- **Dashboard** (`packages/dashboard/`) — provides interactive session management and debugging interface\n- **Trace Viewer** (`packages/trace-viewer/`) — displays recorded browser traces with interactive playback and inspection\n- **HTML Reporter** (`packages/html-reporter/`) — generates and serves interactive HTML test reports\n- **Recorder** (`packages/recorder/`) — captures and displays browser interactions for test generation\n\nEach consumer application builds upon the shared web components to construct specialized UIs tailored to its specific purpose while maintaining consistency across the Playwright tooling ecosystem.\n\n## Role in the Monorepo\n\nThe web package is a private internal package that is not published to npm. It follows the monorepo's import boundary rules as defined in `DEPS.list` files, ensuring that client and server code remain properly separated and that communication flows only through the protocol layer. The package provides foundational UI building blocks that reduce duplication and maintain visual and behavioral consistency across Playwright's web-based tools.\n"}, {"path": "packages/protocol.md", "title": "Protocol Definitions", "parent": null, "description": "Protocol channel and message type definitions for client-server communication.", "files": ["tests/android/android.spec.ts", "tests/android/androidTest.ts", "tests/android/browser.spec.ts", "tests/android/device.spec.ts", "tests/android/launch-server.spec.ts", "tests/android/playwright.config.ts", "tests/android/webview.spec.ts", "tests/assets/axe-core/accessible-text.js", "tests/assets/axe-core/implicit-role.js"], "markdown": "# Protocol Definitions\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [tests/android/android.spec.ts](tests/android/android.spec.ts)\n- [tests/android/androidTest.ts](tests/android/androidTest.ts)\n- [tests/android/browser.spec.ts](tests/android/browser.spec.ts)\n- [tests/android/device.spec.ts](tests/android/device.spec.ts)\n- [tests/android/launch-server.spec.ts](tests/android/launch-server.spec.ts)\n- [tests/android/playwright.config.ts](tests/android/playwright.config.ts)\n- [tests/android/webview.spec.ts](tests/android/webview.spec.ts)\n- [tests/assets/axe-core/accessible-text.js](tests/assets/axe-core/accessible-text.js)\n- [tests/assets/axe-core/implicit-role.js](tests/assets/axe-core/implicit-role.js)\n</cite>\n\n## Android Device Protocol\n\nThe Android device protocol enables remote communication with Android devices through WebSocket endpoints. Devices expose methods that handle shell command execution, file transfer operations, and browser instance launches. Clients connect to devices through proxy servers that forward protocol messages to the underlying device service on port 5037. The protocol supports custom host binding and device serial number targeting to identify specific devices on shared networks.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [tests/android/android.spec.ts:L34-L57](tests/android/android.spec.ts#L34-L57)\n\n## Chrome DevTools Protocol Integration\n\nPlaywright integrates the Chrome DevTools Protocol for direct browser control and inspection. Clients establish CDP sessions with page contexts and send protocol messages to perform runtime operations. The protocol supports message types including `Runtime.enable` for enabling evaluation capabilities and `Runtime.evaluate` for executing JavaScript expressions with result capture. CDP provides low-level access to browser internals including script evaluation and DOM manipulation.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [tests/android/browser.spec.ts:L124-L132](tests/android/browser.spec.ts#L124-L132)\n\n## Device Command and File Operations\n\nThe device protocol supports shell command execution with output capture, file push operations for transferring data to device storage, and screenshot capture in PNG format. Commands execute asynchronously through persistent socket connections, maintaining state across multiple operations. The protocol standardizes device interaction patterns for testing on mobile platforms.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [tests/android/device.spec.ts:L21-L50](tests/android/device.spec.ts#L21-L50)\n\n## Browser Server Protocol and Connection Management\n\nThe browser server protocol enables launching persistent server instances that accept multiple client connections through WebSocket endpoints. The protocol manages connection lifecycle, enforcing exclusive client policies and handling device disconnection events. Clients receive notifications when servers close or connections terminate, allowing graceful cleanup of resources.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [tests/android/launch-server.spec.ts:L24-L50](tests/android/launch-server.spec.ts#L24-L50)\n\n## Accessibility Role and Text Protocol\n\nAccessibility testing relies on a semantic protocol that computes implicit and explicit element roles according to ARIA specifications. Elements expose accessible names through direct attributes like `aria-label` and `aria-labelledby`, HTML label associations, and content traversal rules. The protocol handles role inheritance, label precedence, and content visibility according to W3C accessibility guidelines.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [tests/assets/axe-core/implicit-role.js:L1-L50](tests/assets/axe-core/implicit-role.js#L1-L50)\n"}, {"path": "packages/injected-scripts.md", "title": "Injected Scripts", "parent": null, "description": "Scripts injected into page context for locators, evaluation, and browser interaction.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Injected Scripts\n\n## Overview\n\nInjected scripts are JavaScript code that Playwright injects into browser pages to support locator selection, page evaluation, and browser interaction features. These scripts run in the page context rather than in Playwright's Node.js process, allowing direct access to the DOM and page JavaScript state. They form a critical bridge between Playwright's public API and the actual browser environment.\n\n## The `injected` Package\n\nInjected scripts are maintained in the `packages/injected/` directory as part of the Playwright monorepo. This package contains the scripts that get bundled and injected into page contexts during browser automation. The scripts are compiled and prepared as part of the standard build process (`npm run build`), and their generated output is embedded into the Playwright client for delivery to pages.\n\n## Locator Support\n\nInjected scripts enable Playwright's locator engine by providing runtime utilities in the page context. When locators are evaluated, these scripts execute within the page DOM to perform selector matching, visibility checks, and other locator-related operations. The scripts can traverse the DOM, evaluate CSS selectors, assess element visibility, and handle complex selector strategies that require page context access. This architecture allows locators to work consistently across different page states and DOM structures without round-tripping to the browser process for every operation.\n\n## Page Evaluation\n\nThe `page.evaluate()` and related evaluation APIs rely on injected scripts to execute arbitrary JavaScript within the page context. These scripts provide the foundation for running user-provided code and accessing page state directly. They handle the marshaling of arguments and return values between Playwright's protocol layer and the page's JavaScript execution context, enabling seamless integration between test code and page logic.\n\n## Browser Interaction Integration\n\nInjected scripts support various browser interaction features by providing utilities that run directly in the page context. They assist with actions like filling inputs, clicking elements, and monitoring events by executing directly where the DOM and page JavaScript are available. This direct execution model is more reliable than attempting all interactions through the browser protocol alone.\n\n## Build and Distribution\n\nInjected scripts are part of the standard Playwright build pipeline. When running `npm run build` or `npm run watch`, the scripts in `packages/injected/` are compiled and bundled. The compiled output is then incorporated into the main Playwright packages (`playwright-core` and `playwright`) so that scripts are available when needed during test execution.\n"}, {"path": "packages/isomorphic.md", "title": "Isomorphic Packages", "parent": null, "description": "Isomorphic utility packages shared between client and server code.", "files": [], "markdown": "# Isomorphic Packages\n\nPlaywright's monorepo contains several packages designed to operate across multiple execution contexts—client applications, server processes, and browser environments. These isomorphic packages form the shared foundation that enables both the client and server sides of the browser automation engine to communicate and function cohesively.\n\n## Architecture and Import Boundaries\n\nThe Playwright codebase enforces strict separation between client and server code through a DEPS system (52+ DEPS.list files across the repository). Client code never imports server code, and server code never imports client code. All communication between these layers flows exclusively through the protocol layer, defined in the internal `protocol` package via RPC specifications (`spec/*.yml`).\n\nIsomorphic packages—those that can be safely used in both client and server contexts—occupy a carefully governed middle ground. They contain shared utilities, types, and implementations that do not depend on client-specific or server-specific runtime features.\n\n## Core Isomorphic Packages\n\nThe `protocol` package serves as the primary isomorphic foundation. It defines RPC protocol specifications and generates validators and channel type definitions that both client and server implementations reference. This ensures that the client library at `@playwright/client` and the server implementation in `playwright-core` communicate through a consistent, type-safe contract.\n\nUtility packages in the `utils/` directory—which include build scripts, code generation tools, linting infrastructure, and documentation generation—are designed to operate in a Node.js environment and to be invoked during both build and development workflows. These tools operate on source files and configuration without depending on runtime browser automation features.\n\n## Web and UI Packages\n\nThe `web` package contains shared web UI components used across multiple Playwright tools. These components are designed to run in browser contexts while maintaining compatibility with server-side rendering or build-time composition. The package enables code reuse between tools like the HTML reporter (`html-reporter`), trace viewer (`trace-viewer`), and recorder (`recorder`).\n\nThe `injected` package contains scripts that are injected into browser pages at runtime. These scripts must be fully self-contained and cannot depend on client or server infrastructure; they execute in the browser's page context and communicate back to Playwright through the page's established protocol channels.\n\n## Shared Test Infrastructure\n\nTest packages like `playwright-test` (the test runner entry point) and `@playwright/test` are designed with isomorphic principles in mind. They must function both as a CLI tool that runs in a worker process and as a configuration surface that developers import in their own test files. This dual nature requires careful boundary management and clear separation between what runs in the test runner process versus what runs in a test worker.\n\n## Practical Implications\n\nWhen adding new code to the monorepo, maintainers must declare allowed imports in the relevant `DEPS.list` file. Files marked `\"strict\"` can only import what is explicitly listed, ensuring that isomorphic boundaries remain intact. This prevents accidental coupling between client and server implementations and maintains the protocol as the single source of truth for cross-boundary communication.\n"}, {"path": "examples/github-api-and-svgomg.md", "title": "GitHub API and SVGO Example Projects", "parent": null, "description": "Two minimal example projects: an API-only test suite against the GitHub REST API and a browser suite against an SVG optimizer web app.", "files": ["examples/github-api/.gitignore", "examples/github-api/package.json", "examples/github-api/playwright.config.ts", "examples/github-api/tests/test-api.spec.ts", "examples/svgomg/.gitignore", "examples/svgomg/package.json", "examples/svgomg/playwright.config.ts", "examples/svgomg/tests/example.spec.ts"], "markdown": "# GitHub API and SVGO Example Projects\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [examples/github-api/.gitignore](examples/github-api/.gitignore)\n- [examples/github-api/package.json](examples/github-api/package.json)\n- [examples/github-api/playwright.config.ts](examples/github-api/playwright.config.ts)\n- [examples/github-api/tests/test-api.spec.ts](examples/github-api/tests/test-api.spec.ts)\n- [examples/svgomg/.gitignore](examples/svgomg/.gitignore)\n- [examples/svgomg/package.json](examples/svgomg/package.json)\n- [examples/svgomg/playwright.config.ts](examples/svgomg/playwright.config.ts)\n- [examples/svgomg/tests/example.spec.ts](examples/svgomg/tests/example.spec.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Shared Structure and npm Scripts\n\nBoth examples are minimal, self-contained `@playwright/test` projects. Each has a `package.json`, a `playwright.config.ts`, a `.gitignore`, and a `tests/` directory containing a single spec file at `^1.17.1` of the test runner.\n\n| Property | `examples/github-api` | `examples/svgomg` |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Package name | `github-api` | `svgomg-tests` |\n| Dev dependency | `@playwright/test: ^1.17.1` | `@playwright/test: ^1.17.1` |\n| License | `ISC` | `ISC` |\n| Spec file | `tests/test-api.spec.ts` | `tests/example.spec.ts` |\n| npm scripts | `test` | `test`, `ctest`, `ftest`, `wtest` |\n| Ignored paths | `node_modules/`, `test-results/`, `playwright-report/` | plus `package-lock.json` |\n\nThe SVGO project exposes per-browser scripts, whose exact command values are:\n\n| Script | Command |\n| --- | --- |\n| `test` | `playwright test` |\n| `ctest` | `playwright test --project=chromium` |\n| `ftest` | `playwright test --project=firefox` |\n| `wtest` | `playwright test --project=webkit` |\n\nThe GitHub API project defines only `\"test\": \"playwright test\"`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/github-api/package.json:L1-L13](examples/github-api/package.json#L1-L13)\n- [examples/github-api/.gitignore:L1-L3](examples/github-api/.gitignore#L1-L3)\n- [examples/svgomg/package.json:L1-L16](examples/svgomg/package.json#L1-L16)\n- [examples/svgomg/.gitignore:L1-L4](examples/svgomg/.gitignore#L1-L4)\n\n## GitHub API Example Project\n\nThis suite exercises the GitHub REST API without a browser page. Shared request settings are declared with `test.use`: `baseURL` is `https://api.github.com`, and `extraHTTPHeaders` adds `Accept: application/vnd.github.v3+json` plus `Authorization: token ${process.env.API_TOKEN}`. The spec reads `process.env.GITHUB_USER` into `user` and hardcodes the repository name as `'Test-Repo-1'`.\n\nThe configuration in `examples/github-api/playwright.config.ts` sets:\n\n- `testDir: './tests'`\n- `timeout: 30 * 1000`\n- `forbidOnly: !!process.env.CI`\n- `retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0`\n- `workers: process.env.CI ? 1 : undefined`\n- `reporter: 'html'`\n- `trace: 'on-first-retry'`\n\n`baseURL` in the shared `use` block is commented out, since the spec supplies it through `test.use`.\n\nThe lifecycle hooks manage the fixture repository:\n\n```ts\ntest.beforeAll(async ({ request }) => {\n  const response = await request.post('/user/repos', {\n    data: { name: repo }\n  });\n  expect(response.ok()).toBeTruthy();\n});\n\ntest.afterAll(async ({ request }) => {\n  const response = await request.delete(`/repos/${user}/${repo}`);\n  expect(response.ok()).toBeTruthy();\n});\n```\n\nTwo tests follow the same pattern: `should create bug report` and `should create feature request`. Each posts an issue to `/repos/${user}/${repo}/issues` with a `title` and `body`, asserts the response is ok, then fetches the issue list and asserts it `toContainEqual(expect.objectContaining({...}))` with the matching title and body.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/github-api/playwright.config.ts:L8-L35](examples/github-api/playwright.config.ts#L8-L35)\n- [examples/github-api/tests/test-api.spec.ts:L14-L74](examples/github-api/tests/test-api.spec.ts#L14-L74)\n\n## GitHub API Test Lifecycle\n\nThe request sequence is entirely hook- and test-driven: repository creation happens once before all tests, issue creation and retrieval happen per test, and repository deletion happens once after all tests.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant R as Playwright runner\n    participant G as api.github.com\n    R->>G: POST /user/repos (beforeAll)\n    R->>G: POST /repos/{user}/{repo}/issues\n    R->>G: GET /repos/{user}/{repo}/issues\n    R->>G: DELETE /repos/{user}/{repo} (afterAll)\n```\n\nBecause `afterAll` deletes the repository, the suite leaves no persistent repository behind on the account identified by `GITHUB_USER`.\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [examples/github-api/tests/test-api.spec.ts:L26-L40](examples/github-api/tests/test-api.spec.ts#L26-L40)\n- [examples/github-api/tests/test-api.spec.ts:L42-L74](examples/github-api/tests/test-api.spec.ts#L42-L74)\n\n## SVGO Example Project\n\nThis suite drives the SVG optimizer demo at `https://demo.playwright.dev/svgomg` in a real browser. The spec enables parallel mode with `test.describe.configure({ mode: 'parallel' })` and navigates in `beforeEach`.\n\nConfiguration in `examples/svgomg/playwright.config.ts` mirrors the GitHub API project for CI behavior (`forbidOnly`, `retries`, `workers`, `reporter: 'html'`, `trace: 'on-first-retry'`), and adds:\n\n| Setting | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| `expect.timeout` | `5000` |\n| `use.actionTimeout` | `0` |\n| `use.acceptDownloads` | `true` |\n| Projects | `chromium`, `firefox`, `webkit` |\n| Device presets | `Desktop Chrome`, `Desktop Firefox`, `Desktop Safari` |\n\nMobile viewports, branded browsers, `outputDir`, and `webServer` entries exist only as comments, so they are not active.\n\n| Test | What it asserts |\n| --- | --- |\n| `verify menu items` | `.menu li` text equals `Open SVG`, `Paste markup`, `Demo`, `Contribute` |\n| `verify default global settings` | Global toggles are `Show original`, `Compare gzipped`, `Prettify markup`, `Multipass`; only `Compare gzipped` is checked |\n| `verify default features` | Five listed features are checked; four listed features are not |\n| `reset settings` | Clicking `Show original` checks it; `Reset all` unchecks it |\n| `download result` | Download link `href` matches `/blob/`, suggested filename is `car-lite.svg`, and `fs.readFileSync` content contains `<svg` |\n| `open svg` | A file chooser is triggered by `Open SVG`, an in-memory SVG buffer is set, and the rendered `.svg-frame` SVG outerHTML matches `/<svg.*<\\/svg>/` |\n\nThe `demo tests` block nests the four demo-related tests under a `beforeEach` that clicks `.menu-item >> text=Demo`. The download test relies on `acceptDownloads: true` and reads the saved file through Node's `fs` module:\n\n```ts\nconst [download] = await Promise.all([\n  page.waitForEvent('download'),\n  downloadButton.click()\n]);\nexpect(download.suggestedFilename()).toBe('car-lite.svg');\nconst result = fs.readFileSync(await download.path(), 'utf-8');\nexpect(result).toContain('<svg');\n```\n\nThe `open svg` test avoids a filesystem fixture by supplying `{ name, mimeType, buffer }` to `fileChooser.setFiles`, then verifies rendering with `page.frameLocator('.svg-frame').locator('svg').evaluate(...)`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/svgomg/playwright.config.ts:L8-L60](examples/svgomg/playwright.config.ts#L8-L60)\n- [examples/svgomg/tests/example.spec.ts:L6-L107](examples/svgomg/tests/example.spec.ts#L6-L107)\n"}, {"path": "examples/mock-battery.md", "title": "Mock Battery API Example", "parent": null, "description": "An example demonstrating browser API mocking with a demo battery-status page and tests that stub and verify the Battery API.", "files": ["examples/mock-battery/package.json", "examples/mock-battery/playwright.config.js", "examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/index.html", "examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/LICENSE", "examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/README.md", "examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/src/index.js", "examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/src/styles.css", "examples/mock-battery/tests/show-battery-status.spec.js", "examples/mock-battery/tests/update-battery-status.spec.js", "examples/mock-battery/tests/verify-calls.spec.js"], "markdown": "# Mock Battery API Example\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [examples/mock-battery/package.json](examples/mock-battery/package.json)\n- [examples/mock-battery/playwright.config.js](examples/mock-battery/playwright.config.js)\n- [examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/index.html](examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/index.html)\n- [examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/LICENSE](examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/LICENSE)\n- [examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/README.md](examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/README.md)\n- [examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/src/index.js](examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/src/index.js)\n- [examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/src/styles.css](examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/src/styles.css)\n- [examples/mock-battery/tests/show-battery-status.spec.js](examples/mock-battery/tests/show-battery-status.spec.js)\n- [examples/mock-battery/tests/update-battery-status.spec.js](examples/mock-battery/tests/update-battery-status.spec.js)\n- [examples/mock-battery/tests/verify-calls.spec.js](examples/mock-battery/tests/verify-calls.spec.js)\n</cite>\n\nThis example shows how to test a page that depends on a browser API that is unavailable or unreliable in automation. The demo page reads the Battery Status API through `navigator.getBattery()` (with a `navigator.battery` fallback), and each test replaces that API with a deterministic stub installed via `page.addInitScript()` before any page script runs. Because the stub is plain JavaScript running in the page, tests can both drive battery state changes and record which API calls the application made.\n\n## Example Layout\n\n| Path | Role |\n| --- | --- |\n| `examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/index.html` | Demo page markup with the elements the tests assert on |\n| `examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/src/index.js` | Application logic that reads the Battery API and renders it |\n| `examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/src/styles.css` | Styling, including the `.battery:before` fill rule the script mutates |\n| `examples/mock-battery/tests/show-battery-status.spec.js` | Stubs the API and asserts the rendered values |\n| `examples/mock-battery/tests/update-battery-status.spec.js` | Stubs a stateful mock and drives level/charging changes |\n| `examples/mock-battery/tests/verify-calls.spec.js` | Stubs the API and asserts the exact call sequence |\n| `examples/mock-battery/playwright.config.js` | Serves the demo page and points at the test directory |\n\nThe demo page itself is an external project (`demo-battery-api` by Guille Paz), shipped with its own MIT `LICENSE` and `README.md`, which lists the demo URL and supported browsers.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/mock-battery/package.json:L1-L17](examples/mock-battery/package.json#L1-L17)\n- [examples/mock-battery/playwright.config.js:L1-L13](examples/mock-battery/playwright.config.js#L1-L13)\n- [examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/README.md:L1-L14](examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/README.md#L1-L14)\n- [examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/LICENSE:L1-L4](examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/LICENSE#L1-L4)\n\n## The Demo Battery Page\n\n`index.html` defines the DOM contract used by every test. The percentage is written into `.battery-percentage`, while a definition list exposes power source (`.battery-status`), level (`.battery-level`), time to full (`.battery-fully`) and remaining time (`.battery-remaining`). A `.not-support` paragraph is hidden by default and is revealed only when neither battery API exists.\n\n`src/index.js` implements the rendering. `readBattery(b)` caches the battery object, formats the level as a percentage, and writes an inline `width` rule for `.battery:before` into the first stylesheet. It also converts `chargingTime` and `dischargingTime` through `toTime(sec)`, which returns `HH:MM`, and prints `Calculating...` when the relevant time is `Infinity`. The power source label is `Adapter` when `battery.charging` is true and `Battery` otherwise.\n\nStartup follows a three-branch fallback:\n\n```js\nif (navigator.battery) {\n  readBattery(navigator.battery);\n} else if (navigator.getBattery) {\n  navigator.getBattery().then(readBattery);\n} else {\n  document.querySelector('.not-support').removeAttribute('hidden');\n}\n```\n\n`window.onload` then registers `chargingchange` and `levelchange` listeners that call `readBattery()` again, which is what makes the stateful mock in the update tests observable in the UI.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/index.html:L14-L49](examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/index.html#L14-L49)\n- [examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/src/index.js:L6-L18](examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/src/index.js#L6-L18)\n- [examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/src/index.js:L20-L50](examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/src/index.js#L20-L50)\n- [examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/src/index.js:L52-L71](examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/src/index.js#L52-L71)\n- [examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/src/styles.css:L74-L82](examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/src/styles.css#L74-L82)\n\n## Test Harness Configuration\n\n`package.json` declares two scripts: `playwright test` for the suite and `http-server -c-1 -p 9900 demo-battery-api` to serve the demo with caching disabled. `playwright.config.js` wires the two together through `webServer`, so the suite starts the server on port `9900` itself, and `testDir` points at the `tests` folder resolved from `__dirname`.\n\n```js\nmodule.exports = defineConfig({\n  webServer: {\n    port: 9900,\n    command: 'npm run start',\n  },\n  // Test directory\n  testDir: path.join(__dirname, 'tests'),\n});\n```\n\nBecause the page is served at the server root, every test navigates with `page.goto('/')`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/mock-battery/package.json:L6-L16](examples/mock-battery/package.json#L6-L16)\n- [examples/mock-battery/playwright.config.js:L6-L13](examples/mock-battery/playwright.config.js#L6-L13)\n- [examples/mock-battery/tests/show-battery-status.spec.js:L21-L22](examples/mock-battery/tests/show-battery-status.spec.js#L21-L22)\n\n## Stubbing the API Before Page Load\n\n`show-battery-status.spec.js` installs the stub in `test.beforeEach`. Inside `page.addInitScript()`, it builds a literal mock with `level: 0.90`, `charging: true`, `chargingTime: 1800`, `dischargingTime: Infinity`, and a no-op `addEventListener`. Two steps make the mock authoritative: `delete window.navigator.battery` forces the page past its first branch, and `window.navigator.getBattery = async () => mockBattery` supplies the value the second branch awaits.\n\n```js\ndelete window.navigator.battery;\nwindow.navigator.getBattery = async () => mockBattery;\n```\n\nThe single test then asserts the derived UI values: `90%` for the percentage, `Adapter` for the power source (because `charging` is true), and `00:30` for time to full (1800 seconds formatted by `toTime`).\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/mock-battery/tests/show-battery-status.spec.js:L4-L26](examples/mock-battery/tests/show-battery-status.spec.js#L4-L26)\n- [examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/src/index.js:L6-L18](examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/src/index.js#L6-L18)\n- [examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/src/index.js:L44-L48](examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/src/index.js#L44-L48)\n\n## Driving Battery Changes From Tests\n\n`update-battery-status.spec.js` goes further with a `BatteryMock` class that stores listeners per event and exposes `_setLevel(value)` and `_setCharging(value)`, each of which updates a field and invokes the registered callbacks. The instance is assigned to `window.mockBattery` so the Node-side test can reach it with `page.evaluate()`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"test.beforeEach\"] --> B[\"page.exposeFunction('logCall')\"]\n    A --> C[\"page.addInitScript()\"]\n    C --> D[\"delete window.navigator.battery\"]\n    C --> E[\"window.navigator.getBattery -> mock\"]\n    C --> F[\"window.mockBattery = mock\"]\n    B --> G[\"page.goto('/')\"]\n    G --> H[\"src/index.js reads navigator.getBattery\"]\n    E --> H\n    H --> I[\"readBattery updates DOM\"]\n    I --> J[\"expect locators\"]\n    J --> K[\"page.evaluate _setLevel / _setCharging\"]\n    K --> I\n```\n\nThe mock starts at `level: 0.10` and `charging: false`, so the first assertion is `10%`. Calling `_setLevel(0.275)` fires the `levelchange` listeners, and the test expects `27.5%` plus `Battery` as the power source. Calling `_setCharging(true)` fires the `chargingchange` listeners, flipping the label to `Adapter` and keeping `00:30` as the time to full.\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [examples/mock-battery/tests/update-battery-status.spec.js:L6-L49](examples/mock-battery/tests/update-battery-status.spec.js#L6-L49)\n- [examples/mock-battery/tests/update-battery-status.spec.js:L51-L64](examples/mock-battery/tests/update-battery-status.spec.js#L51-L64)\n- [examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/src/index.js:L52-L71](examples/mock-battery/demo-battery-api/src/index.js#L52-L71)\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/mock-battery/tests/update-battery-status.spec.js:L13-L49](examples/mock-battery/tests/update-battery-status.spec.js#L13-L49)\n- [examples/mock-battery/tests/update-battery-status.spec.js:L51-L64](examples/mock-battery/tests/update-battery-status.spec.js#L51-L64)\n\n## Verifying Which API Calls Were Made\n\nBoth `update-battery-status.spec.js` and `verify-calls.spec.js` record calls by bridging page code to Node code: `page.exposeFunction('logCall', msg => log.push(msg))` creates a page-global function that appends to a module-level `log` array, which `test.beforeEach` resets to `[]` each time. The stubs call `logCall('getBattery')` and `logCall(\\`addEventListener:${name}\\`)`, so the test can assert on the exact sequence rather than only on rendered text.\n\nThe expected sequence is the same in both files:\n\n```js\nexpect(log).toEqual([\n  'getBattery',\n  'addEventListener:chargingchange',\n  'addEventListener:levelchange'\n]);\n```\n\nIn `update-battery-status.spec.js` the log is then cleared and `_setLevel(0.275)` is called; the follow-up `expect(log).toEqual([])` documents that `getBattery` is not called again and the cached battery object is reused. `verify-calls.spec.js` performs the same initial check against a mock at `level: 0.75`, asserting `75%` in the UI before comparing the call log.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/mock-battery/tests/update-battery-status.spec.js:L4-L9](examples/mock-battery/tests/update-battery-status.spec.js#L4-L9)\n- [examples/mock-battery/tests/update-battery-status.spec.js:L67-L81](examples/mock-battery/tests/update-battery-status.spec.js#L67-L81)\n- [examples/mock-battery/tests/verify-calls.spec.js:L4-L39](examples/mock-battery/tests/verify-calls.spec.js#L4-L39)\n"}, {"path": "examples/mock-filesystem.md", "title": "Mock Filesystem Example", "parent": null, "description": "An example showing how to mock the File System Access API so tests can exercise directory and file pickers deterministically.", "files": ["examples/mock-filesystem/package.json", "examples/mock-filesystem/playwright.config.js", "examples/mock-filesystem/src/file-picker.html", "examples/mock-filesystem/src/ls-dir.html", "examples/mock-filesystem/tests/directory-reader.spec.js", "examples/mock-filesystem/tests/file-reader.spec.js", "examples/webauthn/register-and-login.mjs", "examples/webauthn/seed-credential.mjs"], "markdown": "# Mock Filesystem Example\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [examples/mock-filesystem/package.json](examples/mock-filesystem/package.json)\n- [examples/mock-filesystem/playwright.config.js](examples/mock-filesystem/playwright.config.js)\n- [examples/mock-filesystem/src/file-picker.html](examples/mock-filesystem/src/file-picker.html)\n- [examples/mock-filesystem/src/ls-dir.html](examples/mock-filesystem/src/ls-dir.html)\n- [examples/mock-filesystem/tests/directory-reader.spec.js](examples/mock-filesystem/tests/directory-reader.spec.js)\n- [examples/mock-filesystem/tests/file-reader.spec.js](examples/mock-filesystem/tests/file-reader.spec.js)\n- [examples/webauthn/register-and-login.mjs](examples/webauthn/register-and-login.mjs)\n- [examples/webauthn/seed-credential.mjs](examples/webauthn/seed-credential.mjs)\n</cite>\n\n## Purpose and Layout\n\nThe `examples/mock-filesystem` project demonstrates how to test pages that call the File System Access API without a real user picking anything. Both pages under test call a native picker (`window.showOpenFilePicker` or `window.showDirectoryPicker`), and both tests replace that picker with a plain JavaScript stub before the page loads. The result is a deterministic test that never opens an operating-system dialog.\n\nThe project is small and has no application framework: two static HTML pages, two spec files, one config.\n\n| Path | Role |\n| --- | --- |\n| `examples/mock-filesystem/src/file-picker.html` | Page that calls `window.showOpenFilePicker()` and renders file text |\n| `examples/mock-filesystem/src/ls-dir.html` | Page that calls `window.showDirectoryPicker()` and renders a tree |\n| `examples/mock-filesystem/tests/file-reader.spec.js` | Mocks the file picker and asserts textarea content |\n| `examples/mock-filesystem/tests/directory-reader.spec.js` | Mocks the directory picker and asserts the rendered tree |\n| `examples/mock-filesystem/playwright.config.js` | Starts the static server and points `testDir` at `tests` |\n| `examples/mock-filesystem/package.json` | Declares `@playwright/test` and `http-server` dev dependencies |\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/mock-filesystem/package.json:L1-L17](examples/mock-filesystem/package.json#L1-L17)\n- [examples/mock-filesystem/playwright.config.js:L1-L13](examples/mock-filesystem/playwright.config.js#L1-L13)\n\n## The Pages Being Tested\n\n`examples/mock-filesystem/src/file-picker.html` defines a single `loadFile()` function bound to a button. It awaits `window.showOpenFilePicker()`, destructures the first handle from the returned array, calls `fileHandle.getFile()`, then `file.text()`, and writes the string into the element with id `contents` (a `<textarea>`).\n\n```js\nasync function loadFile() {\n    const [fileHandle] = await window.showOpenFilePicker();\n    const file = await fileHandle.getFile();\n    const contents = await file.text();\n    document.getElementById('contents').textContent = contents\n}\n```\n\n`examples/mock-filesystem/src/ls-dir.html` uses a recursive walker instead. `loadDir()` awaits `window.showDirectoryPicker()`, iterates `handle.values()`, and passes each entry to `listEntry`, which indents by two per level, prints the name, and recurses only when `e.kind === 'directory'`. Directory entries get a 📁 marker through the `div.directory::before` CSS rule, and each printed row is appended to `#dir`.\n\nBecause both pages read only `name`, `kind`, `values()`, `getFile()`, and `text()` from the handles, the mocks only need to supply those members.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/mock-filesystem/src/file-picker.html:L1-L15](examples/mock-filesystem/src/file-picker.html#L1-L15)\n- [examples/mock-filesystem/src/ls-dir.html:L1-L35](examples/mock-filesystem/src/ls-dir.html#L1-L35)\n\n## Mocking the Directory Picker\n\n`examples/mock-filesystem/tests/directory-reader.spec.js` installs its mock in `test.beforeEach` via `page.addInitScript`, so the stub exists before any page script runs. The mock class `FileSystemHandleMock` derives `kind` from whether the constructor received children: a non-empty `children` array yields `'directory'`, otherwise `'file'`. Its `values()` method maps each child record into a new `FileSystemHandleMock`, which is what makes the recursion in the page terminate correctly.\n\n```js\nclass FileSystemHandleMock {\n  constructor({name, children}) {\n    this.name = name;\n    children ??= [];\n    this.kind = children.length ? 'directory' : 'file';\n    this._children = children;\n  }\n\n  values() {\n    // Wrap children data in the same mock.\n    return this._children.map(c => new FileSystemHandleMock(c));\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe fixture tree is a `root` directory containing `file1`, `dir1` (with `file2`, `file3`), and `dir2` (with `file4`, `file5`). The final line of the init script assigns `window.showDirectoryPicker = async () => mockDir;`, so the page's `await` resolves immediately to the mock root.\n\nThe test navigates to `/ls-dir.html`, clicks the `Open directory` button, and asserts that `#dir` contains the names in tree order. The expected list uses a nested array literal, which lets `toContainText` match the entries in sequence.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/mock-filesystem/tests/directory-reader.spec.js:L1-L64](examples/mock-filesystem/tests/directory-reader.spec.js#L1-L64)\n\n## Mocking the File Picker\n\n`examples/mock-filesystem/tests/file-reader.spec.js` follows the same pattern with a much smaller mock. `FileSystemFileHandleMock` stores a `File` object and exposes a single async `getFile()` method returning it. The picker override returns an array containing one handle wrapping `new File(['Test content.'], \"foo.txt\")` — matching the page's `const [fileHandle] = await window.showOpenFilePicker()` destructuring.\n\n```js\nwindow.showOpenFilePicker = async () => [new FileSystemFileHandleMock(new File(['Test content.'], \"foo.txt\"))];\n```\n\nThe test opens `/file-picker.html`, clicks `Open File`, and asserts `toHaveValue('Test content.')` on the textarea. Note that the page writes with `textContent`, while the assertion checks the form value; the expectation is on the same visible string in both cases.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/mock-filesystem/tests/file-reader.spec.js:L1-L24](examples/mock-filesystem/tests/file-reader.spec.js#L1-L24)\n\n## Execution Flow\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Test as Spec file\n    participant Page as Browser page\n    participant Picker as window.showDirectoryPicker\n    Test->>Page: addInitScript installs FileSystemHandleMock\n    Test->>Page: goto('/ls-dir.html')\n    Test->>Page: click \"Open directory\"\n    Page->>Picker: await showDirectoryPicker()\n    Picker-->>Page: mockDir (root)\n    Page->>Page: values() recursion -> printEntry -> #dir\n    Test->>Page: expect(#dir).toContainText([...])\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [examples/mock-filesystem/tests/directory-reader.spec.js:L4-L57](examples/mock-filesystem/tests/directory-reader.spec.js#L4-L57)\n- [examples/mock-filesystem/src/ls-dir.html:L8-L28](examples/mock-filesystem/src/ls-dir.html#L8-L28)\n\n## Running the Example\n\n`examples/mock-filesystem/playwright.config.js` defines a `webServer` block with `port: 9900` and `command: 'npm run start'`, and sets `testDir` to the local `tests` directory through `path.join(__dirname, 'tests')`. Playwright therefore starts the static server itself; the pages are served from `src/` because the `start` script in `package.json` runs the server against that folder.\n\n```js\nwebServer: {\n  port: 9900,\n  command: 'npm run start',\n},\n// Test directory\ntestDir: path.join(__dirname, 'tests'),\n```\n\nThe two scripts declared in `package.json` are the entry points used above:\n\n```json\n\"scripts\": {\n  \"test\": \"playwright test\",\n  \"start\": \"http-server -c-1 -p 9900 src/\"\n}\n```\n\nBoth `@playwright/test` (`^1.19.1`) and `http-server` (`^14.1.0`) are dev dependencies of the example, so the directory is self-contained.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/mock-filesystem/playwright.config.js:L6-L12](examples/mock-filesystem/playwright.config.js#L6-L12)\n- [examples/mock-filesystem/package.json:L6-L16](examples/mock-filesystem/package.json#L6-L16)\n"}, {"path": "examples/todomvc.md", "title": "TodoMVC Example: Structure and Fixtures", "parent": null, "description": "The TodoMVC example suite layout: project config, shared fixtures, seed spec, the written test plan, and the add-todo test group.", "files": ["examples/todomvc/.gitignore", "examples/todomvc/package.json", "examples/todomvc/playwright.config.ts", "examples/todomvc/specs/basic-operations.plan.md", "examples/todomvc/tests/fixtures.ts", "examples/todomvc/tests/seed.spec.ts", "examples/todomvc/tests/adding-todos/should-add-multiple-todos.spec.ts", "examples/todomvc/tests/adding-todos/should-add-single-todo.spec.ts", "examples/todomvc/tests/adding-todos/should-not-add-empty-todo.spec.ts", "examples/todomvc/tests/adding-todos/should-trim-whitespace-from-new-todo.spec.ts", "examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/add-multiple-todos.spec.ts", "examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/add-single-todo.spec.ts"], "markdown": "# TodoMVC Example: Structure and Fixtures\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [examples/todomvc/.gitignore](examples/todomvc/.gitignore)\n- [examples/todomvc/package.json](examples/todomvc/package.json)\n- [examples/todomvc/playwright.config.ts](examples/todomvc/playwright.config.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/specs/basic-operations.plan.md](examples/todomvc/specs/basic-operations.plan.md)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/fixtures.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/fixtures.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/seed.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/seed.spec.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/adding-todos/should-add-multiple-todos.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/adding-todos/should-add-multiple-todos.spec.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/adding-todos/should-add-single-todo.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/adding-todos/should-add-single-todo.spec.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/adding-todos/should-not-add-empty-todo.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/adding-todos/should-not-add-empty-todo.spec.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/adding-todos/should-trim-whitespace-from-new-todo.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/adding-todos/should-trim-whitespace-from-new-todo.spec.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/add-multiple-todos.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/add-multiple-todos.spec.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/add-single-todo.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/add-single-todo.spec.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Purpose and Layout\n\nThe `examples/todomvc` directory is a self-contained Playwright Test project that exercises the hosted TodoMVC demo. It has no application source of its own: the suite only contains configuration, a shared fixture that opens the remote app, a written test plan, and the spec files. The package is named `todomvc-test` and depends on `@playwright/test` and `dotenv` as dev dependencies.\n\n| Path | Role |\n| --- | --- |\n| `examples/todomvc/package.json` | Package identity, test scripts, dev dependencies |\n| `examples/todomvc/playwright.config.ts` | Test directory, timeouts, reporters, browser project |\n| `examples/todomvc/tests/fixtures.ts` | Extended `test` fixture that navigates to the app |\n| `examples/todomvc/tests/seed.spec.ts` | Empty seed test used as a recording starting point |\n| `examples/todomvc/specs/basic-operations.plan.md` | Human-readable test plan with steps and expectations |\n| `examples/todomvc/tests/adding-todos/` | Four add-todo specs keyed to the plan |\n| `examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/` | Two additional add-todo specs with `test.step` structure |\n\nIgnored artifacts are limited to installed dependencies and generated output: `node_modules/`, `test-results/`, `playwright-report/`, and `package-lock.json`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/todomvc/.gitignore:L1-L4](examples/todomvc/.gitignore#L1-L4)\n- [examples/todomvc/package.json:L1-L17](examples/todomvc/package.json#L1-L17)\n\n## Project Configuration\n\n`playwright.config.ts` calls `dotenv.config({ quiet: true })` before defining the config, then exports `defineConfig`. Tests are discovered under `./tests`, with a per-test timeout of 15 seconds and an `expect` timeout of 5 seconds. On CI the config sets `forbidOnly`, enables two retries, and forces a single worker; locally retries are `0` and the worker count is left undefined. Reporters are `html` and `list`.\n\nShared `use` options disable the per-action timeout (`actionTimeout: 0`), collect traces on the first retry, and record video in `mode: 'on'` with action labels positioned top-left and test labels top-right.\n\nOnly the `chromium` project is active, using `devices['Desktop Chrome']`. The `firefox`, `webkit`, mobile viewport, and branded-browser project blocks are present but commented out, so the `firefox` and `webkit` scripts in `package.json` reference projects that the checked-in config does not define.\n\n| npm script | Command |\n| --- | --- |\n| `test` | `playwright test` |\n| `ctest` | `playwright test --project=chromium` |\n| `ftest` | `playwright test --project=firefox` |\n| `wtest` | `playwright test --project=webkit` |\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/todomvc/playwright.config.ts:L3-L37](examples/todomvc/playwright.config.ts#L3-L37)\n- [examples/todomvc/playwright.config.ts:L40-L58](examples/todomvc/playwright.config.ts#L40-L58)\n- [examples/todomvc/playwright.config.ts:L61-L69](examples/todomvc/playwright.config.ts#L61-L69)\n- [examples/todomvc/package.json:L4-L9](examples/todomvc/package.json#L4-L9)\n\n## Shared Fixtures and the Seed Spec\n\nAll specs import `test` and `expect` from `../fixtures` rather than from `@playwright/test` directly. The fixture module re-exports `expect` unchanged and extends the base `test` with a single override of the built-in `page` fixture: before yielding the page to the test body, it navigates to `https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc`. Because `page` is overridden, every test in the suite starts on the already-loaded application.\n\n`seed.spec.ts` is a deliberately empty test that uses the same fixture. Its comment states that it tells agents how to start recording a test so that the page is already configured; the plan document also names `tests/seed.spec.ts` as the seed for every scenario group, and individual specs carry a `// seed: tests/seed.spec.ts` header comment.\n\n```ts\nexport const test = baseTest.extend({\n  page: async ({ page }, use) => {\n    await page.goto('https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc');\n    await use(page);\n  },\n});\n```\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"playwright.config.ts<br/>testDir: ./tests\"] --> B[\"fixtures.ts<br/>page fixture navigates to demo.playwright.dev/todomvc\"]\n  B --> C[\"seed.spec.ts<br/>empty recording starting point\"]\n  B --> D[\"adding-todos/*.spec.ts\"]\n  B --> E[\"todo-creation/*.spec.ts\"]\n  F[\"specs/basic-operations.plan.md\"] -.describes.-> D\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [examples/todomvc/playwright.config.ts:L13-L13](examples/todomvc/playwright.config.ts#L13-L13)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/fixtures.ts:L7-L12](examples/todomvc/tests/fixtures.ts#L7-L12)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/seed.spec.ts:L3-L6](examples/todomvc/tests/seed.spec.ts#L3-L6)\n- [examples/todomvc/specs/basic-operations.plan.md:L11-L11](examples/todomvc/specs/basic-operations.plan.md#L11-L11)\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/fixtures.ts:L3-L12](examples/todomvc/tests/fixtures.ts#L3-L12)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/seed.spec.ts:L1-L6](examples/todomvc/tests/seed.spec.ts#L1-L6)\n\n## The Written Test Plan\n\n`specs/basic-operations.plan.md` documents the intended coverage in seven numbered scenario groups: Adding Todos, Completing Todos, Editing Todos, Deleting Todos, Filtering Todos, Persistence, and UI State. Each group declares `**Seed:** tests/seed.spec.ts`, and each scenario lists a target file plus numbered steps with `expect:` lines. For example, scenario 1.1 `should-add-single-todo` targets `tests/adding-todos/should-add-single-todo.spec.ts` and expects the input to be visible, the typed text to appear, the todo to be added, the input cleared, and the counter to read `1 item left`.\n\nThe plan is broader than the checked-in spec tree in places: it references files under `persistence/` and `ui-state/` that do not appear in the provided tree, and only one filtering spec is present. The plan should therefore be read as the design document the specs were derived from, not as an index of files that all exist.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/todomvc/specs/basic-operations.plan.md:L1-L61](examples/todomvc/specs/basic-operations.plan.md#L1-L61)\n- [examples/todomvc/specs/basic-operations.plan.md:L63-L135](examples/todomvc/specs/basic-operations.plan.md#L63-L135)\n- [examples/todomvc/specs/basic-operations.plan.md:L261-L311](examples/todomvc/specs/basic-operations.plan.md#L261-L311)\n\n## The Add-Todo Test Group\n\nThe `adding-todos/` directory holds four specs that map one-to-one onto plan section 1. All four wrap their body in `test.describe('Adding Todos', ...)` and locate the input via `page.getByRole('textbox', { name: 'What needs to be done?' })`.\n\n| Spec | Assertions |\n| --- | --- |\n| `should-add-single-todo.spec.ts` | Input visible; `toHaveValue('Buy groceries')` after fill; todo visible after `Enter`; input cleared; `1 item left` |\n| `should-add-multiple-todos.spec.ts` | After each of `Buy milk`, `Walk the dog`, `Finish report`: todo visible and counter advances to `1`, `2`, `3 items left` |\n| `should-trim-whitespace-from-new-todo.spec.ts` | Fills `'   Todo with spaces   '`; expects `Todo with spaces` and `1 item left` |\n| `should-not-add-empty-todo.spec.ts` | Clicks the empty input, expects `toBeFocused()`, presses `Enter`, then expects `page.getByRole('list')` `not.toBeVisible()` |\n\nThe `todo-creation/` directory covers similar ground with a different style: `add-single-todo.spec.ts` adds a post-condition that the `Toggle Todo` checkbox is unchecked, and `add-multiple-todos.spec.ts` stores the input locator in a `newTodoInput` constant, wraps each addition in `await test.step(...)`, and verifies the final state with `getByRole('listitem').filter({ hasText: 'Buy groceries' }).getByLabel('Toggle Todo')` `not.toBeChecked()` alongside the `3 items left` counter.\n\n```ts\nawait test.step('Add first todo', async () => {\n  await newTodoInput.fill('Buy groceries');\n  await newTodoInput.press('Enter');\n  await expect(page.getByText('Buy groceries')).toBeVisible();\n});\n```\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/adding-todos/should-add-single-todo.spec.ts:L3-L24](examples/todomvc/tests/adding-todos/should-add-single-todo.spec.ts#L3-L24)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/adding-todos/should-add-multiple-todos.spec.ts:L3-L33](examples/todomvc/tests/adding-todos/should-add-multiple-todos.spec.ts#L3-L33)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/adding-todos/should-trim-whitespace-from-new-todo.spec.ts:L3-L15](examples/todomvc/tests/adding-todos/should-trim-whitespace-from-new-todo.spec.ts#L3-L15)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/adding-todos/should-not-add-empty-todo.spec.ts:L3-L19](examples/todomvc/tests/adding-todos/should-not-add-empty-todo.spec.ts#L3-L19)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/add-single-todo.spec.ts:L3-L31](examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/add-single-todo.spec.ts#L3-L31)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/add-multiple-todos.spec.ts:L3-L48](examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/add-multiple-todos.spec.ts#L3-L48)\n"}, {"path": "examples/todomvc-todo-lifecycle.md", "title": "TodoMVC Example: Create, Complete, and Filter", "parent": null, "description": "Todo creation edge cases, completing and uncompleting todos, toggling all items, and filtering to active todos in the TodoMVC example suite.", "files": ["examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/add-todo-special-chars.spec.ts", "examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/prevent-empty-todo.spec.ts", "examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/prevent-whitespace-todo.spec.ts", "examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-complete-multiple-todos.spec.ts", "examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-complete-single-todo.spec.ts", "examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-toggle-all-todos-complete.spec.ts", "examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-toggle-all-todos-incomplete.spec.ts", "examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-uncomplete-completed-todo.spec.ts", "examples/todomvc/tests/filtering-todos/should-filter-active-todos.spec.ts"], "markdown": "# TodoMVC Example: Create, Complete, and Filter\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/add-todo-special-chars.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/add-todo-special-chars.spec.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/prevent-empty-todo.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/prevent-empty-todo.spec.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/prevent-whitespace-todo.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/prevent-whitespace-todo.spec.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-complete-multiple-todos.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-complete-multiple-todos.spec.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-complete-single-todo.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-complete-single-todo.spec.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-toggle-all-todos-complete.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-toggle-all-todos-complete.spec.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-toggle-all-todos-incomplete.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-toggle-all-todos-incomplete.spec.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-uncomplete-completed-todo.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-uncomplete-completed-todo.spec.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/filtering-todos/should-filter-active-todos.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/filtering-todos/should-filter-active-todos.spec.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Shared Conventions Across the Specs\n\nEvery spec in this page imports `test` and `expect` from the local `../fixtures` module and wraps its case in a `test.describe` block named for its concern (`Todo Creation`, `Completing Todos`, `Filtering Todos`). Each file also carries a header comment pointing back to its origin: either a seed file (`// seed: tests/seed.spec.ts`) or a planning document (`// spec: specs/basic-operations.plan.md`).\n\nThe tests address the TodoMVC UI entirely through role-based locators. The new-todo field is always `getByRole('textbox', { name: 'What needs to be done?' })`, individual completion is driven by `getByRole('checkbox', { name: 'Toggle Todo' })`, and the bulk control is `getByRole('checkbox', { name: '❯Mark all as complete' })`. The remaining counter is asserted as plain text such as `1 item left` or `3 items left`, and the footer action is `getByRole('button', { name: 'Clear completed' })`.\n\n| Concern | Locator used | Example file |\n|---|---|---|\n| New todo input | `textbox` named `What needs to be done?` | `prevent-empty-todo.spec.ts` |\n| Per-item completion | `checkbox` named `Toggle Todo` | `should-complete-single-todo.spec.ts` |\n| Bulk completion | `checkbox` named `❯Mark all as complete` | `should-toggle-all-todos-complete.spec.ts` |\n| Item scoping | `getByRole('listitem').filter({ hasText: ... })` | `should-complete-multiple-todos.spec.ts` |\n| Remaining counter | `getByText('N items left')` | `should-complete-multiple-todos.spec.ts` |\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/add-todo-special-chars.spec.ts:L1-L9](examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/add-todo-special-chars.spec.ts#L1-L9)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/prevent-empty-todo.spec.ts:L1-L10](examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/prevent-empty-todo.spec.ts#L1-L10)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-complete-multiple-todos.spec.ts:L1-L20](examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-complete-multiple-todos.spec.ts#L1-L20)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/filtering-todos/should-filter-active-todos.spec.ts:L1-L8](examples/todomvc/tests/filtering-todos/should-filter-active-todos.spec.ts#L1-L8)\n\n## Todo Creation Edge Cases\n\nThree specs probe the boundaries of what the input accepts. They all begin by asserting the input is visible, which stands in for the \"page loaded with an empty list\" precondition.\n\n`prevent-empty-todo.spec.ts` clicks the field, presses `Enter` without typing, then asserts both that `.todo-list li` has count `0` and that the field still has value `''`. `prevent-whitespace-todo.spec.ts` fills the field with three spaces (`'   '`) and presses `Enter`, then asserts `getByRole('listitem')` has count `0` — the same emptiness check expressed with the role locator rather than the CSS class.\n\n`add-todo-special-chars.spec.ts` is the positive counterpart: it fills `Buy @groceries & supplies (urgent!)` and presses `Enter`, then asserts the exact string is visible and that the counter reads `1 item left`. This confirms that punctuation, `@`, `&` and parentheses survive submission unchanged.\n\n```ts\nawait page.getByRole('textbox', { name: 'What needs to be done?' }).fill('Buy @groceries & supplies (urgent!)');\nawait page.getByRole('textbox', { name: 'What needs to be done?' }).press('Enter');\nawait expect(page.getByText('Buy @groceries & supplies (urgent!)')).toBeVisible();\n```\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/prevent-empty-todo.spec.ts:L7-L22](examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/prevent-empty-todo.spec.ts#L7-L22)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/prevent-whitespace-todo.spec.ts:L7-L19](examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/prevent-whitespace-todo.spec.ts#L7-L19)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/add-todo-special-chars.spec.ts:L11-L19](examples/todomvc/tests/todo-creation/add-todo-special-chars.spec.ts#L11-L19)\n\n## Completing and Uncompleting Todos\n\n`should-complete-single-todo.spec.ts` adds `Buy groceries`, checks that the item is visible and the counter shows `1 item left`, then clicks the single `Toggle Todo` checkbox. The assertions after the click cover the checked state, the counter dropping to `0 items left`, and the appearance of the `Clear completed` button.\n\n`should-complete-multiple-todos.spec.ts` seeds `Buy milk`, `Walk dog` and `Finish report`, verifies all three are visible with `3 items left`, then completes items one at a time using the list-item scoping pattern:\n\n```ts\nawait page.getByRole('listitem').filter({ hasText: 'Buy milk' }).getByLabel('Toggle Todo').click();\nawait expect(page.getByRole('listitem').filter({ hasText: 'Buy milk' }).getByLabel('Toggle Todo')).toBeChecked();\nawait expect(page.getByText('2 items left')).toBeVisible();\n```\n\nAfter completing `Finish report` as well, the spec asserts `1 item left` and that `Clear completed` is visible — showing the counter tracks active items only.\n\n`should-uncomplete-completed-todo.spec.ts` is the shortest case: it adds `Buy groceries`, clicks `Toggle Todo` to complete it, then clicks the same checkbox again to revert. This file contains no `expect` calls, so it verifies the round trip only by the actions completing without error.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-complete-single-todo.spec.ts:L7-L23](examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-complete-single-todo.spec.ts#L7-L23)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-complete-multiple-todos.spec.ts:L7-L36](examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-complete-multiple-todos.spec.ts#L7-L36)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-uncomplete-completed-todo.spec.ts:L7-L15](examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-uncomplete-completed-todo.spec.ts#L7-L15)\n\n## Toggling All Items\n\nTwo specs exercise the `❯Mark all as complete` checkbox in opposite directions. Both first create three todos and confirm the `3 items left` baseline.\n\n`should-toggle-all-todos-complete.spec.ts` caches the input locator in a `newTodoInput` variable and reuses it for all three fills, then clicks the bulk checkbox once and asserts `0 items left` plus the visible `Clear completed` button. `should-toggle-all-todos-incomplete.spec.ts` clicks the bulk checkbox, asserts `0 items left`, clicks it again, and asserts the counter returns to `3 items left`.\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> ThreeActive\n    ThreeActive: 3 items left\n    AllComplete: 0 items left\n    ThreeActive --> AllComplete: click \"❯Mark all as complete\"\n    AllComplete --> ThreeActive: click \"❯Mark all as complete\" again\n```\n\nNote that both specs assert the aggregate counter rather than inspecting each item's checkbox state, even though the inline comments describe all checkboxes as checked or unchecked.\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-toggle-all-todos-complete.spec.ts:L7-L29](examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-toggle-all-todos-complete.spec.ts#L7-L29)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-toggle-all-todos-incomplete.spec.ts:L7-L21](examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-toggle-all-todos-incomplete.spec.ts#L7-L21)\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-toggle-all-todos-complete.spec.ts:L7-L29](examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-toggle-all-todos-complete.spec.ts#L7-L29)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-toggle-all-todos-incomplete.spec.ts:L7-L21](examples/todomvc/tests/completing-todos/should-toggle-all-todos-incomplete.spec.ts#L7-L21)\n\n## Filtering to Active Todos\n\n`should-filter-active-todos.spec.ts` combines creation, completion and routing. It adds `Active 1`, `Active 2` and `Will complete`, verifies all three are visible, then completes `Will complete` via the scoped list-item locator and asserts the checked state plus `2 items left`.\n\nThe filter step clicks `getByRole('link', { name: 'Active' })` and then verifies three things: the URL matches `/#\\/active$/`, both remaining active todos are still visible, and `Will complete` is now hidden.\n\n```ts\nawait page.getByRole('link', { name: 'Active' }).click();\nawait expect(page).toHaveURL(/#\\/active$/);\nawait expect(page.getByText('Active 1')).toBeVisible();\nawait expect(page.getByText('Will complete')).not.toBeVisible();\n```\n\nThe final assertion re-checks that the `Active` link itself is visible; the comment describes it as highlighted, but the assertion only establishes visibility.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/filtering-todos/should-filter-active-todos.spec.ts:L7-L43](examples/todomvc/tests/filtering-todos/should-filter-active-todos.spec.ts#L7-L43)\n"}, {"path": "examples/todomvc-editing.md", "title": "TodoMVC Example: Editing and Deleting Todos", "parent": null, "description": "The editing test group (double-click to edit, save on blur, cancel with Escape, delete when cleared, whitespace trimming) plus the deletion test group.", "files": ["examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-cancel-edit-on-escape.spec.ts", "examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-delete-todo-when-edited-to-empty.spec.ts", "examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-edit-todo-by-double-clicking.spec.ts", "examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-save-edit-on-blur.spec.ts", "examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-trim-whitespace-when-editing.spec.ts", "examples/todomvc/tests/deleting-todos/should-clear-all-completed-todos.spec.ts", "examples/todomvc/tests/deleting-todos/should-delete-single-todo.spec.ts", "examples/todomvc/tests/deleting-todos/should-delete-specific-todo-from-multiple.spec.ts"], "markdown": "# TodoMVC Example: Editing and Deleting Todos\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-cancel-edit-on-escape.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-cancel-edit-on-escape.spec.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-delete-todo-when-edited-to-empty.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-delete-todo-when-edited-to-empty.spec.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-edit-todo-by-double-clicking.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-edit-todo-by-double-clicking.spec.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-save-edit-on-blur.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-save-edit-on-blur.spec.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-trim-whitespace-when-editing.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-trim-whitespace-when-editing.spec.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/deleting-todos/should-clear-all-completed-todos.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/deleting-todos/should-clear-all-completed-todos.spec.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/deleting-todos/should-delete-single-todo.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/deleting-todos/should-delete-single-todo.spec.ts)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/deleting-todos/should-delete-specific-todo-from-multiple.spec.ts](examples/todomvc/tests/deleting-todos/should-delete-specific-todo-from-multiple.spec.ts)\n</cite>\n\n## Scope of the Two Test Groups\n\nThe editing group lives in `examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/` and the deletion group in `examples/todomvc/tests/deleting-todos/`. Every file imports `test` and `expect` from the local `../fixtures` module and wraps its single scenario in a `test.describe` block — `'Editing Todos'` or `'Deleting Todos'`. Each test carries a leading comment linking it to a spec document (`specs/editing-todos.plan.md`, `specs/basic-operations.plan.md`) and to `tests/seed.spec.ts`.\n\n| Group | File | Scenario |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Editing | `should-edit-todo-by-double-clicking.spec.ts` | Enter edit mode, rename, save with Enter |\n| Editing | `should-save-edit-on-blur.spec.ts` | Save when focus leaves the edit box |\n| Editing | `should-cancel-edit-on-escape.spec.ts` | Abandon changes with Escape |\n| Editing | `should-trim-whitespace-when-editing.spec.ts` | Trimming on save |\n| Editing | `should-delete-todo-when-edited-to-empty.spec.ts` | Clearing the text removes the todo |\n| Deleting | `should-delete-single-todo.spec.ts` | Delete the only todo via its button |\n| Deleting | `should-delete-specific-todo-from-multiple.spec.ts` | Delete one of three todos |\n| Deleting | `should-clear-all-completed-todos.spec.ts` | Bulk-remove completed todos |\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-edit-todo-by-double-clicking.spec.ts:L1-L8](examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-edit-todo-by-double-clicking.spec.ts#L1-L8)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/deleting-todos/should-delete-single-todo.spec.ts:L1-L8](examples/todomvc/tests/deleting-todos/should-delete-single-todo.spec.ts#L1-L8)\n\n## Locators Used for Editing\n\nEditing tests share a small locator vocabulary: the new-todo input is `getByRole('textbox', { name: 'What needs to be done?' })`, the todo label is `getByTestId('todo-title')`, and the inline editor that appears after double-clicking is `getByRole('textbox', { name: 'Edit' })`. Todos are addressed by text (`page.getByText('Buy milk')`) for content assertions.\n\n```ts\n// 1. Add a todo 'Buy milk'\nawait page.getByRole('textbox', { name: 'What needs to be done?' }).fill('Buy milk');\nawait page.getByRole('textbox', { name: 'What needs to be done?' }).press('Enter');\nawait expect(page.getByText('Buy milk')).toBeVisible();\n\n// 2. Double-click on the todo text\nawait page.getByTestId('todo-title').dblclick();\nawait expect(page.getByRole('textbox', { name: 'Edit' })).toBeVisible();\nawait expect(page.getByRole('textbox', { name: 'Edit' })).toHaveValue('Buy milk');\n```\n\nThe pattern is consistent across the group: fill the new-todo box, press `Enter`, assert the text is visible, then `dblclick()` the `todo-title` test id to reveal the edit textbox. Because the test id targets the single todo created in the test, no filtering is needed.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-edit-todo-by-double-clicking.spec.ts:L8-L16](examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-edit-todo-by-double-clicking.spec.ts#L8-L16)\n\n## Save, Cancel, and Empty-Result Paths\n\nFour exit paths from edit mode are covered, each verified differently:\n\n- **Enter saves** — the textbox is filled with `'Buy organic milk'`, `Enter` is pressed, and `page.getByText('Buy organic milk')` is asserted visible.\n- **Blur saves** — the text is changed to `'Schedule dentist appointment'`, then `page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'todos' }).click()` moves focus away; the new text must be visible.\n- **Escape cancels** — after filling `'Modified text'`, `page.keyboard.press('Escape')` is used and the assertion is that `'Original text'` remains visible, i.e. changes are discarded.\n- **Empty deletes** — the edit textbox is filled with `''` and `Enter` pressed; the test asserts both the original text and `page.getByTestId('todo-title')` are `not.toBeVisible()`, confirming the row is removed rather than blanked.\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> Viewing: add todo with Enter\n    Viewing --> Editing: dblclick todo-title\n    Editing --> Viewing: press Enter (save)\n    Editing --> Viewing: blur / click heading\n    Editing --> Viewing: press Escape (discard)\n    Editing --> [*]: press Enter with empty text (delete)\n```\n\nWhitespace trimming has its own nuance: the value assertion happens *before* saving, so the spec proves the raw input `'   Edited task   '` is preserved inside the editor, and only after `page.keyboard.press('Enter')` does `page.getByText('Edited task')` become the asserted visible text.\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-edit-todo-by-double-clicking.spec.ts:L13-L21](examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-edit-todo-by-double-clicking.spec.ts#L13-L21)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-save-edit-on-blur.spec.ts:L12-L19](examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-save-edit-on-blur.spec.ts#L12-L19)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-cancel-edit-on-escape.spec.ts:L15-L26](examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-cancel-edit-on-escape.spec.ts#L15-L26)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-delete-todo-when-edited-to-empty.spec.ts:L14-L24](examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-delete-todo-when-edited-to-empty.spec.ts#L14-L24)\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-edit-todo-by-double-clicking.spec.ts:L18-L22](examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-edit-todo-by-double-clicking.spec.ts#L18-L22)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-save-edit-on-blur.spec.ts:L15-L20](examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-save-edit-on-blur.spec.ts#L15-L20)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-cancel-edit-on-escape.spec.ts:L21-L27](examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-cancel-edit-on-escape.spec.ts#L21-L27)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-delete-todo-when-edited-to-empty.spec.ts:L18-L24](examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-delete-todo-when-edited-to-empty.spec.ts#L18-L24)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-trim-whitespace-when-editing.spec.ts:L14-L25](examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-trim-whitespace-when-editing.spec.ts#L14-L25)\n\n## Deleting Todos: Single, Targeted, and Bulk\n\nDeletion uses two mechanisms: the per-row `Delete` button (`getByRole('button', { name: 'Delete' })`, reached by hovering the row) and the `Clear completed` button (`getByRole('button', { name: 'Clear completed' })`).\n\nIn `should-delete-single-todo.spec.ts` the hover is explicit — `page.getByTestId('todo-item').hover()` — followed by an assertion that the `Delete` button is visible, then a click and two negative assertions on the text and the `todo-item` test id. The counter check `1 item left` is asserted before deletion.\n\nIn `should-delete-specific-todo-from-multiple.spec.ts` three todos are created and the counter is checked as `3 items left`. The target row is isolated with `page.getByRole('listitem').filter({ hasText: 'Task 2' }).hover()`, then `Delete` is clicked; the test verifies `Task 1` and `Task 3` remain visible and the counter reads `2 items left`.\n\nBulk deletion in `should-clear-all-completed-todos.spec.ts` first verifies the list structurally with an ARIA snapshot rather than individual text checks:\n\n```ts\nawait expect(page.locator('body')).toMatchAriaSnapshot(`\n- list:\n  - listitem: \"Task 1\"\n  - listitem: \"Task 2\"\n  - listitem: \"Task 3\"\n`);\n```\n\nCompletion is toggled per row using `page.getByRole('listitem').filter({ hasText: 'Task 1' }).getByLabel('Toggle Todo').click()` (and the same for `Task 3`). After those toggles the test expects `1 item left` and a visible `Clear completed` button, then clicks it and asserts that only `Task 2` remains while the counter still shows `1 item left`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/deleting-todos/should-delete-single-todo.spec.ts:L8-L23](examples/todomvc/tests/deleting-todos/should-delete-single-todo.spec.ts#L8-L23)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/deleting-todos/should-delete-specific-todo-from-multiple.spec.ts:L8-L30](examples/todomvc/tests/deleting-todos/should-delete-specific-todo-from-multiple.spec.ts#L8-L30)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/deleting-todos/should-clear-all-completed-todos.spec.ts:L8-L32](examples/todomvc/tests/deleting-todos/should-clear-all-completed-todos.spec.ts#L8-L32)\n\n## Assertion Conventions\n\n| Intent | Matcher shown in the sources |\n| --- | --- |\n| Element rendered | `await expect(locator).toBeVisible()` |\n| Element removed | `await expect(locator).not.toBeVisible()` |\n| Editor pre-filled | `await expect(locator).toHaveValue('Buy milk')` |\n| Editor keeps raw input | `await expect(locator).toHaveValue('   Edited task   ')` |\n| Whole list shape | `await expect(page.locator('body')).toMatchAriaSnapshot(...)` |\n| Item counter | `await expect(page.getByText('1 item left')).toBeVisible()` |\n\nNote that visibility, not count, is the criterion for removal in the deletion tests; the counter text (`1 item left`, `2 items left`, `3 items left`) is used as a secondary signal of list state.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-trim-whitespace-when-editing.spec.ts:L18-L25](examples/todomvc/tests/editing-todos/should-trim-whitespace-when-editing.spec.ts#L18-L25)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/deleting-todos/should-delete-specific-todo-from-multiple.spec.ts:L16-L29](examples/todomvc/tests/deleting-todos/should-delete-specific-todo-from-multiple.spec.ts#L16-L29)\n- [examples/todomvc/tests/deleting-todos/should-clear-all-completed-todos.spec.ts:L15-L26](examples/todomvc/tests/deleting-todos/should-clear-all-completed-todos.spec.ts#L15-L26)\n"}, {"path": "examples/webauthn.md", "title": "WebAuthn Example", "parent": null, "description": "Example demonstrating WebAuthn credential registration and authentication flows.", "files": ["examples/webauthn/register-and-login.mjs", "examples/webauthn/seed-credential.mjs"], "markdown": "# WebAuthn Example\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [examples/webauthn/register-and-login.mjs](examples/webauthn/register-and-login.mjs)\n- [examples/webauthn/seed-credential.mjs](examples/webauthn/seed-credential.mjs)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nPlaywright supports WebAuthn credential registration and authentication flows through the `context.credentials` API. The examples in `examples/webauthn/` demonstrate two patterns: a complete interactive registration and login cycle, and a credential seeding workflow that reuses registered credentials across browser contexts.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/webauthn/register-and-login.mjs:L1-L5](examples/webauthn/register-and-login.mjs#L1-L5)\n- [examples/webauthn/seed-credential.mjs:L1-L7](examples/webauthn/seed-credential.mjs#L1-L7)\n\n## Interactive Registration and Login\n\nThe `register-and-login.mjs` example walks through a complete WebAuthn flow on [webauthn.io](https://webauthn.io/). It launches a Chromium browser in headed mode with a 250ms slowdown to observe each step, creates a new browser context, and installs credential support via `context.credentials.install()`. A unique email address is generated using the current timestamp and filled into the registration form. After clicking the register button, the script waits for the success confirmation message, then retrieves the registered credential using `context.credentials.get()` to log the credential ID and relying party ID. The authentication step follows: the login button is clicked, and the script waits for the confirmation that the user is logged in, verifying that Playwright successfully participated in the authentication ceremony.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/webauthn/register-and-login.mjs:L4-L18](examples/webauthn/register-and-login.mjs#L4-L18)\n\n## Credential Seeding Across Contexts\n\nThe `seed-credential.mjs` example demonstrates a two-context pattern for testing scenarios where credentials must be reused. Context A performs the initial registration, storing a credential server-side on webauthn.io. After retrieving the registered credential with `context.credentials.get()`, Context A is closed. Context B then installs credentials and seeds the same credential using `context.credentials.create(registered)` before navigating to the site. Since webauthn.io performs a discoverable credential lookup on page load, the seeded credential satisfies the request automatically, logging the user in without any UI interaction required.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant ContextA as Context A\n    participant Site as webauthn.io\n    participant ContextB as Context B\n    \n    ContextA->>Site: Register credential\n    Site->>Site: Store credential server-side\n    ContextA->>ContextA: Retrieve credential\n    ContextA->>ContextB: Pass credential\n    ContextB->>ContextB: Seed credential via create()\n    ContextB->>Site: Navigate to home page\n    Site->>ContextB: Request discoverable credential\n    ContextB->>Site: Provide seeded credential\n    Site->>Site: Authenticate user\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [examples/webauthn/seed-credential.mjs:L14-L35](examples/webauthn/seed-credential.mjs#L14-L35)\n\n## Credential API Methods\n\nThree credential methods appear in these examples:\n\n| Method | Purpose |\n|--------|---------|\n| `context.credentials.install()` | Enable WebAuthn support in the browser context |\n| `context.credentials.get()` | Retrieve registered credentials from the context |\n| `context.credentials.create(credential)` | Seed a pre-existing credential into a fresh context |\n\nThe `install()` method must be called on a context before any WebAuthn operations. The `get()` method returns an array of credentials registered during the context's lifetime. The `create()` method accepts a credential object (typically obtained from a prior `get()` call) and makes it available for future authentication flows without re-registering.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [examples/webauthn/register-and-login.mjs:L6-L14](examples/webauthn/register-and-login.mjs#L6-L14)\n- [examples/webauthn/seed-credential.mjs:L31-L32](examples/webauthn/seed-credential.mjs#L31-L32)\n"}, {"path": "docs/getting-started/installation.md", "title": "Installation and First Test", "parent": null, "description": "Installing Playwright for each supported language, using the VS Code extension, canary releases, and the language and browser support matrix.", "files": ["docs/src/intro-js.md", "docs/src/intro-python.md", "docs/src/intro-csharp.md", "docs/src/intro-java.md", "docs/src/languages.md", "docs/src/getting-started-vscode-js.md", "docs/src/canary-releases-js.md"], "markdown": "# Installation and First Test\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [docs/src/intro-js.md](docs/src/intro-js.md)\n- [docs/src/intro-python.md](docs/src/intro-python.md)\n- [docs/src/intro-csharp.md](docs/src/intro-csharp.md)\n- [docs/src/intro-java.md](docs/src/intro-java.md)\n- [docs/src/languages.md](docs/src/languages.md)\n- [docs/src/getting-started-vscode-js.md](docs/src/getting-started-vscode-js.md)\n- [docs/src/canary-releases-js.md](docs/src/canary-releases-js.md)\n</cite>\n\n## Language and Platform Support\n\nPlaywright ships in several languages that share the same underlying implementation. All core browser automation features are available in every language; what differs is the testing ecosystem integration. `docs/src/languages.md` recommends picking the language based on experience and project constraints, and using the test runner recommended for that language.\n\n| Language | Recommended test integration | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| JavaScript / TypeScript | Playwright's own test runner | Parallelization, screenshot assertions, HTML reporter, automatic tracing |\n| Python | Playwright Pytest plugin | Context isolation and multiple browser configurations out of the box |\n| Java | Any framework such as JUnit or TestNG | Distributed as Maven modules |\n| .NET | MSTest, NUnit, xUnit, xUnit v3 base classes | Also usable as a library with manual infrastructure |\n\nEvery language targets the same engines: Chromium, WebKit and Firefox on Windows, Linux and macOS, locally or in CI, headless or headed, with native mobile emulation for Chrome (Android) and Mobile Safari.\n\nSystem requirements, as listed per language:\n\n| Requirement | JavaScript | Python | Java | .NET |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Runtime | Node.js latest 22.x, 24.x or 26.x | Python 3.8 or higher | Java 8 or higher | .NET Standard 2.0 library; .NET 8 recommended |\n\nAll languages list the same operating systems: Windows 11+, Windows Server 2019+ or Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL); macOS 14 (Sonoma) or later; Debian 12 / 13, Ubuntu 22.04 / 24.04 / 26.04 (x86-64 or arm64).\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/languages.md:L6-L36](docs/src/languages.md#L6-L36)\n- [docs/src/intro-js.md:L303-L308](docs/src/intro-js.md#L303-L308)\n- [docs/src/intro-python.md:L146-L151](docs/src/intro-python.md#L146-L151)\n- [docs/src/intro-java.md:L129-L134](docs/src/intro-java.md#L129-L134)\n- [docs/src/intro-csharp.md:L284-L289](docs/src/intro-csharp.md#L284-L289)\n\n## Installing Playwright for JavaScript and TypeScript\n\nThe installer both initializes a new project and adds Playwright to an existing one.\n\n```bash\nnpm init playwright@latest\n```\n\n```bash\nyarn create playwright\n```\n\n```bash\npnpm create playwright\n```\n\nWhen prompted, choose or confirm: TypeScript or JavaScript (default: TypeScript); the tests folder name (default `tests`, or `e2e` if `tests` already exists); whether to add a GitHub Actions workflow; and whether to install Playwright browsers (default: yes). The command can be re-run later and does not overwrite existing tests.\n\nInstallation downloads the required browser binaries and produces this scaffold:\n\n```bash\nplaywright.config.ts         # Test configuration\npackage.json\npackage-lock.json            # Or yarn.lock / pnpm-lock.yaml\ntests/\n  example.spec.ts            # Minimal example test\n```\n\n`playwright.config.ts` centralizes target browsers, timeouts, retries, projects and reporters. By default the example tests run headless and in parallel across Chromium, Firefox and WebKit:\n\n```bash\nnpx playwright test\n```\n\nThe equivalent invocations are `yarn playwright test` and `pnpm exec playwright test`. Useful flags documented for this command are `--headed`, `--project=chromium` and `--ui`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[Run installer] --> B[Download browser binaries]\n    B --> C[Create config and tests scaffold]\n    C --> D[Run example test]\n    D --> E[Open HTML report or UI mode]\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [docs/src/intro-js.md:L17-L90](docs/src/intro-js.md#L17-L90)\n- [docs/src/intro-js.md:L137-L180](docs/src/intro-js.md#L137-L180)\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/intro-js.md:L17-L90](docs/src/intro-js.md#L17-L90)\n- [docs/src/intro-js.md:L129-L135](docs/src/intro-js.md#L129-L135)\n\n## Installing Playwright for Python\n\nPlaywright recommends the official Playwright Pytest plugin for end-to-end tests, because it provides context isolation and runs on multiple browser configurations out of the box.\n\n```bash\npip install pytest-playwright\n```\n\n```bash\npoetry add pytest-playwright\n```\n\n```bash\nuv add pytest-playwright\n```\n\nThen install the required browsers:\n\n```bash\nplaywright install\n```\n\nThe example test goes in a file following the `test_` prefix convention, such as `test_example.py`, with test names also prefixed by `test_`:\n\n```py title=\"test_example.py\"\nimport re\nfrom playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\ndef test_has_title(page: Page):\n    page.goto(\"https://playwright.dev/\")\n\n    # Expect a title \"to contain\" a substring.\n    expect(page).to_have_title(re.compile(\"Playwright\"))\n```\n\nTests run on Chromium by default, headless, with results shown in the terminal:\n\n```bash\npytest\n```\n\nStarting with Playwright 1.65, the bundled Node.js 26 requires `libatomic.so.1` on Linux. Minimal images, including `python:*-slim`, may not include it, so on Debian and Ubuntu it must be installed as root before running Playwright commands. The documentation notes that `playwright install --with-deps` cannot install this library if the bundled Node.js cannot start.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/intro-python.md:L18-L26](docs/src/intro-python.md#L18-L26)\n- [docs/src/intro-python.md:L28-L80](docs/src/intro-python.md#L28-L80)\n- [docs/src/intro-python.md:L102-L108](docs/src/intro-python.md#L102-L108)\n\n## Installing Playwright for Java and .NET\n\nJava distributes Playwright as Maven modules; a single dependency on `com.microsoft.playwright:playwright` in `pom.xml` is enough. Running the example compiles and executes the program:\n\n```bash\nmvn compile exec:java -D exec.mainClass=\"org.example.App\"\n```\n\nRunning it downloads the Playwright package and installs browser binaries for Chromium, Firefox and WebKit. Browsers launch headless by default; passing `new BrowserType.LaunchOptions().setHeadless(false)` shows the browser UI, and `setSlowMo` slows execution down.\n\nFor .NET, the documented flow is: create a project with `dotnet new` for the chosen runner (MSTest, NUnit, xUnit or xUnit v3), add the matching package, build, then install browsers.\n\n```bash\ndotnet new mstest -n PlaywrightTests\ncd PlaywrightTests\n```\n\n```bash\ndotnet add package Microsoft.Playwright.MSTest\n```\n\n```bash\ndotnet build\n```\n\n```bash\npwsh bin/Debug/net8.0/playwright.ps1 install\n```\n\nThe `net8.0` segment must be adjusted for a different .NET version, and `pwsh` requires installing PowerShell if unavailable. The equivalent packages are `Microsoft.Playwright.NUnit`, `Microsoft.Playwright.Xunit` and `Microsoft.Playwright.Xunit.v3`. Example tests derive from `PageTest`, and run with:\n\n```bash\ndotnet test\n```\n\nTests run on Chromium by default, configurable via the `BROWSER` environment variable or launch configuration options.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/intro-java.md:L10-L91](docs/src/intro-java.md#L10-L91)\n- [docs/src/intro-java.md:L115-L127](docs/src/intro-java.md#L115-L127)\n- [docs/src/intro-csharp.md:L12-L112](docs/src/intro-csharp.md#L12-L112)\n- [docs/src/intro-csharp.md:L274-L282](docs/src/intro-csharp.md#L274-L282)\n\n## Using the VS Code Extension\n\nThe VS Code extension brings Playwright Test into the editor for running, debugging and generating tests. Prerequisites are Node.js (LTS recommended) and Visual Studio Code.\n\n1. Install the extension from the Extensions view (`Ctrl+Shift+X` or `Cmd+Shift+X`) by searching for \"Playwright\" and installing the official Microsoft extension.\n2. Open the Command Palette (`Ctrl+Shift+P` or `Cmd+Shift+P`) and run **Test: Install Playwright**.\n3. Select the browsers for your tests (e.g. Chromium, Firefox, WebKit) and optionally add a GitHub Actions workflow for CI; these settings can be changed later in `playwright.config.ts`.\n\nThe Testing icon in the Activity Bar opens the Test Explorer along with the Playwright sidebar for managing projects, tools and settings. From there the quick reference maps actions to controls:\n\n| Action | How to do it in VS Code |\n| --- | --- |\n| Install Playwright | Command Palette → `Test: Install Playwright` |\n| Run a Test | Click the \"play\" icon next to the test |\n| Debug a Test | Set a breakpoint, right-click the test → `Debug Test` |\n| Show Live Browser | Enable `Show Browsers` in the Playwright sidebar |\n| Record a New Test | Click `Record new` in the Playwright sidebar |\n| Pick a Locator | Click `Pick locator` in the Playwright sidebar |\n| View Test Trace | Enable `Show Trace Viewer` in the Playwright sidebar |\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/getting-started-vscode-js.md:L17-L45](docs/src/getting-started-vscode-js.md#L17-L45)\n- [docs/src/getting-started-vscode-js.md:L156-L166](docs/src/getting-started-vscode-js.md#L156-L166)\n\n## Canary Releases\n\nPlaywright for Node.js publishes canary releases daily on the `next` NPM tag, allowing new unreleased features to be tested before a full release. The documentation frames this as a way to give feedback to maintainers and confirm a newly implemented feature works as intended. A canary release passes all automated tests and is used to test the HTML report, Trace Viewer and Playwright Inspector with end-to-end tests.\n\n```bash\nnpm install -D @playwright/test@next\n```\n\nThe npm dist tags are `latest` for stable releases, `next` for daily releases, and `beta`, which receives every commit after a release branch is cut, usually about a week before a stable release. The `next` documentation is available on playwright.dev by pressing <kbd>Shift</kbd> five times.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/canary-releases-js.md:L6-L36](docs/src/canary-releases-js.md#L6-L36)\n"}, {"path": "docs/getting-started/library-and-releases.md", "title": "Library Mode, API Testing, and Release Notes", "parent": null, "description": "Using Playwright as a plain automation library in JS, Python, and C#, API-only testing guides, and per-language release notes.", "files": ["docs/src/library-js.md", "docs/src/library-python.md", "docs/src/library-csharp.md", "docs/src/api-testing-js.md", "docs/src/api-testing-python.md", "docs/src/api-testing-csharp.md", "docs/src/api-testing-java.md", "docs/src/release-notes-js.md", "docs/src/release-notes-python.md"], "markdown": "# Library Mode, API Testing, and Release Notes\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [docs/src/library-js.md](docs/src/library-js.md)\n- [docs/src/library-python.md](docs/src/library-python.md)\n- [docs/src/library-csharp.md](docs/src/library-csharp.md)\n- [docs/src/api-testing-js.md](docs/src/api-testing-js.md)\n- [docs/src/api-testing-python.md](docs/src/api-testing-python.md)\n- [docs/src/api-testing-csharp.md](docs/src/api-testing-csharp.md)\n- [docs/src/api-testing-java.md](docs/src/api-testing-java.md)\n- [docs/src/release-notes-js.md](docs/src/release-notes-js.md)\n- [docs/src/release-notes-python.md](docs/src/release-notes-python.md)\n</cite>\n\n## Library Mode vs. Playwright Test\n\nPlaywright Library provides unified APIs for launching and interacting with browsers, while Playwright Test adds a managed end-to-end test runner on top. The JavaScript guide states that for end-to-end testing you will normally want `@playwright/test` rather than the `playwright` library directly.\n\nThe library example launches Chromium, creates a context with `devices['iPhone 11']`, aborts `**.jpg` routes, navigates to `https://example.com/`, and asserts the title with `node:assert` — explicitly labeled as \"not a Web First assertion.\" The equivalent test uses `test.use(devices['iPhone 11'])` and `expect(page).toHaveTitle(...)`, run with `npx playwright test`.\n\nThe documented differences are summarized in the guide:\n\n| Concern | Library | Test |\n| - | - | - |\n| Installation | `npm install playwright` | `npm init playwright@latest` |\n| Browser install | `@playwright/browser-chromium`, `-firefox`, `-webkit` packages | `npx playwright install` |\n| Import from | `playwright` | `@playwright/test` |\n| Initialization | Pick browser, `launch()`, `newContext()`, `newPage()` | Isolated `page` and `context` fixtures |\n| Assertions | No built-in Web-First assertions | Auto-waiting assertions such as `toHaveTitle` |\n| Timeouts | 30s default for most operations | Per-test timeout (30s default) |\n| Cleanup | Explicit `context.close()` and `browser.close()` | Runner closes fixtures |\n| Running | Node script, possibly compiled | `npx playwright test` |\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/library-js.md:L6-L10](docs/src/library-js.md#L6-L10)\n- [docs/src/library-js.md:L16-L95](docs/src/library-js.md#L16-L95)\n- [docs/src/library-js.md:L97-L120](docs/src/library-js.md#L97-L120)\n\n## Running the Library in JavaScript, Python, and C#\n\nThe JavaScript library is installed with `npm i -D playwright`, and browsers with `npx playwright install chromium firefox webkit` or the `@playwright/browser-*` helper packages. The guide documents `HTTPS_PROXY` for proxy downloads, `PLAYWRIGHT_DOWNLOAD_HOST` for an internal artifact repository, and `PLAYWRIGHT_SKIP_BROWSER_DOWNLOAD=1` to skip downloads. Scripts are plain Node programs:\n\n```js\nconst { webkit } = require('playwright');\n\n(async () => {\n  const browser = await webkit.launch();\n  const page = await browser.newPage();\n  await page.goto('https://playwright.dev/');\n  await page.screenshot({ path: `example.png` });\n  await browser.close();\n})();\n```\n\nTypeScript is supported out of the box; JavaScript files can opt into checking with `// @ts-check` or JSDoc types such as `/** @type {import('playwright').Page} */`. Scripts can be recorded with `npx playwright codegen wikipedia.org`.\n\nPython supports both synchronous and asynchronous APIs (`sync_playwright` and `async_playwright`), installed via pip, Poetry, or uv followed by `playwright install`. The guide also covers an interactive REPL, PyInstaller bundling with `PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH=0 playwright install chromium` and `pyinstaller -F main.py`, and known issues: prefer `page.wait_for_timeout(5000)` over `time.sleep(5)`, `ProactorEventLoop` is required on Windows, the API is not thread-safe, and cancelling an asyncio task running a Playwright call is unsupported.\n\nC# uses a console project with `dotnet add package Microsoft.Playwright`, browser installation through `pwsh bin/Debug/netX/playwright.ps1 install`, and `dotnet run`. Web-first assertions are available with your own framework via `using static Microsoft.Playwright.Assertions;` and `SetDefaultExpectTimeout(10_000)`. Driver bundling for additional platforms is controlled by `PlaywrightPlatform` (`all`, `none`, `linux`, `win`, `osx`).\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/library-js.md:L122-L183](docs/src/library-js.md#L122-L183)\n- [docs/src/library-js.md:L185-L207](docs/src/library-js.md#L185-L207)\n- [docs/src/library-js.md:L303-L327](docs/src/library-js.md#L303-L327)\n- [docs/src/library-python.md:L6-L107](docs/src/library-python.md#L6-L107)\n- [docs/src/library-python.md:L109-L186](docs/src/library-python.md#L109-L186)\n- [docs/src/library-python.md:L188-L207](docs/src/library-python.md#L188-L207)\n- [docs/src/library-csharp.md:L10-L59](docs/src/library-csharp.md#L10-L59)\n- [docs/src/library-csharp.md:L61-L96](docs/src/library-csharp.md#L61-L96)\n\n## API-Only Testing with APIRequestContext\n\nPlaywright can access an application's REST API without loading a page, using `APIRequestContext` methods. The documented use cases are testing server APIs, preparing server state before visiting the web app, and validating post-conditions after browser actions.\n\nIn JavaScript, `playwright.config.ts` supplies `baseURL` and `extraHTTPHeaders`, including an `Authorization` token from `process.env.API_TOKEN`; proxy settings placed in `use` are picked up by the `request` fixture. Tests post to `/repos/${USER}/${REPO}/issues` and verify results with `request.get(...)` and `expect(await issues.json())`. Repository creation and deletion are handled in `test.beforeAll` and `test.afterAll`.\n\nFor more control, `request.newContext({ baseURL: 'https://api.github.com' })` creates a standalone context. Two kinds of contexts exist: one associated with a `BrowserContext` (via `context.request` / `page.request`), which populates the `Cookie` header and updates browser cookies from `Set-Cookie`, and an isolated instance from `APIRequest.newContext`, which keeps its own cookie storage. `APIRequestContext.storageState` exports authentication state that can initialize a new `BrowserContext` with `storageState`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"playwright.request.newContext()\"] --> B[\"Isolated APIRequestContext\"]\n  C[\"BrowserContext\"] --> D[\"context.request / page.request\"]\n  D --> E[\"Uses and updates browser cookies\"]\n  B --> F[\"storageState export\"]\n  F --> G[\"Browser.newContext({ storageState })\"]\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [docs/src/api-testing-js.md:L133-L169](docs/src/api-testing-js.md#L133-L169)\n- [docs/src/api-testing-js.md:L273-L298](docs/src/api-testing-js.md#L273-L298)\n- [docs/src/api-testing-js.md:L300-L383](docs/src/api-testing-js.md#L300-L383)\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/api-testing-js.md:L6-L65](docs/src/api-testing-js.md#L6-L65)\n- [docs/src/api-testing-js.md:L67-L131](docs/src/api-testing-js.md#L67-L131)\n- [docs/src/api-testing-js.md:L171-L271](docs/src/api-testing-js.md#L171-L271)\n\n## Language-Specific API Test Setup\n\nEach language guide adapts the same GitHub API scenario to its test framework.\n\n| Language | Setup mechanism | Context creation |\n| - | - | - |\n| JavaScript | `request` fixture, or `request.newContext()` | `playwright.request.newContext` / `request.newContext` |\n| Python | `pytest-playwright` fixtures | `playwright.request.new_context(base_url=..., extra_http_headers=...)` |\n| C# | MSTest, NUnit, xUnit, xUnit v3 base classes | `Playwright.APIRequest.NewContextAsync(new() { BaseURL, ExtraHTTPHeaders })` |\n| Java | JUnit 5 `@BeforeAll` / `@AfterAll` | `playwright.request().newContext(new APIRequest.NewContextOptions()...)` |\n\nPython defines a session-scoped `api_request_context` fixture that yields the context and calls `dispose()`, plus an autouse `create_test_repository` fixture that posts `/user/repos` before `yield` and deletes the repo afterwards. Assertions use `assert new_issue.ok`, and UI checks use `expect(first_issue).to_have_text(...)`.\n\nC# uses `[TestInitialize]`/`[TestCleanup]`, `[SetUp]`/`[TearDown]`, or `InitializeAsync`/`DisposeAsync` overrides depending on the runner, with `Expect(newIssue).ToBeOKAsync()` for response checks and `System.Text.Json` for parsing.\n\nJava creates Playwright and the request context in `@BeforeAll`, disposes them in `@AfterAll`, and sends data with `RequestOptions.create().setData(data)`. Responses are asserted with `assertTrue(newIssue.ok())` and parsed through Gson.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/api-testing-python.md:L19-L62](docs/src/api-testing-python.md#L19-L62)\n- [docs/src/api-testing-python.md:L115-L132](docs/src/api-testing-python.md#L115-L132)\n- [docs/src/api-testing-python.md:L226-L288](docs/src/api-testing-python.md#L226-L288)\n- [docs/src/api-testing-csharp.md:L19-L87](docs/src/api-testing-csharp.md#L19-L87)\n- [docs/src/api-testing-csharp.md:L574-L636](docs/src/api-testing-csharp.md#L574-L636)\n- [docs/src/api-testing-java.md:L19-L96](docs/src/api-testing-java.md#L19-L96)\n- [docs/src/api-testing-java.md:L192-L228](docs/src/api-testing-java.md#L192-L228)\n\n## Per-Language Release Notes\n\nRelease notes are maintained per language and use `toc_max_heading_level: 2`. Version 1.65 of the JavaScript notes introduces running tests with Bun through `bunx --bun playwright test`, noting that without `--bun` the command runs on Node.js. Under Bun there is no watch mode, `--tsconfig` and JSX options have no effect, custom ESM loaders are unsupported, and stack traces omit async frames.\n\nThe JS 1.65 notes also add a `failures` reporter, an `onlyFailures` option for the HTML, JSON and JUnit reporters, the built-in `-exclusive-` test lock, and `PLAYWRIGHT_TRANSFORM_CACHE_DIR`. Breaking changes include `useDefineForClassFields` following `tsconfig.json`, actions waiting for elements to be \"not inert,\" RFC 6265 cookie matching, proxy credentials no longer sent to websites, `params` appended to existing query strings, and case-insensitive `application/json` handling in `APIRequestContext`. Playwright no longer tests on Node.js 20.\n\nPython 1.65 adds Python-code output from `Locator.normalize` (for example `get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Submit\")`), a `CDPSession.send` timeout option, the same inert-element, cookie, proxy, accessible-name and `params` breaking changes, and bundles Node.js 26 instead of 24 — requiring `libatomic.so.1` on Linux, including `python:*-slim` images.\n\nBoth 1.65 notes list Chromium 158.0.8127.18, Mozilla Firefox 159.0 and WebKit 27.2, tested against Google Chrome 157 and Microsoft Edge 156.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/release-notes-js.md:L1-L62](docs/src/release-notes-js.md#L1-L62)\n- [docs/src/release-notes-js.md:L68-L117](docs/src/release-notes-js.md#L68-L117)\n- [docs/src/release-notes-python.md:L1-L50](docs/src/release-notes-python.md#L1-L50)\n"}, {"path": "docs/core-concepts/browsers-and-contexts.md", "title": "Browsers, Contexts, Pages, and Events", "parent": null, "description": "The object hierarchy from browser to context to page to frame, plus the event model for observing browser activity.", "files": ["docs/src/browsers.md", "docs/src/browser-contexts.md", "docs/src/pages.md", "docs/src/frames.md", "docs/src/events.md"], "markdown": "# Browsers, Contexts, Pages, and Events\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [docs/src/browsers.md](docs/src/browsers.md)\n- [docs/src/browser-contexts.md](docs/src/browser-contexts.md)\n- [docs/src/pages.md](docs/src/pages.md)\n- [docs/src/frames.md](docs/src/frames.md)\n- [docs/src/events.md](docs/src/events.md)\n</cite>\n\n## The Object Hierarchy\n\nPlaywright's API is a chain of nested objects. A browser type launches a `Browser`; a browser hosts one or more isolated `BrowserContext`s; a context hosts one or more `Page`s; and a page owns a main `Frame` plus any additional frames attached through `iframe` tags.\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n  BT[\"BrowserType<br/>chromium / firefox / webkit\"] --> B[\"Browser\"]\n  B --> C1[\"BrowserContext\"]\n  B --> C2[\"BrowserContext\"]\n  C1 --> P1[\"Page (tab or popup)\"]\n  C1 --> P2[\"Page (tab or popup)\"]\n  P1 --> F1[\"Frame (main)\"]\n  P1 --> F2[\"Frame (iframe)\"]\n```\n\nEach level has a stated responsibility in the docs. Contexts are \"equivalent to incognito-like profiles\" that are \"fast and cheap to create and are completely isolated, even when running in a single browser\". A page \"refers to a single tab or a popup window within a browser context\". A page \"can have one or more `Frame` objects attached to it. Each page has a main frame and page-level interactions (like `click`) are assumed to operate in the main frame.\"\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [docs/src/browser-contexts.md:L8-L12](docs/src/browser-contexts.md#L8-L12)\n- [docs/src/browser-contexts.md:L42-L46](docs/src/browser-contexts.md#L42-L46)\n- [docs/src/pages.md:L6-L9](docs/src/pages.md#L6-L9)\n- [docs/src/frames.md:L6-L12](docs/src/frames.md#L6-L12)\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/browser-contexts.md:L6-L12](docs/src/browser-contexts.md#L6-L12)\n- [docs/src/pages.md:L6-L9](docs/src/pages.md#L6-L9)\n- [docs/src/frames.md:L6-L12](docs/src/frames.md#L6-L12)\n\n## Browsers\n\nEach Playwright version \"needs specific versions of browser binaries to operate\", installed through the CLI; running it without arguments installs the default browsers.\n\n```bash\nnpx playwright install\n```\n\nA browser is obtained from a browser type, then used to create contexts or pages directly:\n\n```java\nBrowser browser = playwright.chromium().launch();\nPage page = browser.newPage();\n```\n\nPlaywright can run against bundled Chromium, Firefox and WebKit, or against branded Chromium-based browsers by setting `channel`. The documented channel values are `chrome`, `msedge`, `chrome-beta`, `msedge-beta`, `chrome-dev`, `msedge-dev`, `chrome-canary` and `msedge-canary`.\n\n| Channel values | Browser family |\n| --- | --- |\n| `chrome`, `chrome-beta`, `chrome-dev`, `chrome-canary` | Google Chrome |\n| `msedge`, `msedge-beta`, `msedge-dev`, `msedge-canary` | Microsoft Edge |\n\nThe `'chromium'` channel opts into the new headless mode. When the `channel` option is not specified, tests run in the chromium headless shell. In the JS test runner, browsers are selected through **projects**, and `--project` restricts a run to one of them.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/browsers.md:L8-L17](docs/src/browsers.md#L8-L17)\n- [docs/src/browsers.md:L158-L205](docs/src/browsers.md#L158-L205)\n- [docs/src/browsers.md:L223-L231](docs/src/browsers.md#L223-L231)\n- [docs/src/browsers.md:L277-L285](docs/src/browsers.md#L277-L285)\n- [docs/src/browsers.md:L341-L345](docs/src/browsers.md#L341-L345)\n- [docs/src/browsers.md:L367-L373](docs/src/browsers.md#L367-L373)\n- [docs/src/browsers.md:L444-L448](docs/src/browsers.md#L444-L448)\n- [docs/src/browsers.md:L514-L526](docs/src/browsers.md#L514-L526)\n\n## Contexts and Isolation\n\nA `BrowserContext` is the unit of isolation. Every test gets its own context, so each test has \"its own local storage, session storage, cookies etc.\" When using Playwright as a test runner, contexts are created by default; otherwise they are created manually:\n\n```js\nconst browser = await chromium.launch();\nconst context = await browser.newContext();\nconst page = await context.newPage();\n```\n\nIsolation by fresh context is preferred over cleanup between tests, because \"some things are impossible to clean up such as 'visited links'\", and starting from scratch means a failure \"you only have to look within that test to debug.\"\n\nMultiple contexts can coexist in one scenario, which is how multi-user flows such as chat are tested: two contexts are created from the same browser and pages are driven independently. Pages inside a context also \"respect context-level emulation, like viewport sizes, custom network routes or browser locale\", so emulation settings belong to the context rather than the page.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/browser-contexts.md:L8-L26](docs/src/browser-contexts.md#L8-L26)\n- [docs/src/browser-contexts.md:L42-L46](docs/src/browser-contexts.md#L42-L46)\n- [docs/src/browser-contexts.md:L75-L91](docs/src/browser-contexts.md#L75-L91)\n- [docs/src/pages.md:L84-L89](docs/src/pages.md#L84-L89)\n\n## Pages\n\nA page is where navigation and interaction happen. It is created from a context and used to navigate explicitly (`goto`) or implicitly by clicking a link, after which `page.url()` reflects the new location.\n\nA single context can host several pages, each behaving \"like a focused, active page\" — bringing a page to front is not required. The pages of a context are enumerated through `context.pages()`:\n\n```js\nconst pageOne = await context.newPage();\nconst pageTwo = await context.newPage();\nconst allPages = context.pages();\n```\n\nWhen a new page is created by an action such as a `target=\"_blank\"` link, the `page` event on the context delivers it. The documented pattern starts waiting *before* the triggering action, noting \"no await\" on the wait call, and resolves the promise afterwards.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/pages.md:L6-L24](docs/src/pages.md#L6-L24)\n- [docs/src/pages.md:L84-L98](docs/src/pages.md#L84-L98)\n- [docs/src/pages.md:L136-L149](docs/src/pages.md#L136-L149)\n\n## Frames\n\nFrames are addressed either through a frame locator or through frame objects. `page.frameLocator('.frame-class')` scopes subsequent locators to the frame's contents, while `page.frame(...)` returns a `Frame` by name attribute or by URL pattern:\n\n```js\nconst frame = page.frame('frame-login');\nconst frame = page.frame({ url: /.*domain.*/ });\nawait frame.fill('#username-input', 'John');\n```\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/frames.md:L6-L18](docs/src/frames.md#L6-L18)\n- [docs/src/frames.md:L45-L58](docs/src/frames.md#L45-L58)\n\n## The Event Model\n\nPlaywright exposes events for \"network requests, creation of child pages, dedicated workers etc.\" Two subscription styles exist: waiting for a specific event, and attaching/removing listeners for events that happen at unpredictable times.\n\nWaiting is used when a known action triggers the event. A request wait is armed before `goto`, and a popup wait before the click that opens it:\n\n```js\n// Start waiting for popup before clicking. Note no await.\nconst popupPromise = page.waitForEvent('popup');\nawait page.getByText('open the popup').click();\nconst popup = await popupPromise;\n```\n\nFor unknown triggers, a persistent listener is registered instead. Listeners can be removed with `off`, and one-off handlers use `once`:\n\n```js\npage.on('request', request => console.log(`Request sent: ${request.url()}`));\npage.off('requestfinished', listener);\npage.once('dialog', dialog => dialog.accept('2021'));\n```\n\nThe two page-related events differ in scope: the `page` event fires on the `BrowserContext` for every new page in that context, while the `popup` event fires on the `Page` and \"is emitted in addition to the `browserContext.on('page')` event, but only for popups relevant to this page.\"\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/events.md:L6-L22](docs/src/events.md#L6-L22)\n- [docs/src/events.md:L53-L61](docs/src/events.md#L53-L61)\n- [docs/src/events.md:L93-L105](docs/src/events.md#L93-L105)\n- [docs/src/events.md:L162-L170](docs/src/events.md#L162-L170)\n- [docs/src/pages.md:L239-L243](docs/src/pages.md#L239-L243)\n\nTogether, these two events let a script follow every page a context creates while also catching only the popups opened by one specific page.\n"}, {"path": "docs/core-concepts/locators-and-actionability.md", "title": "Locators, Actionability, and ARIA Snapshots", "parent": null, "description": "Resilient element selection, alternative locator strategies, the actionability checks before every action, handle APIs, and ARIA snapshot assertions.", "files": ["docs/src/locators.md", "docs/src/other-locators.md", "docs/src/actionability.md", "docs/src/aria-snapshots.md", "docs/src/handles.md"], "markdown": "# Locators, Actionability, and ARIA Snapshots\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [docs/src/locators.md](docs/src/locators.md)\n- [docs/src/other-locators.md](docs/src/other-locators.md)\n- [docs/src/actionability.md](docs/src/actionability.md)\n- [docs/src/aria-snapshots.md](docs/src/aria-snapshots.md)\n- [docs/src/handles.md](docs/src/handles.md)\n</cite>\n\n## Locators as Deferred Queries\n\n`docs/src/locators.md` describes locators as \"the central piece of Playwright's auto-waiting and retry-ability\", representing a way to find element(s) on the page at any moment. A locator is not a frozen reference: every time it is used for an action, an up-to-date DOM element is located, so a re-render between two calls resolves the new element.\n\n```js\nconst locator = page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' });\n\nawait locator.hover();\nawait locator.click();\n```\n\n`docs/src/handles.md` draws the same distinction from the other side: an `ElementHandle` \"points to a particular element, while Locator captures the logic of how to retrieve that element\". A handle obtained with `page.$('text=Submit')` keeps pointing at that very DOM element even after React renders an entirely different component, which the guide calls a source of unexpected behavior. Locator-creation methods such as `page.getByLabel` are also available on `Locator` and `FrameLocator`, so they can be chained to narrow a query iteratively, including into iframes via `frameLocator`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/locators.md:L6-L9](docs/src/locators.md#L6-L9)\n- [docs/src/locators.md:L111-L121](docs/src/locators.md#L111-L121)\n- [docs/src/locators.md:L152-L160](docs/src/locators.md#L152-L160)\n- [docs/src/handles.md:L259-L308](docs/src/handles.md#L259-L308)\n\n## Built-in Locator Strategies\n\nThe quick guide in `docs/src/locators.md` recommends seven built-in locators, and the guide advises prioritizing user-facing attributes and explicit contracts.\n\n| Locator | Locates by |\n| :- | :- |\n| `page.getByRole()` | explicit and implicit accessibility attributes |\n| `page.getByText()` | text content |\n| `page.getByLabel()` | a form control's associated label text |\n| `page.getByPlaceholder()` | an input's placeholder |\n| `page.getByAltText()` | an element's text alternative, usually an image |\n| `page.getByTitle()` | the `title` attribute |\n| `page.getByTestId()` | the `data-testid` attribute (configurable) |\n\nRole locators follow W3C specifications for ARIA role, ARIA attributes and accessible name, and recognize implicitly defined roles such as `<button>`; the guide notes they \"**do not replace** accessibility audits and conformance tests\". Text matching always normalizes whitespace, even with `exact: true`. Test ids are called the most resilient approach because they survive changes to text or role, but they are explicitly not user-facing. The test id attribute can be changed in config with `testIdAttribute: 'data-pw'` or via `Selectors.setTestIdAttribute`. CSS and XPath remain available through `page.locator()`, with prefixes auto-detected, but long structural chains are labeled a **bad practice**. All locators pierce open shadow DOM by default; XPath does not pierce shadow roots, and closed-mode shadow roots are unsupported.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/locators.md:L13-L21](docs/src/locators.md#L13-L21)\n- [docs/src/locators.md:L190-L268](docs/src/locators.md#L190-L268)\n- [docs/src/locators.md:L432-L438](docs/src/locators.md#L432-L438)\n- [docs/src/locators.md:L514-L548](docs/src/locators.md#L514-L548)\n- [docs/src/locators.md:L610-L698](docs/src/locators.md#L610-L698)\n- [docs/src/locators.md:L700-L716](docs/src/locators.md#L700-L716)\n\n## Alternative and Legacy Locators\n\n`docs/src/other-locators.md` collects the remaining strategies. Playwright augments standard CSS with shadow-DOM piercing and custom pseudo-classes: `:has-text()`, `:text()`, `:text-is()`, `:text-matches()`, `:visible`, and `:has()`. `:has-text()` must be combined with another specifier, otherwise it matches many elements including `<body>`. Layout pseudo-classes — `:right-of()`, `:left-of()`, `:above()`, `:below()`, `:near()` — are deprecated and can match a different element when layout shifts by one pixel. `:nth-match()` is one-based, while the `nth=` locator is zero-based and accepts `-1` for the last match.\n\nFor parents, the guide prefers `Locator.filter({ has: child })` over `xpath=..`, which breaks on DOM changes. XPath supports the pipe operator for unions, and any string starting with `//` or `..` is treated as XPath. Label-to-control retargeting lets actions such as `click`, `fill`, `inputValue`, `selectText`, `setInputFiles` and `selectOption` target a label and act on its control, while assertions like `toHaveText` still target the label itself. The legacy `text=` locator remains documented but is superseded by the modern text locator.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/other-locators.md:L15-L57](docs/src/other-locators.md#L15-L57)\n- [docs/src/other-locators.md:L125-L205](docs/src/other-locators.md#L125-L205)\n- [docs/src/other-locators.md:L241-L263](docs/src/other-locators.md#L241-L263)\n- [docs/src/other-locators.md:L323-L448](docs/src/other-locators.md#L323-L448)\n- [docs/src/other-locators.md:L451-L539](docs/src/other-locators.md#L451-L539)\n- [docs/src/other-locators.md:L575-L616](docs/src/other-locators.md#L575-L616)\n\n## Actionability Checks Before Every Action\n\nBefore performing an action, Playwright runs a range of actionability checks, auto-waits for the relevant ones to pass, and only then acts. If a check does not pass within the timeout, the action fails with a `TimeoutError`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[Locator resolves to exactly one element] --> B[Visible]\n    B --> C[Stable]\n    C --> D[Receives Events]\n    D --> E[Enabled]\n    E --> F[Not Inert]\n    F --> G[Action performed]\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [docs/src/actionability.md:L11-L17](docs/src/actionability.md#L11-L17)\n\n| Action | Visible | Stable | Receives Events | Enabled | Editable | Not Inert |\n| :- | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: | :-: |\n| `Locator.click` | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | - | Yes |\n| `Locator.hover` | Yes | Yes | Yes | - | - | - |\n| `Locator.fill` | Yes | - | - | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| `Locator.selectOption` | Yes | - | - | Yes | - | Yes |\n| `Locator.scrollIntoViewIfNeeded` | - | Yes | - | - | - | - |\n| `Locator.press` | - | - | - | - | - | - |\n\nDefinitions are concrete: **visible** means a non-empty bounding box and no `visibility:hidden`, so `opacity:0` still counts as visible while zero-size and `display:none` do not. **Stable** means the same bounding box for at least two consecutive animation frames. **Enabled** excludes elements with `[disabled]`, controls inside a disabled `<fieldset>`, and descendants of `[aria-disabled=true]`. **Editable** requires enabled and not readonly. **Not inert** excludes elements with the `inert` attribute or inside an inert subtree. **Receives Events** means the element is the hit target at the action point. Actions like `click` accept a `force` option that disables non-essential checks, such as whether the target actually receives click events.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/actionability.md:L6-L48](docs/src/actionability.md#L6-L48)\n- [docs/src/actionability.md:L82-L132](docs/src/actionability.md#L82-L132)\n\n## Handles\n\n`docs/src/handles.md` documents two handle types: `JSHandle` references any JavaScript object in the page, and `ElementHandle` references a DOM element with extra methods for actions and property assertions. Since any DOM element is also a JavaScript object, every `ElementHandle` is a `JSHandle`. The guide marks `ElementHandle` as **discouraged**, recommending `Locator` objects and web-first assertions, and reserving handles for extensive DOM traversal on a static page. When one is needed, it should be fetched with `page.waitForSelector()` or `frame.waitForSelector()`, which wait for the element to be attached and visible. Handles can be passed into `page.evaluate()` and similar methods, and they retain their object from garbage collection unless the page navigates or `JSHandle.dispose()` is called.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/handles.md:L6-L20](docs/src/handles.md#L6-L20)\n- [docs/src/handles.md:L53-L127](docs/src/handles.md#L53-L127)\n- [docs/src/handles.md:L129-L154](docs/src/handles.md#L129-L154)\n- [docs/src/handles.md:L235-L242](docs/src/handles.md#L235-L242)\n\n## ARIA Snapshots\n\n`docs/src/aria-snapshots.md` describes aria snapshots as a YAML representation of the accessibility tree, where a template is \"a constraint, not necessarily a complete serialization\". Each node follows `- role \"name\" [attribute=value]`: quoted names are exact, `/patterns/` are regular expressions, and bracketed attributes cover `checked`, `disabled`, `expanded`, `invalid`, `level`, `pressed`, `required` and `selected`, optionally with values such as `[pressed=mixed]`.\n\n```js\nawait expect(page).toMatchAriaSnapshot(`\n  - heading \"title\"\n`);\n```\n\nMatching is case-sensitive, normalizes whitespace within names and text, is order-sensitive, and uses YAML indentation for nesting. A single-node template can match at any depth, while multiple top-level nodes must match under the same parent in order. Omitting an accessible name or an attribute produces a partial match. The `/children` property controls child matching per node: `contain` (default), `equal` for exact direct children, and `deep-equal` for the whole subtree; it can be set globally via `expect.toMatchAriaSnapshot.children`. Snapshots can be generated from the code generator's \"Assert snapshot\" action and \"Aria snapshot\" tab, updated with `npx playwright test --update-snapshots`, emitted on the fly by passing an empty template, or produced programmatically with `page.ariaSnapshot()` and `locator.ariaSnapshot()`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/aria-snapshots.md:L7-L22](docs/src/aria-snapshots.md#L7-L22)\n- [docs/src/aria-snapshots.md:L140-L219](docs/src/aria-snapshots.md#L140-L219)\n- [docs/src/aria-snapshots.md:L222-L347](docs/src/aria-snapshots.md#L222-L347)\n- [docs/src/aria-snapshots.md:L349-L463](docs/src/aria-snapshots.md#L349-L463)\n"}, {"path": "docs/core-concepts/input-and-artifacts.md", "title": "Input, Artifacts, and Page Evaluation", "parent": null, "description": "Keyboard, mouse, and touch input, dialogs and downloads, screenshots and video capture, in-page evaluation, and navigation.", "files": ["docs/src/input.md", "docs/src/touch-events.md", "docs/src/dialogs.md", "docs/src/downloads.md", "docs/src/screenshots.md", "docs/src/videos.md", "docs/src/evaluating.md", "docs/src/navigations.md"], "markdown": "# Input, Artifacts, and Page Evaluation\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [docs/src/input.md](docs/src/input.md)\n- [docs/src/touch-events.md](docs/src/touch-events.md)\n- [docs/src/dialogs.md](docs/src/dialogs.md)\n- [docs/src/downloads.md](docs/src/downloads.md)\n- [docs/src/screenshots.md](docs/src/screenshots.md)\n- [docs/src/videos.md](docs/src/videos.md)\n- [docs/src/evaluating.md](docs/src/evaluating.md)\n- [docs/src/navigations.md](docs/src/navigations.md)\n</cite>\n\n## Pointer Input and Actionability\n\nPointer-driven methods such as `Locator.click` cover the common gestures: double click, right click, modifier-held clicks (`Shift`, `ControlOrMeta`), `hover`, and clicks at an explicit `position` inside the element. Before dispatching, Playwright performs a fixed sequence of checks: waiting for the element in the DOM, waiting for it to be displayed, waiting for it to stop moving (for example until a CSS transition finishes), scrolling it into view, waiting until it receives pointer events at the action point, and retrying if the element is detached along the way.\n\nWhen an app intentionally overlays an element on hover, the actionability checks can be bypassed with `click({ force: true })`. To skip real conditions entirely, `dispatchEvent('click')` triggers the `HTMLElement.click()` behavior instead of a real mouse interaction.\n\nDrag and drop is expressed as `Locator.dragTo`, which hovers the source, presses the left button, moves to the target, and releases. Manual control uses `Locator.hover` with `Mouse.down`, `Mouse.move`, and `Mouse.up`. Pages that rely on `dragover` need at least two mouse moves, so the drop element is hovered twice. Scrolling is normally automatic; explicit control is available through `scrollIntoViewIfNeeded`, `Mouse.wheel`, or `Locator.evaluate`.\n\n```js\nawait page.locator('#item-to-be-dragged').hover();\nawait page.mouse.down();\nawait page.locator('#item-to-drop-at').hover();\nawait page.mouse.up();\n```\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/input.md:L203-L330](docs/src/input.md#L203-L330)\n- [docs/src/input.md:L332-L378](docs/src/input.md#L332-L378)\n- [docs/src/input.md:L705-L774](docs/src/input.md#L705-L774)\n- [docs/src/input.md:L776-L835](docs/src/input.md#L776-L835)\n\n## Keyboard, Text, and Form Input\n\n`Locator.fill` is the primary way to enter text: it focuses the element and fires an `input` event, working for `<input>`, `<textarea>`, and `[contenteditable]`, including date, time, and local datetime inputs. Checkboxes and radio buttons use `Locator.setChecked` (also valid for `[role=checkbox]`), and `<select>` elements use `Locator.selectOption` with option `value` or `label`, including multi-selection.\n\nCharacter-by-character typing is reserved for pages with special keyboard handling: `Locator.pressSequentially` emits `keydown`, `keyup`, and `keypress` and accepts an optional `delay`. Single keystrokes and shortcuts use `Locator.press`, which accepts logical key names such as `Enter`, `ArrowRight`, `F1`–`F12`, `Digit0`–`Digit9`, and `KeyA`–`KeyZ`, plus single characters and the modifiers `Shift`, `Control`, `Alt`, and `Meta`. Case matters: `Shift+A` produces a capital, `Shift-a` behaves as if `CapsLock` were toggled.\n\nFile uploads use `Locator.setInputFiles` on an `input[type=file]`, supporting a single path, an array of paths, a directory, an empty array to clear the selection, or an in-memory payload with `name`, `mimeType`, and `buffer`. For dynamically created inputs, the `Page.fileChooser` event (or `waitForEvent('filechooser')`) yields a `FileChooser` whose `setFiles` performs the upload. Focus events are triggered with `Locator.focus`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/input.md:L10-L201](docs/src/input.md#L10-L201)\n- [docs/src/input.md:L380-L529](docs/src/input.md#L380-L529)\n- [docs/src/input.md:L531-L703](docs/src/input.md#L531-L703)\n\n## Touch Gestures\n\nLegacy touch handling is tested by dispatching `TouchEvent`s manually with `Locator.dispatchEvent`, passing `Touch` points as arguments. Because `dispatchEvent` does not set `Event.isTrusted`, pages that check that flag must have the check disabled during the test.\n\nThe documented patterns are a pan gesture (a single touch point moving in `steps` increments, with `touchstart`, repeated `touchmove`, then `touchend`) and a pinch gesture (two touch points equidistant from the element center moving toward or away from each other). Both examples run under a mobile device profile such as `devices['Pixel 7']`, and both read the element center from `getBoundingClientRect()` or `boundingBox()`. Only `clientX`/`clientY` are initialized in the examples; apps needing `pageX`/`pageY`/`screenX`/`screenY` require those to be set as well.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/touch-events.md:L6-L65](docs/src/touch-events.md#L6-L65)\n- [docs/src/touch-events.md:L308-L379](docs/src/touch-events.md#L308-L379)\n\n## Dialogs and Downloads\n\nDialogs (`alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, and `beforeunload`) are auto-dismissed by default, so no handler is strictly required. A `Page.dialog` listener that is registered must handle the dialog with `accept` or `dismiss`; otherwise the triggering action stalls, because dialogs are modal and block page execution. A listener that only logs the message therefore hangs the click. With no listener at all, dialogs are dismissed automatically. `Page.close` with `runBeforeUnload` runs unload handlers and is the only case where close does not wait for the page to actually close. Print dialogs are asserted by overriding `window.print` with a promise and waiting for it via `waitForFunction`.\n\nDownloads emit `Page.download` for every attachment; files land in a temporary folder that is deleted when the producing browser context closes. `BrowserType.launch` accepts `downloadsPath` to choose the destination. The common pattern starts waiting before the click and then saves with `download.saveAs` plus `suggestedFilename()`; listening to the event alone also works but forks control flow and may let the scenario end mid-download.\n\n```js\nconst downloadPromise = page.waitForEvent('download');\nawait page.getByText('Download file').click();\nconst download = await downloadPromise;\nawait download.saveAs('/path/to/save/at/' + download.suggestedFilename());\n```\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/dialogs.md:L8-L129](docs/src/dialogs.md#L8-L129)\n- [docs/src/dialogs.md:L130-L179](docs/src/dialogs.md#L130-L179)\n- [docs/src/downloads.md:L6-L101](docs/src/downloads.md#L6-L101)\n\n## Screenshots and Video\n\n`page.screenshot({ path: 'screenshot.png' })` writes an image to disk; the same API accepts format, clip area, and quality parameters. `fullPage: true` captures the entire scrollable page. Omitting `path` returns the image as a buffer for post-processing or pixel diffing, and `Locator.screenshot` captures a single element.\n\nVideo recording is configured per context or via the test `video` option, with modes `'off'`, `'on'`, `'retain-on-failure'`, and `'on-first-retry'`; Playwright Test writes files into the output directory, typically `test-results`. Videos are saved when the browser context closes, so manually created contexts must be awaited on `close`. The video size defaults to the viewport scaled down to fit 800x800 and is placed in the top-left corner. Annotations can highlight actions (`show: { actions }`, with `duration` defaulting to 500 ms) or show current test information (`show: { test }` with a `level`). For multi-page scenarios, `page.video().path()` returns the file, which is only available after the page or context is closed.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/screenshots.md:L8-L120](docs/src/screenshots.md#L8-L120)\n- [docs/src/videos.md:L10-L79](docs/src/videos.md#L10-L79)\n- [docs/src/videos.md:L81-L187](docs/src/videos.md#L81-L187)\n\n## In-Page Evaluation and Navigation\n\n`Page.evaluate` runs a function inside the page context and returns the result; browser globals like `window` and `document` are available, and promises or async functions are awaited automatically. Test variables are not visible in the page, so values must be passed explicitly as an argument. The argument may be any mix of serializable values and `JSHandle` instances, which are converted to the values they represent; destructuring works for objects and arrays. `Page.addInitScript` and `BrowserContext.addInitScript` run code before page load, from a script path or an inline function.\n\nNavigation via `page.goto` loads the URL and waits for the `load` event, following client-side redirects before `load`. In Firefox, a page-triggered navigation can interrupt `goto` with `NS_BINDING_ABORTED`, a known limitation. Because pages keep working after `load`, Playwright does not guess readiness; interactions auto-wait for actionability. Poor hydration can make early clicks no-ops, which is why interactive controls should stay disabled until the page is functional. When a click triggers multiple navigations, `Page.waitForURL` targets a specific URL. BFCache is disabled by default, and BFCache restorations are not supported because they skip the lifecycle events Playwright depends on.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Navigation starts<br/>URL change or page interaction\"] --> B{\"Committed?\"}\n  B -- \"canceled\" --> C[\"Unresolved DNS or file download\"]\n  B -- \"committed\" --> D[\"Loading: response body, parse, scripts, DOMContentLoaded\"]\n  D --> E[\"load event\"]\n  E --> F[\"Dynamically loaded scripts\"]\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [docs/src/navigations.md:L169-L186](docs/src/navigations.md#L169-L186)\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/evaluating.md:L8-L124](docs/src/evaluating.md#L8-L124)\n- [docs/src/evaluating.md:L157-L202](docs/src/evaluating.md#L157-L202)\n- [docs/src/evaluating.md:L375-L436](docs/src/evaluating.md#L375-L436)\n- [docs/src/navigations.md:L10-L167](docs/src/navigations.md#L10-L167)\n"}, {"path": "docs/advanced/tracing.md", "title": "Tracing and Debugging", "parent": null, "description": "Capturing and viewing execution traces, screenshots, videos, and debugging test failures.", "files": ["docs/src/trace-viewer.md", "docs/src/trace-viewer-intro-js.md", "docs/src/debug.md"], "markdown": "# Tracing and Debugging\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [docs/src/trace-viewer.md](docs/src/trace-viewer.md)\n- [docs/src/trace-viewer-intro-js.md](docs/src/trace-viewer-intro-js.md)\n- [docs/src/debug.md](docs/src/debug.md)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nPlaywright provides two complementary mechanisms for understanding test behavior: execution traces capture a complete recording of test actions and state, while debugging tools offer interactive inspection and control during test execution.\n\nTraces record screenshots, snapshots of the DOM, network requests, console logs, and metadata for each action in a test. The Trace Viewer is a GUI that lets you replay this recording step-by-step. Debugging tools such as the Playwright Inspector and VS Code extension allow you to pause execution, step through code, inspect elements, and edit locators live.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/trace-viewer.md:L8-L18](docs/src/trace-viewer.md#L8-L18)\n\n## Recording Execution Traces\n\nTraces are recorded as compressed ZIP files containing action details, DOM snapshots, and media. The recommended approach for the test runner is to record traces on the first retry of a failed test using the `trace: 'on-first-retry'` option in `playwright.config.ts`:\n\n```js\nimport { defineConfig } from '@playwright/test';\nexport default defineConfig({\n  retries: 1,\n  use: {\n    trace: 'on-first-retry',\n  },\n});\n```\n\nOther trace options include `'on'` (record every test), `'on-all-retries'` (record all retries), `'off'` (disable), and `'retain-on-failure'` (record only failed tests).\n\nWhen recording, you can enable several data capture modes: `screenshots: true` records a screencast of the browser, `snapshots: true` captures DOM snapshots before, during, and after each action, and `sources: true` includes test source code locations.\n\nFor non-test-runner usage, call the tracing API on the browser context:\n\n```js\nawait context.tracing.start({ screenshots: true, snapshots: true });\n// ... run test ...\nawait context.tracing.stop({ path: 'trace.zip' });\n```\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/trace-viewer.md:L96-L132](docs/src/trace-viewer.md#L96-L132)\n- [docs/src/trace-viewer-intro-js.md:L23-L44](docs/src/trace-viewer-intro-js.md#L23-L44)\n\n## Opening and Viewing Traces\n\nTraces are stored as `trace.zip` files. After running tests with tracing enabled, open them with the CLI:\n\n```bash\nnpx playwright show-trace path/to/trace.zip\n```\n\nRemote traces are supported by passing a URL:\n\n```bash\nnpx playwright show-trace https://example.com/trace.zip\n```\n\nAlternatively, upload the trace file to [trace.playwright.dev](https://trace.playwright.dev) using drag-and-drop or the file picker. The viewer runs entirely in your browser and transmits no data externally.\n\nThe HTML test report also embeds trace access. After running `npx playwright show-report`, click the trace icon next to a test name to open its trace directly, or navigate to the detailed test view and click the trace screenshot in the Traces tab.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/trace-viewer.md:L20-L72](docs/src/trace-viewer.md#L20-L72)\n\n## Trace Viewer Interface\n\nThe Trace Viewer displays six primary panels for analyzing test execution:\n\n| Panel | Purpose |\n|-------|---------|\n| **Actions** | List of test steps with timing; hover to see before/after snapshots |\n| **Screenshots** | Film strip of rendered frames; hover to magnify and find specific moments |\n| **Snapshots** | DOM tree at three points per action: Before, Action, After |\n| **Call** | Timing, locator used, strictness mode, and keyboard input for the selected action |\n| **Log** | Detailed trace of what Playwright did: scrolling, waiting for visibility, performing clicks |\n| **Errors** | Error messages with timeline highlighting and source location |\n\nAdditional tabs show console logs (with browser vs. test origin indicated) and network requests (sortable by type, status, method, timing, and size).\n\nEach action can be clicked to inspect its details. The timeline slider narrows the view to a time range, filtering console and network logs accordingly. Use the Source panel to see the test code location that triggered each action.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/trace-viewer.md:L686-L770](docs/src/trace-viewer.md#L686-L770)\n\n## Debugging with the Playwright Inspector\n\nThe Playwright Inspector is a GUI for interactive debugging. Launch it with the `--debug` flag:\n\n```bash\nnpx playwright test --debug\n```\n\nThis opens the inspector alongside the browser, configures headed mode, and disables timeouts. While paused, you can step through actions using the toolbar controls, live-edit locators in the Pick Locator field (with matching elements highlighting in the browser), pick new locators by hovering elements, and review actionability logs showing whether the element was visible, enabled, stable, and scrolled into view.\n\nInsert a `page.pause()` call in your test to stop execution at a breakpoint:\n\n```js\nawait page.pause();\n```\n\nWhen running with `--debug`, execution pauses at this call. Click Resume to continue.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/debug.md:L70-L225](docs/src/debug.md#L70-L225)\n"}, {"path": "docs/advanced/network-mocking.md", "title": "Network Mocking", "parent": null, "description": "Mocking network requests, responses, intercepting routes, and simulating offline scenarios.", "files": ["docs/src/mock.md", "docs/src/network.md", "docs/src/mock-browser-js.md"], "markdown": "# Network Mocking\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [docs/src/mock.md](docs/src/mock.md)\n- [docs/src/network.md](docs/src/network.md)\n- [docs/src/mock-browser-js.md](docs/src/mock-browser-js.md)\n</cite>\n\n## Introduction\n\nNetwork mocking intercepts and controls HTTP, HTTPS, and WebSocket traffic between a page and servers. Instead of allowing all requests to reach live APIs, mocking lets tests respond with controlled data, simulate failure conditions, or block requests entirely. This enables reproducible testing without depending on external services, networks, or specific server states.\n\nPlaywright handles network mocking through **routes**. Routes sit between the page and the browser's network stack. The handler runs before the network stack sends the request: [`method: Route.continue`] passes it on, [`method: Route.fulfill`] answers it without touching the network, and [`method: Route.abort`] fails the request.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/network.md:L18-L21](docs/src/network.md#L18-L21)\n- [docs/src/mock.md:L6-L10](docs/src/mock.md#L6-L10)\n\n## Mocking API Requests\n\nThe simplest mocking case is to intercept an API call and return mock data without making a real request. Use [`method: Page.route`] to pattern-match a URL and handle the route:\n\n```js\nawait page.route('*/**/api/v1/fruits', async route => {\n  const json = [{ name: 'Strawberry', id: 21 }];\n  await route.fulfill({ json });\n});\nawait page.goto('https://demo.playwright.dev/api-mocking');\n```\n\nThe route handler intercepts all requests matching the glob pattern before they leave the browser. Calling [`method: Route.fulfill`] responds with the given data and never reaches the server. Routes can be set on a [`method: Page`] for a single page or on [`method: BrowserContext`] to apply to all pages in the context.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/mock.md:L11-L28](docs/src/mock.md#L11-L28)\n- [docs/src/network.md:L408-L415](docs/src/network.md#L408-L415)\n\n## Route Interception\n\nRoutes can do more than mock: they can inspect, modify, or abort requests. The route handler receives a [`method: Route`] object with access to the [`method: Request`]:\n\n- **Abort requests:** Use [`method: Route.abort`] to fail a request, simulating offline or blocked resources. This is useful for testing pages that load without certain resources, like CSS or images.\n- **Modify requests:** Call [`method: Route.continue`] with new headers, method, or POST data to modify the outgoing request before it reaches the server.\n- **Conditional handling:** Routes can inspect `route.request().resourceType()` or headers and choose different actions based on request properties.\n\n```js\n// Abort image requests\nawait page.route('**/*.{png,jpg,jpeg}', route => route.abort());\n\n// Modify headers and continue\nawait page.route('**/*', async route => {\n  const headers = route.request().headers();\n  delete headers['x-secret'];\n  await route.continue({ headers });\n});\n```\n\nRoutes sit between the page and the browser's network stack:\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n  participant Page\n  participant Playwright\n  participant Network as Network stack\n  participant Server\n\n  Page->>Playwright: request\n  Note over Playwright: route handler runs\n  Playwright->>Network: route.continue()\n  Note over Network: adds Cookie, Host, Sec-Fetch-*, ...\n  Network->>Server: request\n  Server-->>Network: response\n  Note over Network: stores cookies\n  Network-->>Playwright: response headers as sent by the server\n  Network-->>Page: response\n```\n\n**Diagram sources**\n- [docs/src/network.md:L706-L722](docs/src/network.md#L706-L722)\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/network.md:L408-L594](docs/src/network.md#L408-L594)\n- [docs/src/network.md:L702-L734](docs/src/network.md#L702-L734)\n\n## Response Modification\n\nSometimes tests need the server to respond, but the response must be modified. Use [`method: Route.fetch`] to get the real response, then [`method: Route.fulfill`] to return it with changes:\n\n```js\nawait page.route('*/**/api/v1/fruits', async route => {\n  const response = await route.fetch();\n  const json = await response.json();\n  json.push({ name: 'Loquat', id: 100 });\n  await route.fulfill({ response, json });\n});\n```\n\nThis approach is useful for injecting test data into real responses or overriding specific fields. The original response headers and status are preserved unless overridden. Response bodies can be replaced as text, JSON, or binary data.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/network.md:L596-L700](docs/src/network.md#L596-L700)\n- [docs/src/mock.md:L106-L218](docs/src/mock.md#L106-L218)\n\n## Mocking WebSockets\n\nWebSocket connections can be mocked and their messages intercepted. Use [`method: Page.routeWebSocket`] to intercept WebSocket connections:\n\n```js\nawait page.routeWebSocket('wss://example.com/ws', ws => {\n  ws.onMessage(message => {\n    if (message === 'request')\n      ws.send('response');\n  });\n});\n```\n\nFor full control, mock the entire WebSocket communication. For partial control, connect to the actual server and intercept or modify messages in-flight:\n\n```js\nawait page.routeWebSocket('wss://example.com/ws', ws => {\n  const server = ws.connectToServer();\n  ws.onMessage(message => {\n    if (message === 'request')\n      server.send('request2');\n    else\n      server.send(message);\n  });\n});\n```\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/mock.md:L449-L565](docs/src/mock.md#L449-L565)\n\n## HAR Files for Offline Testing\n\nHAR (HTTP Archive) files record all network traffic for a page. They are useful for creating offline snapshots of API responses and replaying them in tests without hitting the real server.\n\nTo record a HAR file, use [`method: Page.routeFromHAR`] with `update: true`:\n\n```js\nawait page.routeFromHAR('./hars/fruit.har', {\n  url: '*/**/api/v1/fruits',\n  update: true,\n});\n```\n\nOnce recorded, turn off `update` to replay from the HAR file:\n\n```js\nawait page.routeFromHAR('./hars/fruit.har', {\n  url: '*/**/api/v1/fruits',\n  update: false,\n});\n```\n\nHAR files are JSON-based archives. They can be edited manually to adjust mock data, and optionally packaged as `.zip` files with payloads. HAR replay matches URL and HTTP method strictly; for POST requests, it also matches payloads.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/mock.md:L220-L447](docs/src/mock.md#L220-L447)\n"}, {"path": "docs/advanced/dialogs-downloads.md", "title": "Dialogs and Downloads", "parent": null, "description": "Handling browser dialogs, file downloads, and file picker interactions.", "files": ["docs/src/dialogs.md", "docs/src/downloads.md"], "markdown": "# Dialogs and Downloads\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [docs/src/dialogs.md](docs/src/dialogs.md)\n- [docs/src/downloads.md](docs/src/downloads.md)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nPlaywright handles browser dialogs and file operations automatically in many cases but also provides APIs for custom handling. Dialogs include standard browser prompts like `alert`, `confirm`, and `prompt`, as well as `beforeunload` confirmations and print dialogs. File downloads are captured through event handlers, allowing tests to verify downloads and save files to custom locations.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/dialogs.md:L6-L8](docs/src/dialogs.md#L6-L8)\n- [docs/src/downloads.md:L6-L14](docs/src/downloads.md#L6-L14)\n\n## Handling Standard Dialogs\n\nBy default, Playwright auto-dismisses `alert()`, `confirm()`, and `prompt()` dialogs. To interact with dialogs before they close, register a handler on the `dialog` event and call either `accept()` or `dismiss()`:\n\n```js\npage.on('dialog', dialog => dialog.accept());\nawait page.getByRole('button').click();\n```\n\n```python\npage.on(\"dialog\", lambda dialog: dialog.accept())\nawait page.get_by_role(\"button\").click()\n```\n\nA dialog handler **must dismiss or accept** the dialog, otherwise the action that triggered it will hang indefinitely. Dialogs are modal—the browser blocks page execution until they are resolved. If a handler only logs the message without accepting or dismissing, the subsequent action will stall:\n\n```js\n// WRONG: This will hang\npage.on('dialog', dialog => console.log(dialog.message()));\nawait page.getByRole('button').click();\n```\n\nIf no dialog listener is registered, all dialogs are automatically dismissed.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/dialogs.md:L10-L79](docs/src/dialogs.md#L10-L79)\n\n## beforeunload Dialogs\n\nThe `beforeunload` event fires when a page is closed with unload handlers enabled. Register a dialog handler to manage this special dialog type:\n\n```js\npage.on('dialog', async dialog => {\n  assert(dialog.type() === 'beforeunload');\n  await dialog.dismiss();\n});\nawait page.close({ runBeforeUnload: true });\n```\n\n```python\nasync def handle_dialog(dialog):\n    assert dialog.type == 'beforeunload'\n    await dialog.dismiss()\n\npage.on('dialog', handle_dialog)\nawait page.close(run_before_unload=True)\n```\n\nThe `beforeunload` dialog is unique: calling `Page.close()` with `runBeforeUnload: true` does not guarantee the page actually closes if the dialog is accepted.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/dialogs.md:L81-L128](docs/src/dialogs.md#L81-L128)\n\n## Print Dialogs\n\nPrint dialogs triggered by `window.print()` require custom detection since Playwright does not intercept them directly. Override the `print` function before clicking to detect when it fires:\n\n```js\nawait page.goto('<url>');\nawait page.evaluate('(() => {window.waitForPrintDialog = new Promise(f => window.print = f);})()');\nawait page.getByText('Print it!').click();\nawait page.waitForFunction('window.waitForPrintDialog');\n```\n\n```python\nawait page.goto(\"<url>\")\nawait page.evaluate(\"(() => {window.waitForPrintDialog = new Promise(f => window.print = f);})()\")\nawait page.get_by_text(\"Print it!\").click()\nawait page.wait_for_function(\"window.waitForPrintDialog\")\n```\n\nThe script must be evaluated after the page loads but before the action that triggers printing.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/dialogs.md:L130-L180](docs/src/dialogs.md#L130-L180)\n\n## File Downloads\n\nThe `download` event fires for each file attachment. Wait for the download to complete, then save it using `saveAs()`:\n\n```js\nconst downloadPromise = page.waitForEvent('download');\nawait page.getByText('Download file').click();\nconst download = await downloadPromise;\nawait download.saveAs('/path/to/save/at/' + download.suggestedFilename());\n```\n\n```python\nasync with page.expect_download() as download_info:\n    await page.get_by_text(\"Download file\").click()\ndownload = await download_info.value\nawait download.save_as(\"/path/to/save/at/\" + download.suggested_filename)\n```\n\nBy default, downloaded files go to a temporary folder and are deleted when the browser context closes. Specify a persistent location using the `downloadsPath` option in `BrowserType.launch()`.\n\nAlternatively, listen for the `download` event without awaiting a specific action:\n\n```js\npage.on('download', download => download.path().then(console.log));\n```\n\nThis approach forks control flow and may cause the script to exit before the download completes.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/downloads.md:L6-L98](docs/src/downloads.md#L6-L98)\n\n## File Uploads\n\nFor uploading files, use the file chooser API documented in the input handling section of the repository. See [docs/src/input.md](docs/src/input.md) for details on the `FileChooser` class.\n"}, {"path": "docs/advanced/mobile-testing.md", "title": "Mobile Testing", "parent": null, "description": "Mobile device emulation, Android testing, and touch event handling.", "files": ["docs/src/emulation.md", "docs/src/touch-events.md"], "markdown": "# Mobile Testing\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [docs/src/emulation.md](docs/src/emulation.md)\n- [docs/src/touch-events.md](docs/src/touch-events.md)\n</cite>\n\n## Device Emulation\n\nPlaywright comes with a registry of pre-configured device parameters for selected mobile devices, tablets, and desktops. These device profiles automatically configure browser behavior for specific devices, including user agent, screen size, viewport, and touch support.\n\nTo use a pre-configured device, import the devices registry and pass device parameters when creating a browser context:\n\n```js\nimport { defineConfig, devices } from '@playwright/test';\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  projects: [\n    {\n      name: 'Mobile Safari',\n      use: {\n        ...devices['iPhone 13'],\n      },\n    },\n  ],\n});\n```\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom playwright.async_api import async_playwright\n\nasync def run(playwright):\n    iphone_13 = playwright.devices['iPhone 13']\n    browser = await playwright.webkit.launch()\n    context = await browser.new_context(**iphone_13)\n```\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/emulation.md:L10-L44](docs/src/emulation.md#L10-L44)\n\n## Touch Event Handling\n\nWeb applications that handle touch events can be tested by manually dispatching TouchEvent objects to the page. Playwright provides methods to simulate common touch gestures like pan and pinch by constructing touch point coordinates and dispatching appropriate touch events.\n\n### Pan Gesture\n\nPan gestures move a single touch point across the screen. This is useful for testing scrolling, dragging, or map navigation. The following example implements a reusable pan function that moves a touch point by a given delta over a specified number of steps:\n\n```js\nasync function pan(locator, deltaX = 0, deltaY = 0, steps = 5) {\n  const { centerX, centerY } = await locator.evaluate((target) => {\n    const bounds = target.getBoundingClientRect();\n    const centerX = bounds.left + bounds.width / 2;\n    const centerY = bounds.top + bounds.height / 2;\n    return { centerX, centerY };\n  });\n\n  const touches = [{\n    identifier: 0,\n    clientX: centerX,\n    clientY: centerY,\n  }];\n  await locator.dispatchEvent('touchstart',\n      { touches, changedTouches: touches, targetTouches: touches });\n\n  for (let i = 1; i <= steps; i++) {\n    const touches = [{\n      identifier: 0,\n      clientX: centerX + deltaX * i / steps,\n      clientY: centerY + deltaY * i / steps,\n    }];\n    await locator.dispatchEvent('touchmove',\n        { touches, changedTouches: touches, targetTouches: touches });\n  }\n\n  await locator.dispatchEvent('touchend');\n}\n```\n\n### Pinch Gesture\n\nPinch gestures involve two touch points moving toward or away from each other for zoom effects. The following function implements pinch by converging touch points (zoom out) or diverging them (zoom in):\n\n```js\nasync function pinch(locator,\n  arg = { deltaX: 50, steps: 5, direction: 'in' }) {\n  const { centerX, centerY } = await locator.evaluate((target) => {\n    const bounds = target.getBoundingClientRect();\n    const centerX = bounds.left + bounds.width / 2;\n    const centerY = bounds.top + bounds.height / 2;\n    return { centerX, centerY };\n  });\n\n  const deltaX = arg.deltaX ?? 50;\n  const steps = arg.steps ?? 5;\n  const stepDeltaX = deltaX / (steps + 1);\n\n  const touches = [\n    {\n      identifier: 0,\n      clientX: centerX - (arg.direction === 'in' ? deltaX : stepDeltaX),\n      clientY: centerY,\n    },\n    {\n      identifier: 1,\n      clientX: centerX + (arg.direction === 'in' ? deltaX : stepDeltaX),\n      clientY: centerY,\n    },\n  ];\n  await locator.dispatchEvent('touchstart',\n      { touches, changedTouches: touches, targetTouches: touches });\n\n  for (let i = 1; i <= steps; i++) {\n    const offset = (arg.direction === 'in' ? (deltaX - i * stepDeltaX) : (stepDeltaX * (i + 1)));\n    const touches = [\n      {\n        identifier: 0,\n        clientX: centerX - offset,\n        clientY: centerY,\n      },\n      {\n        identifier: 1,\n        clientX: centerX + offset,\n        clientY: centerY,\n      },\n    ];\n    await locator.dispatchEvent('touchmove',\n        { touches, changedTouches: touches, targetTouches: touches });\n  }\n\n  await locator.dispatchEvent('touchend', { touches: [], changedTouches: [], targetTouches: [] });\n}\n```\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/touch-events.md:L6-L66](docs/src/touch-events.md#L6-L66)\n- [docs/src/touch-events.md:L309-L380](docs/src/touch-events.md#L309-L380)\n\n## Mobile Configuration Options\n\nMobile contexts can be configured with device-specific options. The `isMobile` property determines whether the meta viewport tag is respected and touch events are enabled. Device profiles automatically set appropriate values, but these can be overridden when needed.\n\n```js\nconst context = await browser.newContext({\n  isMobile: true,\n  viewport: { width: 412, height: 915 },\n});\n```\n\nDevice profiles assume a specific platform, with platform-specific user agent strings included. If platform-agnostic testing is required, the user agent can be explicitly unset:\n\n```js\nconst context = await browser.newContext({\n  ...devices['Desktop Chrome'],\n  userAgent: undefined,\n});\n```\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/emulation.md:L265-L309](docs/src/emulation.md#L265-L309)\n- [docs/src/emulation.md:L93-L102](docs/src/emulation.md#L93-L102)\n\n## Android Device Testing\n\nAndroid devices such as Pixel 7 can be tested using pre-configured device profiles. The Pixel 7 profile automatically configures the context with Android-specific settings including mobile user agent, screen dimensions, device scale factor, and touch support. The following example demonstrates touch gesture testing on an Android device:\n\n```js\nimport { test, expect, devices, type Locator } from '@playwright/test';\n\ntest.use({ ...devices['Pixel 7'] });\n\nasync function pan(locator, deltaX = 0, deltaY = 0, steps = 5) {\n  const { centerX, centerY } = await locator.evaluate((target) => {\n    const bounds = target.getBoundingClientRect();\n    const centerX = bounds.left + bounds.width / 2;\n    const centerY = bounds.top + bounds.height / 2;\n    return { centerX, centerY };\n  });\n\n  const touches = [{\n    identifier: 0,\n    clientX: centerX,\n    clientY: centerY,\n  }];\n  await locator.dispatchEvent('touchstart',\n      { touches, changedTouches: touches, targetTouches: touches });\n\n  for (let i = 1; i <= steps; i++) {\n    const touches = [{\n      identifier: 0,\n      clientX: centerX + deltaX * i / steps,\n      clientY: centerY + deltaY * i / steps,\n    }];\n    await locator.dispatchEvent('touchmove',\n        { touches, changedTouches: touches, targetTouches: touches });\n  }\n\n  await locator.dispatchEvent('touchend');\n}\n\ntest('pan gesture on Android', async ({ page }) => {\n  await page.goto('https://www.google.com/maps/place/@37.4117722,-122.0713234,15z',\n      { waitUntil: 'commit' });\n  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Keep using web' }).click();\n  await expect(page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Keep using web' })).not.toBeVisible();\n  const met = page.locator('[data-test-id=\"met\"]');\n  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++)\n    await pan(met, 200, 100);\n  await expect(met).toHaveScreenshot();\n});\n```\n\n**Section sources**\n- [docs/src/touch-events.md:L16-L66](docs/src/touch-events.md#L16-L66)\n"}, {"path": "guides/testing-strategy.md", "title": "Testing Strategy", "parent": null, "description": "Organization of library tests, page tests, MCP tests, and cross-browser testing approach.", "files": ["AGENTS.md"], "markdown": "# Testing Strategy\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md)\n</cite>\n\n## Test Organization\n\nThe Playwright repository organizes tests into four primary directories, each serving a specific purpose in the validation strategy. The `tests/page/` directory holds user interaction tests—click, fill, navigate, and locator operations. The `tests/library/` directory validates browser lifecycle, context management, cookies, and permissions. The `tests/playwright-test/` directory tests the test runner itself, including reporters, configuration, annotations, and retry logic. The `tests/mcp/` directory exercises MCP (Model Context Protocol) tools through the client API. Each directory uses specialized fixtures appropriate to its scope, preventing overuse of heavy resources like browser instances and ensuring tests run efficiently.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [AGENTS.md:L80-L89](AGENTS.md#L80-L89)\n\n## Running Tests\n\nThree command families cover different scopes and browsers. `npm run ctest <filter>` runs Chromium-only library tests for fast development feedback. `npm run test <filter>` runs all library tests across specified browsers using `--project=chromium`, `--project=firefox`, or `--project=webkit`. `npm run ttest <filter>` isolates test runner tests in `tests/playwright-test/`. For MCP tools, `npm run ctest-mcp <filter>` tests Chromium only, while `npm run test-mcp <filter>` runs the full browser matrix. Test filtering supports file paths, specific line numbers, and test names via `--grep`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [AGENTS.md:L60-L78](AGENTS.md#L60-L78)\n\n## Test Directories and Fixtures\n\n| Directory | Fixture Import | Key Fixtures | Purpose |\n|-----------|---|---|---|\n| `tests/page/` | `import { test, expect } from './pageTest'` | `page`, `server`, `browserName` | User interactions: clicks, form fills, navigation |\n| `tests/library/` | `import { browserTest, expect } from '../config/browserTest'` | `browser`, `context`, `browserType` | Browser creation, context lifecycle, permissions |\n| `tests/playwright-test/` | `import { test, expect } from './playwright-test-fixtures'` | test runner fixtures | Test runner behavior: reporters, config, retries |\n| `tests/mcp/` | `import { test, expect } from './fixtures'` | `client`, `server` | MCP tool invocation via `client.callTool()` |\n\nThe decision rule is simple: if a test needs `browser`, `browserType`, or `context`, it belongs in `tests/library/`. If it only needs `page` and `server`, it belongs in `tests/page/`. This boundary keeps resource consumption low and test execution fast.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [AGENTS.md:L80-L89](AGENTS.md#L80-L89)\n\n## Cross-Browser Testing\n\nRegression tests must run on all three browsers—Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit—to validate fixes work across the entire platform. The `npm run ctest <file>`, `npm run ftest <file>`, and `npm run wtest <file>` commands each target a single browser for development iteration. Use `--headed` flag for visual inspection during investigation. Tests should not make assumptions about browser-specific behavior unless that behavior is intentionally browser-specific; use fixtures and conditional logic to isolate browser gaps. When a gap is confirmed as a browser limitation rather than a Playwright issue, mark it with a `fixme` comment and link to the upstream issue.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [AGENTS.md:L99-L104](AGENTS.md#L99-L104)\n\n## Test Scope and Coverage Guidelines\n\nBefore writing a new test, search for an existing test that covers the same feature and API. If one exists, add the new edge case to it rather than creating a new file. New features need only mainstream scenarios and important edge cases—avoid matrices that test every option combination. Regression tests should generalize the provided case into a meaningful test scenario and stop there; do not add extra tests around the regression.\n\nTag regression tests with the issue URL using the annotation: `test('...', { annotation: { type: 'issue', description: '<issue url>' } }, ...)`. Avoid `test.beforeAll`, `test.afterAll`, or manual `http.createServer` setup. Instead, use provided fixtures: `server.setRoute(...)`, `server.setRedirect(...)`, `server.PREFIX`, and `server.EMPTY_PAGE` for endpoint setup. Drive page state with `page.setContent(...)` or `page.goto(server.PREFIX + '/...')` to keep tests self-contained and reproducible.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [AGENTS.md:L91-L104](AGENTS.md#L91-L104)\n"}, {"path": "guides/build-system.md", "title": "Build System", "parent": null, "description": "Playwright's build infrastructure, watch mode, bundling, and artifact generation.", "files": ["package.json", "AGENTS.md"], "markdown": "# Build System\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [package.json](package.json)\n- [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md)\n</cite>\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Playwright monorepo uses a Node.js-based build system. The root `package.json` defines npm scripts that orchestrate compilation, code generation, linting, and testing across the workspace. Build commands operate on a workspace of multiple packages including the core browser automation engine, test runner, and supporting tools.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [package.json:L18-L54](package.json#L18-L54)\n- [AGENTS.md:L41-L48](AGENTS.md#L41-L48)\n\n## Core Build Commands\n\nThree primary commands control the build:\n\n- `npm run build` — Compiles the full monorepo, generating artifacts and bundles in a single pass.\n- `npm run watch` — Runs in watch mode with the `--lint` flag, monitoring source files and regenerating artifacts automatically. Recommended for active development.\n- `npm run clean` — Removes all build artifacts and compiled output.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [package.json:L42-L44](package.json#L42-L44)\n\n## Watch Mode and Development\n\nWatch mode (`npm run watch`) is the standard development setup. It continuously monitors source files and regenerates three categories of output automatically:\n\n- **Protocol channels** — RPC types from `spec/*.yml` protocol definitions\n- **TypeScript types** — Type stubs and type definitions from source code\n- **Validators** — Serialization and validation code for protocol messages\n\nSince watch is running and generating these artifacts in the background, generated files stay current without manual steps.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [AGENTS.md:L44-L48](AGENTS.md#L44-L48)\n\n## Artifact Generation and Type Safety\n\nThe build system auto-generates code from multiple sources:\n\n- `utils/generate_channels.js` — Produces RPC channel types from protocol specifications\n- `utils/generate_types/` — Generates TypeScript type definitions and validates them against test files\n\nBoth generators run during watch mode. The TypeScript type generation is verified by `npm run test-types`, which compiles test type definitions against the generated stubs to catch incompatibilities early.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [AGENTS.md:L38-L48](AGENTS.md#L38-L48)\n\n## Quality Gates: Flint\n\nBefore uploading a PR, run `npm run flint` to execute all lint checks in parallel:\n\n- `eslint` — JavaScript and TypeScript linting\n- `tsc` — Full TypeScript type checking\n- `doc` — Documentation linting via `utils/doclint/cli.js`\n- `check-deps` — Verifies import boundaries using `DEPS.list` files (enforces separation between client and server code)\n- `generate_channels` and `generate_types` — Confirms generated code is up to date\n- `lint-tests` — Test file linting\n- `test-types` — Validates generated type definitions\n- `lint-packages` — Checks workspace consistency\n- Code snippet linting — Ensures documentation examples compile correctly\n- `check-dependency-age` — Validates all `package-lock.json` versions are at least 7 days old unless a newer version fixes a high or critical security advisory\n\nRun `flint` only after `npm run build` completes and only before uploading a PR. Do not run it before each commit, and avoid using `tsc --noEmit` or individual lint commands separately.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [AGENTS.md:L50-L58](AGENTS.md#L50-L58)\n\n## Monorepo Structure\n\nThe build system manages a workspace with multiple packages:\n\n```json\n\"workspaces\": [\n  \"packages/*\"\n]\n```\n\nKey packages include `playwright-core` (browser engine), `playwright` (public package), `@playwright/test` (test runner), `@playwright/client` (standalone client), and tooling packages like `html-reporter`, `trace-viewer`, `recorder`, and `web`. Build commands propagate across all workspace packages via npm workspace semantics.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [package.json:L55-L56](package.json#L55-L56)\n"}, {"path": "guides/code-generation.md", "title": "Code Generation", "parent": null, "description": "Generated code artifacts including protocol channels, type validators, and documentation.", "files": ["utils/generate_channels.js", "AGENTS.md", "utils/avd_start.sh", "utils/build_android_driver.sh", "utils/check_npm_packages.sh", "utils/draft_patch_release_notes.js", "utils/generate_chromium_default_font_families.js", "utils/generate_injected.js", "utils/libwebp-wasm/build.sh", "utils/libwebp-wasm/webp_wasm.c"], "markdown": "# Code Generation\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [utils/generate_channels.js](utils/generate_channels.js)\n- [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md)\n- [utils/avd_start.sh](utils/avd_start.sh)\n- [utils/build_android_driver.sh](utils/build_android_driver.sh)\n- [utils/check_npm_packages.sh](utils/check_npm_packages.sh)\n- [utils/draft_patch_release_notes.js](utils/draft_patch_release_notes.js)\n- [utils/generate_chromium_default_font_families.js](utils/generate_chromium_default_font_families.js)\n- [utils/generate_injected.js](utils/generate_injected.js)\n- [utils/libwebp-wasm/build.sh](utils/libwebp-wasm/build.sh)\n- [utils/libwebp-wasm/webp_wasm.c](utils/libwebp-wasm/webp_wasm.c)\n</cite>\n\n## Protocol Specification System\n\nPlaywright's client-server architecture relies on a protocol specification that defines all communication between the browser automation client and server. The core generator, `utils/generate_channels.js`, reads protocol definitions and produces TypeScript type definitions, runtime validators, and metadata used throughout the codebase.\n\nThe generator processes interface definitions, object types, and enums from the protocol specification. It tracks inheritance relationships between interfaces and maintains consistency across client and server implementations. Protocol specifications define initializer properties, command methods with parameters and return types, and events that channels can emit.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [utils/generate_channels.js:L187-L217](utils/generate_channels.js#L187-L217)\n\n## Channel and Type Generation\n\nThe protocol generator produces three primary channel output files. The `structs.d.ts` file contains shared type definitions for all objects and enums that cross the client-server boundary. The client channels file defines interfaces that the client uses to call server methods and listen for events. The server channels file (dispatcher variant) defines the interface that server implementations must satisfy.\n\nFor each interface in the protocol, the generator creates:\n- An initializer type containing properties passed when the channel is created\n- A channel interface with method signatures for all protocol commands\n- Event signatures for all emitted events\n- Parameter and result types for each method\n- Event trait types for compile-time dispatch\n\nThe generator also produces method metadata that records whether methods are internal or public, their display titles for UI rendering, special handling flags like snapshot and pause, and parameter information for trace visualization.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [utils/generate_channels.js:L239-L405](utils/generate_channels.js#L239-L405)\n- [utils/generate_channels.js:L483-L487](utils/generate_channels.js#L483-L487)\n\n## Validators and Runtime Checks\n\nType validators enforce protocol compliance at runtime. The validator generator produces a scheme-based validation system using primitives like `tString`, `tInt`, `tArray`, `tObject`, `tEnum`, and `tChannel`. Each validator registers schemes for every type that crosses the protocol boundary.\n\nValidators catch serialization errors, type mismatches, and invalid channel references before they propagate. The validation system supports optional fields, nested objects, arrays, binary data, and channel references. When a protocol method is called or returns a value, validators verify the structure matches the protocol specification.\n\nThe validator system also supports polymorphic channels through channel type discrimination—when a channel can be one of several derived types, validators correctly identify which type was received based on inheritance relationships in the protocol.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [utils/generate_channels.js:L141-L163](utils/generate_channels.js#L141-L163)\n\n## Injected Browser Scripts\n\nPlaywright injects scripts into browser pages to implement features like element selection, event listening, storage access, WebAuthn support, and WebSocket interception. These scripts are written in TypeScript, bundled with esbuild, and embedded as string constants in the automation layer.\n\nThe generator `utils/generate_injected.js` processes thirteen source scripts:\n- `utilityScript.ts` and `injectedScript.ts` for DOM interaction and observation\n- `recorder/pollingRecorder.ts` for test recording\n- `webAuthn.ts` for WebAuthn/FIDO2 support\n- `webSocketMock.ts` for WebSocket mocking and interception\n- `clock.ts`, `storageScript.ts`, and others for feature-specific functionality\n\nEach script is bundled to CommonJS format with CSS inlined as strings. The generator replaces the standard esbuild module prefix with a custom one that avoids dependencies on Node.js builtins, ensuring the code runs in browser contexts.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [utils/generate_injected.js:L28-L93](utils/generate_injected.js#L28-L93)\n- [utils/generate_injected.js:L145-L165](utils/generate_injected.js#L145-L165)\n\n## Supporting Generators\n\nAdditional specialized generators produce other required artifacts. The font families generator (`utils/generate_chromium_default_font_families.js`) extracts default font family mappings from Chromium's locale resource files, supporting multiple scripts and platforms to ensure consistent text rendering across browsers.\n\nThe WebP codec generator (`utils/libwebp-wasm/build.sh`) compiles libwebp to WebAssembly using Emscripten, producing a synchronous codec module for image comparison operations. The build includes both SIMD and scalar variants and regenerates third-party license notices from upstream sources.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\nGenerated files are produced automatically during watch mode via `npm run watch`. All generators must run successfully before submitting pull requests. The build and lint process verifies this:\n\n```\nnpm run build\nnpm run flint\n```\n\nThe `flint` command runs all linters in parallel, including protocol channel generation, type validation, and other checks. Generated files must never be edited manually—modify the source specifications, protocol definitions, or the generator scripts instead. Generated files are marked with a header comment indicating they are auto-generated and should not be modified.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [AGENTS.md:L41-L58](AGENTS.md#L41-L58)\n"}, {"path": "guides/dependency-management.md", "title": "Dependency Management", "parent": null, "description": "Monorepo workspace configuration, DEPS rules, and dependency checks.", "files": ["package.json", "tsconfig.json", "utils/check_deps.js", "utils/check_dependency_age.js", "utils/copyright.js", "utils/eslint-plugin-progress/index.js", "utils/generate_cli_help.js", "utils/generate_types/index.js"], "markdown": "# Dependency Management\n\n<cite>\n**Referenced Files in This Document**\n- [package.json](package.json)\n- [tsconfig.json](tsconfig.json)\n- [utils/check_deps.js](utils/check_deps.js)\n- [utils/check_dependency_age.js](utils/check_dependency_age.js)\n- [utils/copyright.js](utils/copyright.js)\n- [utils/eslint-plugin-progress/index.js](utils/eslint-plugin-progress/index.js)\n- [utils/generate_cli_help.js](utils/generate_cli_help.js)\n- [utils/generate_types/index.js](utils/generate_types/index.js)\n</cite>\n\n## Monorepo Workspace Configuration\n\nPlaywright is organized as a monorepo using npm workspaces. The root `package.json` declares workspace packages in the `packages/` directory, enabling local development and cross-package dependency resolution without publishing intermediate packages.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [package.json:L55-L57](package.json#L55-L57)\n\n## DEPS System and Import Boundaries\n\nThe repository enforces strict import boundaries through a `DEPS.list` file system. Over 50 `DEPS.list` files are distributed across the codebase to declare which code can import from which directories. The core rule is absolute: **client code NEVER imports server code, and server code NEVER imports client code**. All cross-module communication flows through the protocol layer only.\n\nEach `DEPS.list` file contains a bracketed group name followed by allowed import paths. Special markers include:\n- `***` — allows imports from anywhere\n- `\"strict\"` — only permits explicitly listed imports\n- `node_modules/<package>` — permits a specific external package\n- `@<package>/<path>` — reference to internal packages\n- Relative paths — allow imports from specific directories\n\nThe `check-deps` script validates these rules at build time by parsing all TypeScript source files and cross-checking their imports against the applicable `DEPS.list` file for each directory.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [utils/check_deps.js:L43-L62](utils/check_deps.js#L43-L62)\n- [utils/check_deps.js:L210-L245](utils/check_deps.js#L210-L245)\n\n## Dependency Validation\n\nThe repository runs two separate dependency checks during the `npm run flint` process:\n\n**check-deps** verifies that all runtime imports match declared boundaries in `DEPS.list` files and that every external import is listed in the package's `package.json`. Vendored packages bundled under `bundles/utils/node_modules/` bypass the standard dependency check; their use is authorized through per-file `node_modules/<pkg>` declarations in `DEPS.list`.\n\n**check-dependency-age** ensures every package in `package-lock.json` was published at least 7 days ago. This delay allows time for community review before a compromised release reaches the repository. The only exception is when a newer version fixes a high or critical severity advisory and no older version resolves it; in that case the script reports the advisory details and approves the exception.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [utils/check_deps.js:L65-L99](utils/check_deps.js#L65-L99)\n- [utils/check_dependency_age.js:L20-L42](utils/check_dependency_age.js#L20-L42)\n- [utils/check_dependency_age.js:L126-L184](utils/check_dependency_age.js#L126-L184)\n\n## TypeScript Path Mapping\n\nThe monorepo uses TypeScript path aliases to simplify imports across workspace packages and internal modules. The `tsconfig.json` defines mappings for internal packages:\n\n- `@dashboard/*` → `./packages/dashboard/src/*`\n- `@trace-viewer/*` → `./packages/trace-viewer/src/*`\n- `@html-reporter/*` → `./packages/html-reporter/src/*`\n- `@injected/*` → `./packages/injected/src/*`\n- `@isomorphic/*` → `./packages/isomorphic/*`\n- `@utils/*` → `./packages/utils/*`\n- `@protocol/*` → `./packages/protocol/src/*`\n- `@recorder/*` → `./packages/recorder/src/*`\n- `playwright-core/lib/*` → `./packages/playwright-core/src/*`\n- `playwright/lib/*` → `./packages/playwright/src/*`\n\nThe two alias patterns serve distinct purposes: `@<pkg>/*` is used for type-only imports, while `<pkg>/lib/*` indicates runtime dependencies that require explicit inclusion in `package.json`.\n\n**Section sources**\n- [tsconfig.json:L7-L26](tsconfig.json#L7-L26)\n"}]};
